rray should be Object\nparams. See `param` argument in `builder.objectType`."},"328":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-114","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#uniontypeoptions","content":"`resolveType`: A function called when resolving the type of a union value. `parent` will be a\nunion of the backing models of the types provided in `types`. Return a member ref, its registered\nclass, or its GraphQL type name. If omitted, GraphQL uses\n`__typename` or member `isTypeOf` checks. See Unions."},"329":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-115","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#uniontypeoptions","content":"`types` can also be a record, or a function returning one. `resolveType` then returns one of its\nkeys:"},"330":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-116","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#uniontypeoptions","content":"With `StrictResolveType: true` in `SchemaTypes`, use the record form, or an array of type names;\n`resolveType` may only return members. See\nStrict resolveType."},"331":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-117","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#enumtypeparam-options","content":"`param`: A string name of the enum or a typescript enum"},"332":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-118","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#enumtypeparam-options","content":"`options`: `EnumTypeOptions`"},"333":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-119","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#enumtypeoptions","content":"`description`: A description of the current type"},"334":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-120","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#enumtypeoptions","content":"`values`: can be either an array of strings (you may need to use `as const` to get proper type\nnames) or a `GraphQLEnumValueConfigMap`. values is only required when param is not an enum"},"335":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-121","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#enumtypeoptions","content":"`name`: required when param is an enum"},"336":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-122","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#enumtypeoptions","content":"When `values` is a map, each entry accepts these options:"},"337":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-123","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#addscalartypename-scalar-options","content":"`name`: A key of the `Scalars` property in `SchemaTypes`"},"338":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-124","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#addscalartypename-scalar-options","content":"`scalar`: A `GraphQLScalarType`"},"339":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-125","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#addscalartypename-scalar-options","content":"`options`: optional scalar options that override the supplied scalar configuration."},"340":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-126","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#scalartypename-options","content":"`name`: A key of the `Scalars` property in `SchemaTypes`"},"341":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-127","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#scalartypename-options","content":"`options`: `ScalarTypeOptions`"},"342":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-128","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#scalartypeoptions","content":"For portable scalar definitions, provide `serialize`, `parseValue`, and `parseLiteral`.\nGraphQL.js 17 also supports `coerceOutputValue`, `coerceInputValue`, `coerceInputLiteral`, and\n`valueToLiteral`. These hooks have version-specific requirements; see Scalars."},"343":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-129","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#inputtypeparam-options","content":"`param`: a string or InputRef created by `builder.inputRef`"},"344":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-130","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#inputtypeparam-options","content":"`options`: `InputTypeOptions`"},"345":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-131","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#inputtypeoptions","content":"`description`: A description of the current type"},"346":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-132","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#inputtypeoptions","content":"`fields`: a function that receives an `InputFieldBuilder`, and returns an object of field names to\nfield definitions. See `InputFieldBuilder` for more details. If `param` is\na key of the `Inputs` property in `SchemaTypes`, shape will show type errors for any fields that do\nnot match the types provided in `SchemaTypes`."},"347":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-133","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#inputtypeoptions","content":"`isOneOf`: defines a OneOf input object on supported GraphQL.js versions. See\nInput Objects. A OneOf input accepts exactly one non-null field; its fields\nmust be optional and have no defaults. Use a GraphQL.js version with OneOf support."},"348":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-134","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#inputreftname","content":"Creates a Ref object representing an input object that has not been implemented. This can be useful for\ndefining recursive input types, for building certain types of plugins, or when building a modular\nschema where you don't want to define all types in SchemaTypes, or import the actual implementation\nof each input type you use."},"349":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-135","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#inputreftname","content":"`name`: string, name of the type that this ref represents. Can be overwritten when implemented."},"350":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-136","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#inputreftname","content":"`T`: a type param to define the backing shape for the type that this ref represents"},"351":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-137","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#argsfields","content":"Creates an arguments object which can be used as the `args` option in a field definition."},"352":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-138","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#argsfields","content":"`fields`: a function that receives an `ArgBuilder`, and returns an object of\nfield names to field definitions. See `ArgBuilder` for more details."},"353":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-139","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#ast-metadata","content":"Type definitions, output fields, input fields, arguments, and enum values accept an optional\n`astNode` from `graphql`. Use the matching definition-node type shown in the option summaries;\nfor example, an object type takes an `ObjectTypeDefinitionNode`, while an enum value takes an\n`EnumValueDefinitionNode`. Pothos attaches the node to the corresponding GraphQL.js schema element\nfor tools that read AST metadata. Continue to define fields, values, and resolver behavior through\nthe Pothos options; attaching a node does not build those definitions from SDL."},"354":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-140","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#ast-metadata","content":"For applied directive metadata, see Directives. GraphQL.js `printSchema`\ndoes not preserve applied custom directives; see Printing Schemas\nwhen choosing an output format for downstream tools."},"355":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-141","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#toschemaoptions","content":"Builds a `GraphQLSchema` from the types registered on the builder. The optional object accepts:"},"356":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-142","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#toschemaoptions","content":"`directives`: custom `GraphQLDirective` instances to include in the schema."},"357":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-143","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#toschemaoptions","content":"`extensions`: schema metadata."},"358":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-144","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#toschemaoptions","content":"`sortSchema`: defaults to `true`; set to `false` to skip lexicographic sorting."},"359":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-145","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#toschemaoptions","content":"`astNode`: a `SchemaDefinitionNode` from `graphql`; see AST metadata."},"360":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-146","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#toschemaoptions","content":"Plugins may contribute build-time options. Calling `toSchema` again creates a new schema and\nnew plugin instances."},"361":{"id":"/docs/api/schema-builder-147","page_id":"/docs/api/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/api/schema-builder#schemabuilderallowpluginreregistration","content":"`SchemaBuilder.allowPluginReRegistration` is a static `boolean` on the SchemaBuilder class that can\nbe set to allow plugins to call registerPlugin multiple times. This is useful for hot-module\nreloading, but is `false` by default to catch any issues with duplicate versions of a plugin."},"362":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","type":"page","content":"File layout","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/app-layout"},"363":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout","content":"Guide for Pothos app layouts"},"364":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#files","content":"Files"},"365":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#imports","content":"Imports"},"366":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#plugins","content":"Plugins"},"367":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#backing-models","content":"Backing models"},"368":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#co-locating-queries","content":"Co-locating queries"},"369":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout","content":"Pothos tries not to be opinionated about how you structure your code, and provides multiple ways of\ndoing many things. This short guide covers a few conventions I use, as a starting place for anyone\nwho is just looking for a decent setup that should just work. Everything suggested here is just a\nrecommendation and is completely optional."},"370":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#files","content":"Here are a few files I create in almost every Pothos schema I have built:"},"371":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#files","content":"`src/server.ts`: Setup and run your server (This might be graphql-yoga or @apollo/server)"},"372":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#files","content":"`src/builder.ts`: Setup for your schema builder. Does not contain any definitions for types in\nyour schema"},"373":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#files","content":"`src/schema.ts` or `src/schema/index.ts`: Imports the schema modules, initializes root types such\nas `Query`, and exports `builder.toSchema()`"},"374":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#files","content":"`src/types.ts`: Define shared types used across your schema including a type for your context\nobject. This should be imported when creating your builder, and may be used by many other files."},"375":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#files","content":"`src/schema/*.ts`: Actual definitions for your schema types."},"376":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#imports","content":"Import types directly from the files that define them rather than importing from another file like\n`index.ts` that re-exports them. `index.ts` files can still be useful for loading all files in a\ndirectory, but they should generally NOT export any values."},"377":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#plugins","content":"Which plugins you use is completely up to you. For my own projects, I will use the `simple-objects`,\n`scope-auth`, and `mocks` plugins in every project, and some of the other plugins as needed."},"378":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#plugins","content":"`mocks` and `scope-auth` are fairly self explanatory. The `simple-objects` plugin can make building\nout a graph much quicker, because you don't have to have explicit types or models for every object\nin your graph. I frequently find that I just want to add an object of a specific shape, and then let\nthe parent field figure out how to return an object of the right shape."},"379":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-16","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#backing-models","content":"You can use your application's TypeScript types as backing models with `objectRef`. For example,\nkeep the user data type in `src/types.ts` and define its GraphQL fields in `src/schema/user.ts`:"},"380":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-17","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#backing-models","content":"Create the query root and import the schema modules before building the schema:"},"381":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-18","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#backing-models","content":"Other schema modules can import `User` directly from `schema/user.ts` when they need to reference\nthe GraphQL type. If your types reference each other, you may need to separate ref creation from\n`implement`, as described in Circular References."},"382":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-19","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#backing-models","content":"Classes and types registered on `SchemaTypes` are also supported. See the\nObjects guide for examples of each style."},"383":{"id":"/docs/guide/app-layout-20","page_id":"/docs/guide/app-layout","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/app-layout#co-locating-queries","content":"The `user` query above lives alongside the `User` type. Other schema modules can add queries with\n`builder.queryField` in the same way."},"384":{"id":"/docs/guide/args","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","type":"page","content":"Using args","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/args"},"385":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/args","content":"Guide for defining field args in Pothos"},"386":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/args#scalars","content":"Scalars"},"387":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/args#using-the-targ-method","content":"Using the `t.arg` method"},"388":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/args#using-convenience-methods","content":"Using convenience methods"},"389":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/args#required-args","content":"Required args"},"390":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/args#lists","content":"Lists"},"391":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/args#other-types","content":"Other types"},"392":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/args#nested-lists","content":"Nested Lists"},"393":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/args#querying-with-arguments","content":"Querying with arguments"},"394":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/args","content":"Arguments let a client pass values to a field's resolver. They work on query, mutation, object,\nand interface fields. Define them in a field's `args` option and read them from the resolver's\nsecond parameter."},"395":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/args#using-the-targ-method","content":"Use `t.arg` with a scalar type name:"},"396":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/args#using-convenience-methods","content":"The equivalent shorthand for the argument above is `t.arg.string({ required: true })`.\nOther scalar helpers include `t.arg.id`, `t.arg.int`, `t.arg.float`, and `t.arg.boolean`.\nEach also has a list helper, such as `t.arg.stringList`."},"397":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/args#required-args","content":"Arguments are optional by default. Set `required: true` to reject omitted or `null` values.\nSee Changing Default Nullability to change this default."},"398":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/args#required-args","content":"An optional argument can be omitted or explicitly set to `null`. A `defaultValue` applies when\nan argument is omitted; it does not replace an explicit `null`. The resolver below handles both:"},"399":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/args#lists","content":"Wrap a type in an array to create a list argument, or use a list helper:"},"400":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/args#lists","content":"List items are non-null by default, even when the list argument is optional. Use separate `list`\nand `items` settings to allow null entries in a required list:"},"401":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-16","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/args#other-types","content":"Arguments can also use enums and input objects. Output objects, interfaces,\nand unions cannot be argument types."},"402":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-17","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/args#other-types","content":"This object field accepts an enum argument to choose the unit for its result:"},"403":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-18","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/args#nested-lists","content":"Use `t.arg.listRef` to nest lists. Its `required` option controls the items of that list;\n`required` on `t.arg` controls whether the outermost list can be omitted or null."},"404":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-19","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/args#nested-lists","content":"Here, `groups` has GraphQL type `[[String]!]!`: the outer list and each inner list are required,\nbut strings inside the inner lists can be null. Without `{ required: false }`, those strings\nwould also be non-null."},"405":{"id":"/docs/guide/args-20","page_id":"/docs/guide/args","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/args#querying-with-arguments","content":"The examples above form one schema. This query exercises required arguments, defaults, lists,\nand the enum argument on an object field:"},"406":{"id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","page_id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","type":"page","content":"Default nullability","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability"},"407":{"id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","content":"Configure schema-wide defaults and override them for individual fields."},"408":{"id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","content":"By default, Pothos output fields are nullable, and arguments and input object fields are optional.\nUse `nullable: false` to make an output field non-null, or `required: true` to require an input."},"409":{"id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","content":"You can change these defaults for a builder. Set each default in both the `SchemaTypes` generic\nfor TypeScript checking and the constructor options for runtime schema generation:"},"410":{"id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","content":"These settings are independent: omit the input setting from both places if you only want to change\noutput nullability, or omit the output setting to change only input requiredness."},"411":{"id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","content":"The example produces these field types:"},"412":{"id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","content":"`nullable: true` and `required: false` override the new defaults on individual fields. List items\nremain non-null by default; changing the builder defaults controls the list itself. Use the object\nforms of `nullable` or `required` to configure items separately, as shown in Fields and\nArguments."},"413":{"id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","content":"Changing defaults changes the public schema for fields without explicit settings. Requiring an\nexisting optional argument or input field can break client requests. Non-null output fields also\nchange error propagation: if a resolver returns null or throws, GraphQL propagates the null to the\nnearest nullable parent, potentially making the entire response's `data` null."},"414":{"id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/changing-default-nullability","content":"For example, if `nickname`'s resolver throws `new Error('Nickname unavailable')`,\n`nullable: true` makes only `nickname` null. Without that override, the builder's non-null default\nmakes the whole response's `data` null."},"415":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","type":"page","content":"Circular References","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/circular-references"},"416":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/circular-references","content":"Managing circular imports and recursive types in Pothos"},"417":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/circular-references#circular-imports","content":"Circular imports"},"418":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/circular-references#typescript-inference","content":"TypeScript inference"},"419":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/circular-references#recursive-inputs","content":"Recursive inputs"},"420":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/circular-references","content":"Two GraphQL types can refer to each other. The problems that sometimes accompany this come from\nJavaScript module initialization or TypeScript inference. They need different fixes."},"421":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/circular-references#circular-imports","content":"If two modules import each other, reading an export before its initialization can produce an\nundefined value or a `ReferenceError`, depending on the module system. Pothos defers field callbacks\nuntil `builder.toSchema()`, so references inside those callbacks can work even when the modules\nimport each other."},"422":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/circular-references#circular-imports","content":"Keep the builder in a module that does not import your schema definitions. Import all schema modules\nbefore calling `toSchema()`, and do not import that schema entry point from a type definition. See\nApp Layout for a complete file layout."},"423":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/circular-references#circular-imports","content":"In this example, `user.ts` and `post.ts` import each other, but neither reads the other module's ref\nuntil its field callback runs. The backing types use type-only imports, which do not run JavaScript."},"424":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/circular-references#circular-imports","content":"The schema supports queries that follow the relationship in both directions:"},"425":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/circular-references#circular-imports","content":"Import values directly from the modules that define them. A barrel file that re-exports every type\ncan introduce extra cycles. An entry point can import type-definition modules for their side effects\nwithout re-exporting their values."},"426":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/circular-references#circular-imports","content":"Deferring fields does not defer every option. For example, an `interfaces: [SomeInterface]` array is\nevaluated immediately. If a cycle reads an uninitialized ref there, change the imports or move the\nrefs into a separate module that does not import their implementations."},"427":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/circular-references#typescript-inference","content":"A chained declaration such as `const User = builder.objectRef<UserShape>('User').implement(...)`\ncan create an inference cycle when its fields refer back to `User`, directly or through another type.\nDeclare the ref first, then call `User.implement(...)` separately, as in the example above. TypeScript\ncan determine the ref's type without inspecting the fields. This breaks the inference dependency;\nit does not remove the JavaScript import cycle."},"428":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/circular-references#typescript-inference","content":"You can also pass the declared ref to `builder.objectType`. As an alternative to the `Post.implement`\ncall above, define its fields in separate calls:"},"429":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/circular-references#typescript-inference","content":"Classes already used by your application and names registered in the builder's `Objects` or\n`Interfaces` SchemaTypes also supply backing types independently of\nfield inference. String names can avoid importing refs between type-definition modules, but all\nimplementations still need to be loaded before `toSchema()`."},"430":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/circular-references#recursive-inputs","content":"Input types infer their shape from their fields. For recursive inputs, declare the backing shape and\nan input ref explicitly, as described in Recursive Inputs."},"431":{"id":"/docs/guide/circular-references-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/circular-references","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/circular-references#recursive-inputs","content":"Plugins that infer backing types from their options may also need explicit types to break an\ninference cycle; check the relevant plugin's documentation."},"432":{"id":"/docs/guide/context","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","type":"page","content":"Using Context","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/context"},"433":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/context","content":"Guide for using context object in Pothos"},"434":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/context#initialize-context-cache","content":"Initialize context cache"},"435":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/context#context-when-using-multiple-protocols","content":"Context when using multiple protocols"},"436":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/context","content":"The GraphQL context object gives resolvers access to shared state for the current request. For\nexample, you can use it to make the current user available throughout your schema."},"437":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/context","content":"First, let's define the user data and add a `Context` type to the builder:"},"438":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/context","content":"Next, we can define a GraphQL object for the user and a query that reads it from context:"},"439":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/context","content":"The resolver's `context` parameter has the type we provided to the builder. Clients can query the\ncurrent user with:"},"440":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/context","content":"The example context values simulate request data; the server setup below performs the actual\nuser lookup."},"441":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/context","content":"When there is no signed-in user, the response is `{ \"data\": { \"currentUser\": null } }`."},"442":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/context","content":"Your GraphQL server creates the context for each request. Here is an example using GraphQL Yoga,\nwhere `getUserFromAuthHeader` is an application function that looks up a user and returns `User | null`:"},"443":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/context#initialize-context-cache","content":"Create a new context object for each request. Plugins such as dataloader and scope-auth use the\ncontext object's identity to cache data for that request's resolvers."},"444":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/context#initialize-context-cache","content":"`initContextCache()` lets those plugins share their cache when a server copies or extends the\ncontext. Call it inside the context factory so each request gets a separate cache key. If your server\npasses the same context object to every resolver, plugins can cache by its identity without this\nhelper."},"445":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/context#context-when-using-multiple-protocols","content":"If your HTTP and WebSocket handlers provide different context properties, a\ndiscriminated union\nlets resolvers check which properties are available:"},"446":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/context#context-when-using-multiple-protocols","content":"The example runs the WebSocket-shaped branch with a JSON context fixture. Its `Context` editor\ncan represent `connectionParams`, but an HTTP `Request` must come from your server; editing the\nplayground's **Headers** does not create one or start a WebSocket connection."},"447":{"id":"/docs/guide/context-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/context","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/context#context-when-using-multiple-protocols","content":"Checking `transport` narrows the context type, so the resolver can access the properties for that\ntransport. Set the discriminator and the corresponding properties when creating the context in each\nhandler."},"448":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","type":"page","content":"Enums","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/enums"},"449":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/enums","content":"Define enum values and use them in fields and arguments."},"450":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/enums#defining-an-enum","content":"Defining an enum"},"451":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/enums#descriptions-and-internal-values","content":"Descriptions and internal values"},"452":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/enums#typescript-enums","content":"TypeScript enums"},"453":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/enums#objects-with-as-const","content":"Objects with `as const`"},"454":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/enums","content":"A GraphQL enum defines a fixed set of named values. `builder.enumType` returns a reference you can\nuse as a field or argument type."},"455":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/enums#defining-an-enum","content":"An array of strings uses the same values in GraphQL and in your resolvers:"},"456":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/enums#defining-an-enum","content":"Pothos infers the inline array as the literal values `'Feet' | 'Meters'`. If you define the array\nin a separate variable, use `as const` to retain those literal types."},"457":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/enums#defining-an-enum","content":"Enum literals in a query are unquoted names. When passing an enum in JSON variables, use a string\nsuch as `\"Meters\"` or `\"Feet\"`."},"458":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/enums#descriptions-and-internal-values","content":"Use a values object to describe or deprecate individual values, or to map GraphQL names to different\nvalues in your application:"},"459":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/enums#descriptions-and-internal-values","content":"The keys are GraphQL names. An argument containing `Northern` reaches the resolver as\n`'camelopardalis'`; returning `'camelopardalis'` from a `GiraffeSpecies` field produces `\"Northern\"`\nin the response. If `value` is omitted, the key is also the internal value. Deprecating a value\nleaves it usable by clients."},"460":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/enums#typescript-enums","content":"If your application already uses a TypeScript enum, register it directly:"},"461":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/enums#typescript-enums","content":"The enum's keys become GraphQL value names. Resolvers receive and return the TypeScript enum's\nvalues, including numeric values in this example. You can use either the TypeScript enum itself\nor the reference returned by `enumType` as the field or argument type."},"462":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/enums#objects-with-as-const","content":"You can also derive enum values from an existing object:"},"463":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/enums#objects-with-as-const","content":"In **Mapped values**, the GraphQL names are `sedan`, `suv`, `truck`, and `motorcycle`, while resolvers\nuse `SEDAN`, `SUV`, `TRUCK`, and `MOTORCYCLE`."},"464":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/enums#objects-with-as-const","content":"**Values as names** uses `Object.values(VehicleType)` for both names and internal values.\n**Keys as names** uses `Object.keys(VehicleType)` for both. TypeScript types `Object.keys` as\n`string[]`, so the assertion preserves the known keys of this local object."},"465":{"id":"/docs/guide/enums-16","page_id":"/docs/guide/enums","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/enums#objects-with-as-const","content":"In each variant, `vehicle` returns the GraphQL enum name, while `internalValue` exposes the value\nthe argument resolver receives."},"466":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","type":"page","content":"Fields","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/fields"},"467":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields","content":"Guide for defining fields in Pothos"},"468":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/fields#scalars","content":"Scalars"},"469":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/fields#field-method","content":"Field method"},"470":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/fields#convenience-methods","content":"Convenience methods"},"471":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/fields#other-types","content":"Other types"},"472":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/fields#lists","content":"Lists"},"473":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/fields#nonnullable-fields","content":"NonNullable fields"},"474":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/fields#exposing-fields-from-the-underlying-data","content":"Exposing fields from the underlying data"},"475":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/fields#arguments","content":"Arguments"},"476":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/fields#adding-fields-to-existing-type","content":"Adding fields to existing type"},"477":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/fields#nested-lists","content":"Nested Lists"},"478":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields","content":"Fields for Object and Interface types are defined using a shape\nfunction. This is a function that takes a FieldBuilder as an argument, and\nreturns an object whose keys are field names, and whose values are fields created by the\nFieldBuilder. These examples will mostly add fields to the `Query` type, but\nthe topics covered in this guide should apply to any object or interface type."},"479":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#scalars","content":"Scalar fields can be defined a couple of different ways:"},"480":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#convenience-methods","content":"Convenience methods are just wrappers around the `field` method that omit the `type` option."},"481":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#convenience-methods","content":"Each Playground action opens a complete schema. In the scalar example, `queryFields` adds\nconvenience fields to the `Query` type already defined with `queryType`."},"482":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#other-types","content":"Fields for non-scalar fields can also be created with the `field` method."},"483":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-16","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#other-types","content":"Some types like Objects and Interfaces can be referenced by name if\nthey have a backing model defined in the schema builder."},"484":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-17","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#other-types","content":"For types not described in the `SchemaTypes` type provided to the builder, including types that can\nnot be added there like Unions and Enums, you can use a `Ref` returned by the\nbuilder method that created them in the `type` parameter. For types created using a class\n(Objects or Interfaces) or Enums created using a typescript\nenum, you can also use the `class` or `enum` that was used to define them."},"485":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-18","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#other-types","content":"Here we add an enum field to the `Giraffe` type defined above:"},"486":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-19","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#lists","content":"To create a list field, you can wrap the type in an array"},"487":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-20","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#nonnullable-fields","content":"Fields in Pothos are nullable by default, but fields can be made NonNullable by setting the\n`nullable` option to `false`. This default can also be changed in the SchemaBuilder constructor, see\nChanging Default Nullability for more details."},"488":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-21","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#nonnullable-fields","content":"Note that by default even if a list field is nullable, the items in that list are not. The last\nexample above shows how you can make list items nullable."},"489":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-22","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#exposing-fields-from-the-underlying-data","content":"Some GraphQL implementations have a concept of \"default resolvers\" that can automatically resolve\nfields that have a property of the same name in the underlying data. In Pothos, these relationships\nneed to be explicitly defined, but there are helper methods that make exposing fields easier."},"490":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-23","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#exposing-fields-from-the-underlying-data","content":"These helpers are not available for root types (Query, Mutation and Subscription), but will work\non any other object type or interface."},"491":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-24","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#exposing-fields-from-the-underlying-data","content":"The available expose helpers are:"},"492":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-25","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#exposing-fields-from-the-underlying-data","content":"`exposeString`"},"493":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-26","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#exposing-fields-from-the-underlying-data","content":"`exposeInt`"},"494":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-27","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#exposing-fields-from-the-underlying-data","content":"`exposeFloat`"},"495":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-28","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#exposing-fields-from-the-underlying-data","content":"`exposeBoolean`"},"496":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-29","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#exposing-fields-from-the-underlying-data","content":"`exposeID`"},"497":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-30","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#exposing-fields-from-the-underlying-data","content":"`exposeStringList`"},"498":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-31","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#exposing-fields-from-the-underlying-data","content":"`exposeIntList`"},"499":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-32","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#exposing-fields-from-the-underlying-data","content":"`exposeFloatList`"},"500":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-33","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#exposing-fields-from-the-underlying-data","content":"`exposeBooleanList`"},"501":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-34","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#exposing-fields-from-the-underlying-data","content":"`exposeIDList`"},"502":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-35","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#arguments","content":"Arguments for a field can be defined in the options for a field:"},"503":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-36","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#arguments","content":"For more information see the Arguments Guide."},"504":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-37","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#adding-fields-to-existing-type","content":"In addition to being able to define fields when defining types, you can also add additional fields\nindependently. This is useful for breaking up types with a lot of fields into multiple files, or\nco-locating fields with their type (e.g., add all query/mutation fields for a type in the same file\nwhere the type is defined)."},"505":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-38","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#adding-fields-to-existing-type","content":"To see all the methods available for defining fields see the SchemaBuilder API"},"506":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-39","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#adding-fields-to-existing-type","content":"The example adds `newestGiraffe` separately from the original `giraffe` field. Query\n`newestGiraffe { name nameLength }` to see both the added root field and the computed object field."},"507":{"id":"/docs/guide/fields-40","page_id":"/docs/guide/fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/fields#nested-lists","content":"You can use `t.listRef` to create a list of lists"},"508":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","type":"page","content":"Generating client types","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types"},"509":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","content":"Guide for generating client types from a Pothos schema"},"510":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#export-your-schema","content":"Export your schema"},"511":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#setting-up-graphql-code-generator","content":"Setting up graphql-code-generator"},"512":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#install-the-codegen-packages","content":"Install the codegen packages"},"513":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#configure-the-codegen-to-import-your-schema","content":"Configure the codegen to import your schema"},"514":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#run-generation","content":"Run generation"},"515":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#generating-a-schemagraphql-file-with-graphql-code-generator","content":"Generating a schema.graphql file with graphql-code-generator"},"516":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#adding-scalars","content":"Adding scalars"},"517":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#alternatives","content":"Alternatives"},"518":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#printing-the-schema-to-a-file","content":"Printing the schema to a file"},"519":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#using-introspection-from-your-dev-or-production-server","content":"Using introspection from your dev (or production) server"},"520":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","content":"Pothos does not have a built in mechanism for generating types to use with a client, but\ngraphql-code-generator can be configured to consume a\nschema directly from your typescript files."},"521":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#export-your-schema","content":"The first thing you will need is a file that exports your built schema. The schema should be\nexported as `schema` or as the default export. This will be used to generate your client types, but\ncan also be the schema you use in your server."},"522":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#export-your-schema","content":"Save the example as `src/schema.ts` and the operation as `src/queries.graphql` to use them with the local Codegen configuration below. Codegen runs in your project, not in the playground."},"523":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#export-your-schema","content":"In a larger application, this entry point can import your builder and type-definition modules before exporting `builder.toSchema()`. Keep server startup separate so Codegen can import the schema without opening a listening port."},"524":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#setting-up-graphql-code-generator","content":"There are many different ways to set up graphql-code-generator, and the details depend a lot on your\nneeds."},"525":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-16","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#setting-up-graphql-code-generator","content":"See the\ngraphql-code-generator documentation\nfor more details."},"526":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-17","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#configure-the-codegen-to-import-your-schema","content":"Create a `codegen.ts` file in the root of your project:"},"527":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-18","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#configure-the-codegen-to-import-your-schema","content":"You can customize this config as needed, but the relevant parts are:"},"528":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-19","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#configure-the-codegen-to-import-your-schema","content":"Importing your GraphQL schema, this should be the result of calling `builder.toSchema({})`"},"529":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-20","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#configure-the-codegen-to-import-your-schema","content":"using `printSchema` from `graphql` to convert the schema to a string"},"530":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-21","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#run-generation","content":"The `documents` pattern must match your client queries, mutations, and fragments. Adjust it for your\nproject and exclude the generated directory. For example, a file named `src/queries.graphql` can\ncontain a named query that selects fields from your schema."},"531":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-22","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#run-generation","content":"Run this again when your schema or operations change. The\nclient preset generates typed\noperation documents and helpers in `src/gql/`; the result types reflect the fields each operation\nselects. The CLI imports your schema module, so its environment variables and other runtime\ndependencies must be available. Keep server startup outside that module."},"532":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-23","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#generating-a-schemagraphql-file-with-graphql-code-generator","content":"You can generate a schema.graphql file with graphql-code-generator by adding the `schema-ast`\nplugin:"},"533":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-24","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#adding-scalars","content":"Configure custom scalars to match their serialized values on the client. Add the following `config`\nproperty to the configuration above:"},"534":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-25","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#adding-scalars","content":"A DateTime scalar commonly serializes to an ISO date string. Mapping it to `Date` changes only the\nTypeScript type; Codegen does not convert JSON strings into JavaScript `Date` instances. Use `Date`\nonly if your client actually performs that conversion. `strictScalars` makes generation fail when a\ncustom scalar has no mapping; see the client preset configuration."},"535":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-26","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#alternatives","content":"In some cases you may want to use an alternative method for loading your schema."},"536":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-27","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#printing-the-schema-to-a-file","content":"You can use the `printSchema` function from `graphql` to print your schema to a file, see\nPrinting Schemas for more details:"},"537":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-28","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#printing-the-schema-to-a-file","content":"By writing the schema to a file, you will be able to load the schema from a file instead of importing\nit each time you want to generate your schema."},"538":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-29","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#printing-the-schema-to-a-file","content":"Having your schema written to a file, and checked into source control has many benefits, like easier\ncode reviews, and better interoperability with other schema dependent graphql tools, so setting this\nup is worthwhile even if you do not need it for generating client types:"},"539":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-30","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#using-introspection-from-your-dev-or-production-server","content":"Rather than using a schema SDL file, graphql-code-generator can also use introspection to load\nyour schema. To do this, you will need to ensure that your server has introspection enabled and that Codegen can authenticate if the endpoint requires it.\nIntrospection defaults depend on the server and its configuration."},"540":{"id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types-31","page_id":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/generating-client-types#using-introspection-from-your-dev-or-production-server","content":"You can then configure graphql-code-generator to use introspection by passing the URL to your\ngraphql endpoint:"},"541":{"id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","page_id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","type":"page","content":"Inferring Types","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/inferring-types"},"542":{"id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","content":"Extract backing types and builder types for TypeScript helpers"},"543":{"id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","content":"In some cases you may want to use the types from your input or object refs to build helpers, or\nprovide accurate types for other functions."},"544":{"id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","content":"To get types from any Pothos `ref` object, you can use the `$inferType` and `$inferInput` properties\non the corresponding output or input ref. Use these properties in TypeScript type expressions,\nnot to read runtime data. An object ref carries its backing model, not a GraphQL query result: clients\nselect fields, and resolvers can expose fields that are not properties of the backing model."},"545":{"id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","content":"When building helpers, most Pothos types have a generic called `Types` that extends `SchemaTypes`.\nThis combines all the defaults and settings passed in when creating the SchemaBuilder. To make your\nown type helpers and utility functions, you often need access to the `Types` used by your builder."},"546":{"id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","content":"This can be inferred from the builder using `typeof builder.$inferSchemaTypes`."},"547":{"id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","content":"The following is a simple helper for creating objects that have an `id` field. The helper\nitself isn't that useful, but shows how inferring SchemaTypes from a builder can work."},"548":{"id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","content":"Rather than explicitly using the inferred type, you can also infer SchemaTypes from the builder in\nan argument. In the following example, we pass in the builder to the createPaginationArgs, and infer\nthe `Types` from the provided builder. This is useful when building helpers that might be used with\nmultiple builder instances."},"549":{"id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inferring-types","content":"The example uses the inferred input shape in `toUser` and returns real users from `getUsers`.\nA `limit` of `1` returns one user; `2` returns both. If `MyInput.name` is defined with\n`t.int({ required: true })` instead of `t.string({ required: true })`, the inferred input has a\nnumeric name, so returning it from\n`toUser` produces a TypeScript error: `UserType` still requires a string. The GraphQL input field\nalso changes from `String!` to `Int!`."},"550":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","type":"page","content":"Input Objects","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/inputs"},"551":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs","content":"Guide for defining Input Object types in Pothos"},"552":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/inputs#creating-input-objects","content":"Creating input objects"},"553":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"OneOf inputs"},"554":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/inputs#recursive-inputs","content":"Recursive inputs"},"555":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/inputs#declaring-inputs-in-schematypes","content":"Declaring inputs in SchemaTypes"},"556":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs","content":"Input objects group related argument values. Define one with `builder.inputType`, then use the\nreturned ref as an argument type. Pothos infers the input shape from its fields."},"557":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#creating-input-objects","content":"The “Creating input objects” and “Recursive inputs” sections build on the same schema. Start with a backing model and\nan object ref for the mutation result:"},"558":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#creating-input-objects","content":"Input objects and output objects are separate GraphQL types, even when they have the same fields.\nHere, `GiraffeInput` describes the argument and `Giraffe` describes the result. The array stores the\ncreated giraffes in memory for this example."},"559":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#creating-input-objects","content":"`required: true` on the argument requires an input object. Each input field has its own\nrequiredness; here all three fields must also be provided and cannot be `null`."},"560":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#creating-input-objects","content":"The mutation appends each created giraffe to the in-memory array, which the `giraffes` query returns."},"561":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"A OneOf input accepts exactly one of its fields, with a non-null value. This is useful for a lookup\nthat accepts either an ID or a name. Use a GraphQL.js version with OneOf support, and define the\ninput with `isOneOf: true`:"},"562":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"Each member must be optional (`required: false`) and must not have a `defaultValue`. Explicitly\nsetting `required: false` also works when the builder uses `defaultInputFieldRequiredness: true`.\nThe selected member still cannot be `null`: OneOf coercion enforces that constraint."},"563":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"The `by` argument above is required. This requires the input object itself; it does not make both\nalternatives required. With the default scalar types, Pothos infers `by` as\n`{ id: string; name?: never } | { name: string; id?: never }`, so checking `by.id !== undefined`\nselects the ID branch without a type assertion."},"564":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"Both `{ id: \"1\" }` and `{ name: \"Gina\" }` select Gina in this example. The following operation takes the lookup as a variable:"},"565":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"Value of the `by` variable"},"566":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"Result"},"567":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-16","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"`{ \"id\": \"1\" }`"},"568":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-17","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"`giraffeName` is `\"Gina\"`"},"569":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-18","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"`{ \"name\": \"Gina\" }`"},"570":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-19","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"`giraffeName` is `\"Gina\"`"},"571":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-20","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"`{}`"},"572":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-21","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"Rejected: exactly one field is required"},"573":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-22","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"`{ \"id\": \"1\", \"name\": \"Gina\" }`"},"574":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-23","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"Rejected: more than one field is provided"},"575":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-24","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"`{ \"id\": null }`"},"576":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-25","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"Rejected: the selected value must be non-null"},"577":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-26","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"`null`, or an omitted `by` variable"},"578":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-27","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"Rejected: the argument uses `GiraffeLookup!`"},"579":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-28","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#oneof-inputs","content":"Invalid inputs are rejected before the resolver runs. The same OneOf constraints apply to inline\ninput literals."},"580":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-29","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#recursive-inputs","content":"Input objects can reference other input refs directly. For circular references, declare the input\nshape explicitly with `builder.inputRef` so TypeScript does not have to infer it through the cycle.\nCreate the ref before implementing its fields."},"581":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-30","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#recursive-inputs","content":"Continue with the builder from Creating input objects, and add this input\nand mutation field:"},"582":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-31","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#recursive-inputs","content":"The `friends` list can be omitted or set to `null`, but its items cannot be `null`. The TypeScript\nshape includes both optionality and `null` to match those values. The resolver uses each friend's\nown fields and follows nested `friends` lists, returning the parent before its descendants."},"583":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-32","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#declaring-inputs-in-schematypes","content":"Alternatively, register input shapes in the builder's `Inputs` map to reference them by name.\nTo use this approach for `createGiraffe`, keep the import and `Giraffe` backing model from the first\nexample and replace its builder declaration with:"},"584":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-33","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#declaring-inputs-in-schematypes","content":"Keep the `giraffes` array, `GiraffeRef`, and query definition. Replace the `GiraffeInput` ref\ndeclaration with this definition; registering a shape alone does not create the GraphQL input type."},"585":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-34","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#declaring-inputs-in-schematypes","content":"In the `createGiraffe` mutation, the `input` argument now references that registered name:"},"586":{"id":"/docs/guide/inputs-35","page_id":"/docs/guide/inputs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/inputs#declaring-inputs-in-schematypes","content":"Keep the rest of that mutation unchanged and call `builder.toSchema()` after defining it. The first\nmutation and result on this page also work with this schema; the recursive example is independent\nof this alternative."},"587":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","type":"page","content":"Interfaces","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/interfaces"},"588":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/interfaces","content":"Guide for defining Interface types in Pothos"},"589":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#defining-interface-types","content":"Defining Interface Types"},"590":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#implementing-interfaces-with-object-types","content":"implementing interfaces with object types"},"591":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#using-an-interface-as-a-return-type","content":"Using an Interface as a return type"},"592":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#querying-interface-fields","content":"Querying interface fields"},"593":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#other-ways-to-resolve-an-interface","content":"Other ways to resolve an interface"},"594":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#interfaces-implementing-interfaces","content":"Interfaces implementing interfaces"},"595":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#defining-interface-types","content":"An interface defines fields that several object types have in common. Like objects, interfaces have\na backing model that describes the data their field resolvers receive. You can\nuse `builder.interfaceRef` to associate a TypeScript type with a GraphQL interface."},"596":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#defining-interface-types","content":"In this example, giraffes and lions have a diet, but each has some other data of its own:"},"597":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#defining-interface-types","content":"The `resolveType` function returns the GraphQL object type name to use when a field returns an\n`Animal`. Here, the `kind` property distinguishes the two shapes. It is part of our backing data,\nbut isn't exposed as a GraphQL field."},"598":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#implementing-interfaces-with-object-types","content":"Use the `interfaces` option to specify which interfaces an object implements:"},"599":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#implementing-interfaces-with-object-types","content":"Pothos adds the interface's fields to each implementing object, so both types have a `diet` field\nwithout defining it again. The object's backing model must be assignable to the interface's backing\nmodel so that the inherited field resolvers can use it. In this example, both `Giraffe` and `Lion`\nare members of the `Animal` TypeScript union."},"600":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#using-an-interface-as-a-return-type","content":"A field returning a list of animals can include both giraffes and lions. The return annotation\nkeeps each `kind` value typed as its specific string literal:"},"601":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#querying-interface-fields","content":"We can query `diet` on every animal. Fields that belong to a particular object type go inside an\ninline fragment. The `__typename` field tells us which object type each result has:"},"602":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#querying-interface-fields","content":"The result includes the shared field and the matching fragment's fields for each animal:"},"603":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#other-ways-to-resolve-an-interface","content":"If your application already uses classes for its data, prefer those classes as backing models.\nYou can pass them to `builder.interfaceType` and `builder.objectType`, as described in the\nObjects guide."},"604":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-16","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#other-ways-to-resolve-an-interface","content":"Classes make automatic interface resolution straightforward. For the `Giraffe` class in that\nexample, set `isTypeOf: (value) => value instanceof Giraffe` on its object type. GraphQL can then\nselect the matching type without a `resolveType` function on the interface. Return actual class\ninstances so the check succeeds."},"605":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-17","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#other-ways-to-resolve-an-interface","content":"Existing `Error` subclasses are another good use case: the Errors plugin\nmatches errors against the configured classes using `instanceof`."},"606":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-18","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#other-ways-to-resolve-an-interface","content":"Another option is to return data with a `__typename` property containing the GraphQL object type's\nname, such as `'Giraffe'`. GraphQL uses this property when the interface has no `resolveType`\nfunction. If an object also defines `isTypeOf`, that check must pass as well."},"607":{"id":"/docs/guide/interfaces-19","page_id":"/docs/guide/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/interfaces#interfaces-implementing-interfaces","content":"Interfaces can also implement other interfaces using the `interfaces` option. See the\nSchemaBuilder API for the available options."},"608":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","type":"page","content":"Objects","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/objects"},"609":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects","content":"Define GraphQL object types from your application's data."},"610":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/objects#defining-an-object-type","content":"Defining an Object type"},"611":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/objects#add-some-fields","content":"Add some fields"},"612":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/objects#add-a-query","content":"Add a query"},"613":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/objects#create-a-server","content":"Create a server"},"614":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/objects#query-your-data","content":"Query your data"},"615":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/objects#different-ways-to-define-object-types","content":"Different ways to define Object types"},"616":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/objects#using-refs","content":"Using Refs"},"617":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/objects#using-classes","content":"Using classes"},"618":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/objects#using-schematypes","content":"Using SchemaTypes"},"619":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects","content":"We'll define a GraphQL type for a giraffe, using a TypeScript interface to describe its data."},"620":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#defining-an-object-type","content":"Create `schema.ts` with a builder and a reference for the `Giraffe` type:"},"621":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#defining-an-object-type","content":"The interface describes the **backing model**: the data returned by resolvers for this type.\n`objectRef` associates that model with the GraphQL name `Giraffe`. We can use the ref when defining\nfields that return a giraffe."},"622":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#add-some-fields","content":"Next, call `implement` to define the object's fields. It accepts the options for the object type,\nincluding an optional description:"},"623":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#add-some-fields","content":"The `fields` function receives a field builder, usually named `t`. Here, `t.exposeString('name')`\nreads the `name` property from the backing model. The `height` field reads `heightInMeters`, giving\nthat property a different name in the GraphQL schema."},"624":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#add-some-fields","content":"For `birthYear`, we write a resolver that computes an integer from the giraffe's birthday. Pothos\ninfers the resolver's first argument as `Giraffe` from the ref."},"625":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-16","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#add-some-fields","content":"The backing model and GraphQL fields can have different shapes. Clients can query `name`, `height`,\nand `birthYear`; `birthday` is available to resolvers but isn't exposed as a field. We can add\ncomputed fields without adding properties to the backing model. The fields guide\ncovers more field options."},"626":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-17","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#add-some-fields","content":"For a smaller example of mapping data to a different API shape, try\nconverting a stored height in meters to feet."},"627":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-18","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#add-a-query","content":"Add a field to the root `Query` type that returns a giraffe, then build the schema:"},"628":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-19","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#add-a-query","content":"The `type: GiraffeRef` option tells Pothos to check that the resolver returns data matching the\n`Giraffe` interface. That returned object becomes the first argument to the resolvers for `name`,\n`height`, and `birthYear`."},"629":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-20","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#add-a-query","content":"You can run this schema immediately with a Playground action above. To run it in your own\napplication, add the server below."},"630":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-21","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#create-a-server","content":"`builder.toSchema()` builds a standard `GraphQLSchema`. To serve it with `graphql-yoga`, install\n`graphql-yoga` and save this as `server.ts` alongside `schema.ts`:"},"631":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-22","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#query-your-data","content":"Run `npx tsx server.ts`, then open http\\://localhost:3000/graphql\nand run this query:"},"632":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-23","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#query-your-data","content":"The result is:"},"633":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-24","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#different-ways-to-define-object-types","content":"This guide uses object refs with a TypeScript interface. You can also use an existing class or\nregister backing models by name on the builder. These forms can be used together in the same schema."},"634":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-25","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#using-refs","content":"You can create and implement a ref in one expression:"},"635":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-26","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#using-refs","content":"For types that reference each other, TypeScript may need ref creation and `implement` to be separate\nstatements, as in the walkthrough above. See circular references for details."},"636":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-27","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#using-refs","content":"You can also pass a ref to `builder.objectType(GiraffeRef, { ... })` with the same options."},"637":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-28","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#using-classes","content":"If your app already represents its data with classes, prefer using those classes as backing models.\nPass the class to `builder.objectType`: its instance type becomes the backing model, and the class\nitself can be used as a field's `type`:"},"638":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-29","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#using-classes","content":"The Classes tab shows this definition. Its playground uses `type: Giraffe` in the query field\nand returns a `new Giraffe(...)` instance."},"639":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-30","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#using-classes","content":"The `name` option supplies the GraphQL type name. When resolving an\ninterface or union, you can also use an explicit\n`isTypeOf: (value) => value instanceof Giraffe` option to identify instances of the class."},"640":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-31","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#using-schematypes","content":"You can declare backing models in `SchemaTypes` and pass it to `SchemaBuilder`. This lets you\nreference object types by their GraphQL names instead of importing refs. Using the `Giraffe`\ninterface from above:"},"641":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-32","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#using-schematypes","content":"The SchemaTypes tab shows the registration. Its playground uses `type: 'Giraffe'` in the query field."},"642":{"id":"/docs/guide/objects-33","page_id":"/docs/guide/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/objects#using-schematypes","content":"The `Objects` mapping tells Pothos which backing model belongs to each name. Here, it types the\n`Giraffe` field resolvers and checks the object returned by the `giraffe` query. You still define\nthe GraphQL fields with `builder.objectType`; declaring a backing model doesn't expose its properties.\nThe SchemaBuilder guide covers the other type settings."},"643":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","type":"page","content":"Patterns","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/patterns"},"644":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/patterns","content":"Guide for using common patterns in Pothos"},"645":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/patterns#sharing-fields-between-types","content":"Sharing fields between types"},"646":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/patterns#objects-and-interfaces","content":"Objects and Interfaces"},"647":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/patterns#args","content":"Args"},"648":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/patterns#input-fields","content":"Input fields"},"649":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/patterns#sharing-fields-between-types","content":"If you have common fields or arguments that are shared across multiple types (but you don't want to\nuse an interface to share the common logic) you can write helper functions to generate these fields\nfor you."},"650":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/patterns#objects-and-interfaces","content":"The shared `idLength` resolver computes the length of each object's ID."},"651":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/patterns#objects-and-interfaces","content":"This will apply the `id` and `idLength` fields to both of the object types. The `ObjectRef` type is\nwhat is returned when creating an object (or when calling `builder.objectRef`). It takes three generic\nparameters: the builder's SchemaTypes, the shape a resolver can return for that type, and the shape\nof the parent argument when defining fields. The parent shape defaults to the resolver shape.\nThe resolver and parent shapes are generally the same, but can differ for some special cases (like with `loadableObject` from the dataloader plugin,\nwhich allows resolvers to resolve to an `ID` rather than the actual object). In this case,\nthe helper requires an `id` on the parent shape because its field resolvers read that property."},"652":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/patterns#objects-and-interfaces","content":"If you want to define fields on an interface, you can use `InterfaceRef` instead. If your helper\naccepts both, you can differentiate the refs by using `ref.kind` which will be either `Object` or\n`Interface`. Call `builder.interfaceFields` for interface refs."},"653":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/patterns#args","content":"The example mutation passes a reason to one field and omits it from the other."},"654":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/patterns#args","content":"Args are a little more complicated than fields on objects and interfaces. Pothos infers the shape of args\nfor your resolvers, so you can't just add on more args later. Instead, we can define a helper that\nreturns a set of args to apply to your field. To make this work, we need to get a few extra types:"},"655":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/patterns#args","content":"`typeof builder.$inferSchemaTypes` includes the defaults Pothos applies to the builder's settings.\nUse it to type the helper's `ArgBuilder` parameter, then spread the returned arguments into each\nfield. The resolver's `args` type includes those arguments. If you already export the builder's\nsettings as a `SchemaTypes` interface,\n`PothosSchemaTypes.ExtendDefaultTypes<SchemaTypes>` is another way to obtain the extended types."},"656":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/patterns#args","content":"For a shared set of args tied to one builder, you can also use\n`builder.args`. See Inferring Types for a helper\nthat accepts different builders."},"657":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/patterns#input-fields","content":"Both input types use the helper to define their `reason` field."},"658":{"id":"/docs/guide/patterns-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/patterns","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/patterns#input-fields","content":"Input fields are similar to args, and also all need to be present when the type is defined so that\nPothos can infer the correct types."},"659":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","type":"page","content":"Using the playground","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/playground"},"660":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/playground","content":"Run a Pothos schema, edit its source, and use variables to explore your data."},"661":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/playground#run-and-edit-a-greeting","content":"Run and edit a greeting"},"662":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/playground#explore-fields-and-arguments","content":"Explore fields and arguments"},"663":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/playground#share-your-changes","content":"Share your changes"},"664":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/playground","content":"The playground runs Pothos schemas in your browser. You can edit the TypeScript source, inspect the generated GraphQL schema, and execute queries without starting a server."},"665":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/playground#run-and-edit-a-greeting","content":"Open the greeting example and click **Run**. The response contains `\"Hello, Sam!\"`."},"666":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/playground#run-and-edit-a-greeting","content":"Change `Sam` in the query to your name and run again. This example has one source file and one query."},"667":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/playground#run-and-edit-a-greeting","content":"The resolver in `schema.ts` uses the `name` argument to build the greeting:"},"668":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/playground#run-and-edit-a-greeting","content":"In `schema.ts`, change `Hello` to `Welcome`. Run the query again to see the edited response. **Reset example** restores the original source and query."},"669":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/playground#explore-fields-and-arguments","content":"Choose **Explore a schema** in the step controls, or open step 2. Each step loads a complete example; edits from the previous step are not carried forward."},"670":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/playground#explore-fields-and-arguments","content":"Run **01-Tall giraffes**. With `minimumHeight` set to `5`, the response includes James. Run **02-All giraffes** to include Lucy too. Change `minimumHeight` in **Variables** and run again to try the filter."},"671":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/playground#explore-fields-and-arguments","content":"Open `models/giraffe.ts` in **Files** to change the backing data. Open `schema.graphql` to inspect the generated field and argument types. The schema explorer also lets you browse the types. Resolver logs and errors appear in **Console**."},"672":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/playground#share-your-changes","content":"Use **Share** to copy a link containing your edited source and operations. A reader opening shared code must review it and choose **Trust and build schema** before running it. **Read the guide** returns to this page; **Reset example** discards edits and reloads the original example."},"673":{"id":"/docs/guide/playground-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/playground","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/playground#share-your-changes","content":"The playground executes queries and mutations locally. It does not start an HTTP server, provision a database, or run live subscriptions. Examples that require those services need their documented local setup."},"674":{"id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","page_id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","type":"page","content":"Printing Schemas","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas"},"675":{"id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","content":"Guide for printing a Pothos schema to an SDL schema file"},"676":{"id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas#using-graphql-code-generator","content":"Using graphql-code-generator"},"677":{"id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","content":"The playground example runs the schema below and shows its SDL in `schema.graphql`. Field descriptions appear in the SDL. Writing the result to a file is a Node.js task."},"678":{"id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","content":"Sometimes it's useful to have an SDL version of your schema. To do this, you can use some tools from\nthe `graphql` package to write your schema out as SDL to a file."},"679":{"id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","content":"Save the schema as `schema.ts`:"},"680":{"id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","content":"Write its SDL from a separate Node.js script:"},"681":{"id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","content":"`builder.toSchema()` sorts the schema by default."},"682":{"id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","content":"Save the script as `print-schema.ts` and run it with a TypeScript runner:"},"683":{"id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","content":"In an existing application, import your exported `schema` instead of creating another builder. Keep\nschema construction separate from starting your server so this script does not open a listening port.\nThe generated SDL can be checked into source control for schema reviews or read by client tooling."},"684":{"id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","content":"`printSchema` prints type definitions, not resolver functions or backing data. It also does not\npreserve applied custom directives; use a printer that supports those directives if downstream tools\nneed them, such as when exporting a federation subgraph."},"685":{"id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas#using-graphql-code-generator","content":"An alternative to printing your schema directly is to generate your schema file using\ngraphql-code-generator."},"686":{"id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas#using-graphql-code-generator","content":"You can add the `schema-ast` plugin to have graphql-code-generator generate your schema file for\nyou."},"687":{"id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/printing-schemas#using-graphql-code-generator","content":"See Generating Client Types for more details"},"688":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","type":"page","content":"Queries, Mutations and Subscriptions","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions"},"689":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","content":"Guide for adding queries, mutations and subscriptions to your schema"},"690":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions#queries","content":"Queries"},"691":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions#mutations","content":"Mutations"},"692":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions#subscriptions","content":"Subscriptions"},"693":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","content":"Queries, mutations, and subscriptions are fields on the schema's root types. Each root type has\nmethods for defining the type, adding one field, and adding several fields."},"694":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions#queries","content":"Use `builder.queryType()` to define the `Query` type. This example returns plain backing data through\nan object reference:"},"695":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions#queries","content":"Define the `Query` type once. To split its fields across files, use `builder.queryField()` or\n`builder.queryFields()`. The following is an alternative to the `builder.queryType()` call above,\nusing the same builder, object reference, and data:"},"696":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions#mutations","content":"Use `builder.mutationType()`, `builder.mutationField()`, and `builder.mutationFields()` in the same\nway. Continuing the query example, this mutation adds a giraffe to the array returned by `giraffes`:"},"697":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions#mutations","content":"The required argument provides a `string` for the backing model's `name`. This example stores data\nin memory; an application can perform its database write in the resolver instead."},"698":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions#mutations","content":"After this mutation, `{ giraffes { name } }` returns both James and Morgan. Each mutation adds\nanother entry to the in-memory store."},"699":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions#subscriptions","content":"Live subscription delivery requires a GraphQL server and event provider. The playground executes\nqueries and mutations locally, so run the subscription example below in your server rather than\nthe playground."},"700":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions#subscriptions","content":"Use `builder.subscriptionType()`, `builder.subscriptionField()`, and `builder.subscriptionFields()`\nto define subscription fields. Each field has a `subscribe` function that returns an async iterable\nof events, and a `resolve` function that converts each event into the field's result."},"701":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions#subscriptions","content":"This separate example defines the context contract for a counter and its event provider. Supply a\nshared counter and provider through your server's context so the mutation publishes to the same\nstream that subscriptions consume. The provider's `subscribe()` method must register the listener\nwhen called and release it when the returned iterator is closed."},"702":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions#subscriptions","content":"Start a subscription before calling the mutation to receive its event:"},"703":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions#subscriptions","content":"With an initial count of `0`, the mutation returns `1` and the subscription emits\n`{ \"data\": { \"incrementedCount\": 1 } }`. Configure a subscription transport in your GraphQL server\nto deliver these results to clients."},"704":{"id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/queries-mutations-and-subscriptions#subscriptions","content":"Define `subscribe` before `resolve` so TypeScript can infer the resolver's event type."},"705":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","type":"page","content":"Scalars","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/scalars"},"706":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars","content":"Register custom scalars and define their input and output behavior."},"707":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/scalars#defining-a-scalar","content":"Defining a scalar"},"708":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/scalars#adding-an-existing-scalar","content":"Adding an existing scalar"},"709":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/scalars#customizing-built-in-scalar-types","content":"Customizing built-in scalar types"},"710":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/scalars#graphql-17-coercion-hooks","content":"GraphQL 17 coercion hooks"},"711":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars","content":"Pothos includes GraphQL's `String`, `Boolean`, `Int`, `Float`, and `ID` scalars. Use their names as\nfield and argument types, or the shorthand builders shown in Fields."},"712":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars","content":"A custom scalar needs both a TypeScript declaration in `SchemaTypes` and a runtime implementation.\nThe declaration describes the values your resolvers work with:"},"713":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars","content":"`Input` is the value a resolver receives after GraphQL parses an argument or input field."},"714":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars","content":"`Output` is the value a resolver returns, before GraphQL serializes it for the response."},"715":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars","content":"These are application types, not necessarily the JSON types sent by clients. A date scalar can\nreceive a string from a client, parse it into a `Date`, and serialize a resolver's `Date` back to a\nstring. In that case both `Input` and `Output` are `Date`."},"716":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#defining-a-scalar","content":"This scalar accepts positive integers and checks both input values and resolver results:"},"717":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#defining-a-scalar","content":"`parseValue` handles variables, `parseLiteral` handles inline GraphQL literals, and `serialize`\nhandles output. Validate each path: TypeScript declarations alone do not validate values at runtime.\nThis example explicitly rejects string and float literals, even when they represent whole numbers."},"718":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#defining-a-scalar","content":"Doubling the largest safe integer passes input parsing, but output serialization rejects the result."},"719":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#defining-a-scalar","content":"Passing `0`, `-1`, `1.5`, or `\"3\"` is an error. An output outside the scalar's accepted range is\nalso an error; GraphQL applies the field's nullability rules when reporting it."},"720":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#adding-an-existing-scalar","content":"Use `builder.addScalarType` to register an existing graphql-js `GraphQLScalarType`. Declare its\napplication types in the builder first. For example, with `graphql-scalars` installed:"},"721":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#adding-an-existing-scalar","content":"The same method accepts your own `GraphQLScalarType` instance. Check an implementation's parsing\nand serialization contracts when choosing its `Input` and `Output` types; Pothos does not infer\nthose types from the scalar instance. The two types can differ, and an implementation may accept\nmore output types than your application needs to return."},"722":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-16","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#adding-an-existing-scalar","content":"For a general JSON scalar, `unknown` lets resolvers check an input's structure before using it.\nThe global TypeScript `JSON` type describes the `JSON.parse` and `JSON.stringify` API, not JSON data."},"723":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-17","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#customizing-built-in-scalar-types","content":"You can override the TypeScript types of built-in scalars through `Scalars` too. This changes\nresolver checking; it does not replace GraphQL's parsing or serialization behavior. For example,\nto restrict your application to string IDs:"},"724":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-18","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#customizing-built-in-scalar-types","content":"The example queries `echo(id: 42)` and reports the parsed argument's JavaScript type with\n`inputType`. Both show that GraphQL accepts integer ID inputs and parses them to strings. Use `scalarType` or\n`addScalarType` if you also need to replace a scalar's runtime implementation."},"725":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-19","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#graphql-17-coercion-hooks","content":"Starting with Pothos 4.13, `scalarType` also accepts GraphQL 17's coercion hooks:"},"726":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-20","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#graphql-17-coercion-hooks","content":"Hook"},"727":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-21","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#graphql-17-coercion-hooks","content":"Purpose"},"728":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-22","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#graphql-17-coercion-hooks","content":"`coerceOutputValue`"},"729":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-23","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#graphql-17-coercion-hooks","content":"Serialize a resolver result."},"730":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-24","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#graphql-17-coercion-hooks","content":"`coerceInputValue`"},"731":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-25","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#graphql-17-coercion-hooks","content":"Parse an external input value."},"732":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-26","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#graphql-17-coercion-hooks","content":"`coerceInputLiteral`"},"733":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-27","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#graphql-17-coercion-hooks","content":"Parse a constant AST value, with variables already substituted."},"734":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-28","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#graphql-17-coercion-hooks","content":"`valueToLiteral`"},"735":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-29","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#graphql-17-coercion-hooks","content":"Convert an external value to a constant AST value."},"736":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-30","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#graphql-17-coercion-hooks","content":"These hooks are used on GraphQL 17. GraphQL 16 ignores them, so keep `serialize`, `parseValue`, and\n`parseLiteral` for implementations that also run on GraphQL 16. The earlier example works on both\nversions."},"737":{"id":"/docs/guide/scalars-31","page_id":"/docs/guide/scalars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/scalars#graphql-17-coercion-hooks","content":"On GraphQL 17, you can provide `coerceOutputValue` instead of `serialize`. If both are present,\nGraphQL uses `serialize`. A `coerceInputLiteral` implementation must be paired with\n`coerceInputValue`; otherwise schema construction throws. `addScalarType` preserves these hooks\nwhen registering an existing scalar that provides them."},"738":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","type":"page","content":"SchemaBuilder","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder"},"739":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","content":"Create a builder, configure TypeScript types, and build a GraphQL schema."},"740":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#creating-a-builder","content":"Creating a builder"},"741":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#building-the-schema","content":"Building the schema"},"742":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#backing-models","content":"Backing models"},"743":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#other-schematypes-entries","content":"Other SchemaTypes entries"},"744":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","content":"A `SchemaBuilder` registers your types and fields, then builds a GraphQL schema. Create one builder\nfor each schema, and share it between the modules that define that schema."},"745":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#creating-a-builder","content":"The generic parameter is an object Pothos calls `SchemaTypes`. Its entries configure TypeScript\nchecking; they do not create runtime values. `Context` above types the third argument of each\nresolver. Your server must supply that object when executing a request; see Context.\nIf you do not need to configure any types, use `new SchemaBuilder({})`."},"746":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#creating-a-builder","content":"The constructor's options object configures runtime behavior. It can include plugins and their\noptions, as described in Using plugins, and\ndefault nullability settings."},"747":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#creating-a-builder","content":"The `requestId` resolver returns the value supplied in the runtime context. Declaring its\nTypeScript type does not supply that value."},"748":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#building-the-schema","content":"Call `builder.toSchema()` after registering your types and fields. It returns a standard\n`GraphQLSchema` that you can pass to a GraphQL server or execute with graphql-js. A schema needs a\nQuery root, even when it also defines mutations or subscriptions."},"749":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#building-the-schema","content":"By default, Pothos sorts the schema lexicographically. Use `builder.toSchema({ sortSchema: false })`\nto keep definition order. See the SchemaBuilder API for build options and\nApp layout for registering types from multiple files before building the schema."},"750":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#backing-models","content":"A backing model is the TypeScript type of the data a resolver returns for an object or interface.\nIt also determines the `parent` type of that object's field resolvers. It can contain properties\nthat are not exposed in GraphQL, while GraphQL fields can compute or load values absent from the\nbacking model."},"751":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#backing-models","content":"Pothos associates backing models with GraphQL types in three ways:"},"752":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#backing-models","content":"**Type references:** `builder.objectRef<T>('Name')` or `builder.interfaceRef<T>('Name')` carries\nthe backing type `T`. Methods that define types also return references you can use elsewhere."},"753":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#backing-models","content":"**Classes:** Register a class directly when your application already uses class instances as\nbacking data. Pothos uses the class's instance type for resolver checking."},"754":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#backing-models","content":"**SchemaTypes:** Declare backing models in the builder's `Objects`, `Interfaces`, or `Inputs`\nmaps to reference those types by their string names. These declarations still need corresponding\nruntime type definitions."},"755":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-16","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#backing-models","content":"The Objects, Interfaces, and Input objects guides show\nthese alternatives. Using a reference or class does not require adding its backing model to\n`SchemaTypes`."},"756":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-17","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#other-schematypes-entries","content":"The generic can also configure:"},"757":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-18","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#other-schematypes-entries","content":"`Scalars`: the values resolvers receive and return for each scalar; see Scalars."},"758":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-19","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#other-schematypes-entries","content":"`DefaultFieldNullability` and `DefaultInputFieldRequiredness`: defaults for output and input\nfields. Set their matching constructor options too; see Default nullability."},"759":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-20","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#other-schematypes-entries","content":"`StrictResolveType`: when `true`, unions list their members as a record (or by name), and their\n`resolveType` must return a member; see Unions."},"760":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-21","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#other-schematypes-entries","content":"Plugin-specific types, such as the context-dependent scopes used by the scope auth plugin."},"761":{"id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder-22","page_id":"/docs/guide/schema-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/schema-builder#other-schematypes-entries","content":"Each entry is optional unless a plugin requires it. For the complete list, see the\nSchemaBuilder reference."},"762":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","type":"page","content":"Troubleshooting","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting"},"763":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","content":"Guide for troubleshooting common Pothos issues"},"764":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#type-issues","content":"Type issues"},"765":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#slow-vs-code-or-typescript-performance","content":"Slow VS Code or TypeScript performance"},"766":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#runtime-issues","content":"Runtime issues"},"767":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#plugin-methods-are-not-defined","content":"Plugin methods are not defined"},"768":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#received-multiple-implementations-for-plugin","content":"Received multiple implementations for plugin"},"769":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#refs-are-undefined","content":"Refs are undefined"},"770":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","content":"Start with the section that matches the error. If the issue persists, include the error message,\npackage versions, and a minimal reproduction when reporting it."},"771":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#type-issues","content":"Ensure that TypeScript is using `strict` mode"},"772":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#type-issues","content":"Move your builder types to a separate named interface"},"773":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#type-issues","content":"This will make many type errors significantly more readable"},"774":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#type-issues","content":"The example query reads `user.id` from the request context. Adapt the named interface and query into a small reproduction of your own issue. The playground uses strict TypeScript settings; it does not reproduce your application's package installation or editor configuration."},"775":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#slow-vs-code-or-typescript-performance","content":"Ensure you are not including any very complex objects in your `Context` type. See\nhttps\\://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/issues/45405"},"776":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#plugin-methods-are-not-defined","content":"Check that the plugin is imported and configured as its documentation describes. Pothos plugins\nextend classes imported from `@pothos/core`; the application and plugin must resolve the same core\ninstance. Duplicate installations or incompatible package versions can leave methods on a different\ncopy of the class."},"777":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#plugin-methods-are-not-defined","content":"Inspect the dependency tree with your package manager:"},"778":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#plugin-methods-are-not-defined","content":"Align the package versions and dependency resolution if they load different copies. The packages do\nnot have to live in a particular root `node_modules` directory."},"779":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-16","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#received-multiple-implementations-for-plugin","content":"By default, Pothos doesn't allow multiple plugin registrations with the same name. During\ndevelopment, it can be helpful to disable this check by setting\n`SchemaBuilder.allowPluginReRegistration = true`."},"780":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-17","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#received-multiple-implementations-for-plugin","content":"Keep in mind that this not only allows plugins to be updated when for example, using HMR but can\nalso lead to unexpected behavior with plugins using the same name."},"781":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-18","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#refs-are-undefined","content":"If refs are undefined, a circular import may be reading them before initialization. Check these\nboundaries:"},"782":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-19","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#refs-are-undefined","content":"The `builder` is defined in a file that does not import any files that use the builder (or\nindirectly import it)."},"783":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-20","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#refs-are-undefined","content":"`builder.toSchema()` is called in a file that is not imported by any files that use the builder."},"784":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-21","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#refs-are-undefined","content":"This is generally done by having a simple `builder.ts` that initializes the builder to export. This\nfile can also define some core parts of your schema (the query object, scalars etc). The rest of the\nschema can then import the builder from `builder.ts`. A `schema.ts` file can then import all files\nthat define parts of the schema. `schema.ts` can then call `builder.toSchema()` and export the\nresult for use by the server."},"785":{"id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting-22","page_id":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/troubleshooting#refs-are-undefined","content":"These boundaries let deferred field callbacks read initialized refs. Options evaluated immediately\ncan still fail in an import cycle. See Circular References for the distinction\nbetween module initialization errors and recursive TypeScript inference, and App Layout\nfor an example of these modules."},"786":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","type":"page","content":"Unions","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/unions"},"787":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/unions","content":"Guide for defining Union types in Pothos"},"788":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/unions#defining-union-types","content":"Defining Union Types"},"789":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/unions#strict-resolvetype","content":"Strict resolveType"},"790":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/unions#using-union-types","content":"Using Union Types"},"791":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/unions#querying-union-types","content":"Querying Union Types"},"792":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/unions#other-ways-to-resolve-a-union","content":"Other ways to resolve a union"},"793":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/unions","content":"A union lets a field return one of several object types. Unlike an interface, a union\nhas no fields of its own, and its members don't need to share any fields."},"794":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/unions#defining-union-types","content":"This example uses two kinds of giraffe facts. Each has a backing model and an\nobject reference:"},"795":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/unions#defining-union-types","content":"The `types` array lists the union's object types. Pothos infers the union's backing type from these\nmembers, so `fact` in `resolveType` can have either backing shape."},"796":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/unions#defining-union-types","content":"The `resolveType` function selects a member object type for each returned value. Here, the\n`factKind` property distinguishes the two shapes. It is part of the backing data, but isn't exposed\nas a GraphQL field. You can return an object reference or the member's GraphQL type name."},"797":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/unions#defining-union-types","content":"You can also use registered classes or object type names declared in `SchemaTypes` in the `types`\narray. See the Objects guide for these alternatives."},"798":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/unions#strict-resolvetype","content":"By default, `resolveType` accepts any string, and a name that isn't a member only fails at runtime.\nRefs that share a backing type are also the same TypeScript type, so returning `Dog` from a union\nof `Cat` alone type-checks when both are backed by the same model."},"799":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/unions#strict-resolvetype","content":"Set `StrictResolveType: true` in `SchemaTypes` to rule both out, and give `types` as a record.\n`resolveType` then returns one of the record's keys, which TypeScript checks even when members\nshare a backing type:"},"800":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/unions#strict-resolvetype","content":"Several keys can point at the same member, so the keys can match a discriminant in your data\ndirectly. With the flag on, an array of refs in `types` is a type error. An array of object type\nnames declared in `SchemaTypes` also works, with `resolveType` returning one of those names. The\ndataloader plugin's `loadableUnion` is checked the same way and takes names."},"801":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/unions#strict-resolvetype","content":"The record form also works without the flag. The flag doesn't change interfaces, or unions whose\n`resolveType` a plugin provides, such as the errors plugin's error unions."},"802":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/unions#using-union-types","content":"Use the union reference as a field's return type. A list can contain values for any of its members.\n`$inferType` gives us the union's backing type for the data array:"},"803":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-16","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/unions#querying-union-types","content":"Use inline fragments to select fields from each member. Even though both types define `fact`, it\nmust be selected through a fragment because the union itself defines no fields. You can select\n`__typename` directly on the union to identify each result's object type:"},"804":{"id":"/docs/guide/unions-17","page_id":"/docs/guide/unions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/unions#other-ways-to-resolve-a-union","content":"Without a `resolveType` function, GraphQL can use a `__typename` property on the returned data or\nan `isTypeOf` check on the member object types, just as it does for\ninterfaces. If your application uses classes,\nset `isTypeOf: (value) => value instanceof YourClass` on each member object type and return class\ninstances. Registering a class does not add this check automatically. If an object defines\n`isTypeOf`, that check must pass even when its type was selected by `resolveType` or `__typename`."},"805":{"id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","page_id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","type":"page","content":"Using plugins","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/using-plugins"},"806":{"id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","content":"Guide for using plugins with Pothos"},"807":{"id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/using-plugins#setup","content":"Setup"},"808":{"id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/using-plugins#ordering","content":"Ordering"},"809":{"id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","content":"Using plugins with Pothos is fairly easy, but works a little differently than other plugin systems\nyou may be familiar with. One of the most important things to note is that importing plugins may\nhave some side effects on the Schema builder, and it is recommended to only import the plugins you\nare actually using."},"810":{"id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","content":"The reason for this is that Pothos's plugin system was designed to allow plugins to contribute\nfeatures in a way that feels like they are built into the core API, and allow the plugins to take\nfull advantage of the type system. This means that plugins can extend the core types in Pothos with\ntheir own properties, which happens as soon as the plugin is imported."},"811":{"id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/using-plugins#setup","content":"Each plugin should have setup instructions, but should work in a similar way."},"812":{"id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/using-plugins#setup","content":"First install the plugin:"},"813":{"id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/using-plugins#setup","content":"Next import the plugin's default export (which should just be the name of the plugin), and pass it\nwhen you create your schema builder."},"814":{"id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/using-plugins#setup","content":"Some plugins may allow you to use your own types for one of their features. This is done by passing\ntypes in through the Generic SchemaTypes used by the Schema builder:"},"815":{"id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/using-plugins#setup","content":"The second example permits the `allowed` field and denies the `denied` field according to their scopes."},"816":{"id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/using-plugins#setup","content":"These types can then be used in other parts of the API (eg. defining the scopes on a field), but\nthe details of how these types are used will be specific to each plugin, and should be covered in\nthe documentation for the plugin."},"817":{"id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/using-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/using-plugins#ordering","content":"Config hooks, resolver wrappers, and `afterBuild` are applied in reverse plugin order.\n`beforeBuild` runs in list order. This is done to ensure that the most important\n(first) plugins are applied after all other effects have been applied. For plugins that wrap\nresolvers, because the first plugins are applied last, they will be the outermost layer of wrapping\nand executed first. This means it is important to have plugins like `scope-auth` listed\nbefore other less critical plugins in your SchemaBuilder."},"818":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"page","content":"Writing plugins","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins"},"819":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-0","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","content":"Guide for writing plugins for Pothos"},"820":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-1","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#the-type-system","content":"The type system"},"821":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-2","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#pothosschematypes","content":"`PothosSchemaTypes`"},"822":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-3","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#schematypes","content":"`SchemaTypes`"},"823":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-4","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#getting-started","content":"Getting Started"},"824":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-5","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"`global-types.ts`"},"825":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-6","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#indexts","content":"`index.ts`"},"826":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-7","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#run-a-complete-plugin","content":"Run a complete plugin"},"827":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-8","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"Life cycle hooks"},"828":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-9","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#use-cases","content":"Use cases"},"829":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-10","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-to-the-schemabuilder-constructor","content":"Adding options to the SchemaBuilder constructor"},"830":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-11","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-when-building-a-schema-toschema","content":"Adding options when building a schema (`toSchema`)"},"831":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-12","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-to-types","content":"Adding options to types"},"832":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-13","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-to-fields","content":"Adding options to fields"},"833":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-14","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-new-methods-on-builder-classes","content":"Adding new methods on builder classes"},"834":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-15","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#wrapping-resolvers-to-add-runtime-functionality","content":"Wrapping resolvers to add runtime functionality"},"835":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-16","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#transforming-a-schema","content":"Transforming a schema"},"836":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-17","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#using-schematypes","content":"Using SchemaTypes"},"837":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-18","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#using-user-defined-types","content":"Using user defined types"},"838":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-19","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#request-data","content":"Request data"},"839":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-20","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#wrapping-arguments-and-inputs","content":"Wrapping arguments and inputs"},"840":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-21","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#argument-mapping-and-errors","content":"Argument mapping and errors"},"841":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-22","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#removing-fields-and-enum-values","content":"Removing fields and enum values"},"842":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-23","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#useful-methods","content":"Useful methods:"},"843":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-24","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","content":"Writing plugins for Pothos may seem a little intimidating at first, because the types used by Pothos\nare fairly complex. Fortunately, for many types of plugins, the process is actually pretty easy,\nonce you understand the core concepts of how Pothos's type system works. Don't worry if the\ndescriptions don't make complete sense at first. Going through the examples in this guide will\nhopefully make things seem a lot easier. This guide aims to cover a lot of the most common use cases\nfor creating plugins, but does not contain full API documentation. Exploring the types or source\ncode to see what all is available is highly encouraged, but should not be required for most use\ncases."},"844":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-25","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#the-type-system","content":"Pothos has 2 main pieces to its type system:"},"845":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-26","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#the-type-system","content":"`PothosSchemaTypes`: A global namespace for shared types"},"846":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-27","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#the-type-system","content":"`SchemaTypes`: A collection of types passed around through Generics specific to each instance of\n`SchemaBuilder`"},"847":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-28","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#pothosschematypes","content":"The `PothosSchemaTypes` contains interfaces for all the various options objects used throughout the\nAPI, along with some other types that plugins may want to extend. Each of the interfaces can be\nextended by a plugin to add new options. Each interface takes a number of relevant generic\nparameters that can be used to make the options more useful. For example, the interface for field\noptions will be passed the shape of the parent, the expected return type, and any arguments."},"848":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-29","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#schematypes","content":"The `SchemaTypes` type is based on the Generic argument passed to the `SchemaBuilder`, and extended\nwith reasonable defaults. Almost every interface in the `PothosSchemaTypes` will have access to it\n(look for `Types extends SchemaTypes` in the generics of almost any interface). This Type contains\nthe types for Scalars, backing models for some object and interface types, and many custom\nproperties from various plugins. If your plugin needs the user to provide some types that will be\nshared across the whole schema, this is how you will be able to access them when adding fields to\nthe options objects defined in `PothosSchemaTypes`."},"849":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-30","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#getting-started","content":"The best place to start is by looking through the\nexample plugin."},"850":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-31","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#getting-started","content":"The general structure of a plugin has 3 main parts:"},"851":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-32","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#getting-started","content":"`index.ts` which contains a plugin's actual implementation"},"852":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-33","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#getting-started","content":"`global-types.ts` which contains any additions to Pothos's built in types."},"853":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-34","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#getting-started","content":"`types.ts` which should contain any types that do NOT belong to the global `PothosSchemaTypes`\nnamespace."},"854":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-35","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#getting-started","content":"To get set up quickly, you can copy these files from the example plugin to suit your needs. The\nfirst few things to change are:"},"855":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-36","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#getting-started","content":"The plugin name in `index.ts`"},"856":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-37","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#getting-started","content":"The name of the Plugin class in `index.ts`"},"857":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-38","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#getting-started","content":"The name key/name for the plugin in the `Plugins` interface in `global-types.ts`"},"858":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-39","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#getting-started","content":"After setting up the basic layout of your plugin, I recommend starting by defining the types for\nyour plugin first (in `global-types.ts`) and setting up a test schema that uses your plugin. This\nallows you to get the user facing API for your plugin working first, so you can see that any new\noptions you add to the API are working as expected, and that any type constraints are enforced\ncorrectly. Once you are happy with your API, you can start building out the functionality in\nindex.ts. Building the types first also make the implementation easier because the properties you\nwill need to access in your extension may not exist on the config objects until you have defined\nyour types."},"859":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-40","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"`global-types.ts` must contain the following:"},"860":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-41","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"A declaration of the `PothosSchemaTypes` namespace"},"861":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-42","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"An addition to the `Plugins` interface that maps the plugin name, to the plugin type (this needs\nto be inside the `PothosSchemaTypes` namespace)"},"862":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-43","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"`global-types.ts` should NOT include definitions that do not belong to the `PothosSchemaTypes`\nnamespace. Types for your plugin should be added to a separate `types.ts` file, and imported as\nneeded into `global-types.ts`."},"863":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-44","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"To add properties to the various config objects used by the `SchemaBuilder`, you should start by\nfinding the interface that defines that config object in `@pothos/core`. Currently there are 4 main\nfiles that define the types that make up `PothosSchemaTypes` namespace."},"864":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-45","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"`type-options.ts`:"},"865":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-46","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"Contains the interfaces that define the options objects for the various types (Object,\nInterface, Enum, etc)."},"866":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-47","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"`field-options.ts`:"},"867":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-48","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"Contains the interfaces that define the options objects for creating fields"},"868":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-49","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"`schema-types.ts`:"},"869":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-50","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"Contains the interfaces for SchemaBuilder options, SchemaTypes, options for `toSchema`, and other\nutility interfaces that may be useful for plugins to extend that do not fall into one of the\nother categories."},"870":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-51","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"`classes.ts`:"},"871":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-52","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"Contains interfaces that describe the classes used by Pothos, include `SchemaBuilder` and the\nvarious field builder classes."},"872":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-53","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#global-typests","content":"Once you have identified a type you wish to extend, copy it into the `PothosSchemaTypes` namespace\nin your `global-types.ts`, but remove all the existing properties. You will need to keep all the\nGenerics used by the interface, and should import the types used in generics from `@pothos/core`.\nYou can now add any new properties to the interface that your plugin needs. Making new properties\noptional (`newProp?: TypeOfProp`) is recommended for most use cases."},"873":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-54","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#indexts","content":"`index.ts` must contain the following:"},"874":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-55","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#indexts","content":"A bare import of the global types (`import './global-types';`)"},"875":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-56","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#indexts","content":"The plugins name, which should be typed as a string literal rather than as a generic string:"},"876":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-57","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#indexts","content":"`const pluginName = 'example'`"},"877":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-58","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#indexts","content":"A default export of the plugin name `export default pluginName`"},"878":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-59","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#indexts","content":"A class that extends BasePlugin:\n`export class PothosExamplePlugin<Types extends SchemaTypes> extends BasePlugin<Types> {}`"},"879":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-60","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#indexts","content":"`BasePlugin` and `SchemaTypes` can both be imported from `@pothos/core`"},"880":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-61","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#indexts","content":"A call to register the plugin: `SchemaBuilder.registerPlugin(pluginName, PothosExamplePlugin);`"},"881":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-62","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#indexts","content":"`SchemaBuilder` can also be imported from `@pothos/core`"},"882":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-63","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#run-a-complete-plugin","content":"The example adds a `logLabel` builder option, registers a local plugin, and wraps each resolver. The query logs two field resolutions. The request counter starts at one for each execution because it receives a fresh context; `logLabel` labels the messages."},"883":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-64","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#run-a-complete-plugin","content":"The plugin is registered before it is passed to the builder:"},"884":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-65","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#run-a-complete-plugin","content":"Open `global-types.ts` to see the plugin name and `logLabel` option added to `PothosSchemaTypes`. The query includes synchronous and asynchronous resolvers; the wrapper returns their values unchanged."},"885":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-66","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#run-a-complete-plugin","content":"The sections below link runnable hooks to complete examples. Interface declarations describe the types to merge into `PothosSchemaTypes`; they are reference fragments rather than independent schemas. Subscription hooks need a runtime that executes subscriptions."},"886":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-67","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"The `SchemaBuilder` will instantiate plugins each time the `toSchema` method is called on the\nbuilder. As the schema is built, it will invoke the various life cycle methods on each plugin if\nthey have been defined."},"887":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-68","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"To hook into each lifecycle event, simply define the corresponding function in your plugin class.\nFor the exact function signature, see the `index.ts` of the example plugin."},"888":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-69","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"`onTypeConfig`: Invoked for each type, with the config object that will be used to construct the\nunderlying GraphQL type."},"889":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-70","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"`onOutputFieldConfig`: Invoked for each Object, or Interface field, with the config object\ndescribing the field."},"890":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-71","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"`onInputFieldConfig`: Invoked for each InputObject field, or field argument, with the config\nobject describing the field."},"891":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-72","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"`onEnumValueConfig`: Invoked for each value in an enum"},"892":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-73","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"`beforeBuild`: Invoked before building schemas, last chance to add new types or fields."},"893":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-74","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"`afterBuild`: Invoked with the fully built Schema."},"894":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-75","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"`wrapResolve`: Invoked when creating the resolver for each field"},"895":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-76","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"`wrapSubscribe`: Invoked for each field in the `Subscriptions` object."},"896":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-77","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"`wrapArgMappers`: Wraps outside argument mapping for resolvers and subscription setup; see\nArgument mapping and errors."},"897":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-78","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"`wrapResolveType`: Invoked for each Union and Interface."},"898":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-79","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"`wrapIsTypeOf`: Invoked for each Object, including objects without an `isTypeOf` function."},"899":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-80","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"Each of the lifecycle methods above (except `beforeBuild`) expect a return value that matches\ntheir first argument (either a config object, or the resolve/subscribe/resolveType function). If\nyour plugin does not need to modify these values, it can simply return the value that was passed in.\nWhen your plugin does need to change one of the config values, you should return a copy of the\nconfig object with your modifications, rather than modifying the config object that was passed in.\nThis can be done by either using `Object.assign`, or spreading the original config into a new object\n`{...originalConfig, newProp: newValue }`."},"900":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-81","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"Inherited interface fields have a separate output field config for each owning object or interface.\n`onOutputFieldConfig` runs once for each declaration and owner pair in a schema build. On an inherited\nfield, `parentType` names the owner and `declaringType` names the interface that originally declared\nthe field. Arguments also have their own configs with the owner's `parentType`. `kind` and\n`graphqlKind` still describe the field builder that declared the field, so plugin-specific field\noptions keep their original meaning. Resolver and subscriber wrappers receive the owner's config."},"901":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-82","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"Plugins that generate shared output types for interface fields must use\n`config.declaringType ?? config.parentType` for the generated type's identity. Generating a different\nunion for each owner would make the object field incompatible with the interface field. Plugins\nthat apply type-level behavior should use `parentType` to look up the owner."},"902":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-83","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"Each config object will have the properties expected by the GraphQL for creating the types or fields\n(although some properties like `resolve` will be added later), but will also include a number of\nPothos specific properties. These properties include `graphqlKind` to indicate what kind of GraphQL\ntype the config object is for, `pothosOptions`, which contains all the options passed in to the\nschema builder when creating the type or field."},"903":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-84","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"If your plugin needs to add additional types or fields to the schema it should do this in the\n`beforeBuild` hook. Any types added to the schema after this, may not be included correctly. Plugins\nshould also account for the fact that a new instance of the plugin will be created each time the\nschema is called, so any types or fields added to the schema should only be applied once (per\nbuilder), even if multiple instances of the plugin are created. To help with this, there is a\n`runUnique` helper on the base plugin class, which accepts a key, and a callback, and will only run\na callback once per builder for the given key, even across multiple `toSchema` calls."},"904":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-85","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#life-cycle-hooks","content":"The example calls `toSchema()` twice, but the preparation callback runs only once:"},"905":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-86","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#use-cases","content":"Below are a few of the most common use cases for how a plugin might extend the Pothos with very\nsimplified examples. Most plugins will likely need a combination of these strategies, and some uses\ncases may not be well documented. If you are unsure about how to solve a specific problem, feel free\nto open a GitHub Issue for more help."},"906":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-87","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#use-cases","content":"In the examples below, when \"extending an interface\", the interface should be added to the\n`PothosSchemaTypes` namespace in `global-types.ts`."},"907":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-88","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-to-the-schemabuilder-constructor","content":"You may have noticed that plugins are not instantiated by the user, and therefore users can't pass\noptions directly into your plugin when creating it. Instead, the recommended way to configure your\nplugin is by contributing new properties to the options object passed to the SchemaBuilder\nconstructor. This can be done by extending the `SchemaBuilderOptions` interface."},"908":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-89","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-to-the-schemabuilder-constructor","content":"Extending this interface will allow the user to pass in these new options when creating an instance\nof `SchemaBuilder`."},"909":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-90","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-to-the-schemabuilder-constructor","content":"You can then access the options through `this.builder.options` in your plugin, with everything\ncorrectly typed. The example reflects `nestedOptionsObject.exampleOption` in the object description:"},"910":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-91","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-when-building-a-schema-toschema","content":"In some cases, your plugin may be designed for schemas that be built in different modes. For example\nthe mocks plugin allows the schema to be built repeatedly with different sets of mocks, or the\nsubGraph allows building a schema multiple times to generate separate subgraphs. For these cases,\nyou can extend the options passed to `toSchema` instead:"},"911":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-92","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-when-building-a-schema-toschema","content":"These options can be accessed through `this.options` in your plugin:"},"912":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-93","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-to-types","content":"Each GraphQL type has its own options interface which can be extended. For example, to extend the\noptions for creating an Object type:"},"913":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-94","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-to-types","content":"These options can then be accessed in your plugin when you receive the config for the type:"},"914":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-95","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-to-types","content":"In the example above, we need to check `typeConfig.kind` to ensure that the type config is for an\nobject. Without this check, typescript will not know that the config object is for an object, and\nwill not let us access the property. `typeConfig.kind` corresponds to how Pothos splits up Types for\nits config objects, meaning that it has separate `kind`s for `Query`, `Mutation`, and `Subscription`\neven though these are all `Objects` in GraphQL terminology. The `typeConfig.graphqlKind` can be used\nto get the actual GraphQL type instead."},"915":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-96","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-to-fields","content":"Similar to Types, fields also have a number of interfaces that can be extended to add options to\nvarious types of fields:"},"916":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-97","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-to-fields","content":"Field interfaces have a few more generics than other interfaces we have looked at. These generics\ncan be used to make the options you add more specific to the field currently being defined. It is\nimportant to copy all the generics of the interfaces as they are defined in `@pothos/core` even if\nyou do not use the generics in your own properties. If the generics do not match, typescript won't\nbe able to merge the definitions. You do NOT need to include the `extends` clause of the interface,\nif the interface extends another interface (like `FieldOptions`)."},"917":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-98","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-options-to-fields","content":"Similar to Type options, Field options will be available in the fieldConfigs in your plugin, once\nyou check that the fieldConfig is for the correct `kind` of field."},"918":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-99","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-new-methods-on-builder-classes","content":"Adding new methods to `SchemaBuilder` or one of the `FieldBuilder` classes is also done through\nextending interfaces. Extending these interfaces is how typescript is able to know these methods\nexist, even though they are not defined on the original classes."},"919":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-100","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-new-methods-on-builder-classes","content":"The above is a simple example of defining a new `buildCustomObject` method that takes no arguments,\nand returns a reference to a new custom object type. Defining this type will not work on its own,\nand we still need to define the actual implementation of this method. This might look like:"},"920":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-101","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#adding-new-methods-on-builder-classes","content":"Note that the above function does NOT use an arrow function, so that the function can access `this`\nas a reference to the SchemaBuilder instance."},"921":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-102","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#wrapping-resolvers-to-add-runtime-functionality","content":"Some plugins will need to add runtime behavior. There are a few lifecycle hooks for wrapping\n`resolve`, `subscribe`, and `resolveType`. These hooks will receive the function they are wrapping,\nalong with a config object for the field or type they are associated with, and should return either\nthe original function, or a wrapper function with the same API."},"922":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-103","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#wrapping-resolvers-to-add-runtime-functionality","content":"It is important to remember that resolvers can resolve values in a number of ways (normal values,\npromises, or even something as complicated `Promise<(Promise<T> | T)[]>`. So be careful when using a\nwrapper that introspected the return value of a resolve function. Plugins should only wrap resolvers\nwhen absolutely necessary."},"923":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-104","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#transforming-a-schema","content":"For some plugins the other provided lifecycle hooks may not be sufficiently powerful to modify the schema\nin all the ways a plugin may want. For example removing types from the schema (eg. the `SubGraph`\nplugin). In these cases, the `afterBuild` hook can be used. It receives the built schema, and is\nexpected to return either the schema it was passed, or a completely new schema. This allows plugins\nto use 3rd party libraries like `graphql-tools` to arbitrarily transform schemas if desired."},"924":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-105","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#transforming-a-schema","content":"This example returns a new schema with a changed description. The changed description is available through `__schema.description`:"},"925":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-106","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#using-schematypes","content":"You may have noticed that almost every interface and type in `@pothos/core` take a generic that\nlooks like: `Types extends SchemaTypes`. This type is what allows Pothos and its plugins to share\ntype information across the entire schema, and to incorporate user defined types into that system.\nThese SchemaTypes are a combination of default types merged with the Types provided in the Generic\nparameter of the SchemaBuilder constructor, and includes a wide variety of useful types:"},"926":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-107","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#using-schematypes","content":"Types for all the scalars"},"927":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-108","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#using-schematypes","content":"Types for backing models used by objects and interfaces when referenced via strings"},"928":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-109","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#using-schematypes","content":"The type used for the context and root objects"},"929":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-110","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#using-schematypes","content":"Settings for default nullability of fields"},"930":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-111","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#using-schematypes","content":"Any user defined types specific to plugins (more info below)"},"931":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-112","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#using-schematypes","content":"There are many ways these types can be used, but one of the most common is to access the type for\nthe context object, so that you can correctly type a callback function for your plugin that accepts\nthe context object."},"932":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-113","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#using-user-defined-types","content":"As mentioned above, your plugin can also contribute its own user definable types to the SchemaTypes\ninterface. You can see examples of this in the several of the plugins including the directives and\n`scope-auth` plugins. Adding your own types to SchemaTypes requires extending 2 interfaces: The\n`UserSchemaTypes` which describes the type the user will need to provide, and the\n`ExtendDefaultTypes` interface, which is used to set default values if the User does not provide\ntheir own types."},"933":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-114","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#using-user-defined-types","content":"The User provided type can then be accessed using `Types['NewExampleTypes']` in any interface or\ntype that receives `SchemaTypes` as a generic argument."},"934":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-115","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#request-data","content":"Plugins that wrap resolvers may need to store some data that is unique to the current request. In these\ncases your plugin can define a `createRequestData` method, and use the `requestData` method to get\nthe data for the current request."},"935":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-116","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#request-data","content":"The shape of requestData can be defined via the second generic parameter of the `BasePlugin` class.\nThe `requestData` method expects the context object as its only argument, which is used to uniquely\nidentify the current request."},"936":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-117","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#wrapping-arguments-and-inputs","content":"The plugin API does not directly wrap individual input fields. `wrapResolve` and `wrapSubscribe`\ncan transform the `args` object before passing it to the original function. These wrappers receive\narguments after any registered argument mappers have run; use `fieldConfig.argMappers` when the\ntransformation needs to happen before resolver or subscription wrappers."},"937":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-118","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#wrapping-arguments-and-inputs","content":"Figuring out how to wrap inputs can be a little complex, especially when dealing with recursive\ninputs, and optimizing to wrap as little as possible. To help with this, Pothos has a couple of\nutility functions that can make this easier:"},"938":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-119","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#wrapping-arguments-and-inputs","content":"`mapInputFields`: Used to select affected input fields and extract some configuration"},"939":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-120","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#wrapping-arguments-and-inputs","content":"`createInputValueMapper`: Creates a mapping function that uses the result of `mapInputFields` to\nmap inputs in an args object to new values."},"940":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-121","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#wrapping-arguments-and-inputs","content":"The relay plugin uses these methods to decode `globalID` inputs. This example uses the same utilities to trim marked strings, including strings inside a nested input list. Fields without the `trimInput` extension retain their whitespace:"},"941":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-122","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#wrapping-arguments-and-inputs","content":"Using these utilities allows moving more logic to build time (figuring out which fields need\nmapping) so that the runtime overhead is as small as possible."},"942":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-123","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#wrapping-arguments-and-inputs","content":"`createInputValueMapper` may be useful for some use cases, for some plugins it may be better to\ncreate a custom mapping function, but still use the result of `mapInputFields`."},"943":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-124","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#wrapping-arguments-and-inputs","content":"`mapInputFields` returns a map whose keys are field/argument names, and whose values are objects\nwith the following shape:"},"944":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-125","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#wrapping-arguments-and-inputs","content":"if the `kind` is `InputObject` then the mapping object will also have a fields property with an\nobject of the following shape:"},"945":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-126","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#wrapping-arguments-and-inputs","content":"Both the root map and nested `fields.map` maps contain fields with non-null mappings and\ninput objects with mapped descendants. If the mapping function returned null for all fields, the\n`mapInputFields` will return null instead of returning a map to indicate no wrapping should occur"},"946":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-127","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#argument-mapping-and-errors","content":"Register an argument mapper in `onOutputFieldConfig` by returning a config with an updated\n`argMappers` array. Each mapper receives `(args, context, info)` and returns the next argument object,\nor a promise of it. `args` is a `Record<string, unknown>`; `info` is Pothos's `PartialResolveInfo`.\nPreserve existing mappers when appending your own. Mappers run sequentially in array order, awaiting\neach result before passing it to the next mapper."},"947":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-128","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#argument-mapping-and-errors","content":"For a resolver call, execution enters these layers in order:"},"948":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-129","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#argument-mapping-and-errors","content":"`wrapArgMappers` wrappers, which see the arguments before mapping."},"949":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-130","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#argument-mapping-and-errors","content":"The field's `argMappers`."},"950":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-131","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#argument-mapping-and-errors","content":"`wrapResolve` wrappers, which see the mapped arguments."},"951":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-132","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#argument-mapping-and-errors","content":"The application's resolver."},"952":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-133","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#argument-mapping-and-errors","content":"Subscription setup follows the same order with `wrapSubscribe` and the application's subscribe\nfunction in the last two positions. The subscription's event resolver has its own resolver path;\ndo not assume mapping runs only once per subscription. Within each wrapper hook, the first plugin\nin the builder's `plugins` array is outermost. Configuration hooks run from the last plugin to the\nfirst, so appending in `onOutputFieldConfig` determines the final mapper-array order at build time."},"953":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-134","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#argument-mapping-and-errors","content":"A `try/catch` inside `wrapResolve` cannot catch a mapper failure: mapping happens before that wrapper\nis entered. Use `wrapArgMappers` for an outer error boundary. The example below appends a synchronous\nnormalizer and an asynchronous validator for fields marked with `extensions: { normalizeName: true }`.\nThe boundary converts their `NameInputError` failures to a GraphQL error with a `BAD_USER_INPUT` code."},"954":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-135","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#argument-mapping-and-errors","content":"The `await` inside `try` catches both a synchronous throw and an asynchronous rejection. Returning\nthe promise without awaiting it would let its rejection escape that `catch`. Preserve an absent\nfunction by returning `undefined` unchanged: this hook is also called when a field has no subscribe\nfunction. It should not accidentally manufacture one."},"955":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-136","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#argument-mapping-and-errors","content":"The boundary also encloses resolver/subscriber wrappers and the application function, so it can\nreceive their errors too. Handle only errors belonging to the policy you intend to implement;\nthis example rethrows every error other than `NameInputError`. It does not catch GraphQL document\nvalidation or variable-coercion errors, which occur before field execution, or errors thrown by a\nsubscription's async iterable while producing events."},"956":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-137","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#argument-mapping-and-errors","content":"Use the registered plugin and opt in on the field in a separate `schema.ts`:"},"957":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-138","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#argument-mapping-and-errors","content":"`greeting(name: \"  Gina  \")` returns `\"Hello, Gina!\"`. A blank name throws synchronously; `\"reserved\"`\nrejects from the asynchronous mapper, even when surrounded by whitespace. Both failures prevent the\nresolver from running. `\"broken\"` reaches the resolver and retains the `\"Greeting unavailable\"` error\nwithout a `BAD_USER_INPUT` code."},"958":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-139","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#argument-mapping-and-errors","content":"These mappers preserve the schema's string argument shape. Registering a mapper does not itself\nchange the TypeScript argument types inferred from field definitions. The earlier trim example\nremains a valid `wrapResolve` transformation; moving its mapper into `argMappers` would make it run\nbefore all resolver wrappers instead of inside that plugin's wrapper."},"959":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-140","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#removing-fields-and-enum-values","content":"Plugins can remove fields from objects, interfaces, and input objects, and remove specific values\nfrom enums. To do this, simply return null from the corresponding on\\*Config plugin hook:"},"960":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-141","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#removing-fields-and-enum-values","content":"Introspection omits the `removeMe` output and input fields and the enum value backed by `removeMe`. The other fields and enum values remain."},"961":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-142","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#removing-fields-and-enum-values","content":"Removing whole types from the schema needs to be done by transforming the schema during the\n`afterBuild` hook. See the `sub-graph` plugin for a more complete example of removing types."},"962":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-143","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#useful-methods","content":"`builder.configStore.onTypeConfig`: Takes a type ref and a callback, and will invoke the callback\nwith the config for the referenced type once available."},"963":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-144","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#useful-methods","content":"`fieldRef.onFirstUse` Takes a callback to invoke once the config for the field is available."},"964":{"id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins-145","page_id":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guide/writing-plugins#useful-methods","content":"`buildCache.getTypeConfig` Gets the config for a given type after it has been passed through any\nmodifications applied by plugins."},"965":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","type":"page","content":"Giraphql to Pothos","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Migrations"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos"},"966":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-0","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","content":"Migration guide for upgrading from GiraphQL 2.* to Pothos 3.0"},"967":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-1","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#migrating-from-giraphql-to-pothos","content":"Migrating from GiraphQL to Pothos"},"968":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-2","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#changes-for-consumers-of-giraphql","content":"Changes for consumers of GiraphQL"},"969":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-3","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#plugin-specific-changes","content":"Plugin specific changes"},"970":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-4","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#prisma-plugin","content":"Prisma plugin"},"971":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-5","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#for-plugin-authors","content":"For plugin authors"},"972":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-6","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#migrating-from-giraphql-to-pothos","content":"As of 3.0 GiraphQL has been renamed to Pothos. The primary motivation for this rename is to make\nthis library and associated projects, guides, and other content to be more discoverable. GiraphQL is\nnot visually distinct from GraphQL, and has often been interpreted as a typo. Search engines tend to\nauto-correct the name to GraphQL, making it hard to search for."},"973":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-7","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#changes-for-consumers-of-giraphql","content":"All packages have been moved from the `@giraphql/*` scope to `@pothos/*` scope."},"974":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-8","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#changes-for-consumers-of-giraphql","content":"The `GiraphQLSchemaTypes` global typescript scope has been renamed to `PothosSchemaTypes`"},"975":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-9","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#changes-for-consumers-of-giraphql","content":"Exported types prefixed with `GiraphQL` have had that prefix replaced with `Pothos`"},"976":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-10","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#changes-for-consumers-of-giraphql","content":"For the most part, the easiest way to upgrade is by doing a CASE SENSITIVE search and replace of\n`giraphql` -> `pothos` and `GiraphQL` -> `Pothos`. The only non-documentation change between the\nlatest version of GiraphQL and the initial version of Pothos (`v3.0.0`) are renaming of types and\npackages."},"977":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-11","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#prisma-plugin","content":"The generator/provider for prisma types has been renamed to `prisma-pothos-types`."},"978":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-12","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#prisma-plugin","content":"You will need to update your prisma schema to use the new provider:"},"979":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-13","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#for-plugin-authors","content":"Some `extensions` fields in the build schemas have been renamed. Specifically:"},"980":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-14","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#for-plugin-authors","content":"`giraphQLOptions` has been renamed to `pothosOptions`"},"981":{"id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos-15","page_id":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/giraphql-pothos#for-plugin-authors","content":"`giraphQLConfig` has been renamed to `pothosConfig`"},"982":{"id":"/docs/migrations","page_id":"/docs/migrations","type":"page","content":"Migrations","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Migrations"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations"},"983":{"id":"/docs/migrations-0","page_id":"/docs/migrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations","content":"List of Pothos migration guides"},"984":{"id":"/docs/migrations-1","page_id":"/docs/migrations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations#migration-to-pothos-from-other-graphql-libraries","content":"Migration to Pothos from other GraphQL libraries"},"985":{"id":"/docs/migrations-2","page_id":"/docs/migrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations","content":"3.\\* to (4.0)"},"986":{"id":"/docs/migrations-3","page_id":"/docs/migrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations","content":"GiraphQL (2.\\*) to Pothos (3.0)"},"987":{"id":"/docs/migrations-4","page_id":"/docs/migrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations","content":"1.\\* to 2.0"},"988":{"id":"/docs/migrations-5","page_id":"/docs/migrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations#migration-to-pothos-from-other-graphql-libraries","content":"The following tools can help migrate an existing schema. Review their generated code and adapt\nthe resolvers and application integration to your project."},"989":{"id":"/docs/migrations-6","page_id":"/docs/migrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations#migration-to-pothos-from-other-graphql-libraries","content":"Nexus to Pothos codemod"},"990":{"id":"/docs/migrations-7","page_id":"/docs/migrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations#migration-to-pothos-from-other-graphql-libraries","content":"This 3rd party code-mod aims to transform all the nexus types, queries and mutations to Pothos\nequivalents. This codemod will still require some manual adjustments to get everything working\ncorrectly, but can be a huge help in the migration process."},"991":{"id":"/docs/migrations-8","page_id":"/docs/migrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations#migration-to-pothos-from-other-graphql-libraries","content":"Pothos Generator"},"992":{"id":"/docs/migrations-9","page_id":"/docs/migrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations#migration-to-pothos-from-other-graphql-libraries","content":"This is an undocumented CLI that can convert a schema into valid Pothos code. Resolvers are all\nplaceholders that throw errors, so this is not quite as useful as it sounds, but can be helpful,\nespecially for generating input types."},"993":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","type":"page","content":"v2.0","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Migrations"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v2"},"994":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-0","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2","content":"Migration guide for upgrading from GiraphQL 1.* to GiraphQL 2.0"},"995":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-1","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v2#breaking-changes","content":"Breaking changes"},"996":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-2","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v2#auth-plugin","content":"Auth plugin"},"997":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-3","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v2#plugin-names","content":"Plugin names"},"998":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-4","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v2#plugin-order","content":"Plugin Order"},"999":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-5","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v2#inputfieldbuilderbool-and-inputfieldbuilderboollist","content":"InputFieldBuilder.bool and InputFieldBuilder.boolList"},"1000":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-6","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v2#args-on-exposed-fields","content":"args on \"exposed\" fields"},"1001":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-7","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v2#plugin-api","content":"Plugin API"},"1002":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-8","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v2#new-features","content":"New features"},"1003":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-9","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2","content":"The 2.0 release was mostly focused around re-designing the plugin system so it could be properly\ndocumented, and made available for broader adoption. The previous plugin system allowed plugins to\nuse the FieldWrapper base class to wrap fields. Unfortunately the overhead of this wrapping strategy\nwas significantly higher than expected, and could not be optimized in a way that justified the\nconveniences it provided."},"1004":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-10","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2#auth-plugin","content":"The auth plugin has been replaced by a new `scope-auth` plugin. Unfortunately due to the performance\nproblems with the original field wrapping API, the auth plugin had to be re-designed, and\nmaintaining the existing API at the cost of significant performance overhead did not seem justified."},"1005":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-11","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2#auth-plugin","content":"Any existing usage of the `auth` plugin will need to be replaced with the new `scope-auth` plugin.\nThe API of the new `scope-auth` plugin is substantially different, and the specifics of the\nmigration will depend on the exact usage of the original auth plugin. Documentation on the new\nplugin can be found here."},"1006":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-12","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2#plugin-names","content":"Plugin names have been normalized, and are now exported as the default export of the plugin\npackages."},"1007":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-13","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2#plugin-names","content":"Change:"},"1008":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-14","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2#plugin-order","content":"The old plugin API did not make strong guarantees about the order in which plugin hooks would be\nexecuted. Config hooks and resolver wrappers are applied in reverse order; `beforeBuild` runs in list order. The most critical plugins (like\n`scope-auth`) should appear first in the list of plugins. This ensures that any modifications made\nby other plugins are applied first, and lets the more important plugins be at the top of the call\nstack when resolving fields."},"1009":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-15","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2#inputfieldbuilderbool-and-inputfieldbuilderboollist","content":"The `bool` alias on `InputFieldBuilder` has been removed, as it was inconsistent with the other\nfield builders and general naming convention of other methods. Usage of this method should be\nconverted to the canonical `boolean` and `booleanList` methods."},"1010":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-16","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2#inputfieldbuilderbool-and-inputfieldbuilderboollist","content":"Change:"},"1011":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-17","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2#args-on-exposed-fields","content":"Fields defined with the `expose` helpers no longer accept `args` since they also do not have a\nresolver."},"1012":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-18","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2#plugin-api","content":"The Plugin API has been completely re-designed and is now\ndocumented here. new instances of plugins are now instantiated each time\n`toSchema` is called on the `SchemaBuilder`, rather than being tied to the lifetime of the\n`SchemaBuilder` itself."},"1013":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-19","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2#new-features","content":"Lots of new documentation"},"1014":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-20","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2#new-features","content":"New scope-auth plugin"},"1015":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-21","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2#new-features","content":"New directives plugin"},"1016":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-22","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2#new-features","content":"New plugin API"},"1017":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v2-23","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v2#new-features","content":"Significant performance improvements in smart-subscriptions and scope-auth plugins"},"1018":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"page","content":"v4.0","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Migrations"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4"},"1019":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-0","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4","content":"Migration guide for upgrading from Pothos 3.x to Pothos 4.0"},"1020":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-1","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#overview","content":"Overview"},"1021":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-2","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-minimum-versions","content":"New minimum versions"},"1022":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-3","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#simple-upgrade-restore-30-options-and-defaults","content":"Simple Upgrade (restore 3.0 options and defaults)"},"1023":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-4","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#manual-update","content":"Manual update"},"1024":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-5","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#breaking-api-changes","content":"Breaking API Changes:"},"1025":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-6","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#pothoscore","content":"`@pothos/core`"},"1026":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-7","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#default-field-nullability","content":"Default field nullability"},"1027":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-8","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#default-id-scalar-types","content":"Default ID Scalar types"},"1028":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-9","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#pothosplugin-relay","content":"`@pothos/plugin-relay`"},"1029":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-10","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#renamed-options","content":"Renamed options"},"1030":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-11","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-defaults","content":"New defaults"},"1031":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-12","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#pothosplugin-prisma","content":"`@pothos/plugin-prisma`"},"1032":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-13","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#nullable-relations","content":"Nullable relations"},"1033":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-14","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#pothosplugin-directives","content":"`@pothos/plugin-directives`"},"1034":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-15","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#pothosplugin-errors","content":"`@pothos/plugin-errors`"},"1035":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-16","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#renamed-options-1","content":"Renamed options"},"1036":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-17","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#pothosplugin-scope-auth","content":"`@pothos/plugin-scope-auth`"},"1037":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-18","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#renamed-options-2","content":"Renamed options"},"1038":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-19","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#pothosplugin-zod-previously-pothosplugin-validation","content":"`@pothos/plugin-zod` (previously `@pothos/plugin-validation`)"},"1039":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-20","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#renamed-options-3","content":"Renamed options"},"1040":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-21","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#pothosplugin-authz-has-been-removed","content":"`@pothos/plugin-authz` has been removed"},"1041":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-22","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#plugin-api-and-type-changes","content":"Plugin API and Type changes"},"1042":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-23","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#classes","content":"Classes"},"1043":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-24","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#exported-types","content":"Exported types"},"1044":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-25","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/migrations/v4#field-options","content":"Field options"},"1045":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-26","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#overview","content":"Migrating from Pothos 3.x to 4.0"},"1046":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-27","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#overview","content":"The `4.0` release of Pothos is largely focused on updating 4 things:"},"1047":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-28","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#overview","content":"Improving outdated defaults to be more consistent and aligned with best practices"},"1048":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-29","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#overview","content":"Updating naming of some config options to be more consistent"},"1049":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-30","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#overview","content":"Updating minimum versions of peer dependencies"},"1050":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-31","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#overview","content":"Updating internal types to support some previously challenging plugin patterns"},"1051":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-32","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#overview","content":"While the internals of Pothos have almost entirely been re-written, the public API surface should\nhave minimal changes for most users. The first 2 sets of changes will cover the majority of\nchanges relevant to the majority of applications. To make the upgrade as simple as\npossible, some options were added to maintain the defaults and option names from `3.x` which are\ndescribed in the simple upgrade section below."},"1052":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-33","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-minimum-versions","content":"`typescript`: `5.0.2` (`5.4` since `@pothos/core` 4.16)"},"1053":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-34","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-minimum-versions","content":"`graphql`: `16.6.0`"},"1054":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-35","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-minimum-versions","content":"`node`: `18.0`"},"1055":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-36","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#simple-upgrade-restore-30-options-and-defaults","content":"You can restore the 3.x defaults by adding the Defaults versions to both the SchemaTypes and the\nbuilder options:"},"1056":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-37","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#simple-upgrade-restore-30-options-and-defaults","content":"This will restore all the defaults and config options from previous Pothos versions for both core\nand plugins."},"1057":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-38","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#simple-upgrade-restore-30-options-and-defaults","content":"If you are using `@pothos/plugin-validation`, it has been renamed to `@pothos/plugin-zod`, as part of the v4 release. The name\n`@pothos/plugin-validation` now refers to the Standard Schema validation plugin."},"1058":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-39","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#manual-update","content":"There are a number of new defaults and changes to options for various plugins. To fully upgrade to\n4.0 see the full list of breaking changes below:"},"1059":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-40","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#breaking-api-changes","content":"This section covers breaking API changes that can be automatically reverted by using the Simple\nUpgrade process described above."},"1060":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-41","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#breaking-api-changes","content":"Changes to types and classes outside the main Pothos API are described in the next section. Those\nchanges will primarily affect other plugins and tools written for pothos, but may be relevant to\nsome type helpers you have created."},"1061":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-42","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#default-field-nullability","content":"In previous versions of Pothos, fields were non-nullable by default. This is inconsistent with the\nrest of the GraphQL ecosystem, so the default is being changed to make fields nullable by default."},"1062":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-43","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#default-field-nullability","content":"To restore the previous behavior you can set the `defaultFieldNullability` option when creating your\nbuilder:"},"1063":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-44","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#default-field-nullability","content":"Alternatively, fields can be updated to add `nullable: false` to the fields options."},"1064":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-45","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#default-id-scalar-types","content":"The default types for the built in `ID` Scalar has been changed to more closely match the behavior\nof Javascript GraphQL server implementations:"},"1065":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-46","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#default-id-scalar-types","content":"This will make working with IDs in arguments and input types easier by avoiding unnecessary type\nchecks to see if an `ID` is a `number` or `string`."},"1066":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-47","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#default-id-scalar-types","content":"When returning an `ID` from a scalar you will be able to return a `string`, `number`, or `bigint`."},"1067":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-48","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#default-id-scalar-types","content":"To restore the previous defaults you can customize the `ID` scalar types when creating your builder:"},"1068":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-49","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#renamed-options","content":"The base relay plugin options have moved from `relayOptions` to `relay` to be more consistent with\noptions for other plugins."},"1069":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-50","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-defaults","content":"A number of the default values for relay options have changed:"},"1070":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-51","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-defaults","content":"`clientMutationId`: Now defaults to `\"omit\"` and was previously `\"required\"`"},"1071":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-52","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-defaults","content":"`clientMutationId` was only required in early versions of the relay client, and is no-longer\nrecommended."},"1072":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-53","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-defaults","content":"`cursorType`: Now defaults to `\"String\"` and was previously `\"ID\"`"},"1073":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-54","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-defaults","content":"The previous defaults were inconsistent about the type of a cursor. Cursors generally should not\nbe treated as IDs as they are meant to indicate a position in a list, and may contain\ninformation specific to other filters or arguments applied to the connection."},"1074":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-55","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-defaults","content":"`brandLoadedObjects`: Now defaults to `true` and was previously `false`"},"1075":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-56","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-defaults","content":"This change will improve developer experience for most node implementations, as it removes the\nneed for `isTypeOf` to be defined for most nodes."},"1076":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-57","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-defaults","content":"`edgesFieldOptions.nullable`: Now defaults to\n`{ list: options.defaultFieldNullability, items: true }` and was previously\n`{ list: false, items: true }`"},"1077":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-58","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-defaults","content":"`nodeFieldOptions.nullable`: Now defaults to `options.defaultFieldNullability` and was previously\n`false`"},"1078":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-59","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-defaults","content":"This new default is intended to align with the relay connection spec, which does not expect\nconnections to be NonNullable by default"},"1079":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-60","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#new-defaults","content":"To restore the previous defaults you can pass the old values when setting up the builder:"},"1080":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-61","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#nullable-relations","content":"Previously the prisma would allow t.relation to define non-nullable fields using nullable relations.\nThe plugin option now requires an `onNull` option to handle null relations on NonNullable fields"},"1081":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-62","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#nullable-relations","content":"To restore the previous behavior you can set the `onNull` option to `'error'`, which will result in\na runtime error when the field returns null"},"1082":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-63","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#nullable-relations","content":"Alternatively you can mark the field as nullable:"},"1083":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-64","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#nullable-relations","content":"`onNull` can also be set to a function that returns either a record matching the type of the\nrelation, or a custom Error to throw when the relation is null."},"1084":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-65","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#pothosplugin-directives","content":"`useGraphQLToolsUnorderedDirectives` has been nested inside a `directives` options object:"},"1085":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-66","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#renamed-options-1","content":"The base error plugin options have moved from `errorOptions` to `errors` to be more consistent with\noptions for other plugins."},"1086":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-67","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#renamed-options-2","content":"The base scope-auth plugin options have moved from `scopeAuthOptions` to `scopeAuth` to be more\nconsistent with options for other plugins. The `authScopes` option has been moved to\n`scopeAuth.authScopes` to keep all options for the plugin in one options object."},"1087":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-68","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#renamed-options-3","content":"The base validation plugin options have moved from `validationOptions` to `zod` to be more\nconsistent with options for other plugins."},"1088":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-69","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#pothosplugin-authz-has-been-removed","content":"The `@pothos/plugin-authz` plugin has been removed, because the underlying `@graphql-authz/core` is\nnot actively maintained, and has left critical security vulnerabilities unaddressed."},"1089":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-70","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#plugin-api-and-type-changes","content":"Unlike the defaults and config changes, the changes to the types and classes used throughout Pothos\ncan't be easily made backwards compatibility with the 3.x releases. Below is a summary of the main\nchanges made to the types and classes that may be used by plugins, helpers, or other libraries. Many\nof these types and classes are primarily intended for internal use, and should not affect most\napplications using pothos, but the changes are documented here to help upgrades for those of you\nbuilding your own plugins, or using these types in your applications."},"1090":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-71","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#plugin-api-and-type-changes","content":"The 4.0 release is intended to allow pothos to become more modular and extensible. This requires\nRefs and many associated type helpers to propagate the SchemaTypes from the builder that originated\nthem, meaning most of the changes listed below are adding `Types extends SchemaTypes` as the first\ngeneric argument to the type."},"1091":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-72","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#classes","content":"`InputFieldBuilder`"},"1092":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-73","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#classes","content":"Removed the `typename` argument from the constructor"},"1093":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-74","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#classes","content":"Updated field methods to return a new `GenericInputRef`"},"1094":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-75","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#classes","content":"`InterfaceFieldBuilder`"},"1095":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-76","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#classes","content":"Removed the `typename` argument from the constructor"},"1096":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-77","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#classes","content":"`ObjectFieldBuilder`"},"1097":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-78","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#classes","content":"Removed the `typename` argument from the constructor"},"1098":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-79","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#classes","content":"`BaseTypeRef`"},"1099":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-80","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#classes","content":"Added `SchemaTypes` as a new Generic parameter"},"1100":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-81","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#classes","content":"`EnumTypeRef`"},"1101":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-82","page_id":"/docs/migrat