id":"/docs/migrations/v4-105","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#classes","content":"removed the typename and kind from constructor args"},"1125":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-106","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#classes","content":"add the builder and Field options as arguments for the constructor"},"1126":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-107","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#classes","content":"split argument refs into a new `ArgumentRef` class"},"1127":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-108","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#exported-types","content":"`*FieldThunk`"},"1128":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-109","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#exported-types","content":"Updated to return a `GenericFieldRef<unknown>`"},"1129":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-110","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#exported-types","content":"`FieldMap`"},"1130":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-111","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#exported-types","content":"Updated to `Record<string, GenericFieldRef<unknown>>;`"},"1131":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-112","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#exported-types","content":"`InputFieldMap`"},"1132":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-113","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#exported-types","content":"Updated to `Record<string, GenericInputFieldRef<unknown>>;`"},"1133":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-114","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#exported-types","content":"`InputFieldsFromShape`"},"1134":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-115","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#exported-types","content":"Added `SchemaTypes` as a new Generic parameter"},"1135":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-116","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#exported-types","content":"`InputShapeFromField`"},"1136":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-117","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#exported-types","content":"Updated to accept a `GenericFieldRef`"},"1137":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-118","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#field-options","content":"The global interfaces for FieldOptions no-longer include the `resolve` option, which has moved to\nthe `InferredFieldOptions` interface to allow plugins to replace or change the resolve functions\ntypes globally."},"1138":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-119","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#field-options","content":"This means that when extending the `FieldOptionsByKind` interface, if you previously extended one of\nthe built in Field option interfaces, you will need to update your types to include the `resolve`\nfunction types as well:"},"1139":{"id":"/docs/migrations/v4-120","page_id":"/docs/migrations/v4","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migrations/v4#field-options","content":"The `InferredFieldOptionsByKind` interface can be used to get the `resolve` option by default, but\nwill also work for plugins that replace the `resolve` function with a different options for\nconfiguring how a field is resolved. Some custom object types may want to explicitly define a\n`resolve` option type, or omit it entirely (eg, the SimpleObject plugin does not use resolvers)."},"1140":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","type":"page","content":"Add GraphQL plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql"},"1141":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","content":"Import existing GraphQL types and executable schemas into Pothos."},"1142":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#install","content":"Install"},"1143":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-schema","content":"Import a schema"},"1144":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-selected-types","content":"Import selected types"},"1145":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"Import a type as a ref"},"1146":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#interface-union-enum-and-input-refs","content":"Interface, union, enum, and input refs"},"1147":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#referencing-imported-interfaces-and-scalars-by-name","content":"Referencing imported interfaces and scalars by name"},"1148":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#scalars","content":"Scalars"},"1149":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","content":"Import types from an existing executable GraphQL schema while migrating fields to Pothos. Use the\nbuilder's `add` option to import a schema or a group of types. Use `addGraphQLObject` and the other\nbuilder methods to obtain refs for individual types and customize their fields."},"1150":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-schema","content":"This example starts with a small executable schema defined with GraphQL.js. In an application,\n`existingSchema` can instead be an imported schema from another schema-building library."},"1151":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-schema","content":"Both `{ user { name } }` and `{ otherUser { name } }` work on the resulting schema. The imported\n`user` field keeps its resolver, and the new field returns the same backing shape."},"1152":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-schema","content":"Non-root types are imported only if Pothos has no registered type with that name. For roots of\n`add.schema`, a configured type with the same name keeps its fields and type metadata, while\nnon-overlapping imported fields are added. For example, a field in `queryType({ fields })` overrides\na same-named imported query field without discarding the imported root's other fields. Mutation\nand subscription roots follow the same rule. Inherited interface fields also take precedence, and\nlocal overrides can be added before or after building the schema."},"1153":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-schema","content":"Use `queryFields` to add fields without configuring a replacement root. Those additions must have\nunique names; a collision with an imported field remains an error. To customize an individual\nimport explicitly, use `addGraphQLObject` with a `fields` callback as shown below. Duplicate local\nfield declarations remain errors."},"1154":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-selected-types","content":"Use `add.types` instead of `add.schema` to import individual named types:"},"1155":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-selected-types","content":"This is an alternative to the schema-import builder above. Dependencies reached through fields,\narguments, interfaces, and union members are imported recursively."},"1156":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-selected-types","content":"Declare imported object, interface, and scalar backing shapes in the builder's `Objects`,\n`Interfaces`, or `Scalars` schema types to reference them by name. For other kinds, use a ref from\none of the methods below."},"1157":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"The individual methods return refs that work in ordinary Pothos field definitions. This alternative\nbuilder imports `ExistingUser`, renames it, and replaces its `name` field with `displayName`:"},"1158":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"The resulting field accepts `{ user { displayName } }`. A `null` entry removes an imported field;\na field ref adds or replaces one. Fields not mentioned in the callback retain their imported definitions."},"1159":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"Method"},"1160":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"Source type"},"1161":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"Returned ref"},"1162":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"`addGraphQLObject<Shape>`"},"1163":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"`GraphQLObjectType`"},"1164":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"Object"},"1165":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"`addGraphQLInterface<Shape>`"},"1166":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"`GraphQLInterfaceType`"},"1167":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"Interface"},"1168":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"`addGraphQLUnion<Shape>`"},"1169":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"`GraphQLUnionType`"},"1170":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"Union"},"1171":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"`addGraphQLEnum<Shape>`"},"1172":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"`GraphQLEnumType`"},"1173":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"Enum"},"1174":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"`addGraphQLInput<Shape>`"},"1175":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"`GraphQLInputObjectType`"},"1176":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"Input object"},"1177":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"Pass the backing shape as the generic parameter. Keep the source type in a typed variable, as\n`ExistingUser` is above; when looking up a type by name with `schema.getType`, check that it exists\nand is the expected GraphQL kind before passing it to a method."},"1178":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-37","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#import-a-type-as-a-ref","content":"Object, interface, and input methods support field overrides. Unions accept a `types` override,\nand enums accept `values`. Object and interface descriptions and type-resolution functions are\ncopied from the source, so configure those on the source type. The `name` option renames the import,\nand `extensions` are merged with the imported extensions."},"1179":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-38","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#interface-union-enum-and-input-refs","content":"This separate example imports an interface and union backed by member records, an enum whose\nvalues are strings, and an input with an optional name. The refs can be used in output fields and\narguments like types defined directly with Pothos:"},"1180":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-39","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#interface-union-enum-and-input-refs","content":"The source interface and union keep their `resolveType` functions. The enum exposes `ASC` and\n`DESC` to GraphQL clients while resolvers receive `'asc'` and `'desc'`:"},"1181":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-40","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#referencing-imported-interfaces-and-scalars-by-name","content":"To use names instead of refs, declare the backing shapes in `Interfaces` and `Scalars`. This\nalternative builder uses `ExistingNamed` above and a `DateTime` GraphQL scalar imported from your\napplication. Its parser must produce JavaScript `Date` values, and its serializer must accept them:"},"1182":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-41","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#referencing-imported-interfaces-and-scalars-by-name","content":"Include concrete implementations such as `ExistingMember` when importing an interface alone;\nits field definitions do not identify all the types that implement it."},"1183":{"id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql-42","page_id":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/add-graphql#scalars","content":"Use core Pothos's `builder.addScalarType` for an existing `GraphQLScalarType`; this plugin does not\nadd a scalar-specific method. Declare that scalar's input and output shapes in the builder's\n`Scalars` schema type, as described in Scalars."},"1184":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","type":"page","content":"Complexity plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/complexity"},"1185":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity","content":"Complexity plugin docs for Pothos"},"1186":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#usage","content":"Usage"},"1187":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#install","content":"Install"},"1188":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#setup","content":"Setup"},"1189":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#configure-defaults-and-limits","content":"Configure defaults and limits"},"1190":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#options","content":"Options"},"1191":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#how-complexity-is-calculated","content":"How complexity is calculated"},"1192":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#example","content":"Example"},"1193":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#defining-complexity-of-a-field","content":"Defining complexity of a field"},"1194":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#example-a-bounded-list","content":"Example: a bounded list"},"1195":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#utilities","content":"Utilities"},"1196":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#complexityfromqueryquery-options","content":"`complexityFromQuery(query, options)`"},"1197":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#createcomplexityruleoptions","content":"`createComplexityRule(options)`"},"1198":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity","content":"This plugin allows you to define complexity of fields and limit the maximum complexity, depth, and\nbreadth of queries."},"1199":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#configure-defaults-and-limits","content":"To limit query complexity you can specify a maximum complexity either in the builder setup, or when\nbuilding the schema:"},"1200":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#options","content":"fieldComplexity: (optional,\n`(args, ctx, field) => { field: number, multiplier: number } | number`): default complexity\ncalculation for fields. `defaultComplexity` and `defaultListMultiplier` will not be used if this\nis set."},"1201":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#options","content":"defaultComplexity: (optional `number`) defines the default complexity for every field in the\nschema"},"1202":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#options","content":"defaultListMultiplier: (optional `number`) defines a default complexity multiplier for a list\nfields sub selections"},"1203":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#options","content":"limit: Defines limits for queries. For request-specific limits, pass a function such as\n`limit: (ctx) => ({ complexity: 500, depth: 10, breadth: 50 })`."},"1204":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#options","content":"complexity: defines the maximum complexity allowed for queries"},"1205":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#options","content":"depth: defines the maximum depth of selections in a query"},"1206":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#options","content":"breadth: defines the maximum total selections in a query"},"1207":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#options","content":"complexityError: (optional `function`) defines the error to throw when the query complexity\nexceeds the limit. The function is passed the errorKind (depth, breadth, or complexity), the\nresult (with the depth, breadth, complexity, and max values), and a GraphQL `info` object. It should\nreturn (or throw) an error, or an error message as a string"},"1208":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#how-complexity-is-calculated","content":"Complexity is calculated before resolving any root level fields (query, mutation,\nsubscription), and is based purely on the shape of the query before execution begins."},"1209":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#how-complexity-is-calculated","content":"The complexity of a query is the sum of the complexity of each selected field. If a field has\nsub-selections, the complexity of its sub-selections are multiplied by a fields multiplier, and then\nadded to the fields own complexity. The default multiplier for fields is 1, and 10 for list fields.\nThis multiplier is meant to represent the n+1 complexity of list fields."},"1210":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#example","content":"The following query has a complexity of `131` (assuming we are using the default options), a depth\nof `3`, and a breadth of `5`:"},"1211":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#defining-complexity-of-a-field","content":"The following alternatives assume a `Post` object ref and a `loadPosts(limit)` function returning\nthat model's records. Set a fixed cost with a number:"},"1212":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#defining-complexity-of-a-field","content":"The complexity option can also set the multiplier for a field:"},"1213":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#defining-complexity-of-a-field","content":"A fields complexity can also be based on the fields arguments, or the context value:"},"1214":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#example-a-bounded-list","content":"This configuration caps query cost at `20`, depth at `3`, and breadth at `5`:"},"1215":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#example-a-bounded-list","content":"For a `Post` object with a `title` field, the list's base cost is `5` and its `limit` argument\nsets the multiplier. Here, `posts` is an array of records and `resolverCalls` is a counter used\nto observe whether the resolver is invoked:"},"1216":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#example-a-bounded-list","content":"The selection `{ posts(limit: 2) { title } }` costs `5 + 2 × 1 = 7`. A limit of `16` costs `21`,\nso the budget rejects that query before invoking the resolver. A limit of `15` costs exactly\n`20` and is accepted. The multiplier follows the requested limit even when fewer records exist;\nquery cost estimates the operation rather than measuring the records actually returned."},"1217":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#complexityfromqueryquery-options","content":"Returns the query complexity for a given GraphQL query."},"1218":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#createcomplexityruleoptions","content":"Use this validation rule with GraphQL's `specifiedRules` to enforce `maxComplexity`, `maxDepth`,\nor `maxBreadth` during validation. Supply the request's `context` and raw `variableValues`;\noperation variable defaults and input defaults are applied before complexity callbacks run."},"1219":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#createcomplexityruleoptions","content":"Pass `operationName` when the request selects a named operation. Only that operation is measured,\nand an unknown name or invalid variables for it produce validation errors. Without `operationName`,\nall operations whose variables can be coerced are measured independently. In a multi-operation\ndocument, variable errors for other possible operations are left to GraphQL execution to report for\nthe selected operation. A single operation's variable errors are reported during validation."},"1220":{"id":"/docs/plugins/complexity-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/complexity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/complexity#createcomplexityruleoptions","content":"These validation results depend on the request's variables, context, and operation name. Do not\nreuse them across requests based only on the query text. `complexityFromQuery` also applies variable\ndefaults and throws for invalid variables; it measures the first operation in the document."},"1221":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"page","content":"Dataloader plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader"},"1222":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","content":"Batch object and relation loads, preserve result order, and cache records per request."},"1223":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#usage","content":"Usage"},"1224":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#install","content":"Install"},"1225":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#setup","content":"Setup"},"1226":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-objects","content":"loadable objects"},"1227":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-fields","content":"loadable fields"},"1228":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadablelist-fields-for-one-to-many-relations","content":"loadableList fields for one-to-many relations"},"1229":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadablegroup-fields-for-one-to-many-relations","content":"loadableGroup fields for one-to-many relations"},"1230":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#accessing-args-on-loadable-fields","content":"Accessing args on loadable fields"},"1231":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#dataloader-options","content":"dataloader options"},"1232":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#manually-using-dataloader","content":"Manually using dataloader"},"1233":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#errors","content":"Errors"},"1234":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#optional-adding-loaders-to-context","content":"(Optional) Adding loaders to context"},"1235":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#using-with-the-relay-plugin","content":"Using with the Relay plugin"},"1236":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-connections","content":"Loadable connections"},"1237":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-refs-and-circular-references","content":"Loadable Refs and Circular references"},"1238":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#caching-resources-loaded-manually-in-a-resolver","content":"Caching resources loaded manually in a resolver"},"1239":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#sorting-results-from-your-load-function","content":"Sorting results from your `load` function"},"1240":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#shared-tokey-method","content":"Shared `toKey` method."},"1241":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#subscriptions","content":"Subscriptions"},"1242":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","content":"The dataloader plugin batches loads for fields and object types. Resolvers return record keys,\nand the plugin loads those records through a DataLoader shared within the request. This reduces\nrepeated database calls when a query selects the same relation for many objects."},"1243":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#install","content":"To use the dataloader plugin you will need to install both the `dataloader` package and the Pothos\ndataloader plugin:"},"1244":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-objects","content":"Later examples that redefine `User` replace this definition; choose the options needed by your\napplication. They are not additional declarations in the same schema."},"1245":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-objects","content":"To create an object type that can be loaded with a dataloader use the new `builder.loadableObject`\nmethod:"},"1246":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-objects","content":"Return one value from `load` for each requested ID, in the same order. The order is used to map results to their IDs, and if the results are returned in\na different order, your GraphQL requests will end up with the wrong data. Correctly sorting results\nreturned from a database or other data source can be tricky, so this plugin has a `sort`\noption (described below) to simplify the sorting process. For more details on how the load function\nworks, see the dataloader docs."},"1247":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-objects","content":"Within one request, repeated keys share the same loader entry. For example, requesting\n`[\"3\", \"1\", \"3\"]` can load keys `\"3\"` and `\"1\"` once while returning three results in the\nrequested order. Later loads of the same key reuse the cached result. Pass a fresh context object\nfor each request to keep those cached records separate."},"1248":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-objects","content":"When defining fields that return `User`s, you will now be able to return either a `string` (based on the\nids param of `load`), or a User object (type based on the return type of `loadUsersById`)."},"1249":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-objects","content":"Pothos will detect when a resolver returns `string`, `number`, or `bigint` (typescript will\nconstrain the allowed types to whatever is expected by the load function). If a resolver returns an\nobject instead, Pothos knows it can skip the dataloader for that object."},"1250":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-fields","content":"The relation examples below extend `ContextType` with the post-loading methods they call.\n`loadPosts` returns one `PostShape` per post ID; `postsByUserIds` and `loadPostsByUserIds` return\none `PostShape[]` per user ID, preserving the requested ID order. Each `posts` definition is an\nalternative for the same field. For the grouped variant, add\n`postsForUserIds: (ids: string[]) => Promise<PostShape[]>` to `ContextType`; it returns a flat\nlist with `authorId` on each post."},"1251":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-fields","content":"In some cases you may need more granular dataloaders. To handle these cases there is a new\n`t.loadable` method for defining fields with their own dataloaders."},"1252":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadablelist-fields-for-one-to-many-relations","content":"`loadable` fields can return lists, but do not work for loading a list of records from a single id."},"1253":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadablelist-fields-for-one-to-many-relations","content":"The `loadableList` method can be used to define loadable fields that represent this kind of\nrelationship."},"1254":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadablegroup-fields-for-one-to-many-relations","content":"In many cases, it's easier to load a flat list in a dataloader rather than loading a list of lists.\nthe `loadableGroup` method simplifies this."},"1255":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#accessing-args-on-loadable-fields","content":"By default the `load` method for fields does not have access to the fields arguments. This is\nbecause the dataloader will aggregate the calls across different selections and aliases that may not\nhave the same arguments. To access the arguments, you can pass `byPath: true` in the fields options.\nThis will cause the dataloader to only aggregate calls for the same \"path\" in the query, meaning all\ncalls share the same arguments. This will allow you to access a 3rd `args` argument on the `load`\nmethod."},"1256":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#dataloader-options","content":"You can provide additional options for your dataloaders using `loaderOptions`."},"1257":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#dataloader-options","content":"See dataloader docs for all available options."},"1258":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#manually-using-dataloader","content":"Dataloaders for \"loadable\" objects can be accessed via their ref by passing in the context object\nfor the current request. Pass a fresh context object for each request so cached records are not\nshared between users.\nReusing the context also reuses its loaders:"},"1259":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-37","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#errors","content":"Calling dataloader.loadMany will resolve to a value like `(Type | Error)[]`. Your `load` function\nmay also return results in that format if your loader can have partial failures. GraphQL does not\nhave special handling for Error objects. Instead Pothos will map these results to something like\n`(Type | Promise<Type>)[]` where Errors are replaced with promises that will be rejected. This\nallows the normal graphql resolver flow to correctly handle these errors."},"1260":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-38","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#errors","content":"If you are using the `loadMany` method from a dataloader manually, you can apply the same mapping\nusing the `rejectErrors` helper:"},"1261":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-39","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#optional-adding-loaders-to-context","content":"If you want to make dataloaders accessible via the context object directly, there is some additional\nsetup required. Below are a few options for different ways you can load data from the context\nobject. You can determine which of these options works best for you or add you own helpers."},"1262":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-40","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#optional-adding-loaders-to-context","content":"The following interface replaces `ContextType` from the setup; include its original fields as\nwell as the loader helpers:"},"1263":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-41","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#optional-adding-loaders-to-context","content":"Next you'll need to update your context factory function. The exact format of this depends on what\ngraphql server implementation you are using."},"1264":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-42","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#optional-adding-loaders-to-context","content":"Call `createContext` for each request with the authenticated user and that request's data access\nfunction. You can then use these helpers from resolvers:"},"1265":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-43","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#using-with-the-relay-plugin","content":"Register both `DataloaderPlugin` and `RelayPlugin` and set `relay: {}` on the builder to use\n`loadableNode`. You can use this method to create `node` objects that work like other loadable objects."},"1266":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-44","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-connections","content":"This example adds `loadFriendsByUserIds: (ids: string[]) => Promise<Record<string, UserShape[]>>`\nto `ContextType`. Return an entry for every requested ID, with an empty array for users without\nfriends. The helper indexes that result by user ID before paginating each list."},"1267":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-45","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-connections","content":"To data-load a connection, you can use a combination of helpers:"},"1268":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-46","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-connections","content":"`builder.connectionObject` To create the connection and edge types"},"1269":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-47","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-connections","content":"`t.loadable` with the `byPath` option to create a loadable field with access to arguments"},"1270":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-48","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-connections","content":"`t.arg.connectionArgs` to add the standard connection arguments to the field"},"1271":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-49","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-refs-and-circular-references","content":"You may run into type errors if you define 2 loadable objects that circularly reference each other\nin their definitions."},"1272":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-50","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-refs-and-circular-references","content":"There are a some general strategies to avoid this outlined in the\ncircular-references guide."},"1273":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-51","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-refs-and-circular-references","content":"This plugin also has methods for creating refs (similar to `builder.objectRef`) that can be used to\nsplit the definition and implementation of your types to avoid any issues with circular references."},"1274":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-52","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-refs-and-circular-references","content":"All the plugin specific options should be passed when defining the ref. This allows the ref to be\nused by any method that accepts a ref to implement an object:"},"1275":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-53","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#loadable-refs-and-circular-references","content":"The above example is not useful on its own, but this pattern will allow these refs to be used with\nothers that also allow you to define object types with additional behaviors."},"1276":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-54","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#caching-resources-loaded-manually-in-a-resolver","content":"When manually loading a resource in a resolver it is not automatically added to the dataloader\ncache. If you want any resolved value to be stored in the cache in case it is used somewhere else in\nthe query you can use the `cacheResolved` option."},"1277":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-55","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#caching-resources-loaded-manually-in-a-resolver","content":"The `cacheResolved` option takes a function that converts the loaded object into its cache key:"},"1278":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-56","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#caching-resources-loaded-manually-in-a-resolver","content":"Whenever a resolver returns a User or list of Users, those objects will automatically be added to the\ndataloaders cache, so they can be re-used in other parts of the query."},"1279":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-57","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#sorting-results-from-your-load-function","content":"As mentioned above, the `load` function must return results in the same order as the provided array\nof IDs. Doing this correctly can be a little complicated, so this plugin includes an alternative.\nFor any type or field that creates a dataloader, you can also provide a `sort` option which will\ncorrectly map your results into the correct order based on their ids. To do this, you will need to\nprovide a function that accepts a result object, and returns its id."},"1280":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-58","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#sorting-results-from-your-load-function","content":"The `loadUsersById` function can return records in storage order. `sort` uses each user's ID to\nput them in the requested order:"},"1281":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-59","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#sorting-results-from-your-load-function","content":"This also works with loadable nodes, interfaces, unions, and fields. Missing keys become `null`\nafter sorting. Choose field and list-item nullability to match that behavior; a missing record in\na non-null position produces a GraphQL error. Without `sort`, return one result for every key in\nthe same order, using `null` or an `Error` for missing records as appropriate for your loader."},"1282":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-60","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#sorting-results-from-your-load-function","content":"When sorting, if the list of results contains an Error the error is thrown because it can not be\nmapped to the correct location. This `sort` option should NOT be used for cases where the result\nlist is expected to contain errors."},"1283":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-61","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#shared-tokey-method","content":"Defining multiple functions to extract the key from a loaded object can become redundant. In cases\nwhen you are using both `cacheResolved` and `sort` you can use a `toKey` function instead:"},"1284":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-62","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#subscriptions","content":"Dataloaders are stored on the context object of the subscription.  This means that values are cached across the full lifetime of the subscription."},"1285":{"id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader-63","page_id":"/docs/plugins/dataloader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/dataloader#subscriptions","content":"To reset all data loaders for the current subscription, you can use the `clearAllDataLoaders` helper."},"1286":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","type":"page","content":"Directives plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/directives"},"1287":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives","content":"Attach directive metadata to types, fields, and schemas for other tools to consume."},"1288":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/directives#install","content":"Install"},"1289":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/directives#declare-and-attach-directives","content":"Declare and attach directives"},"1290":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/directives#directive-formats","content":"Directive formats"},"1291":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/directives#locations","content":"Locations"},"1292":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/directives#apply-directive-behavior","content":"Apply directive behavior"},"1293":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives","content":"Attach schema directives to Pothos types and fields. The plugin records directive metadata in\nGraphQL extensions and AST nodes. A schema transformer or another consumer implements the\nbehavior of directives such as rate limits or authorization checks."},"1294":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#declare-and-attach-directives","content":"The `Directives` schema type declares each directive's allowed locations and argument shape.\nThis example records a `rateLimit` directive on the `hello` field:"},"1295":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#declare-and-attach-directives","content":"The schema still returns `world` on every request until a consumer applies the rate limit.\nThe generic checks your Pothos definitions; it does not register a GraphQL directive definition."},"1296":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#directive-formats","content":"An array preserves order and allows a directive to occur more than once:"},"1297":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#directive-formats","content":"For a directive that occurs once, an object is also accepted. This is an alternative definition\nof the same field:"},"1298":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#directive-formats","content":"By default, extensions contain an array of `{ name, args }` entries. Set\n`directives: { useGraphQLToolsUnorderedDirectives: true }` on the builder when a consumer requires\nan object keyed by directive name. That output format does not preserve order across directive names."},"1299":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#locations","content":"Use `directives` in the options for the type, field, argument, or enum value being annotated.\nThe supported locations are:"},"1300":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#locations","content":"`ARGUMENT_DEFINITION`"},"1301":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#locations","content":"`ENUM_VALUE`"},"1302":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#locations","content":"`ENUM`"},"1303":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#locations","content":"`FIELD_DEFINITION`"},"1304":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#locations","content":"`INPUT_FIELD_DEFINITION`"},"1305":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#locations","content":"`INPUT_OBJECT`"},"1306":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#locations","content":"`INTERFACE`"},"1307":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#locations","content":"`OBJECT`"},"1308":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#locations","content":"`SCALAR`"},"1309":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#locations","content":"`SCHEMA`"},"1310":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#locations","content":"`UNION`"},"1311":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#locations","content":"For `SCHEMA` directives, use `schemaDirectives` on `builder.toSchema()`. Declare `SCHEMA` among\nthat directive's allowed locations in the `Directives` generic. The separate `directives` option\non `toSchema()` accepts GraphQL directive definitions; it does not attach directives to the schema."},"1312":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#apply-directive-behavior","content":"Pass the built schema to the transformer supplied by your directive library. For example, with\n`graphql-rate-limit-directive` installed:"},"1313":{"id":"/docs/plugins/directives-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/directives","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/directives#apply-directive-behavior","content":"Check the consumer's required extensions format and directive-definition setup when integrating\nanother library. Pothos stores the annotations; the consumer determines their runtime behavior."},"1314":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"page","content":"Errors plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors"},"1315":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors","content":"Errors plugin docs for Pothos"},"1316":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#usage","content":"Usage"},"1317":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#install","content":"Install"},"1318":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#example-usage","content":"Example Usage"},"1319":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#expected-and-unexpected-errors","content":"Expected and unexpected errors"},"1320":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#builder-options","content":"Builder options"},"1321":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#options-on-fields","content":"Options on Fields"},"1322":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#a-shared-error-interface","content":"A shared Error interface"},"1323":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#with-validation-plugin","content":"With validation plugin"},"1324":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#with-the-dataloader-plugin","content":"With the dataloader plugin"},"1325":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#with-the-prisma-plugin","content":"With the prisma plugin"},"1326":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#list-item-errors","content":"List item errors"},"1327":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#custom-error-union-fields","content":"Custom error union fields"},"1328":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#type-resolution","content":"Type resolution"},"1329":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#using-buildererrorunion","content":"Using `builder.errorUnion`"},"1330":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/errors#options","content":"Options"},"1331":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors","content":"Represent expected failures as GraphQL result unions. Register error classes as object types, then\nlist them in a field's `errors` option. The plugin catches matching errors and returns an error\nobject that clients can query with fragments. Errors that do not match still become GraphQL errors."},"1332":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#example-usage","content":"The above example will produce a GraphQL schema that looks like:"},"1333":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#example-usage","content":"This field can be queried using fragments like:"},"1334":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#example-usage","content":"This plugin works by wrapping fields that define error options in a union type. This union consists\nof an object type for each error type defined for the field, and a Success object type that wraps\nthe returned data. If the fields resolver throws an instance of one of the defined errors, the\nerrors plugin will automatically resolve to the corresponding error object type."},"1335":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#expected-and-unexpected-errors","content":"Only errors matching a field's registered classes become typed results. With the imports and\nbuilder setup above, this alternative query definition catches `NameTooShort` and exposes its\n`message` and `minimum` fields. Successful greetings use `QueryGreetingSuccess`; a plain `Error`\nremains a GraphQL error because this field does not register that class."},"1336":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#builder-options","content":"`defaultTypes`: An array of Error classes to include in every field with error handling."},"1337":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#builder-options","content":"`directResult`: Sets the default for `directResult` option on fields (only affects non-list\nfields)"},"1338":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#builder-options","content":"`onResolvedError`: A callback function that is called when an error is handled by the plugin"},"1339":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#builder-options","content":"`defaultResultOptions`: Defaults for generated success object types."},"1340":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#builder-options","content":"`defaultUnionOptions`: Defaults for generated result union types."},"1341":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#builder-options","content":"`defaultItemResultOptions`: Defaults for per-item success object types."},"1342":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#builder-options","content":"`defaultItemUnionOptions`: Defaults for per-item result union types."},"1343":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#builder-options","content":"`unsafelyHandleInputErrors`: Also catch input mapping and validation errors; see\nWith validation plugin."},"1344":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#builder-options","content":"The four type-options objects accept a `name` function receiving `{ parentTypeName, fieldName }`.\nUse distinct names for success objects and unions:"},"1345":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#options-on-fields","content":"`types`: An array of Error classes to catch and handle as error objects in the schema. Will be\nmerged with `defaultTypes` from builder."},"1346":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#options-on-fields","content":"`union`: An options object for the union type. Can include any normal union type options, and\n`name` option for setting a custom name for the union type."},"1347":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#options-on-fields","content":"`result`: An options object for result object type. Can include any normal object type options,\nand `name` option for setting a custom name for the result type."},"1348":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#options-on-fields","content":"`dataField`: An options object for the data field on the result object. This field will be named\n`data` by default, but can be renamed by passing a custom `name` option."},"1349":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#options-on-fields","content":"`directResult`: Boolean, can only be set to true for non-list fields. This will directly include\nthe fields type in the union rather than creating an intermediate Result object type. This will\nthrow at build time if the type is not an object type."},"1350":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#a-shared-error-interface","content":"Set up an Error interface"},"1351":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#a-shared-error-interface","content":"Create a BaseError object type"},"1352":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-37","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#a-shared-error-interface","content":"Include the Error interface in any custom Error types you define"},"1353":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-38","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#a-shared-error-interface","content":"Include the BaseError type in the `defaultTypes` in the builder config"},"1354":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-39","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#a-shared-error-interface","content":"This pattern will allow you to consistently query your schema using a `... on Error { message }`\nfragment since all Error classes extend that interface. If your client wants to query details of\nmore specialized error types, they can just add a fragment for the errors it cares about. This\npattern should also make it easier to make future changes without unexpected breaking changes for\nyour clients."},"1355":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-40","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#a-shared-error-interface","content":"This is a separate setup from the first example:"},"1356":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-41","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#with-validation-plugin","content":"The validation plugin runs before field resolution. Set `unsafelyHandleInputErrors: true` to\nreturn its failures as typed results. A validation failure then returns without running field\nauthorization hooks, so only enable this when those error details may be returned before authorization."},"1357":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-42","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#with-validation-plugin","content":"This standalone example registers the validation error and its issues. Valid input reaches the\nresolver; invalid input returns `InputValidationError`."},"1358":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-43","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#with-validation-plugin","content":"Example query:"},"1359":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-44","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#with-validation-plugin","content":"Validation also protects writes inside a mutation resolver. This alternative builder stores\naccepted names and returns structured issues for invalid input without changing the stored data.\nIt uses the same imports as the validation example above and requires\n`@pothos/plugin-validation` and `zod` alongside the errors plugin."},"1360":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-45","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#with-the-dataloader-plugin","content":"To use this in combination with the dataloader plugin, ensure that the errors plugin is listed\nBEFORE the dataloader plugin in your plugin list."},"1361":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-46","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#with-the-dataloader-plugin","content":"If a field with `errors` returns a `loadableObject`, or `loadableNode` the errors plugin will now\ncatch errors thrown when loading ids returned by the `resolve` function."},"1362":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-47","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#with-the-dataloader-plugin","content":"For a list of loadable IDs, loading happens per item. A load failure is not a whole-field failure,\nso the field's `errors` option does not catch it. Use explicit error unions when you need typed\nresults for individual loads; see List item errors for the separate\n`itemErrors` handling of errors returned or thrown while iterating the resolver's list."},"1363":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-48","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#with-the-prisma-plugin","content":"To use this in combination with the prisma plugin, ensure that the errors plugin is listed BEFORE\nthe prisma plugin in your plugin list. This will enable `errors` option to work correctly with any\nfield builder method from the prisma plugin."},"1364":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-49","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#with-the-prisma-plugin","content":"`errors` can be configured for any field, but if there is an error pre-loading a relation the error\nwill always be surfaced at the field that executed the query. Because there are cases that fall back to\nexecuting queries for relation fields, these fields may still have errors if the relation was not\npre-loaded. Detection of nested relations will continue to work if those relations use the `errors`\nplugin"},"1365":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-50","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#list-item-errors","content":"Use `itemErrors` on a list field to wrap each item in its own result union. It accepts the same\noptions as `errors`. The following addition to the shared Error interface example yields one success, then throws an error. The plugin returns that error as the final item:"},"1366":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-51","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#list-item-errors","content":"At runtime, `itemErrors` also handles returned error instances. If the resolver's ordinary return\ntype does not include errors, use an explicit error union for a list that mixes returned data and errors."},"1367":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-52","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#list-item-errors","content":"The plugin also wraps sync and async iterators. A yielded error becomes an error item; a thrown\nerror becomes the final item and closes the iterator. Async iterable execution requires an executor\nthat supports it, such as GraphQL 17."},"1368":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-53","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#list-item-errors","content":"Combine `errors: {}` with `itemErrors: {}` to handle a whole-field failure as well. The outer\n`QueryListWithErrorsResult` union contains `BaseError` and `QueryListWithErrorsSuccess`; the latter's\n`data` field contains the list of `QueryListWithErrorsItemResult` items."},"1369":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-54","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#list-item-errors","content":"For an async source, the same field can use an async generator. Replace its resolver with:"},"1370":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-55","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#list-item-errors","content":"This requires an executor that supports async iterables. A thrown error ends the iterator;\nuse an explicit error union when the source needs to return an error item and continue."},"1371":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-56","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#list-item-errors","content":"To handle both a failure before the list is returned and a failure during iteration, replace\n`listWithErrors` with:"},"1372":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-57","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#list-item-errors","content":"The generated result types are:"},"1373":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-58","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#custom-error-union-fields","content":"Use `t.errorUnionField` and `t.errorUnionListField` to directly specify all members of the returned union type,\nincluding multiple success types and error types."},"1374":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-59","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#custom-error-union-fields","content":"The following additions use the `Error` class registered as `BaseError` in the shared-interface\nexample. Each success shape has an `isTypeOf` check so Pothos can distinguish plain objects.\nThe resolver bodies simulate create and update outcomes to demonstrate the union branches;\nreplace them with your application's persistence logic."},"1375":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-60","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#type-resolution","content":"Union members use standard Pothos type resolution. Registering an error class with\n`builder.objectType` provides an `instanceof` check. Plain object types can supply `isTypeOf`, as\nabove, or you can pass a custom `resolveType` in the field's `union` options. Error instances matched\nby the plugin are resolved before that custom callback."},"1376":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-61","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#type-resolution","content":"For example, this alternative field resolves its success branches explicitly using the\n`CreateResult` and `UpdateResult` types above:"},"1377":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-62","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#type-resolution","content":"Matched error instances use the plugin's error map, so this callback only needs to distinguish\nthe success values."},"1378":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-63","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#using-buildererrorunion","content":"You can use `builder.errorUnion` to manually construct an error union type that can be used with any field.  Fields returning an error union will automatically handle returned or thrown errors."},"1379":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-64","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#using-buildererrorunion","content":"This addition to the shared-interface example distinguishes a missing user from invalid input.\nBoth error types implement the shared `Error` interface, and validation failures expose the field\nthat needs correction:"},"1380":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-65","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#using-buildererrorunion","content":"Clients can select the common message or the details of a particular failure:"},"1381":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-66","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#options","content":"`types`: Array of member types (object refs, error classes, etc.)"},"1382":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-67","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#options","content":"`omitDefaultTypes`: Set to `true` to exclude `defaultTypes` from the builder options (default: `false`)"},"1383":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-68","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#options","content":"`resolveType`: Optional custom resolve function. Called after the internal error map check."},"1384":{"id":"/docs/plugins/errors-69","page_id":"/docs/plugins/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/errors#options","content":"All other standard union type options are supported"},"1385":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","type":"page","content":"Federation plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/federation"},"1386":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation","content":"Federation plugin docs for Pothos"},"1387":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/federation#usage","content":"Usage"},"1388":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/federation#install","content":"Install"},"1389":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/federation#setup","content":"Setup"},"1390":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/federation#run-the-local-examples","content":"Run the local examples"},"1391":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/federation#defining-entities","content":"Defining entities"},"1392":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/federation#extending-external-entities","content":"Extending external entities"},"1393":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/federation#selections-with-inline-fragments","content":"Selections with inline fragments"},"1394":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/federation#adding-a-provides-directive","content":"Adding a provides directive"},"1395":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/federation#building-your-schema-and-starting-a-server","content":"Building your schema and starting a server"},"1396":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/federation#printing-the-schema","content":"Printing the schema"},"1397":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/federation#field-directives","content":"Field directives"},"1398":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/federation#interface-entities-and-interfaceobject","content":"interface entities and @interfaceObject"},"1399":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/federation#composedirective","content":"composeDirective"},"1400":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation","content":"Build Apollo Federation 2 subgraphs with Pothos.\nRegister entity keys, resolve references from other services, and extend external types.\nThe examples below describe separate users, inventory, and reviews services."},"1401":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#usage","content":"This page will describe the basics of the Pothos API for federation, but will not cover detailed\ninformation on how federation works, or what all the terms on this page mean. For more general\ninformation on federation, see the\nofficial docs"},"1402":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#install","content":"You will need to install the plugin, as well as the directives plugin (`@pothos/plugin-directives`)\nand `@apollo/subgraph`"},"1403":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#install","content":"You will likely want to install @apollo/server as well, but it is not required if you want to use a\ndifferent server"},"1404":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#run-the-local-examples","content":"The complete users, inventory, and reviews examples live in\nwebsite/local-examples/federation.\nFrom a repository checkout with Node.js 22 or newer, install dependencies and run the local suites:"},"1405":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#run-the-local-examples","content":"This builds the required packages, installs the isolated local-example dependencies, and runs\nthe federation, Grafast, and smart-subscription suites. The federation check executes each `.graphql` operation with its variable sidecar and asserts the checked-in\nexpected response. Edit a representation in `users/02-entities.variables.json`, or the price and\nweight in `inventory/query.variables.json`, then update the corresponding expectation to test the\nchanged result. These checks use in-process GraphQL execution. Serving each schema and composing\na gateway are separate steps below. Apollo's schema tooling needs Node.js, so these examples\nrun locally instead of in the browser playground."},"1406":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#defining-entities","content":"Defining entities for your schema is a 2 step process. First you will need to define an object type\nas you would normally, then you can convert that object type to an entity by providing a `key` (or\n`keys`), and a method to load that entity."},"1407":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#defining-entities","content":"Run the local users example's `Entities` operation. Its representations load Leia by ID and return `null`\nfor a missing user. Change the first representation's ID to `missing` to see reference resolution\nchange the response. This executes one subgraph's `_entities` field locally; composition,\ngateway planning, and cross-service `requires`/`provides` behavior still need the local services\ndescribed below."},"1408":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#defining-entities","content":"`keys` are defined using `builder.selection`. This method *MUST* be called with a generic argument\nthat defines the types for any fields that are part of the key. `key` may also be an array.\n`resolveReference` will be called with the type used by the `key` selection."},"1409":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#defining-entities","content":"Entities are Object types that may be extended with or returned by fields in other services.\n`builder.asEntity` describes how the Entity will be loaded when used by another service. The `key`\nselect (or selection) should use the types of scalars your server will produce for inputs. For\nexample, Apollo server will convert all ID fields to `string`s, even if resolvers in other services\nreturn IDs as numbers."},"1410":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#extending-external-entities","content":"External entities can be extended by calling `builder.externalRef`, and then calling implement on\nthe returned ref."},"1411":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#extending-external-entities","content":"`builder.externalRef` takes the name of the entity, a selection (using `builder.selection`, just\nlike a `key` on an entity object), and a resolve method that loads an object given a `key`. The\nreturn type of the resolver is used as the backing type for the ref, and will be the type of the\n`parent` arg when defining fields for this type. The `key` also describes what fields will be\nselected from another service to use as the `parent` object in resolvers for fields added when\nimplementing the `externalRef`."},"1412":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#extending-external-entities","content":"The inventory service uses a separate builder with the setup above:"},"1413":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#extending-external-entities","content":"The inventory example supplies `price` and `weight` explicitly in `_entities` representations.\nRun it to compare a shipping estimate of `2` with free shipping for an expensive item. A gateway\nwould obtain the required fields from another service; this isolated local example does not perform that fetch."},"1414":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#selections-with-inline-fragments","content":"The template-literal type that checks selection strings can not express selections that use inline\nfragments (e.g. selecting through a union or interface field). For these cases, a string can be cast\nto `FieldSet<Shape>` to bypass the selection string checks. The cast replaces the generic argument\nof `builder.selection` — the shape is inferred from the cast, and is still used for the resolver's\n`parent` type:"},"1415":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#selections-with-inline-fragments","content":"This independent example assumes an external `Post` reference and a `MediaUnion` whose members\nare named `Image` and `Video`, both exposing a `url` field:"},"1416":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#selections-with-inline-fragments","content":"`FieldSet` is accepted anywhere a selection string is expected, including `builder.selection` and\n`ref.provides`. The selection string is not validated against the shape, so make sure the selection\nmatches the fields described by the generic argument."},"1417":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#selections-with-inline-fragments","content":"For a reference-only entity that this service does not resolve, use this alternative key definition:"},"1418":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#adding-a-provides-directive","content":"To add a `@provides` directive, you will need to implement the Parent type of the field being\nprovided as an external ref, and then use the `.provides` method of the returned ref when defining\nthe field that will have the `@provides` directive. The provided field must be listed as an\n`externalField` in the external type."},"1419":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#adding-a-provides-directive","content":"The reviews service uses another separate builder. It returns a known username with a user\nreference, so the gateway does not need to fetch that field from the users service for this path:"},"1420":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#adding-a-provides-directive","content":"The reviews example returns the provided username with the reference. Run it to observe the\nresolved value; proving that `@provides` saves a cross-service fetch requires a composed gateway."},"1421":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#building-your-schema-and-starting-a-server","content":"Each service builds and serves its own schema:"},"1422":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#building-your-schema-and-starting-a-server","content":"For a functional example that combines multiple graphs built with Pothos into a single schema see\nhttps\\://github.com/hayes/pothos/tree/main/packages/plugin-federation/tests/example"},"1423":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-37","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#printing-the-schema","content":"If you are printing the schema as a string for any reason, and then using the printed schema for\nApollo Federation(submitting if using Managed Federation, or composing manually with `rover`), you\nmust use `printSubgraphSchema`(from `@apollo/subgraph`) or another compatible way of printing the\nschema(that includes directives) in order for it to work."},"1424":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-38","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#field-directives","content":"Several federation directives can be configured directly when defining a field includes\n`@shareable`, `@tag`, `@inaccessible`, and `@override`."},"1425":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-39","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#field-directives","content":"For more details on these directives, see the official Federation documentation."},"1426":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-40","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#interface-entities-and-interfaceobject","content":"Federation 2.3 introduces new features for federating interface definitions."},"1427":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-41","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#interface-entities-and-interfaceobject","content":"Pass an interface to `asEntity` to define its keys. This example assumes your service already\nimplements `Media` with concrete types and has a `loadMediaById` function returning those models:"},"1428":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-42","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#interface-entities-and-interfaceobject","content":"In a separate service, extend that interface by creating an `interfaceObject`:"},"1429":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-43","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#interface-entities-and-interfaceobject","content":"See federation documentation for more details on `interfaceObject`s"},"1430":{"id":"/docs/plugins/federation-44","page_id":"/docs/plugins/federation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/federation#composedirective","content":"You can apply the `composeDirective` directive when building the subgraph schema:"},"1431":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","type":"page","content":"Grafast plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/grafast"},"1432":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast","content":"A plugin for building schemas with Grafast plans instead of resolvers"},"1433":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#install","content":"Install"},"1434":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#setup","content":"Setup"},"1435":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#usage","content":"Usage"},"1436":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#adding-plans-to-fields","content":"Adding plans to fields"},"1437":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#using-resolvers","content":"Using resolvers"},"1438":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#abstract-types","content":"Abstract types"},"1439":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#interfaces","content":"Interfaces"},"1440":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#unions","content":"Unions"},"1441":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#planfortype","content":"`planForType`"},"1442":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#run-the-plans-locally","content":"Run the plans locally"},"1443":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast","content":"This package is experimental."},"1444":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast","content":"Plugins that add runtime behavior by wrapping resolvers do not apply that behavior to Grafast plans."},"1445":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#install","content":"Grafast 1.0 requires GraphQL 16. Use a GraphQL version supported by your Grafast release."},"1446":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#usage","content":"For documentation on how to write plans, see the Grafast documentation."},"1447":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#using-resolvers","content":"As an alternative to the previous query definition, you can write a resolver,\nbut you will not have access to the 4th `GraphqlResolveInfo` argument:"},"1448":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#using-resolvers","content":"Resolvers should not be used to load data, but can make it easier to define a field\nthat would otherwise use a simple `lambda` plan."},"1449":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#abstract-types","content":"Abstract types (Unions and Interfaces) may require defining a plan to resolve to the correct type.\nFor more details on how polymorphic types work in Grafast, see the Grafast documentation."},"1450":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#interfaces","content":"To implement an interface, you can implement it as you normally would in Pothos, and then call the\n`.withPlan` method on the interface ref to provide a plan for resolving the correct type."},"1451":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#interfaces","content":"You can now define a query to resolve this interface:"},"1452":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#unions","content":"Add another object type and combine it with `Cat` and `Dog` in a union:"},"1453":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#planfortype","content":"When planning polymorphic types, Grafast allows you to provide a `planForType` function that\nallows you to load the correct data for the current type."},"1454":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#planfortype","content":"This also enables changing the type of plan required for fields that return the abstract type:"},"1455":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#planfortype","content":"`planForType` is not entirely type-safe, and will allow plans that resolve to data for the wrong type."},"1456":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#planfortype","content":"This replaces the previous `Entity` definition. The lookup uses the same animals and one alien:"},"1457":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#run-the-plans-locally","content":"The browser playground executes GraphQL resolvers with `graphql()`. This example requires the\nGrafast executor to run its plans. The complete local example\ncombines the setup, addition query, animal interface, and `planForType` union shown above."},"1458":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#run-the-plans-locally","content":"With Node.js 22 or newer, run from a checkout of the Pothos repository:"},"1459":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#run-the-plans-locally","content":"The local examples use the checkout's built Pothos packages. Their separate locked installation\nuses GraphQL 16, as required by Grafast 1.0; it does not change the repository's GraphQL version."},"1460":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#run-the-plans-locally","content":"`grafast/check.ts` executes the schema with `grafast()` and checks addition, both animal types,\nthe alien union member, and a missing entity. It also changes the arguments and checks the new sum.\nTo try your own values, edit the query or `variableValues` in that file and update its expected result."},"1461":{"id":"/docs/plugins/grafast-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/grafast","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/grafast#run-the-plans-locally","content":"The execution call has this form:"},"1462":{"id":"/docs/plugins","page_id":"/docs/plugins","type":"page","content":"Plugins","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins"},"1463":{"id":"/docs/plugins-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins","content":"List of plugins for Pothos"},"1464":{"id":"/docs/plugins-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins","content":"Plugins extend the builder with fields, options, and methods for specific tasks. See\nUsing plugins for installation and builder configuration."},"1465":{"id":"/docs/plugins-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins","content":"<Card title=\"Add GraphQL\" href=\"/docs/plugins/add-graphql\" description=\"'Add existing GraphQL types to your schema'\" />"},"1466":{"id":"/docs/plugins-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins","content":"<Card title=\"Auth\" href=\"/docs/plugins/scope-auth\" description=\"'Authorize access to fields and types using request data'\" />"},"1467":{"id":"/docs/plugins-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins","content":"<Card title=\"Complexity\" href=\"/docs/plugins/complexity\" description=\"'Define field costs and limit query complexity'\" />"},"1468":{"id":"/docs/plugins-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins","content":"<Card title=\"Dataloader\" href=\"/docs/plugins/dataloader\" description=\"'Batch and cache data loading for types and fields'\" />"},"1469":{"id":"/docs/plugins-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins","content":"<Card title=\"Directives\" href=\"/docs/plugins/directives\" description=\"'Attach directive metadata to your schema'\" />"},"1470":{"id":"/docs/plugins-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins","content":"<Card title=\"Drizzle\" href=\"/docs/plugins/drizzle\" description=\"'Build queries from GraphQL selections using Drizzle’s relational query builder'\" />"},"1471":{"id":"/docs/plugins-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins","content":"<Card title=\"Errors\" href=\"/docs/plugins/errors\" description=\"'Expose expected resolver errors as typed GraphQL results'\" />"},"1472":{"id":"/docs/plugins-9","p