lugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins","content":"<Card title=\"Sub-Graph\" href=\"/docs/plugins/sub-graph\" description=\"'Build separate schemas from subsets of your types and fields'\" />"},"1481":{"id":"/docs/plugins-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins","content":"<Card\n  title=\"Tracing\"\n  href=\"/docs/plugins/tracing\"\n  description=\"\n    'Trace resolver execution with OpenTelemetry, New Relic, Sentry, or custom tracers'\n  \"\n/>"},"1482":{"id":"/docs/plugins-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins","content":"<Card title=\"Validation\" href=\"/docs/plugins/validation\" description=\"'Validate inputs with Standard Schema libraries such as Zod, Valibot, and ArkType'\" />"},"1483":{"id":"/docs/plugins-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins","content":"<Card title=\"With-Input\" href=\"/docs/plugins/with-input\" description=\"'Define fields with inline input objects'\" />"},"1484":{"id":"/docs/plugins-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins","content":"The Zod plugin documents the older Zod-specific API. For new validation fields,\nstart with the Validation plugin."},"1485":{"id":"/docs/plugins/mocks","page_id":"/docs/plugins/mocks","type":"page","content":"Mocks plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/mocks"},"1486":{"id":"/docs/plugins/mocks-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/mocks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/mocks","content":"Mocks plugin docs for Pothos"},"1487":{"id":"/docs/plugins/mocks-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/mocks","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/mocks#usage","content":"Usage"},"1488":{"id":"/docs/plugins/mocks-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/mocks","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/mocks#install","content":"Install"},"1489":{"id":"/docs/plugins/mocks-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/mocks","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/mocks#setup","content":"Setup"},"1490":{"id":"/docs/plugins/mocks-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/mocks","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/mocks#adding-mocks","content":"Adding mocks"},"1491":{"id":"/docs/plugins/mocks-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/mocks","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/mocks#adding-mocks-for-subscribe-functions","content":"Adding mocks for subscribe functions"},"1492":{"id":"/docs/plugins/mocks-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/mocks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/mocks","content":"Replace selected field resolvers when building a schema for tests or local development.\nMocks are configured per `toSchema()` call, so the same builder can produce mocked and unmocked schemas."},"1493":{"id":"/docs/plugins/mocks-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/mocks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/mocks#adding-mocks","content":"You can mock any field by adding a mock in the options passed to `builder.toSchema` under\n`mocks.{typeName}.{fieldName}`."},"1494":{"id":"/docs/plugins/mocks-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/mocks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/mocks#adding-mocks","content":"A mock resolver receives the usual `parent`, `args`, `context`, and `info` arguments. Configured\nmocks replace their field resolvers whenever those fields execute; unlisted fields keep their\noriginal resolvers. Without a `mocks` option, the original `greeting` resolver throws its error.\nA schema can be built multiple times with different mocks."},"1495":{"id":"/docs/plugins/mocks-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/mocks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/mocks#adding-mocks-for-subscribe-functions","content":"To add a mock for a subscriber you can nest the mocks for subscribe and resolve in an object.\nSubscription execution uses GraphQL's `subscribe()` in a server or test:"},"1496":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"page","content":"Relay plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay"},"1497":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay","content":"Add globally identifiable nodes, cursor pagination, and Relay mutation fields."},"1498":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#usage","content":"Usage"},"1499":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#install","content":"Install"},"1500":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#setup","content":"Setup"},"1501":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"Options"},"1502":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#customizing-type-names","content":"Customizing type names"},"1503":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-nodes","content":"Creating Nodes"},"1504":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#parsing-node-ids","content":"parsing node ids"},"1505":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#global-ids","content":"Global IDs"},"1506":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#limiting-global-id-args-to-specific-types","content":"Limiting global ID args to specific types"},"1507":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-connections","content":"Creating Connections"},"1508":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#relay-mutations","content":"Relay Mutations"},"1509":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#reusing-connection-objects","content":"Reusing connection objects"},"1510":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#reusing-edge-objects","content":"Reusing edge objects"},"1511":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#expose-nodes","content":"Expose nodes"},"1512":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#decoding-and-encoding-global-ids","content":"decoding and encoding global ids"},"1513":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#using-custom-encoding-for-global-ids","content":"Using custom encoding for global ids"},"1514":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#using-custom-resolve-for-node-and-or-nodes-field","content":"Using custom resolve for node and or nodes field"},"1515":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#extending-all-connections","content":"Extending all connections"},"1516":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#changing-nullability-of-edges-and-nodes","content":"Changing nullability of edges and nodes"},"1517":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#globally","content":"Globally"},"1518":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#per-connection","content":"Per connection"},"1519":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/relay#extending-the-node-interface","content":"Extending the `Node` interface"},"1520":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay","content":"The Relay plugin adds globally identifiable nodes, cursor-based connections, and mutation helpers.\nUse nodes when clients need to refetch an object by ID, and connections when clients need to page\nthrough a collection. A connection can return ordinary object types; its items do not have to\nimplement the `Node` interface."},"1521":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"The `relay` options object passed to builder can contain the following properties:"},"1522":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`idFieldName`: The name of the field that contains the global id for the node. Defaults to `id`."},"1523":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`idFieldOptions`: Options to pass to the id field."},"1524":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`clientMutationId`: `omit` (default) | `required` | `optional`. Determines if clientMutationId\nfields are created on `relayMutationFields`, and if they are required."},"1525":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`relayMutationFieldOptions`: Default options for the `relayMutationField` method."},"1526":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`cursorType`: `String` | `ID`. Determines type used for cursor fields. Defaults to `String`"},"1527":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`nodeQueryOptions`: Options for the `node` field on the query object, set to false to omit the\nfield"},"1528":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`nodesQueryOptions`: Options for the `nodes` field on the query object, set to false to omit the\nfield"},"1529":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`nodeTypeOptions`: Options for the `Node` interface type. Supports a `name` property to customize\nthe type name. Options can also be passed to\n`builder.nodeInterfaceRef`."},"1530":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`pageInfoTypeOptions`: Options for the `PageInfo` object type. Supports a `name` property to\ncustomize the type name."},"1531":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`clientMutationIdFieldOptions`: Options for the `clientMutationId` field on mutation payloads"},"1532":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`clientMutationIdInputOptions`: Options for the `clientMutationId` field on mutation inputs"},"1533":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`mutationInputArgOptions`: Options for the input argument on Relay mutation fields"},"1534":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-37","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`cursorFieldOptions`: Options for the `cursor` field on an edge object."},"1535":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-38","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`nodeFieldOptions`: Options for the `node` field on an edge object."},"1536":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-39","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`edgesFieldOptions`: Options for the `edges` field on a connection object."},"1537":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-40","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`pageInfoFieldOptions`: Options for the `pageInfo` field on a connection object."},"1538":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-41","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`hasNextPageFieldOptions`: Options for the `hasNextPage` field on the `PageInfo` object."},"1539":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-42","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`hasPreviousPageFieldOptions`: Options for the `hasPreviousPage` field on the `PageInfo` object."},"1540":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-43","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`startCursorFieldOptions`: Options for the `startCursor` field on the `PageInfo` object."},"1541":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-44","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`endCursorFieldOptions`: Options for the `endCursor` field on the `PageInfo` object."},"1542":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-45","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`beforeArgOptions`: Options for the `before` arg on a connection field."},"1543":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-46","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`afterArgOptions`: Options for the `after` arg on a connection field."},"1544":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-47","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`firstArgOptions`: Options for the `first` arg on a connection field."},"1545":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-48","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`lastArgOptions`: Options for the `last` arg on a connection field."},"1546":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-49","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`defaultConnectionTypeOptions`: Default options for the `Connection` Object types."},"1547":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-50","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`defaultEdgeTypeOptions`: Default options for the `Edge` Object types."},"1548":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-51","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`defaultPayloadTypeOptions`: Default options for the `Payload` Object types."},"1549":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-52","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`defaultMutationInputTypeOptions`: default options for the mutation `Input` types."},"1550":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-53","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`nodesOnConnection`: If true, the `nodes` field will be added to the `Connection` object types."},"1551":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-54","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`defaultConnectionFieldOptions`: Default options for connection fields defined with t.connection"},"1552":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-55","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#options","content":"`brandLoadedObjects`: Defaults to `true`. This will add a hidden symbol to objects returned from\nthe `load` methods of Nodes that allows the default `resolveType` implementation to identify the\ntype of the node. When this is enabled, you will not need to implement an `isTypeOf` check for\nmost common patterns."},"1553":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-56","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#customizing-type-names","content":"If you have existing `Node` or `PageInfo` types in your schema that conflict with the ones generated\nby the relay plugin, you can customize the names using `nodeTypeOptions` and `pageInfoTypeOptions`:"},"1554":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-57","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#customizing-type-names","content":"Both options support all standard type options including `name`, `description`, and `extensions`."},"1555":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-58","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-nodes","content":"Defining a node adds direct lookups through `node` and `nodes`. Filters or permission checks on\nother root fields do not protect these lookups."},"1556":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-59","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-nodes","content":"With Scope Auth, require scopes on the `node` and `nodes` fields, put scopes on the `Node`\ninterface or each node type, or disable the lookup fields. You can also enforce visibility where the\nnode loads, so inaccessible records look missing. See\nRelay node authorization for each option and its\nlimits."},"1557":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-60","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-nodes","content":"To create objects that extend the `Node` interface, use `builder.node`. The `id` resolver supplies\nthe local key, and `loadOne` uses that key to retrieve the record:"},"1558":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-61","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-nodes","content":"`builder.node` will create an object type that implements the `Node` interface. It will also create\nthe `Node` interface the first time it is used. The `resolve` function for `id` should return a\nnumber or string, which will be converted to a globalID. The relay plugin adds two new query fields\n`node` and `nodes` which can be used to directly fetch nodes using global IDs by calling the\nprovided `loadOne` or `loadMany` method. Each node will only be loaded once by id, and cached if the\nsame node is loaded multiple times in the same request. You can provide `loadWithoutCache` or\n`loadManyWithoutCache` instead if caching is not desired, or you are already using a caching\ndatasource like a dataloader."},"1559":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-62","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-nodes","content":"With the default encoding, Ada's local ID `\"1\"` becomes the global ID `\"VXNlcjox\"`.\nA `node(id: \"VXNlcjox\")` query returns Ada with that same global ID, wherever she appears in the\nschema. Global IDs identify records; they do not grant access. The loader must still enforce the\napplication's access rules when needed."},"1560":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-63","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-nodes","content":"For an application that already uses classes, pass the class to `builder.node` and provide\n`name: 'User'`. The default class check uses `instanceof` or the constructor on the prototype.\nUse this as an alternative to the ref above, with a loader that returns class instances."},"1561":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-64","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-nodes","content":"Choose one loading option: `loadOne`, `loadMany`, `loadWithoutCache`, or\n`loadManyWithoutCache`. Batch loaders must return one value per ID in the same order, including\n`null` for missing nodes. Use a fresh context object for every request to isolate the node cache."},"1562":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-65","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-nodes","content":"By default (unless `brandLoadedObjects` is set to `false`) any nodes loaded through one of the\n`load*` methods will be branded so that the default `resolveType` method can identify the GraphQL\ntype for the loaded object. This means `isTypeOf` is only required for `union` and `interface`\nfields that return node objects that are manually loaded, where the union or interface does not have\na custom `resolveType` method that knows how to resolve the node type."},"1563":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-66","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#parsing-node-ids","content":"By default all node ids are parsed as string. This behavior can be customized by providing a custom\nparse function for your node's ID field:"},"1564":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-67","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#global-ids","content":"To make it easier to create globally unique ids the relay plugin adds new methods for creating\nglobalID fields."},"1565":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-68","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#global-ids","content":"The returned IDs can either be a string (which is expected to already be a globalID), or an object\nwith an `id` and a `type`. The type can be either the name of a type as a string, or any object\nthat can be used in a type parameter."},"1566":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-69","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#global-ids","content":"There are also new methods for adding globalIDs in arguments or fields of input types:"},"1567":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-70","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#global-ids","content":"globalIDs used in arguments expect the client to send a globalID string, but will automatically be\nconverted to an object with 2 properties (`id` and `typename`) before they are passed to your\nresolver in the arguments object."},"1568":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-71","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#limiting-global-id-args-to-specific-types","content":"`globalID` input's can be configured to validate the type of the globalID. This is useful if you\nonly want to accept IDs for specific node types."},"1569":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-72","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#limiting-global-id-args-to-specific-types","content":"To accept several node types, pass an array of their refs to `for`. The plugin rejects IDs whose\ntype is outside that list before calling the resolver."},"1570":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-73","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-connections","content":"The `t.connection` field builder method can be used to define connections. This method will\nautomatically create the `Connection` and `Edge` objects used by the connection, and add `before`,\n`after`, `first`, and `last` arguments. The first time this method is used, it will also create the\n`PageInfo` type."},"1571":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-74","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-connections","content":"The following field continues the `User` example. `resolveArrayConnection` slices the array and\ncomputes cursors and page information:"},"1572":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-75","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-connections","content":"Query `users(first: 2)`, then pass its `pageInfo.endCursor` to `users(first: 2, after: ...)`.\nThe first page contains Ada and Grace; the next contains Katherine. A node's `id` identifies the\nobject across the schema, while an edge's `cursor` identifies a position in this connection.\nPass a returned node `id` to the root `node(id: ...)` field to refetch the same user."},"1573":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-76","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-connections","content":"Treat connection cursors as opaque values. Forward pagination uses `first` with `after`;\nbackward pagination uses `last` with `before`. The array helper rejects negative page sizes and\nreturns an empty edge list when the requested page falls beyond the data."},"1574":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-77","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-connections","content":"Connections can also contain ordinary objects. This independent schema uses four users with\nlocal IDs: Ada, Grace, Katherine, and Dorothy. Its `User` type does not implement `Node`:"},"1575":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-78","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-connections","content":"`resolveArrayConnection` paginates data already in memory. The offset and cursor helpers below\nlet a data source fetch only the needed records from a larger collection."},"1576":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-79","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-connections","content":"The remaining connection examples are independent patterns using application types and data\nsources. For a custom connection resolver, return the following shape:"},"1577":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-80","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-connections","content":"Manually implementing connections can be cumbersome, so there are a couple of helper methods that\ncan make resolving connections a little easier."},"1578":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-81","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-connections","content":"For a data source that accepts an offset and limit, use `resolveOffsetConnection`. Its callback\nrequests one extra record to determine `hasNextPage`; honor the supplied `limit` and return records\nin a stable order:"},"1579":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-82","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-connections","content":"`resolveOffsetConnection` has a few default limits to prevent unintentionally allowing too many\nrecords to be fetched at once. These limits can be configured using the following options:"},"1580":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-83","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-connections","content":"For APIs where you have the full array available you can use `resolveArrayConnection`, which works\njust like `resolveOffsetConnection` and accepts the same options."},"1581":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-84","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#creating-connections","content":"Cursor based pagination can be implemented using the `resolveCursorConnection` method. The following\nexample uses Prisma and assumes `createdAt` is unique. If timestamps can repeat, use a unique\ncursor and matching ordering/filtering (for example, a timestamp and ID pair) so records are not\nskipped between pages. The callback must honor `limit`, including the extra record requested by\nthe helper, and reverse the ordering when `inverted` is true."},"1582":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-85","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#relay-mutations","content":"You can use the `relayMutationField` method to define relay compliant mutation fields. This method\ngenerates a mutation field, input object, and payload object. By default it omits\n`clientMutationId`; set `relay.clientMutationId` to `required` or `optional` to include matching\ninput and payload fields."},"1583":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-86","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#relay-mutations","content":"Example usage:"},"1584":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-87","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#relay-mutations","content":"Which produces the following graphql types:"},"1585":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-88","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#relay-mutations","content":"The `relayMutationField` has 4 arguments:"},"1586":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-89","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#relay-mutations","content":"`name`: Name of the mutation field"},"1587":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-90","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#relay-mutations","content":"`inputOptions`: Options for the `input` object or a ref to an existing input object"},"1588":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-91","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#relay-mutations","content":"`fieldOptions`: Options for the mutation field"},"1589":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-92","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#relay-mutations","content":"`payloadOptions`: Options for the Payload object"},"1590":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-93","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#relay-mutations","content":"The `inputOptions` has a couple of non-standard options:"},"1591":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-94","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#relay-mutations","content":"`name` which can be used to set the name of the input object"},"1592":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-95","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#relay-mutations","content":"`argName` which can be used to overwrite the default arguments name (`input`)."},"1593":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-96","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#relay-mutations","content":"The `payloadOptions` object also accepts a `name` property for setting the name of the payload\nobject."},"1594":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-97","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#relay-mutations","content":"You can also access refs for the created input and payload objects so you can re-use them in other\nfields:"},"1595":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-98","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#reusing-connection-objects","content":"In some cases you may want to create a connection object type that is shared by multiple fields. To\ndo this, you will need to create the connection object separately and then create a fields using a\nref to your connection object:"},"1596":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-99","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#reusing-connection-objects","content":"`builder.connectionObject` creates the connect object type and the associated Edge type.\n`t.arg.connectionArgs()` will create the default connection args."},"1597":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-100","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#reusing-edge-objects","content":"This is an alternative to the connection definition above. Create an edge ref, then pass it to\nconnection definitions that share its shape:"},"1598":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-101","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#reusing-edge-objects","content":"`builder.connectionObject` creates the connect object type and the associated Edge type.\n`t.arg.connectionArgs()` will create the default connection args."},"1599":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-102","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#expose-nodes","content":"The `t.node` and `t.nodeList` methods can be used to add additional node fields. the expected return\nvalues of `id` and `ids` fields is the same as the resolve value of `t.globalID`, and can either be\na globalID or an object with an `id` and a `type`."},"1600":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-103","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#expose-nodes","content":"Loading nodes by `id` uses a request cache, so the same node will only be loaded once per request,\neven if it is used multiple times across the schema."},"1601":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-104","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#decoding-and-encoding-global-ids","content":"The relay plugin exports `decodeGlobalID` and `encodeGlobalID` as helper methods for interacting\nwith global IDs directly. If you accept a global ID as an argument you can use the `decodeGlobalID`\nfunction to decode it:"},"1602":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-105","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#using-custom-encoding-for-global-ids","content":"In some cases you may want to encode global ids differently than the build in ID encoding. To do\nthis, you can pass a custom encoding and decoding function into the relay options of the builder:"},"1603":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-106","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#using-custom-resolve-for-node-and-or-nodes-field","content":"If you need to customize how nodes are loaded for the `node` and or `nodes` fields you can provide\ncustom resolve functions in the builder options for these fields. This alternative builder uses\nthe `users` array above; register its `User` node after creating the builder. Manually loaded\nobjects include `__typename` so the `Node` interface can resolve their type:"},"1604":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-107","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#extending-all-connections","content":"There are 2 builder methods for adding fields to all connection objects: `builder.globalConnectionField`\nand `builder.globalConnectionFields`. These methods work like many of the other methods on the builder for\nadding fields to objects or interfaces."},"1605":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-108","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#extending-all-connections","content":"In the above example, we are just returning a static number for our `totalCount` field. To make this\nmore useful, we need to have our resolvers for each connection actually return an object that\ncontains a totalCount for us. To guarantee that resolvers correctly implement this behavior, we can\ndefine custom properties that must be returned from connection resolvers when we set up our builder:"},"1606":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-109","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#extending-all-connections","content":"Register the same `User` node and `users` array with this replacement builder. TypeScript will ensure that objects returned from each connection resolver include a totalCount\nproperty, which we can use in our connection fields:"},"1607":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-110","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#extending-all-connections","content":"Note that adding additional required properties will make it harder to use the provided connection\nhelpers since they will not automatically return your custom properties. You will need to manually\nadd in any custom props after getting the result from the helpers:"},"1608":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-111","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#changing-nullability-of-edges-and-nodes","content":"If you want to change the nullability of the `edges` field on a `Connection` or the `node` field on\nan `Edge` you can configure this in 2 ways:"},"1609":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-112","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#globally","content":"The types provided for `DefaultEdgesNullability` and `DefaultNodeNullability` must match the values\nprovided in the nullable option of `edgesFieldOptions` and `nodeFieldOptions` respectively. This\nwill set the default nullability for all connections created by your builder."},"1610":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-113","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#globally","content":"nullability for `edges` fields defaults to `{ list: options.defaultFieldNullability, items: true }`\nand the nullability of `node` fields is the same as `options.defaultFieldNullability` (which\ndefaults to `true`)."},"1611":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-114","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#extending-the-node-interface","content":"Use `relay.nodeTypeOptions` to configure the interface itself, including `authScopes` when using\nScope Auth. Options that reference node types can be passed to `builder.nodeInterfaceRef` instead.\nEach option can be set once, in either place; `extensions` are merged. See\nRelay node authorization for how interface scopes apply to implementing types and how\nthey differ from authorizing the root lookup fields."},"1612":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-115","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#extending-the-node-interface","content":"Use the `nodeInterfaceRef` method of your Builder to reference the interface."},"1613":{"id":"/docs/plugins/relay-116","page_id":"/docs/plugins/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/relay#extending-the-node-interface","content":"For example, to add a field on the interface:"},"1614":{"id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","page_id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","type":"page","content":"Simple objects plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects"},"1615":{"id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","content":"Simple objects plugin docs for Pothos"},"1616":{"id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects#usage","content":"Usage"},"1617":{"id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects#install","content":"Install"},"1618":{"id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects#example","content":"Example"},"1619":{"id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects#extending-simple-objects","content":"Extending simple objects"},"1620":{"id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects#limitations","content":"Limitations"},"1621":{"id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","content":"Infer an object's backing shape from its GraphQL fields. Use `simpleObject` or `simpleInterface`\nwhen the resolver returns data in the same shape as the schema. For an existing application model,\nuse a core `objectRef` or `interfaceRef` with that model's type."},"1622":{"id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects#example","content":"`User` inherits `id` from the simple `Node` interface and contains nested `ContactInfo` data.\nIts resolver returns those fields directly. The additional `fullName` field computes a value from\n`firstName` and `lastName` instead of requiring a `fullName` property on the returned object."},"1623":{"id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects#extending-simple-objects","content":"In some cases, you may want to add more complex fields with resolvers or args where the value isn't\njust passed down from the parent."},"1624":{"id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects#extending-simple-objects","content":"In these cases, you can either add the field in the 3rd arg (fields) as shown above, or you can add\nadditional fields with `builder.objectFields`. This adds a different computed field to `User`:"},"1625":{"id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/simple-objects#limitations","content":"When using simpleObjects in combination with other plugins like authorization, those plugins may use\n`unknown` as the parent type in some custom fields (eg. `parent` of a permission check function on\na field)."},"1626":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","type":"page","content":"Smart subscriptions plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions"},"1627":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","content":"Re-execute selected query fields when their data sources publish events."},"1628":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#install","content":"Install"},"1629":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#connect-an-event-source","content":"Connect an event source"},"1630":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#async-iterator-sources","content":"Async iterator sources"},"1631":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#define-a-smart-subscription","content":"Define a smart subscription"},"1632":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#refetch-an-object","content":"Refetch an object"},"1633":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#refetch-a-field","content":"Refetch a field"},"1634":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#limitations","content":"Limitations"},"1635":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#run-a-live-subscription-locally","content":"Run a live subscription locally"},"1636":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","content":"Turn a query field into a subscription that sends its initial result, then updates it when relevant\ndata changes. Fields and object types register event names as they resolve. When an event arrives,\nthe plugin re-executes the affected query and sends the new result."},"1637":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","content":"Use field or object refetch options to update a smaller part of the result when the rest can be reused."},"1638":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#connect-an-event-source","content":"Provide callbacks that subscribe and unsubscribe by event name. This example uses an in-process\nevent emitter. The listener map belongs to one subscription operation's context, so cleaning up one\noperation does not remove another operation's listeners."},"1639":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#connect-an-event-source","content":"Pass a fresh `{ listeners: new Map() }` context for each subscription operation. A distributed\napplication can connect the same callbacks to its shared event service instead. Call `callback`\nwith an error to report a source failure, or `callback(null, value)` for an event."},"1640":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#connect-an-event-source","content":"`debounceDelay: null` disables debouncing. Other values enable the plugin's default debounce\nwindow; the current implementation does not use the supplied numeric value as a custom delay. Both `subscribe` and `unsubscribe` callbacks may return promises."},"1641":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#async-iterator-sources","content":"If your event service already returns async iterators, use `subscribeOptionsFromIterator` as an\nalternative to the manual callbacks. It manages each iterator's lifetime:"},"1642":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#async-iterator-sources","content":"The examples below continue with the event-emitter builder."},"1643":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#define-a-smart-subscription","content":"The same `polls` field is available on `Query` and `Subscription`. Register collection events on the\nfield and per-poll events on the object type:"},"1644":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#define-a-smart-subscription","content":"The subscription first sends the current polls. After updating stored data, emit the corresponding\nevent to send a new result:"},"1645":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#define-a-smart-subscription","content":"Calling `vote('1')` updates `votes` in the next result. Adding or deleting a poll should emit\n`poll-added` or `poll-deleted` after updating the map. Each execution registers the events for its\ncurrent results, and subscriptions that are no longer needed are removed."},"1646":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#refetch-an-object","content":"Replace the `PollType` subscription callback with this version to load only the changed poll:"},"1647":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#refetch-an-object","content":"A type's registration also accepts `filter: (value) => boolean` to ignore events and\n`invalidateCache: (value) => void` to clear a cached value before refetching. Event values are typed\nas `unknown`; narrow them before reading properties."},"1648":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#refetch-a-field","content":"A field can register its own event and set `canRefetch: true`. Replace the `votes` field above with:"},"1649":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#refetch-a-field","content":"Remove the object-level registration when using this field-only alternative. The plugin can then\nre-run `votes` without reloading the whole polls list. Field registrations support `filter` and\n`invalidateCache`, just like type registrations; `canRefetch` reuses the field resolver instead of\ntaking a separate `refetch` callback."},"1650":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#limitations","content":"Smart subscriptions do not support list fields implemented with async generators for `@stream`."},"1651":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#run-a-live-subscription-locally","content":"The browser playground runs queries and mutations; it does not consume subscription iterators.\nThe complete local example\nuses GraphQL's `subscribe()` with the schema and event source above. No database or external event\nservice is required."},"1652":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#run-a-live-subscription-locally","content":"With Node.js 22 or newer, run from a checkout of the Pothos repository:"},"1653":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#run-a-live-subscription-locally","content":"The local examples use the checkout's built Pothos packages. Their separate locked installation\nuses GraphQL 16, as required by Grafast 1.0; it does not change the repository's GraphQL version."},"1654":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#run-a-live-subscription-locally","content":"`smart-subscriptions/check.ts` opens two subscriptions with separate contexts, checks their initial\nresults, calls `vote('1')`, and checks both updates. It closes the first iterator and verifies that\nthe second still receives events, then closes the second and verifies that no listeners remain.\nTo change the example, adjust the poll's starting votes in `schema.ts` and update the expected results."},"1655":{"id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/smart-subscriptions#run-a-live-subscription-locally","content":"When using this schema in a server, pass a new listener map for each operation and arrange for the\ntransport to close its iterator on disconnect. The local check makes that lifecycle explicit:"},"1656":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","type":"page","content":"SubGraph plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph"},"1657":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","content":"Build public and internal API variants from one set of types and fields."},"1658":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#install","content":"Install"},"1659":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#build-public-and-internal-schemas","content":"Build public and internal schemas"},"1660":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#combine-variants","content":"Combine variants"},"1661":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#membership-options","content":"Membership options"},"1662":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#inputs-and-missing-types","content":"Inputs and missing types"},"1663":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#include-unreachable-types","content":"Include unreachable types"},"1664":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","content":"Use one set of type definitions to build several API variants. Tag types and fields with named\nsub-graphs, then pass `subGraph` to `builder.toSchema()` to select a variant. This is separate from\nApollo Federation: a sub-graph here is a filtered view of your own schema."},"1665":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#build-public-and-internal-schemas","content":"Declare the sub-graph names in `SubGraphs`. In this example, types and fields belong to both variants\nby default, while `internalNotes` is available only in the internal API:"},"1666":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#build-public-and-internal-schemas","content":"The `subGraph` option selects which types and fields appear in the built schema:"},"1667":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#build-public-and-internal-schemas","content":"`{ product { name } }` works in either variant. `{ product { internalNotes } }` fails GraphQL\nvalidation against the Public variant. Calling `toSchema()` without a sub-graph retains the full schema."},"1668":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#combine-variants","content":"An array includes types and fields belonging to any listed sub-graph:"},"1669":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#combine-variants","content":"The `all` form includes only membership shared by every listed sub-graph:"},"1670":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#combine-variants","content":"For the example above, the combined schema includes `internalNotes`; the shared schema omits it."},"1671":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#membership-options","content":"Set `subGraphs` on a type or field to override its defaults. A type without this option uses\n`subGraphs.defaultForTypes` from the builder."},"1672":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#membership-options","content":"A field's membership is determined in this order:"},"1673":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#membership-options","content":"Its own `subGraphs` option."},"1674":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#membership-options","content":"Its parent type's `defaultSubGraphsForFields` option."},"1675":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#membership-options","content":"Its parent type's membership, when `subGraphs.fieldsInheritFromTypes` is `true`."},"1676":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#membership-options","content":"The builder's `subGraphs.defaultForFields` option."},"1677":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#membership-options","content":"An empty array."},"1678":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#membership-options","content":"`fieldsInheritFromTypes` defaults to `false`. An explicit empty array overrides the fallback, so\n`defaultSubGraphsForFields: []` on a type makes its fields opt in individually."},"1679":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#inputs-and-missing-types","content":"Nullable arguments and input fields can also have `subGraphs`. Required arguments and input fields\ncannot be removed from a retained field or input object: resolvers may depend on them being present.\nThe plugin rejects a schema that tries to remove one."},"1680":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#inputs-and-missing-types","content":"An output field is omitted when its return type is excluded. Other references can make a filtered\nschema invalid, such as a retained union containing an excluded member or a required argument using\nan excluded input type. Include those dependencies in the variant or exclude the referring field or\ntype as well. Build and validate each variant you intend to serve."},"1681":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#include-unreachable-types","content":"Types that remain unreachable after filtering are normally omitted. The\n`subGraphs.explicitlyIncludeType` callback retains matching types that already belong to the selected\nsub-graph, even when no field reaches them."},"1682":{"id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/sub-graph#include-unreachable-types","content":"For federation entities, `hasResolvableKey` can retain an otherwise unreachable external reference.\nUse this as a separate builder setup with both plugins:"},"1683":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"page","content":"Tracing plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing"},"1684":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing","content":"Select resolvers to trace and connect them to a logger or tracing provider."},"1685":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#install","content":"Install"},"1686":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#log-resolver-duration","content":"Log resolver duration"},"1687":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#choose-fields-to-trace","content":"Choose fields to trace"},"1688":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#custom-tracing-options","content":"Custom tracing options"},"1689":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#implementing-a-tracer","content":"Implementing a tracer"},"1690":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#tracing-integrations","content":"Tracing integrations"},"1691":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#opentelemetry","content":"OpenTelemetry"},"1692":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#setting-up-a-tracer","content":"Setting up a tracer"},"1693":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#adding-custom-attributes-to-spans","content":"Adding custom attributes to spans"},"1694":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#instrumenting-the-execution-phase","content":"Instrumenting the execution phase"},"1695":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#using-the-envelop-opentelemetry-plugin","content":"Using the Envelop OpenTelemetry plugin"},"1696":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#optional-http-instrumentation","content":"Optional HTTP instrumentation"},"1697":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#datadog","content":"Datadog"},"1698":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#new-relic","content":"New Relic"},"1699":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-attributes","content":"Operation attributes"},"1700":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#sentry","content":"Sentry"},"1701":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-spans","content":"Operation spans"},"1702":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#using-the-envelop-sentry-plugin","content":"Using the Envelop Sentry plugin"},"1703":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#aws-xray","content":"AWS XRay"},"1704":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-segments","content":"Operation segments"},"1705":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing","content":"Trace selected resolver calls with a wrapper you provide. Set a field's `tracing` option to enable,\ndisable, or configure its trace; use `tracing.default` for fields without an explicit setting.\nThe `tracing.wrap` callback receives the resolver, its tracing options, and the field configuration."},"1706":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing","content":"The plugin measures resolver execution. Instrument your GraphQL server separately to trace an\nentire operation, including parsing, validation, and execution."},"1707":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#log-resolver-duration","content":"This schema traces root fields by default and logs the time spent resolving `hello`:"},"1708":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#log-resolver-duration","content":"`wrapResolver` calls its completion callback for a synchronous result, fulfilled promise, thrown\nerror, or rejected promise. It preserves the resolver's result or error. The callback receives\n`null` for a successful call and the thrown value for a failure; duration is in milliseconds."},"1709":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#choose-fields-to-trace","content":"Set `tracing: true` on a field to enable tracing regardless of the default, or `tracing: false` to\ndisable it. These settings go alongside `type`, `args`, and `resolve` in the field options."},"1710":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#choose-fields-to-trace","content":"Use the exported predicates in `tracing.default` to select fields:"},"1711":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#choose-fields-to-trace","content":"Helper"},"1712":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#choose-fields-to-trace","content":"Matches"},"1713":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#choose-fields-to-trace","content":"`isRootField(config)`"},"1714":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#choose-fields-to-trace","content":"Fields whose parent is named `Query`, `Mutation`, or `Subscription`."},"1715":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#choose-fields-to-trace","content":"`isScalarField(config)`"},"1716":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#choose-fields-to-trace","content":"Scalars and lists of scalars."},"1717":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#choose-fields-to-trace","content":"`isEnumField(config)`"},"1718":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#choose-fields-to-trace","content":"Enums and lists of enums."},"1719":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#choose-fields-to-trace","content":"`isExposedField(config)`"},"1720":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#choose-fields-to-trace","content":"`t.expose*` fields, fields without a resolver, and fields using GraphQL's default resolver."},"1721":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-37","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#choose-fields-to-trace","content":"Tracing every property read can create many spans. Start with root fields and add fields that perform\nwork you need to inspect. If you use custom root type names, match those names in your default predicate."},"1722":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-38","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#custom-tracing-options","content":"Declare `Tracing` in the builder's schema types to accept your own field options. This is an\nalternative builder and query definition to the first example:"},"1723":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-39","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#custom-tracing-options","content":"A field can also compute its tracing options from resolver arguments. Replace the `tracing` option\nabove with this expression to use a different label for named greetings:"},"1724":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-40","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#custom-tracing-options","content":"With static options, `wrap` runs once per field when the schema is built. A function-valued\n`tracing` option is evaluated per resolver invocation, and `wrap` runs for that invocation unless\nthe result is `false` or `null`. The builder's `default` callback can return a function in the same way."},"1725":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-41","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#custom-tracing-options","content":"If you only need resolver arguments inside the wrapper, keep the tracing options static and read\nthose arguments from the returned function. This avoids constructing a wrapper for each call."},"1726":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-42","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#implementing-a-tracer","content":"`wrap` returns a resolver with the same arguments as the original. Use `runFunction` to run code\nbefore calling the resolver and observe its completion, including failures. For example, this\nalternative setup logs the start and end of each root resolver:"},"1727":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-43","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#implementing-a-tracer","content":"For custom span hierarchies, the plugin exports `pathToString(info)`, `getParentSpan(context, info)`,\nand `createSpanWithParent(context, info, createSpan)`. The latter caches a span on the request\ncontext and passes the closest cached parent span to your callback. Use a fresh context for each\noperation so this cache belongs to that operation."},"1728":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-44","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#tracing-integrations","content":"Provider packages implement `tracing.wrap` for common tracing systems. Initialize the provider SDK\nand its exporter in your application before executing requests. Each setup below is an alternative\nbuilder; add your application's types and fields to it."},"1729":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-45","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#setting-up-a-tracer","content":"For a local example, create `tracer.ts` with a console exporter:"},"1730":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-46","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#setting-up-a-tracer","content":"Initialize the provider once, before handling requests. Use your application's existing provider if\nit already configures OpenTelemetry. See OpenTelemetry's Node.js instrumentation guide\nfor SDK configuration beyond this example."},"1731":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-47","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#setting-up-a-tracer","content":"Save the following schema as `schema.ts`. Importing `tracer` connects Pothos to the configured provider:"},"1732":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-48","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#setting-up-a-tracer","content":"The wrapper records field name, response path, and return type, and connects nested resolver spans\nto their nearest traced ancestor. It makes the resolver span active while the resolver runs.\nOptions passed to `createOpenTelemetryWrapper(tracer, options)` are:"},"1733":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-49","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#setting-up-a-tracer","content":"`includeArgs`: attach serialized field arguments; defaults to `false`."},"1734":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-50","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#setting-up-a-tracer","content":"`includeSource`: attach the selected field's GraphQL source; defaults to `false`."},"1735":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-51","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#setting-up-a-tracer","content":"`ignoreError`: skip recording resolver exceptions; defaults to `false`."},"1736":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-52","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#setting-up-a-tracer","content":"`onSpan`: customize the span with `(span, fieldOptions, parent, args, context, info)`."},"1737":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-53","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#setting-up-a-tracer","content":"You can also pass these options as the third argument to `createSpan` for an individual field.\nBoolean options there override the defaults; both `onSpan` callbacks run when both are supplied."},"1738":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-54","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#adding-custom-attributes-to-spans","content":"Use the `Tracing` schema type and `onSpan` callback together. This replaces the OpenTelemetry\nbuilder above and accepts a static category on each field:"},"1739":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-55","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#instrumenting-the-execution-phase","content":"The Pothos wrapper creates resolver spans. To group them under an operation span, wrap the server's\nexecution call. With GraphQL Yoga, save this plugin as `tracing.ts`:"},"1740":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-56","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#instrumenting-the-execution-phase","content":"This hook covers queries and mutations that return a single result. It keeps the operation span\nactive while resolvers execute and ends it when execution finishes. Subscriptions and incremental\nresponses need hooks that follow the returned iterator; use an integration that supports those\nlifecycles, such as the Envelop alternative below."},"1741":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-57","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#instrumenting-the-execution-phase","content":"Register the plugin in `server.ts`:"},"1742":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-58","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#instrumenting-the-execution-phase","content":"Run the server and query `{ hello }`. The console exporter logs an execution span and its resolver\nspan. The New Relic, Sentry, and X-Ray hooks below can use this same server registration with their\ncorresponding schema and `tracing.ts`."},"1743":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-59","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#using-the-envelop-opentelemetry-plugin","content":"As an alternative to the custom hook, use `@envelop/opentelemetry` for operation instrumentation.\nDisable its resolver tracing when Pothos already creates those spans:"},"1744":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-60","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#optional-http-instrumentation","content":"To include HTTP request spans, install the HTTP instrumentation packages:"},"1745":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-61","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#optional-http-instrumentation","content":"Add this to `tracer.ts` after creating `provider`. It also works with the Datadog provider below:"},"1746":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-62","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#optional-http-instrumentation","content":"Load `tracer` before importing HTTP and server modules so instrumentation can attach in time.\nFor the JavaScript server entry point, preload it:"},"1747":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-63","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#datadog","content":"Keep the Pothos schema and execution instrumentation above. Replace `tracer.ts` with an OTLP\nexporter pointed at your Datadog Agent:"},"1748":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-64","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#datadog","content":"Enable the Agent's OTLP HTTP receiver. For an application running on the same host:"},"1749":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-65","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#datadog","content":"For containers or a remote Agent, configure a reachable receiver address and set\n`OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT` to its `/v1/traces` endpoint. See\nDatadog's OTLP Agent setup\nfor the matching deployment configuration."},"1750":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-66","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#new-relic","content":"Save this schema as `schema.ts`. The operation setup below loads the New Relic agent before the application:"},"1751":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-67","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#new-relic","content":"The wrapper creates a `graphql.resolve` segment. It accepts `includeArgs` and `includeSource`, both\ndefaulting to `false`, either at wrapper creation or as the third argument to `createSegment`."},"1752":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-68","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-attributes","content":"Start the server with the New Relic agent loaded before application modules so its HTTP\ninstrumentation creates the request transaction:"},"1753":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-69","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-attributes","content":"Configure the agent's application name and license key through your existing New Relic setup.\nSave the following Yoga plugin as `tracing.ts` to add GraphQL operation attributes to that transaction. The Pothos\nwrapper above adds the individual resolver segments; this hook does not start another transaction."},"1754":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-70","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-attributes","content":"Register `tracingPlugin` in the shared Yoga server setup. `getOperationAST` also finds the operation\nname when the document contains a single named operation but the request omits `operationName`."},"1755":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-71","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-attributes","content":"Alternatively, `@envelop/newrelic` can report operation metadata. Version 10.2 requires New Relic\nagent 7–11; use the direct hook above with agent 13."},"1756":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-72","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-attributes","content":"For a compatible installation, replace the manual hook with:"},"1757":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-73","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-attributes","content":"Install `@envelop/newrelic` alongside compatible `newrelic` and `@envelop/core` versions.\n`trackResolvers: false` leaves resolver tracing to Pothos instead of creating duplicate resolver\nsegments. Use this plugin or the manual operation hook, not both."},"1758":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-74","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#sentry","content":"Initialize Sentry and arrange for an active request or operation span before resolving fields.\nWithout an active parent span, the wrapper calls the resolver without creating a span."},"1759":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-75","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#sentry","content":"Save this schema as `schema.ts`:"},"1760":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-76","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#sentry","content":"Options are `includeArgs`, `includeSource`, `ignoreError`, and\n`onSpan(span, fieldOptions, parent, args, context, info)`. The boolean options default to `false`.\nPass options at wrapper creation or as the third argument to `createSpan`."},"1761":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-77","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-spans","content":"Save the initialization below as `instrumentation.ts`:"},"1762":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-78","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-spans","content":"For Sentry, replace the shared `server.ts` with this entry point so initialization runs before\nloading the schema and tracing plugin:"},"1763":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-79","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-spans","content":"Save the following Yoga plugin as `tracing.ts`. It creates an active `graphql.execute` span. Pothos resolver spans become\nchildren of it. This wrapper handles ordinary query and mutation results; streaming responses and\nsubscriptions need a lifecycle wrapper that keeps the span open while the iterator is consumed."},"1764":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-80","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-spans","content":"`Sentry.startSpan` keeps the operation span active during the callback and ends it when the returned\npromise settles. It marks thrown execution failures as errors. GraphQL usually returns resolver\nerrors in the result instead of throwing, so the callback also marks those results as failed.\nRegister this `tracingPlugin` in the shared Yoga setup along with the schema built using the Sentry\nresolver wrapper."},"1765":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-81","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#using-the-envelop-sentry-plugin","content":"Alternatively, keep the Sentry initialization and resolver wrapper, and replace `tracing.ts` with\n`@envelop/sentry`:"},"1766":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-82","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#using-the-envelop-sentry-plugin","content":"This plugin supplies an active operation span and reports execution errors. When using its error\nreporting, set `ignoreError: true` in `createSentryWrapper` to avoid reporting the same resolver error\ntwice. Use this alternative or the custom Sentry operation hook, not both."},"1767":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-83","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#aws-xray","content":"Configure an active X-Ray segment for the request. The wrapper creates resolver subsegments within\nthat segment, or calls the resolver without a subsegment when no parent segment is available."},"1768":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-84","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#aws-xray","content":"Save this schema as `schema.ts`:"},"1769":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-85","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#aws-xray","content":"Options are `includeArgs`, `includeSource`, and\n`onSegment(segment, fieldOptions, parent, args, context, info)`. Both boolean options default to\n`false`. Pass options at wrapper creation or as the third argument to `createSegment`."},"1770":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-86","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-segments","content":"Save this standalone Yoga plugin as `tracing.ts`. It creates a root segment for each GraphQL operation and an execution\nsubsegment within it. It makes the execution subsegment active so the Pothos wrapper can attach\nresolver subsegments. The SDK uses automatic context mode by default."},"1771":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-87","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-segments","content":"Register this `tracingPlugin` in the shared Yoga setup. The `finally` block closes both segments on\nsuccessful execution and on a thrown failure; returned GraphQL errors are recorded on the execution\nsubsegment. This recipe is for ordinary query and mutation results. For streaming responses or\nsubscriptions, close the segments when the returned iterator finishes or is cancelled instead."},"1772":{"id":"/docs/plugins/tracing-88","page_id":"/docs/plugins/tracing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/tracing#operation-segments","content":"When your HTTP middleware already owns a request segment, use that segment as `parent` and leave\nclosing it to the middleware. Do not create a second root segment or close a segment owned by another\nintegration. Configure the X-Ray daemon or collector using the SDK's application setup so closed\nsegments can be exported."},"1773":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"page","content":"Validation plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation"},"1774":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation","content":"Validation plugin docs for Pothos"},"1775":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#usage","content":"Usage"},"1776":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#install","content":"Install"},"1777":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#setup","content":"Setup"},"1778":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-api-overview","content":"Validation API Overview"},"1779":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-patterns","content":"Validation Patterns"},"1780":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#argument-validation","content":"Argument Validation"},"1781":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#data-transformation-with-argument-validation","content":"Data Transformation with Argument Validation"},"1782":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validating-all-field-arguments-together","content":"Validating all Field Arguments Together"},"1783":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#with-transforms","content":"With transforms"},"1784":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#input-type-validation","content":"Input Type Validation"},"1785":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#input-type-transformation","content":"Input Type Transformation"},"1786":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#input-field-validation","content":"Input Field Validation"},"1787":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#input-field-transformation","content":"Input Field Transformation"},"1788":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-before-mutation-side-effects","content":"Validation before mutation side effects"},"1789":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#plugin-options","content":"Plugin Options"},"1790":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validationerror","content":"'validationError'"},"1791":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#return-values","content":"Return Values"},"1792":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-execution-order","content":"Validation Execution Order"},"1793":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation","content":"Validate arguments and input objects before a field resolver runs. Attach a schema from any library\nthat implements Standard Schema, including Zod, Valibot, and ArkType.\nValidation can be asynchronous, and chained validators can transform the values passed to your resolver.\nIf validation fails, the resolver does not run."},"1794":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#install","content":"To use the validation plugin, you'll need to install the validation plugin and a compatible validation library:"},"1795":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-api-overview","content":"The validation plugin supports validating inputs and arguments in several different ways:"},"1796":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-api-overview","content":"**Argument validation**: `t.arg.string({ validate: schema })` or `t.arg.string().validate(schema)` - Validate individual arguments"},"1797":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-api-overview","content":"**Validate all field args**: `t.field({ args, validate: schema, ... })` or `t.field({ args: t.validate(args, schema), ... })` - Validate all arguments together"},"1798":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-api-overview","content":"**Input type validation**: `builder.inputType('Input', { validate: schema, ... })` or `builder.inputType('Input', { ... }).validate(schema)` - Validate entire input objects"},"1799":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-api-overview","content":"**Input field validation**: `t.string({ validate: schema })` or `t.string().validate(schema)` - Validate individual input type fields"},"1800":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-api-overview","content":"Each example below is independent. Reuse the setup imports and builder configuration, then use the\nexample's query and input definitions in place of any earlier ones."},"1801":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#argument-validation","content":"Validate each field argument independently using either the object syntax or chaining API:"},"1802":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#argument-validation","content":"Chained `.validate(schema)` calls receive omitted (`undefined`) and explicit `null` values.\nUse a nullish schema when those inputs are allowed; a transform can supply a default, and the\nresolver receives its inferred output. Validators passed in the `validate` option continue to\nskip nullish values. Input type validators run only when an input object is present."},"1803":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#data-transformation-with-argument-validation","content":"When using the chaining API, you can transform data as part of the validation process:"},"1804":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validating-all-field-arguments-together","content":"Pass `validate` on the output field to check its arguments together. Optional GraphQL arguments\ncan be omitted or explicitly `null`; use a schema that accepts both when those are valid inputs."},"1805":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#with-transforms","content":"Use `t.validate(args, schema)` to transform all arguments and infer the transformed resolver arguments:"},"1806":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#input-type-validation","content":"Validate entire input objects with complex validation logic using either object syntax or chaining:"},"1807":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#input-type-transformation","content":"Transform entire input types:"},"1808":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#input-field-validation","content":"Validate individual fields within input types:"},"1809":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#input-field-transformation","content":"Transform field values during validation:"},"1810":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-before-mutation-side-effects","content":"Validation finishes before a mutation resolver runs. A rejected input therefore cannot trigger\nwrites placed inside that resolver. The following example trims accepted names and stores them in\nan in-memory array; invalid names or email addresses produce typed validation issues."},"1811":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-37","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-before-mutation-side-effects","content":"This separate builder uses the errors plugin integration.\nInstall `@pothos/plugin-errors` alongside the validation plugin and Zod. Setting\n`unsafelyHandleInputErrors: true` allows validation details to be returned before field authorization\nhooks run, so only enable it when those details may be public."},"1812":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-38","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validationerror","content":"By default, failed validation throws `InputValidationError`, which contains Standard Schema issues\nwith their messages and paths. Set `validationError` to return your own error or message."},"1813":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-39","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#return-values","content":"Your error handler can return:"},"1814":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-40","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#return-values","content":"**Error object**: Return a custom Error instance"},"1815":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-41","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#return-values","content":"**String**: Return a string message (will be wrapped in a PothosValidationError)"},"1816":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-42","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#return-values","content":"**Throw**: Throw an error directly"},"1817":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-43","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#return-values","content":"To return validation failures as typed GraphQL results, see the\nerrors plugin integration. This requires\n`unsafelyHandleInputErrors`, because input validation runs before field authorization hooks."},"1818":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-44","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-execution-order","content":"Understanding when and how validations are executed:"},"1819":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-45","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-execution-order","content":"**Input Field Validation**: Individual input fields are validated first"},"1820":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-46","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-execution-order","content":"**Input Type Validation**: Whole input object validation runs after field validation passes"},"1821":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-47","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-execution-order","content":"**Argument Validation**: Individual field arguments are validated"},"1822":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-48","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-execution-order","content":"**Field-Level Validation**: The output field's `validate` option and `t.validate()` run last"},"1823":{"id":"/docs/plugins/validation-49","page_id":"/docs/plugins/validation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/validation#validation-execution-order","content":"When there are multiple validations for the same field or type, they are executed in order, so that any transforms are applied before passing to the next schema.\nValidations for separate fields or arguments are executed in parallel, and their results are merged into a single set of issues."},"1824":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","type":"page","content":"With-Input plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/with-input"},"1825":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/with-input","content":"With-Input plugin docs for Pothos"},"1826":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#usage","content":"Usage"},"1827":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#install","content":"Install"},"1828":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#setup","content":"Setup"},"1829":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#defining-fields-with-inputs","content":"Defining fields with inputs"},"1830":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#customizing-your-input-object","content":"Customizing your input object"},"1831":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#changing-the-nullability-of-the-input-arg","content":"Changing the nullability of the input arg"},"1832":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#input-arguments-and-resolver-values","content":"Input arguments and resolver values"},"1833":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#prisma-plugin-integration","content":"Prisma plugin integration"},"1834":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#customizing-the-default-naming-conventions","content":"Customizing the default naming conventions"},"1835":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/with-input","content":"Define a field and its input object together with `t.fieldWithInput`. The plugin creates the\ninput type and a required `input` argument. Use a core `builder.inputType` instead when several\nfields should share the same input type."},"1836":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#defining-fields-with-inputs","content":"This will produce a schema like:"},"1837":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#defining-fields-with-inputs","content":"The input name will default to `${ParentType.name}${Field.name}Input`."},"1838":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#customizing-your-input-object","content":"Replace the previous query definition with this version to customize both names:"},"1839":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#changing-the-nullability-of-the-input-arg","content":"You can configure the global default for input args when creating the builder by providing\n`WithInputArgRequired` in the builder's `SchemaTypes`, and setting `withInput.argOptions.required`."},"1840":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#changing-the-nullability-of-the-input-arg","content":"Alternatively, keep the original builder and set `argOptions.required` on an individual field."},"1841":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#input-arguments-and-resolver-values","content":"These field definitions belong in a query type's `fields` callback. The default argument is named\n`input`, while `argOptions.name` changes both the GraphQL argument name and the resolver's argument\nproperty. `typeOptions.name` changes the generated input object's name."},"1842":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#input-arguments-and-resolver-values","content":"An optional input may be omitted or explicitly `null`. The optional field uses a nullish fallback\nfor both cases; required inputs are rejected by GraphQL validation when omitted."},"1843":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#prisma-plugin-integration","content":"If you are using the prisma plugin you can use `t.prismaFieldWithInput` to add prisma fields with\ninput objects. This example assumes a builder configured for the Prisma and With-Input plugins,\na `User` Prisma object, and a Prisma client named `prisma`:"},"1844":{"id":"/docs/plugins/with-input-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/with-input","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/with-input#customizing-the-default-naming-conventions","content":"If you want to customize how the default input type names are generated you can provide a name\ncallback in `withInput.typeOptions`:"},"1845":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","type":"page","content":"Zod validation plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/zod"},"1846":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod","content":"Validate field arguments and input fields with a validate option that maps onto zod constraints."},"1847":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/zod#install","content":"Install"},"1848":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/zod#setup","content":"Setup"},"1849":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/zod#validating-a-single-argument","content":"Validating a single argument"},"1850":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/zod#validating-all-arguments-together","content":"Validating all arguments together"},"1851":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/zod#custom-messages","content":"Custom messages"},"1852":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/zod#lists","content":"Lists"},"1853":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/zod#input-objects","content":"Input objects"},"1854":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/zod#bring-your-own-zod-schema","content":"Bring your own zod schema"},"1855":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"Constraint reference"},"1856":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/zod#how-it-works","content":"How it works"},"1857":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/zod#sharing-schemas-with-client-code","content":"Sharing schemas with client code"},"1858":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod","content":"The validation plugin is now the recommended way to validate a\nschema. It supports zod alongside several other validation libraries. Use this page to maintain schemas that already use the zod plugin."},"1859":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod","content":"The zod plugin validates field arguments and input fields with zod. You attach a `validate` option wherever you accept input (a single argument, a whole field's args, an input object, or one of its fields) and the plugin builds a zod validator that runs before your resolver. It does not re-export zod; instead `validate` takes a small options object whose keys map onto the zod methods you already know (`min`, `max`, `email`, `regex`, and so on), or an actual zod schema when you want the full API."},"1860":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#setup","content":"Add the plugin, then optionally hand it a `validationError` callback to shape what clients see when validation fails."},"1861":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#setup","content":"`validationError` receives the `ZodError`, the field's `args`, the `context`, and the GraphQL `info`. Return a `string` (thrown as a `PothosValidationError`), return an `Error` instance (thrown as-is), or throw directly. Skip it to surface the raw zod error."},"1862":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#setup","content":"Examples below are separate additions to this setup; query and mutation examples are alternatives."},"1863":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#validating-a-single-argument","content":"Add `validate` to any argument. The keys you pass are constraints for that argument's type; here an email string capped at 254 characters."},"1864":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#validating-all-arguments-together","content":"Cross-field rules (\"at least one of these,\" \"start before end\") belong on the field's own `validate`, which receives the whole args object. It can be a function, or a `[function, options]` pair when you want a message."},"1865":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#custom-messages","content":"Every constraint accepts either a bare value or a `[value, { message }]` pair. The pair form is a `Constraint`; the options object is passed straight to the underlying zod method, so anything zod's method accepts (a `message`, and a `path` for object refinements) works."},"1866":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#lists","content":"List arguments validate the list and its items in one options object. Constraints like `minLength` / `maxLength` / `length` apply to the array; `items` carries the constraints for each element."},"1867":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#lists","content":"The following resolver replaces an in-memory roster only after both the list length and each\nemail address pass validation. A failed constraint leaves the previous roster unchanged."},"1868":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#lists","content":"`items` also accepts a refinement function or a `[function, { message }]` pair. For example,\ninside a field's arguments:"},"1869":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#input-objects","content":"`validate` works the same on an input type and on its fields. Put per-field rules on each field, and cross-field rules on the input type itself, where the callback receives the whole object."},"1870":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#bring-your-own-zod-schema","content":"Pass an existing zod schema with the `schema` key to reuse its rules on an argument:"},"1871":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#bring-your-own-zod-schema","content":"...or on the whole field, validating every argument at once:"},"1872":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#bring-your-own-zod-schema","content":"You can combine `schema` with the constraint keys. The plugin pipes your schema into the generated validator, so both run. Validation uses `parseAsync` before the resolver, so refinements may return a `Promise<boolean>`. The parsed value is passed to the resolver. This plugin does not infer transformed argument types; use the validation plugin when transforms change the resolver argument shape."},"1873":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"`validate` accepts a bare refinement function, an array of them, or an options object. The options object always allows these keys:"},"1874":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"Key"},"1875":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"Type"},"1876":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"Purpose"},"1877":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"`type`"},"1878":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"`'number' \\| 'bigint' \\| 'boolean' \\| 'date' \\| 'string' \\| 'object' \\| 'array'`"},"1879":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"Pin the base zod type. See How it works for why this matters."},"1880":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"`refine`"},"1881":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"function or `[function, { message?, path? }]`, or an array of either"},"1882":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"A predicate handed to zod's `refine`. Receives the validated value; returns `boolean` or `Promise<boolean>`."},"1883":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-37","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"`schema`"},"1884":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-38","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"`ZodType`"},"1885":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-39","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"A zod schema piped into the generated validator."},"1886":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-40","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"The remaining keys depend on the field's type. Each value is a `Constraint`, either a bare value or `[value, { message }]`:"},"1887":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-41","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"Type"},"1888":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-42","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"Additional keys"},"1889":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-43","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"Number"},"1890":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-44","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"`min`, `max`, `int`, `positive`, `nonnegative`, `negative`, `nonpositive`"},"1891":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-45","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"String"},"1892":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-46","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"`minLength`, `maxLength`, `length`, `email`, `url`, `uuid`, `regex`"},"1893":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-47","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"Array"},"1894":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-48","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"`minLength`, `maxLength`, `length`, `items` (nested validation options or a refinement for each element)"},"1895":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-49","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"BigInt"},"1896":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-50","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"*(base keys only)*"},"1897":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-51","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"Boolean"},"1898":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-52","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"*(base keys only)*"},"1899":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-53","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"Date"},"1900":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-54","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"*(base keys only)*"},"1901":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-55","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"Object"},"1902":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-56","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#constraint-reference","content":"*(base keys only)*"},"1903":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-57","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#how-it-works","content":"Each argument and input field builds its own zod validator from its `validate` options. At runtime the plugin has no access to the JavaScript type behind a GraphQL type, so when you pass plain constraints it builds a union of every base type that could satisfy them. A lone `maxLength`, for example, fits both strings and arrays:"},"1904":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-58","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#how-it-works","content":"An `email` constraint only fits strings, so the union collapses to one member. When the argument is not required, the whole validator is wrapped `.optional().nullable()` rather than folded into the union."},"1905":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-59","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#how-it-works","content":"Set `type` to skip the guessing and pin one base type:"},"1906":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-60","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#how-it-works","content":"Three cases sidestep the union entirely:"},"1907":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-61","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#how-it-works","content":"**Input object** args and fields always validate with `z.looseObject(...)`."},"1908":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-62","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#how-it-works","content":"**List** args and fields always validate with `.array()`."},"1909":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-63","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#how-it-works","content":"**Refinement-only** validators (a bare function, or an options object with just `refine` and/or `schema`) validate against `z.unknown()`, since no constraint narrows the type."},"1910":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-64","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#how-it-works","content":"A `schema` is piped into whatever the plugin generates (`yourSchema.pipe(generated)`), so your schema and the constraints both run."},"1911":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-65","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#sharing-schemas-with-client-code","content":"To reuse a validator on the client, write it as an ordinary zod schema in a shared module, then attach it with `schema`:"},"1912":{"id":"/docs/plugins/zod-66","page_id":"/docs/plugins/zod","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/zod#sharing-schemas-with-client-code","content":"If you would rather share the constraint options object, the plugin exports `createZodSchema` to turn one into a zod schema on demand. Type the options with the exported `ValidationOptions`:"},"1913":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","type":"page","content":"Async selections","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Drizzle"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections"},"1914":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","content":"Planning selections that depend on asynchronous request data"},"1915":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections#async-selections","content":"Async selections"},"1916":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections#await-nested-selections","content":"Await nested selections"},"1917":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections#connection-helpers","content":"Connection helpers"},"1918":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","content":"Pothos selections are synchronous by default. Set `AsyncSelections: true` when a selection or\nrelation query must await request data before returning its query options."},"1919":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections#async-selections","content":"Use the client and relations configured in Setup. The context method below supplies\na preview size loaded asynchronously, for example from account settings:"},"1920":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections#async-selections","content":"With the opt-in, `select` functions, relation `query` callbacks, `relatedCount` `where` callbacks,\nand the `select` and `query` callbacks of `drizzleConnectionHelpers` may be async. Without it they\nare typed as synchronous, and an async callback is a type error."},"1921":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections#async-selections","content":"Pothos waits for these callbacks before executing the planned query. `t.relation`, `t.relatedCount`,\n`t.drizzleField`, `t.drizzleConnection` and `t.relatedConnection` settle their plan before the\nresolver runs, and need no changes."},"1922":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections#await-nested-selections","content":"`await` what `nestedSelection` returns before putting it in the selection, and the same for the\n`nestedQuery` passed as the fourth argument of a `t.relatedField` `select`. A selection that\ncontains the promise itself will throw, and so will a `select` that returns while a nested\nselection it started is still pending. Include the nested selection in the returned query to\nload its data."},"1923":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections#connection-helpers","content":"Pass `awaitSelections: true` to `drizzleConnectionHelpers(...).getQuery`, and `await` the query it\nreturns. Without it, an async selection beneath the field throws, and whether there is one\ndepends on the incoming document rather than on the callback you wrote. A connection helper also\nthrows when its own `select` or `query` is async, whatever the document asked for:"},"1924":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections#connection-helpers","content":"`awaitSelections` is a per-call option, and is available whether or not the schema sets\n`AsyncSelections`."},"1925":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections#connection-helpers","content":"When `AsyncSelections: true`, also `await` a connection helper's `resolve()` before inspecting or\nspreading its result: an async `query` callback makes resolution asynchronous. Returning its\nresult directly from a GraphQL resolver remains supported."},"1926":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/async-selections#connection-helpers","content":"The connection-helper excerpt uses the Post attachments relation and `attachments` helper from\nConnection helpers. Request-context\nmethods must be supplied by the application; they are not created by declaring the Context type."},"1927":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","type":"page","content":"Connection helpers","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Drizzle"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers"},"1928":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","content":"Paginate attachment rows while returning Media nodes and custom edge fields"},"1929":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers#select-the-nodes-through-the-attachment","content":"Select the nodes through the attachment"},"1930":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers#add-the-connection-and-its-edge-fields","content":"Add the connection and its edge fields"},"1931":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers#querying-the-rows-in-a-resolver","content":"Querying the rows in a resolver"},"1932":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","content":"An image can appear in several posts, with a different caption in each. The Media row describes\nthe image; the PostMedia row describes its attachment to a post. A connection can expose the\nimage as its node and the attachment's caption as an edge field:"},"1933":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","content":"`t.relatedConnection` handles a relation whose rows are the connection's nodes. Use\n`drizzleConnectionHelpers` with `t.connection` when the rows being paginated differ from those\nnodes, or when you need to load the rows yourself. The helper supplies the selection, cursor,\nand result handling that a normal Relay connection field does not have."},"1934":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers#select-the-nodes-through-the-attachment","content":"Import `drizzleConnectionHelpers` from `@pothos/plugin-drizzle` and use the builder configured\nwith the Drizzle and Relay plugins. The helper targets `postMedia`. Its `select` uses\n`nestedSelection()` to request the Media fields\nselected beneath the connection's nodes; `resolveNode` returns that related Media row:"},"1935":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers#select-the-nodes-through-the-attachment","content":"The connection orders and creates cursors from attachment IDs, not Media IDs. An image shared\nby two posts can therefore have a different caption and connection position in each post.\nThe nested uploader selection is still planned from the requested Media fields."},"1936":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers#select-the-nodes-through-the-attachment","content":"The helper's `args` and `query` options define a `hasCaption` argument. When it is true, the\nconnection includes only attachments with a caption. Its default, false, includes all attachments.\n`getArgs()` exposes that argument on the field, and `getQuery()` combines the filter and ordering\nwith the requested page and node selection."},"1937":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers#add-the-connection-and-its-edge-fields","content":"`Media` below is the object ref returned by `builder.drizzleObject('media', ...)`.\nA normal `t.connection` needs an explicit `select` to load the helper's query on the parent.\nIts resolver passes the selected attachment rows to the helper's `resolve` method:"},"1938":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers#add-the-connection-and-its-edge-fields","content":"The third argument to `t.connection` configures the Edge type. Each edge retains the selected\nattachment fields, so its `caption` resolver reads the attachment's caption, while `node` returns\nthe Media selected by `resolveNode`. A missing caption returns null."},"1939":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers#add-the-connection-and-its-edge-fields","content":"The post has three attachments. Its first unfiltered page of two has `hasNextPage: true`;\nfiltering to captioned attachments returns two and has no next page. Pagination uses the same\n`first`/`after` and `last`/`before` arguments as other connections."},"1940":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers#querying-the-rows-in-a-resolver","content":"The same helper can build a query for a resolver that fetches the attachment rows itself.\nPass GraphQL resolve `info` to `getQuery`, merge any additional filter with its cursor filter,\nand pass the result to `resolve`:"},"1941":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers#querying-the-rows-in-a-resolver","content":"This alternative needs `db` imported from the application's database module and `id` selected\non Post. Remove the field's relation `select` when using it, so the rows are not also loaded\nthrough the parent query. Replacing the helper's `where` rather than combining it would discard\npagination constraints."},"1942":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connection-helpers#querying-the-rows-in-a-resolver","content":"If the rows and nodes are the same type, omit `resolveNode` and the node-mapping `select` when\ncreating the helper. Its `ref` provides the node type for `t.connection`. Prefer\n`t.relatedConnection` when you can expose that relation directly without custom loading."},"1943":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","type":"page","content":"Connections","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Drizzle"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections"},"1944":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","content":"Paginate Drizzle queries and relations using GraphQL selections"},"1945":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#page-through-published-posts","content":"Page through published posts"},"1946":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#related-connections","content":"Related connections"},"1947":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#connection-totalcount","content":"Connection totalCount"},"1948":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#connections-with-custom-edges","content":"Connections with custom edges"},"1949":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","content":"Connection fields combine cursor pagination with Pothos's selection planning. Define the rows a\nfield may return; Pothos adds pagination constraints and selects the columns and relations\nrequested under each node. The same field supports forward and backward pagination."},"1950":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","content":"Use `t.drizzleConnection` when your resolver queries the rows and `t.relatedConnection` for a\nDrizzle relation. Both require the Relay plugin."},"1951":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#page-through-published-posts","content":"The feed returns published posts ordered by creation time. Its resolver passes the result of\n`query()` to Drizzle, just as a `t.drizzleField` does:"},"1952":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#page-through-published-posts","content":"Pothos uses the selected node fields to plan the database query, including the nested author.\nIt also adds the ordering columns needed for cursors, even when the client does not select them.\nWhen creation times tie, Pothos appends a primary-key tie breaker so posts have distinct positions.\nSee Ordering and cursors for other orderings and column requirements."},"1953":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#page-through-published-posts","content":"A client requests the first page with `first`:"},"1954":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#page-through-published-posts","content":"For the next page, pass the previous `endCursor` as `after`. To paginate backward, use `last`\nand `before`, taking `before` from a page's `startCursor`:"},"1955":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#page-through-published-posts","content":"Backward pagination retains the connection's declared ordering; it selects the preceding rows\nrather than reversing the response. `nodes` is a convenience field enabled by\n`relay: { nodesOnConnection: true }`. With the default Relay configuration, use `edges { node }`."},"1956":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#related-connections","content":"An author's `postsConnection` applies the same publication filter as the feed. `t.relatedConnection`\nplans that relation from the parent selection without a custom resolver:"},"1957":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#related-connections","content":"The `query` option accepts an object or a callback receiving field arguments and context, as it\ndoes on `t.relation`. Connection `orderBy` accepts column names with `'asc'` or `'desc'`, or a\ncolumn or array of columns for ascending order. Pothos can invert these orderings when fetching\na backward page. The default ordering uses the table's primary key."},"1958":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#related-connections","content":"Both connection methods create Connection and Edge types. Pass additional options to customize\nthose types, as with the Relay plugin's connection fields."},"1959":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#connection-totalcount","content":"`totalCount` describes the matching rows across all pages. The author's `totalCount: true`\ncounts both published posts even when `first: 1` returns only one. Pothos loads the count only\nwhen it is selected; a query for only `totalCount` does not load the related posts."},"1960":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#connection-totalcount","content":"For `t.relatedConnection`, Pothos derives the count from the relation and its filter. For a root\n`t.drizzleConnection`, supply a `totalCount` resolver, as the feed does above. Its filter must\nmatch the rows returned by the connection. This resolver receives the usual\n`parent`, `args`, `context`, and `info` arguments. A count-only root query skips the main resolver."},"1961":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#connection-totalcount","content":"By default, a related count includes the `where` returned by the connection's `query`. Setting\n`filterConnectionTotalCount: false` in the builder's `drizzle` options ignores that field filter\nfor counts. A filter on the Drizzle relation itself still applies. Keep the default for the\npublic author field: counting drafts would reveal data excluded by its publication filter."},"1962":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#connection-totalcount","content":"For a many-to-many relation defined with `through`, the count matches the rows being paginated.\nIf two junction rows reach the same target, that target appears and is counted twice. This differs\nfrom `t.relatedCount`, which counts distinct related targets."},"1963":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/connections#connections-with-custom-edges","content":"A direct many-to-many relation works with `t.relatedConnection`. When the attachment itself has\nfields, such as a caption, the edge needs data from the junction row while its node represents\nMedia. Connection helpers shows how to combine that mapping with selection\nplanning, pagination, and custom edge fields in one connection."},"1964":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","type":"page","content":"Drizzle plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Drizzle"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle"},"1965":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","content":"A plugin to support efficient queries through drizzles relational query builder API"},"1966":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle#getting-started","content":"Getting started"},"1967":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","content":"This plugin uses drizzle's relational query builder v2,\nwhich ships in `drizzle-orm` 1.0. That release is still a release candidate, and npm's `latest`\ntag is the 0.x line, so install drizzle with the `rc` tag. Its API can still change before 1.0 is\nfinal."},"1968":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","content":"If you are upgrading from an older version of this plugin, read drizzle's relations v1 to v2\nguide, then this package's changelog for the\nPothos specific changes."},"1969":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","content":"The Drizzle plugin maps database tables to GraphQL types and plans database queries from the\nfields a client selects. It supports relations, computed fields, and Relay nodes and connections."},"1970":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","content":"The examples share a small author-and-post schema. The\npublishing playground includes its database definitions\nand initialization, and executes queries against SQLite in the browser."},"1971":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle#getting-started","content":"Setup connects your Drizzle client to Pothos.\nObjects covers type definitions and fields such as this lookup:"},"1972":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle#getting-started","content":"The resolver passes Pothos's selection plan to Drizzle, loading the requested posts with their\nauthor. Adding GraphQL fields does not require rewriting this root resolver."},"1973":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle#getting-started","content":"Relations covers filtering and counts; Selections explains computed\nfields and profiles; Connections handles pagination. Variants and\nInterfaces keep private account data separate from public authors. For more\nspecialized schemas, Connection helpers and Async selections\ncover custom pagination and asynchronous planning."},"1974":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces","type":"page","content":"Interfaces","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Drizzle"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces"},"1975":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces","content":"Creating interfaces for drizzle tables that can be shared by variants"},"1976":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces#interfaces","content":"Interfaces"},"1977":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces#define-the-interface","content":"Define the interface"},"1978":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces#selecting-a-viewer-implementation","content":"Selecting a viewer implementation"},"1979":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces#extending-an-interface","content":"Extending an interface"},"1980":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces#interfaces","content":"`builder.drizzleInterface` works like `builder.drizzleObject`. It can define the primary type\nor a variant of a table. Here Viewer is a variant, leaving User as the public primary type."},"1981":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces#define-the-interface","content":"`resolveType` uses the selected `role` discriminator and returns GraphQL type names. Returning\nnames avoids a circular reference between the interface and its implementations."},"1982":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces#selecting-a-viewer-implementation","content":"The interface's `select` is planned whenever a field returns the interface. An implementation's\nown selection is planned when a fragment narrows to it. Selections are not inherited; put data\nrequired by an implementation in its selection as well. An implementation may select additional\ncolumns or expressions for fields that do not belong on the interface."},"1983":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces#selecting-a-viewer-implementation","content":"Maya (`userId: 1`) resolves to EditorViewer with `canReviewSubmissions: true`. Leo (`userId: 2`)\nresolves to AuthorViewer, so the editor fragment contributes no field. The interface describes\nthose result shapes; the authenticated root lookup enforces whose drafts are returned."},"1984":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces#extending-an-interface","content":"Fields can be added to an interface later with `builder.drizzleInterfaceField` and\n`builder.drizzleInterfaceFields`, which take the interface ref (or the table name) like their\n`drizzleObjectField(s)` counterparts:"},"1985":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/interfaces#extending-an-interface","content":"An object type implementing a drizzle interface must be based on the same table. A plain object\ntype that implements one is planned with the interface's table, so fragments on it will select the\nrelations it inherits."},"1986":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","type":"page","content":"Drizzle Objects","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Drizzle"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects"},"1987":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","content":"Defining GraphQL object types based on drizzle tables"},"1988":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#defining-objects","content":"Defining Objects"},"1989":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#custom-fields","content":"Custom fields"},"1990":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#drizzle-fields","content":"Drizzle Fields"},"1991":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#a-nullable-author-lookup","content":"A nullable author lookup"},"1992":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#drizzlefieldwithinput","content":"`drizzleFieldWithInput`"},"1993":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#building-a-query-from-resolve-info","content":"Building a query from resolve info"},"1994":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","content":"Use the configured builder to map a Drizzle table to a GraphQL type. Defining a type\nregisters its fields; a root lookup makes them queryable."},"1995":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#defining-objects","content":"The `builder.drizzleObject` method can be used to define GraphQL Object types based on a drizzle\ntable:"},"1996":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#defining-objects","content":"You will be able to \"expose\" any column in the table, and GraphQL fields do not need to match the\nnames of the columns in your database. The returned `User` can be used like any other `ObjectRef`\nin Pothos."},"1997":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#defining-objects","content":"The example uses `builder.drizzleNode`, which adds a Relay ID and node refetching to the\nsame object API. See Relay for node IDs and refetching."},"1998":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#custom-fields","content":"A GraphQL field can combine several columns. The author’s full name declares both columns in\nits selection and computes the string from the loaded row:"},"1999":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#custom-fields","content":"This field belongs in User's `fields` callback. With the default type selection, all scalar\ncolumns are available; with `select: {}`, computed fields must declare what they read.\nSelections explains when those columns and relations are loaded."},"2000":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#drizzle-fields","content":"`t.drizzleField` can return a Drizzle object from Query or from any other object type. Its\nresolver receives a `query` function before the usual resolver arguments. Call that function and\npass its result to `findFirst` or `findMany`: it merges your filters and ordering with the\nrequirements of the GraphQL selection."},"2001":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#a-nullable-author-lookup","content":"The author page accepts an integer ID. A missing author returns null, matching the result of\n`findFirst` and the field's `nullable: true` option:"},"2002":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#a-nullable-author-lookup","content":"`author(id: 1) { fullName }` returns Maya Chen. `author(id: 999) { fullName }` returns null.\nAdding `posts { title }` loads the public posts through the same root resolver."},"2003":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#a-nullable-author-lookup","content":"For a list field, use `type: ['users']` and `db.query.users.findMany(query())`. A plain lookup\nwithout arguments can pass no options to `query`; fields with filters pass those options to it.\nIf the `with` you pass to `query` names a relation the type's `select` also loads, the arguments\nfrom the type's `select` are used for that relation."},"2004":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#drizzlefieldwithinput","content":"With the with-input plugin,\n`t.drizzleFieldWithInput` combines `t.drizzleField` with `t.fieldWithInput`. The `input` fields\nbecome an input object argument, and the resolver still receives the `query` function as its first\nargument. This alternative adds an `authorWithInput` lookup to the same public User type.\nInstall and register WithInputPlugin as described in Setup:"},"2005":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#building-a-query-from-resolve-info","content":"Use `t.drizzleQueryFromInfo` inside a regular field resolver to combine its GraphQL selection\nwith Drizzle query options. Pass a table name or Drizzle object/interface ref, along with the\nresolver's `context` and `info`. Pass the result directly to Drizzle:"},"2006":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#building-a-query-from-resolve-info","content":"Pass `path: ['user']` when the resolver returns a payload containing a `user` field. `paths`\naccepts multiple paths. Use a Drizzle ref instead of a table name to select a specific variant\nof that table's GraphQL type."},"2007":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#building-a-query-from-resolve-info","content":"The field builder checks the context and query options against its schema and table. Explicit\ncolumns and relations retain their types; omitted columns are not statically guaranteed, even\nif the GraphQL selection loads them. Put filters, selections, ordering, and limits inside the\nhelper call so they are merged with the planned selection."},"2008":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#building-a-query-from-resolve-info","content":"With `AsyncSelections: true`, await the result before passing it to Drizzle:"},"2009":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/objects#building-a-query-from-resolve-info","content":"Without `AsyncSelections`, the helper is typed as synchronous, consistent with the schema's\nselection callbacks. There is no per-call async option."},"2010":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","type":"page","content":"Ordering and cursors","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Drizzle"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors"},"2011":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","content":"How a connection orders rows, and the cursors that ordering produces"},"2012":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#tied-timestamps-in-a-feed","content":"Tied timestamps in a feed"},"2013":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#unique-orderings","content":"Unique orderings"},"2014":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#nullable-ordering-columns","content":"Nullable ordering columns"},"2015":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#cursor-values-and-precision","content":"Cursor values and precision"},"2016":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#ordering-by-an-expression","content":"Ordering by an expression"},"2017":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","content":"Connections page with a cursor that records where in the ordering the previous page ended. This\napplies to `t.relatedConnection`, `t.drizzleConnection`, and `drizzleConnectionHelpers`."},"2018":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#tied-timestamps-in-a-feed","content":"The post connection orders by `createdAt`, which\ncan be identical for several posts. Pothos adds a primary-key tie breaker so each post has a\ndistinct position in the connection."},"2019":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#unique-orderings","content":"`orderBy: { createdAt: 'desc' }` does not describe a unique ordering. Rows with the same timestamp\ncan be returned in a different order on each query, so paging through the connection may return a row\ntwice, or skip it."},"2020":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#unique-orderings","content":"Pothos adds the primary key to the ordering when the columns you provide are not already unique, so\nthis:"},"2021":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#unique-orderings","content":"is queried as `createdAt desc, id desc`. You can add the tie breaker yourself if you want it in a\ndifferent position or direction:"},"2022":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#unique-orderings","content":"Orderings that already contain the primary key, or a unique column or constraint whose columns are\nall marked `notNull()`, are used as provided. Nullable unique columns are not treated as unique,\nbecause rows containing a `null` are still tied with each other. Uniqueness declared with\n`uniqueIndex()` is not detected, so those orderings still get the primary key appended."},"2023":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#unique-orderings","content":"Pothos also recognizes composite primary keys declared with `primaryKey({ columns: [...] })`.\nPrimary-key columns are treated as non-null for ordering; ensure your database enforces that\nconstraint."},"2024":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#unique-orderings","content":"Nothing is appended when Pothos cannot find a key it can rely on, which means tables with no primary\nkey, and tables whose only unique column is nullable. Those connections keep the ordering you wrote,\nso give them a tie breaker yourself."},"2025":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#nullable-ordering-columns","content":"If you order a connection by a nullable column, paging with a cursor loses rows. `null` is not\ngreater or less than anything in SQL, so a cursor from a row with a value never returns the rows\nwhere the column is `null`. A cursor from a `null` row only returns the remaining `null` rows, or\nnothing when the ordering has no tie breaker. Which rows you lose depends on whether your database\nsorts nulls first or last, and on which direction you page. A page requested without a cursor is\ncomplete."},"2026":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#nullable-ordering-columns","content":"The same applies to an ordering expression that can evaluate to `null`. A nullable column later in\nthe ordering loses rows the same way, but only among rows that have the same values in the columns\nbefore it."},"2027":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#nullable-ordering-columns","content":"Order by columns that are `notNull()`, or give the expression a real value to fall back to with\n`coalesce`."},"2028":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#cursor-values-and-precision","content":"A cursor stores the values your driver returned to JavaScript, and those values are compared against\nthe column when the next page is requested. If the value in the cursor is not the value the database\nhas, the comparison will not match the rows you expect."},"2029":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#cursor-values-and-precision","content":"This comes up with timestamps. `timestamp({ mode: 'date' })` returns a JavaScript `Date`, which only\nholds milliseconds, while a Postgres `timestamptz` column stores microseconds. A row stored at\n`.086068` produces a cursor containing `.086`, and `created_at < '.086'` does not match the other\nrows in that millisecond, so they are never returned on any page."},"2030":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#cursor-values-and-precision","content":"There are two ways to avoid this."},"2031":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#cursor-values-and-precision","content":"The first is to map the column so the full value reaches JavaScript. `mode: 'string'` returns the\ntimestamp as text, with the microseconds intact:"},"2032":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#cursor-values-and-precision","content":"The cursor stores that text, and Postgres parses it back to the value it stored. The column is still\nwhat gets ordered and compared, so an index on it is still used. The field is typed as a `string`\nrather than a `Date`."},"2033":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#cursor-values-and-precision","content":"The second is to order by an expression that returns the full value, which lets you keep the `Date`\nmapping:"},"2034":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#ordering-by-an-expression","content":"`orderBy` accepts the name of any extra declared in the same query. Pothos orders by the expression,\nbuilds the cursor from the value it returns, and compares the expression when paging:"},"2035":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#ordering-by-an-expression","content":"Extras used this way should be written as a callback. Drizzle aliases the table it queries and passes\nthat alias to the callback, so an expression built from the imported table object will reference a\nname the query does not have."},"2036":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#ordering-by-an-expression","content":"With `drizzleConnectionHelpers`, declare the extra in `query` rather than in `select`. Only `query`\nis read when the ordering is resolved."},"2037":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/ordering-and-cursors#ordering-by-an-expression","content":"The expression also needs to sort in the same order its values compare. A timestamp formatted as\nfixed width UTC text sorts the same way it does chronologically, but a local time or variable width\nformat does not, and will skip rows."},"2038":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","type":"page","content":"Query planning","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Drizzle"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning"},"2039":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","content":"How the plugin turns a GraphQL query into drizzle queries"},"2040":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#how-fields-get-their-data","content":"How fields get their data"},"2041":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#compare-two-orderings-of-one-relation","content":"Compare two orderings of one relation"},"2042":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#nested-selections","content":"Nested selections"},"2043":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#async-selections","content":"Async selections"},"2044":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#conflicting-selections-between-variants","content":"Conflicting selections between variants"},"2045":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","content":"This page describes how the plugin turns a GraphQL query into drizzle queries, which is worth\nknowing when a schema issues more queries than you expect."},"2046":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#how-fields-get-their-data","content":"A field either reads its data from a row that has already been loaded, or runs a query of its own."},"2047":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#how-fields-get-their-data","content":"A field's `select` is planned into the query of the nearest ancestor that runs one: a\n`t.drizzleField`, a `t.relation`, a connection, or a fallback query. The field\nthen reads what it needs off the loaded row, without a query of its own. A field runs its own query\nwhen its `resolve` queries drizzle directly, and when the plugin issues a fallback query for a\nrelation that is missing from the row."},"2048":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#how-fields-get-their-data","content":"A field can do both. A `t.drizzleField`, or any other field with a `select`, nested under one of\nthose ancestors has its `select` planned into the parent's row, and still runs its own query when\nit resolves."},"2049":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#how-fields-get-their-data","content":"A field-level `select` is merged into the same query as its siblings and the type-level selection,\nrather than being kept separate for that field. Two selections of the same relation share a place\nin that query only when their arguments (`where`, `orderBy`, `limit`, ...) match. When they differ,\nthe first one planned wins, and the other is loaded with a query of its own. A type-level `select`\nis planned before any field's selection, no matter where they appear in the document, and fields\nare planned in the order they are selected."},"2050":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#compare-two-orderings-of-one-relation","content":"A client may need both the newest and oldest published posts on one author page:"},"2051":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#compare-two-orderings-of-one-relation","content":"The published-posts field translates those arguments\ninto different database orderings. The two fields cannot reuse the same loaded\nrelation because their orderings differ. The additional ordering is loaded through a fallback\nquery."},"2052":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#compare-two-orderings-of-one-relation","content":"In this example, selecting one ordering executes one SQL statement; selecting both executes\ntwo. The number of GraphQL field resolvers does not determine the number of database queries."},"2053":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#nested-selections","content":"A `select` function receives `nestedSelection`, which plans the selection beneath the field for the\nfield's own type. It returns the query config for that table, or a `many` config for a list field.\nAny keys you pass in are kept as they were given, so `columns` passed to `nestedSelection` will\nstill narrow the parent shape:"},"2054":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#nested-selections","content":"`nestedSelection(query, path, type)` takes two more arguments:"},"2055":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#nested-selections","content":"`path`: a list of field names, for selecting a field nested under the field's type. A segment can\nalso be written as `{ name, type }` to pin the implementation the field must be found under."},"2056":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#nested-selections","content":"`type`: a member of an interface or union, to read the selection as that type."},"2057":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#nested-selections","content":"The `nestedSelection` function also carries the `path` and `segments` of the field being planned,\nwhich are described under Relation queries."},"2058":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#async-selections","content":"When a selection depends on asynchronous request data, enable `AsyncSelections: true` and await\nnested plans. Async selections covers the builder configuration, supported\ncallbacks, helper options, and errors caused by unsettled promises."},"2059":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#conflicting-selections-between-variants","content":"When a query selects two variants of one table for the same row, either with a fragment on each\nunder one field, or through a `t.variant` field, the request fails if their `select` options ask\nfor the same relation with different arguments. The client sees `Unexpected error.`. The\noriginal error is on `error.cause`:"},"2060":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#conflicting-selections-between-variants","content":"The same applies to two variants defining the same `extras` key with different functions\n(`... conflict on extra \"lowercaseName\"`). Both variants describe one row, so their `select`\noptions are merged into a single query."},"2061":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/query-planning#conflicting-selections-between-variants","content":"To fix this, move the relation with its arguments, or the extra, into the `select` of the field\nthat needs it on one of the variants. A field-level selection that conflicts with what the row\nalready holds falls back to a query for that field, rather than failing the request."},"2062":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","type":"page","content":"Relations","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Drizzle"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations"},"2063":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","content":"Adding relations to drizzle objects"},"2064":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#published-posts-and-profiles","content":"Published posts and profiles"},"2065":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#relation-queries","content":"Relation queries"},"2066":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#fallback-queries","content":"Fallback queries"},"2067":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#related-count","content":"Related count"},"2068":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#many-to-many-relations","content":"Many-to-many relations"},"2069":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#related-field","content":"Related field"},"2070":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","content":"`t.relation` turns a Drizzle relation into a GraphQL field with its target type and cardinality.\nRegister the target GraphQL type as well as the relation."},"2071":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#published-posts-and-profiles","content":"The public author page returns published posts. Drafts belong on the private Viewer,\nso the filter belongs on the relation wherever User can be reached:"},"2072":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#published-posts-and-profiles","content":"The filter applies whenever this User type is queried. An author with no matching posts returns\nan empty list. The `oldestFirst` argument selects the ordering."},"2073":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#published-posts-and-profiles","content":"To return the Profile object, register its GraphQL type and use `t.relation('profile')`. To\nreturn only its nullable biography, use a field selection."},"2074":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#relation-queries","content":"`query` accepts a static object or a callback that converts field arguments into Drizzle query\noptions. Alongside filters and ordering, a relation may use `limit` and `offset`. For example,\nthis alternative adds offset pagination to the public posts field:"},"2075":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#relation-queries","content":"The query API enables you to define args and convert them into parameters that will be passed into\nthe relational query builder. The `query` callback receives `(args, ctx, pathInfo)` where `pathInfo`\ndescribes where in the GraphQL query the relation is being loaded:"},"2076":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#relation-queries","content":"`path`: a list of `ParentType.fieldName` strings, from the root field down to the field being\nresolved (eg. `['Query.user', 'User.posts']`)."},"2077":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#relation-queries","content":"`segments`: one object per entry in `path`, with `field` (the field name), `alias` (the alias used\nin the query, or the field name if none), `parentType` (the name of the type the field is defined\non), and `isList` (whether the field returns a list)."},"2078":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#relation-queries","content":"You can read more about the relation query builder api\nhere"},"2079":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#fallback-queries","content":"A field whose data is not on the row it resolves from is loaded with a fallback query. This happens\nwhen:"},"2080":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#fallback-queries","content":"The parent row was not loaded through a `t.drizzleField`, `t.relation`, or connection. This\ncovers rows a resolver queried itself, and rows that came from somewhere else entirely."},"2081":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#fallback-queries","content":"A `drizzleField` resolver did not pass the result of `query()` to drizzle."},"2082":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#fallback-queries","content":"A relation's arguments conflict with a sibling selection of the same relation that was planned\nfirst."},"2083":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#fallback-queries","content":"Fallback queries are batched. Every row of a table that needs the same selection in the same tick\nis loaded with one `findMany` filtered on the primary key, or on the first unique column for a\ntable that has no primary key, and the rows are matched back to their parents. If the query does\nnot return a row for a parent, because it was deleted since it was loaded, or never came from the\ntable, that field fails with `Unexpected error.`. The original error (`Model users(1) not found`)\nis on `error.cause`."},"2084":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#fallback-queries","content":"The alias query demonstrates\nthis fallback: newest and oldest cannot share one loaded posts list."},"2085":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#related-count","content":"An author’s count must use the same publication filter as the list. Otherwise the number can\nreveal that unpublished posts exist:"},"2086":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#related-count","content":"`t.relatedCount` returns the number of related rows without loading them. Without a `where`,\nit counts all rows in the relation."},"2087":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#related-count","content":"The `where` option accepts either a static SQL filter or a function that receives the field arguments\nand context:"},"2088":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#related-count","content":"For a many-to-many relation (one defined with `.through(...)`), `t.relatedCount` counts distinct\nrelated rows, so a row reachable through two junction rows counts once. A `t.relatedConnection`'s\n`totalCount` counts the rows the connection pages over instead, which is one per junction row,\nsince that is what the relational query builder returns for the relation."},"2089":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#many-to-many-relations","content":"A post exposes attached Media objects even though the database stores an attachment row. The\n`through` relation handles that join; Pothos exposes its target like any other relation:"},"2090":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#many-to-many-relations","content":"`t.relation` and `t.relatedConnection` expose the Media type directly; no GraphQL type for the\njunction table is needed."},"2091":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#related-field","content":"The `t.relatedField` method allows you to define a field based on a relation that uses custom\nselections, including aggregations like counts. This is useful when you want to expose derived data\nfrom a relation without loading the full related records."},"2092":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#related-field","content":"For a simple count, prefer `t.relatedCount`. Its equivalent using `t.relatedField` illustrates\nhow `buildFilter` restricts an expression to the parent's related rows:"},"2093":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#related-field","content":"`buildFilter(parent)` includes the relation's join conditions and any filter defined on the\nrelation. Combine it with the conditions your field needs, then return the selected value from\n`resolve`. Use this helper for custom expressions that `t.relatedCount` does not cover."},"2094":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relations#related-field","content":"`t.relatedField` also accepts the normal field options (`description`, `deprecationReason`,\n`extensions`, and options added by other plugins like `authScopes`). Its `resolve` may be async,\nand receives the resolve `info` as its fourth argument."},"2095":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","type":"page","content":"Relay nodes","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Drizzle"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay"},"2096":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","content":"Defining refetchable nodes with the Drizzle plugin"},"2097":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay#define-a-node","content":"Define a node"},"2098":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay#refetch-a-public-author","content":"Refetch a public author"},"2099":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay#authorize-the-node-load-path","content":"Authorize the node load path"},"2100":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","content":"The Relay plugin supplies node IDs and cursor connections. Register it with Drizzle as shown in\nSetup; Connections covers pagination."},"2101":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","content":"Defining a node creates a direct lookup through `node` and `nodes`. These lookups bypass custom\nroot resolvers, so permission checks or visibility filters on a list or parent field do not protect\nnode refetches. An encoded global ID is an identifier, not proof of permission."},"2102":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","content":"With Scope Auth, require scopes on the `node` and `nodes` fields, put scopes on the `Node`\ninterface or each node type, or disable the lookup fields. You can also enforce visibility where the\nnode loads, so inaccessible records look missing. See\nRelay node authorization for each option and its\nlimits."},"2103":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay#define-a-node","content":"`builder.drizzleNode` uses the same fields and selections as `builder.drizzleObject`, and adds\nan ID and a root node lookup. The example User uses its integer primary key:"},"2104":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay#define-a-node","content":"This minimal alternative shows the node options. The example User adds full name, profile,\nand published posts to the same type. Other ID field options can be passed alongside `column`;\nfor a composite primary key, `column` can return a list of columns."},"2105":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay#refetch-a-public-author","content":"The ID returned by User can be passed back to\n`node` to fetch the author with a new selection:"},"2106":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay#refetch-a-public-author","content":"The refetch operation uses the ID of User 1. The node\nreturns the same public fields as the author lookup, including only published posts."},"2107":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/relay#authorize-the-node-load-path","content":"The public User type here can be refetched by anyone. Its\npublished-posts relation applies the publication filter\nwherever User is reached, including through `node`. Post stays an ordinary Drizzle object;\ndefining a public feed does not also make individual drafts globally refetchable."},"2108":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","type":"page","content":"Selections","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Drizzle"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections"},"2109":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","content":"Selecting the columns and relations your fields need"},"2110":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections#field-selections","content":"Field selections"},"2111":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections#only-load-a-profile-when-requested","content":"Only load a profile when requested"},"2112":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections#computed-sql-values","content":"Computed SQL values"},"2113":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections#type-selections","content":"Type selections"},"2114":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","content":"Selections describe the database data a GraphQL field needs. The example User starts with\n`select: {}` so requesting one field does not load unrelated columns or a profile."},"2115":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections#field-selections","content":"An exposed column, such as `t.exposeString('firstName')`, contributes its own selection. A custom\nresolver must declare the columns it reads:"},"2116":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections#only-load-a-profile-when-requested","content":"A biography is a string in GraphQL, but it comes from the optional profile relation. The field\nselects that relation only when requested:"},"2117":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections#only-load-a-profile-when-requested","content":"A missing profile returns null. A name-only query does not load the profile; adding `bio` adds\nthe relation to the database query."},"2118":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections#computed-sql-values","content":"The same mechanism supports SQL expressions through `extras`. For example, a field can select\n`lowercaseName` only when the GraphQL operation requests it:"},"2119":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections#type-selections","content":"By default, a `drizzleObject` gives its resolvers access to all columns of the table. A type-level\n`select` replaces that default and makes its selected data available to every field resolver.\nThis alternative always loads the name, profile, and expression:"},"2120":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections#type-selections","content":"Any selections added to the type will be available to consume in all resolvers. Columns that are not\nselected can still be exposed as before."},"2121":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections#type-selections","content":"Use type selections for data required whenever the type is loaded, and field selections for data\nneeded by a particular field. Both are merged into the parent query; they do not make each field\nan independent database query. See Query planning."},"2122":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/selections#type-selections","content":"With `ScopeAuthPlugin`, scope checks that read the parent see what its type loads (its `select`, or\nevery column), even for a row returned from a plain `t.field`. A row missing that data is reloaded,\nso a row that no longer exists fails the field with `Unexpected error.`. To return a deleted row,\nload it with a `t.drizzleField` query before deleting it. Only top-level keys are checked, so a row\nwith a partly loaded relation isn't reloaded."},"2123":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","type":"page","content":"Setup","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Drizzle"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup"},"2124":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","content":"Setting up the Drizzle plugin"},"2125":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#installing","content":"Installing"},"2126":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#configure-the-builder","content":"Configure the builder"},"2127":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#integration-with-other-plugins","content":"Integration with other plugins"},"2128":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#plugin-options","content":"Plugin options"},"2129":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","content":"Pass your existing Drizzle client and relations to the plugin. These guides assume familiarity\nwith Drizzle; use its driver setup and\nrelations documentation to configure the database."},"2130":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#installing","content":"The plugin requires Drizzle's relational query builder v2. See the\nversion requirements before installing or upgrading Drizzle."},"2131":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#configure-the-builder","content":"`DrizzleRelations` provides the table and relation types used by Pothos. Import `getTableConfig`\nfrom the dialect used by your client (`sqlite-core` above, or `pg-core` / `mysql-core`). `client`\nmay also be a function `(ctx) => db` for a request-specific client. The examples use\nrequest context to supply the current `userId`."},"2132":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#configure-the-builder","content":"Use this builder to define GraphQL types and fields, then call `builder.toSchema()`."},"2133":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#integration-with-other-plugins","content":"Install and register `@pothos/plugin-relay` to use `drizzleNode` and connection fields:"},"2134":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#integration-with-other-plugins","content":"`nodesOnConnection` adds the `nodes` convenience field used in these guides. Without it,\nselect `edges { node { ... } }` instead."},"2135":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#integration-with-other-plugins","content":"The with-input plugin is another optional integration. To use\n`t.drizzleFieldWithInput`, install `@pothos/plugin-with-input`, import it, and add it to the\nbuilder's plugins:"},"2136":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#plugin-options","content":"`client`: the drizzle client, or a function returning one from the request context."},"2137":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#plugin-options","content":"`getTableConfig`: the `getTableConfig` of your dialect, used to read primary keys and unique\nconstraints."},"2138":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#plugin-options","content":"`relations`: the relations passed to `drizzle()`."},"2139":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#plugin-options","content":"`defaultConnectionSize` / `maxConnectionSize`: the page size a connection uses when the query\ndoes not ask for one (defaults to 20), and the largest size it will accept (defaults to 100).\nBoth can also be set per field with `defaultSize` and `maxSize`."},"2140":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#plugin-options","content":"`filterConnectionTotalCount`: see Connection totalCount."},"2141":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#plugin-options","content":"`skipDeferredFragments`: selections inside a `@defer` fragment are left out of the planned query\nby default, and the fragment's fields are loaded through fallback queries\nwhen it resolves. Set this to `false` to plan deferred selections with the rest of the query."},"2142":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/setup#plugin-options","content":"`skipDeferredFragments` controls query planning; it does not enable incremental execution. The application\nmust register the defer directive and use an executor, server transport, and client that support\nthe same incremental delivery protocol. With GraphQL.js 17, ordinary `execute` rejects schemas\ncontaining `@defer` or `@stream`; incremental execution uses `experimentalExecuteIncrementally`.\nFor a server integration, see GraphQL Yoga's defer and stream setup,\nwhich uses `@graphql-yoga/plugin-defer-stream`. Check the integration's supported versions when\nchoosing an executor and client; adding directives or changing `skipDeferredFragments` alone is\nnot sufficient."},"2143":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","type":"page","content":"Type variants","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Drizzle"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants"},"2144":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","content":"How to define multiple GraphQL types based on the same drizzle table"},"2145":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants#a-single-object-variant","content":"A single object variant"},"2146":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants#a-viewer-with-role-specific-fields","content":"A viewer with role-specific fields"},"2147":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants#conditionally-expose-another-variant","content":"Conditionally expose another variant"},"2148":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants#relation-variants","content":"Relation variants"},"2149":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","content":"A variant gives another GraphQL representation to the same database row. The publishing API\nuses public User fields for author pages and a private Viewer for the signed-in account."},"2150":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","content":"The API has three parts:"},"2151":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","content":"`variant` registers an additional type instead of naming the primary type with `name`."},"2152":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","content":"`t.variant` returns another representation of the same row."},"2153":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","content":"A relation's `type` option can select a variant using its returned object ref."},"2154":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants#a-single-object-variant","content":"Use `variant` to define Viewer alongside the primary User type. `t.variant('users')` exposes\nthe public representation of the same row:"},"2155":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants#a-viewer-with-role-specific-fields","content":"When viewer fields differ by role, Viewer can instead be an interface with object variants\nfor each role. The following definition uses this structure; Interfaces explains\nits implementations:"},"2156":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants#a-viewer-with-role-specific-fields","content":"The root lookup uses the authenticated context ID. It does not accept an arbitrary author's ID:"},"2157":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants#a-viewer-with-role-specific-fields","content":"Maya (`userId: 1`) sees “Planning the spring exchange”; Leo (`userId: 2`) sees “Saving rainwater.”\nThe interface and variants describe the shapes; the root lookup restricts ownership. Public\nauthor fields and post connections return published posts only."},"2158":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants#conditionally-expose-another-variant","content":"The publishing User also exposes its Viewer conditionally. Define that field after Viewer is registered,\nwhich avoids a circular reference between their definitions:"},"2159":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants#conditionally-expose-another-variant","content":"The ownership check is essential: without it an arbitrary author's public row could reveal that\nauthor's drafts. `isNull` makes the variant field null when its parent is another account."},"2160":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants#conditionally-expose-another-variant","content":"A variant field accepts a `select` just like other fields. Here it loads `id` for the ownership\ncheck; the target variant contributes the selections required by its own fields."},"2161":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants#relation-variants","content":"To use a different representation of a related row, pass its object ref to `t.relation`'s `type`\noption. The variant must represent the relation's target table. For example, this alternative\nadds a compact PostSummary representation and selects it for a public author's posts:"},"2162":{"id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/drizzle/variants#relation-variants","content":"Two variants selected for the same row have their type-level selections merged. Conflicting\nrelation arguments or extras can fail; see\nConflicting selections between variants."},"2163":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","type":"page","content":"Connections","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma ORM 8"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections"},"2164":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","content":"Relay connections backed by Prisma Collections"},"2165":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#paginate-a-query-field","content":"Paginate a query field"},"2166":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#paginate-a-relation","content":"Paginate a relation"},"2167":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#configure-a-connection","content":"Configure a connection"},"2168":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#ordering-and-cursors","content":"Ordering and cursors"},"2169":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#use-a-custom-collection","content":"Use a custom Collection"},"2170":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#custom-connections","content":"Custom connections"},"2171":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#load-a-page-in-your-resolver","content":"Load a page in your resolver"},"2172":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","content":"Connections add cursor pagination to a list. They require the Relay plugin. Use\n`t.prismaConnection` to paginate a model's rows, and `t.relatedConnection` to paginate a relation."},"2173":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#paginate-a-query-field","content":"Clients request pages with `first` and `after`, or `last` and `before`. For example:"},"2174":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#paginate-a-relation","content":"To paginate a relation, such as a user's posts, use `t.relatedConnection`:"},"2175":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#paginate-a-relation","content":"It takes the same options as `t.prismaConnection` except `collection`. `totalCount` counts the rows\nmatching `query.where`, unless `filterConnectionTotalCount` is `false`."},"2176":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#configure-a-connection","content":"`t.prismaConnection` takes:"},"2177":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#configure-a-connection","content":"`query`: `{ where, orderBy }`, or `(args, ctx) => ({ where, orderBy })`. Rows are ordered by the\nprimary key when `orderBy` is omitted. See Ordering and cursors."},"2178":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#configure-a-connection","content":"`defaultSize` / `maxSize`: page sizes, or `(args, ctx) => number`. Default to the plugin's\nconnection size options."},"2179":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#configure-a-connection","content":"`totalCount`: `true` counts the rows matching `query.where`. Pass\n`(parent, args, ctx, info) => number` to count them yourself. The count only runs when\n`totalCount` is selected."},"2180":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#configure-a-connection","content":"`args`: extra arguments for filtering or sorting. The Relay pagination arguments are added automatically."},"2181":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#configure-a-connection","content":"`collection`: an optional starting ORM query, returned by `(parent, args, ctx, info) => Collection`.\nSee Use a custom Collection."},"2182":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#configure-a-connection","content":"Don't call `orderBy()`, `include()`, `limit()`, `offset()`, `cursor()`, `distinct()` or\n`distinctOn()` on the Collection you return. The first two are type errors, and the rest throw\nbefore any SQL runs."},"2183":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#configure-a-connection","content":"Connection and edge types can be customized as with the Relay plugin, including\n`edgesNullable` and `nodeNullable`."},"2184":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#ordering-and-cursors","content":"A connection's `query.orderBy` takes columns of the model, each with a direction, as a\nrelation's does. You can't order a connection by relation\ncolumns, aggregates or expressions. Nullable columns sort nulls last unless you set `nulls`:"},"2185":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#ordering-and-cursors","content":"If the order doesn't include a unique key, the plugin adds the primary key to the end, in the same\ndirection as the last column. If the primary key can't be ordered, it uses the first non-null unique\nkey that can."},"2186":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#ordering-and-cursors","content":"To let clients choose the order, pass a function of the arguments:"},"2187":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#ordering-and-cursors","content":"Cursors contain the values of the ordered columns and clients can read them, so don't order by\ncolumns clients shouldn't see."},"2188":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#ordering-and-cursors","content":"A timestamp read as a JavaScript `Date` only keeps milliseconds, so you can't order by one with a\nprecision above 3. A plain `DateTime` is fine. On PostgreSQL, use `TimestamptzTemporal` for more\nprecise timestamps."},"2189":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#ordering-and-cursors","content":"An invalid `orderBy` in a `query` object throws when the schema is built. If a `query` function\nreturns one, that request fails."},"2190":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#use-a-custom-collection","content":"Use `collection` for an existing ORM query or to run code before the connection loads. For example,\ncheck access before returning the query for draft posts:"},"2191":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#use-a-custom-collection","content":"The callback can be async. Queries and selections explains its arguments and how\n`collection` and `query` combine."},"2192":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#custom-connections","content":"Use `prismaConnectionHelpers` with `t.connection` when a connection's nodes are a different model\nthan the rows being paged, or when your resolver needs to work with the loaded rows. For\neverything else, use `t.prismaConnection` and `t.relatedConnection`."},"2193":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#custom-connections","content":"For example, a post's `commenters` connection pages through comments but returns each comment's\nauthor. The helper takes a model name or a Prisma type ref, plus `query`, `defaultSize`, `maxSize`,\n`totalCount` and `args`. `totalCount` takes `true`, but not a function."},"2194":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#custom-connections","content":"`select` sets what is loaded for each comment. Use `nodeSelection()` for the relation that holds\nthe node. A helper can only have one. Other relations can be selected with `true`.\n`resolveNode` returns the node for each comment:"},"2195":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#custom-connections","content":"`getQuery` loads one page of comments with the post, and the count when the connection selects a\nfield named `totalCount`. `resolve` builds the connection from the rows that `getQuery` or `load`\nloaded. You can also pass it rows you loaded yourself with the same order and paging. `totalCount`\nis only set for a list that `getQuery` or `load` loaded, or when you pass a count. Without\n`resolveNode`, the rows are the nodes, and the connection's `type` should be the helper's `ref`."},"2196":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#custom-connections","content":"Call `getQuery` at the top level of the connection field's own `select`, not inside another\nrelation's entry. To put the connection on another type, make that type a variant of\nthe model the field is on."},"2197":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#custom-connections","content":"`select` and `query` also receive the field's arguments and context. Add the helper's `args` to the\nfield with `args: commenters.getArgs()`."},"2198":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#custom-connections","content":"To count the rows yourself, for example on MySQL where Prisma can't count them yet, pass the count\nto `resolve`: `commenters.resolve(rows, args, ctx, { totalCount })`."},"2199":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#load-a-page-in-your-resolver","content":"On a root field, use `load` to page a Collection of the helper's model. It loads the page, along\nwith whatever the query selects on the nodes, in one statement, and the count when `totalCount` is\nselected. The Collection has the same restrictions as a `t.prismaConnection`'s `collection`. Pass\nthe rows to `resolve`. With `resolveNode`, each edge also has its row, so edge fields can read it:"},"2200":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/connections#load-a-page-in-your-resolver","content":"`load` returns one extra row, which `resolve` uses to tell whether there is another page. Pass the\nlist to `resolve` unchanged."},"2201":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","type":"page","content":"Prisma ORM 8 plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma ORM 8"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next"},"2202":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","content":"An experimental Pothos plugin for the Prisma ORM 8 collection client"},"2203":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next#example","content":"Example"},"2204":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next#relay-connections","content":"Relay connections"},"2205":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next#next-steps","content":"Next steps"},"2206":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","content":"This plugin integrates Prisma ORM 8 with Pothos to build\na strongly typed GraphQL API from your Prisma models. Expose database fields and relations, add\ncomputed fields, and support Relay nodes and paginated connections. Your GraphQL types and fields can\nhave different names and shapes from the underlying models."},"2207":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","content":"Each field declares the columns and relations it needs, and the plugin loads what the incoming\nGraphQL query uses. Relations, counts and connection pages load with their parent, usually in one\nSQL statement per root field, so you don't need a resolver or database call for every relation.\nField types are inferred from your Prisma contract."},"2208":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","content":"This plugin targets Prisma ORM `8.0.0-rc.23` exactly. Its API, and Prisma's, may change in any\nrelease. For Prisma ORM 7, use the Prisma plugin."},"2209":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","content":"The playground example runs the examples below against\nSQLite in the browser, and its **SQL** tab shows the statements each query runs."},"2210":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next#example","content":"With a configured builder, define the posts your API exposes\nand their author relation. These examples use the playground's `Post` and `User` models:"},"2211":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next#example","content":"`fullName` is a computed field. Its `select` lists the columns the resolver reads:"},"2212":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next#example","content":"Add a recent-posts query that returns the ten latest published posts:"},"2213":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next#example","content":"For `{ recentPosts { title author { fullName } } }`, the posts and their authors load in one SQL\nstatement, including the columns needed to compute each author's name."},"2214":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next#relay-connections","content":"With the Relay plugin, `t.relatedConnection` adds a paginated list of\npublished posts to an author profile:"},"2215":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next#relay-connections","content":"`t.prismaConnection` does the same for query fields. See\nConnections."},"2216":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next#next-steps","content":"To get started, follow Setup, then read\nQueries and selections, Objects and Relations.\nFor an existing Prisma ORM 7 schema, see Migrating from the Prisma plugin."},"2217":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","type":"page","content":"Indirect relations","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma ORM 8"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations"},"2218":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","content":"Loading relations for fields that aren't t.relation"},"2219":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations#selecting-fields-from-a-nested-graphql-field","content":"Selecting fields from a nested GraphQL field"},"2220":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations#selecting-as-a-specific-type","content":"Selecting as a specific type"},"2221":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations#join-models","content":"Join models"},"2222":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","content":"Use `t.relation` for a direct relation, including a renamed or filtered field.\nUse `t.field` with a `select` when the field goes through a join model, wraps the rows in another\nGraphQL type, or needs its own resolver logic."},"2223":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","content":"A field's `select` function gets `nestedSelection` as its third argument. Use it as the value for\na relation, and the plugin loads that relation in the parent's query, with whatever the client\nselects on the field's type:"},"2224":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","content":"For this filtered field alone, prefer\n`publishedPosts: t.relation('posts', { query: { where: { published: 1 } } })`."},"2225":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","content":"With `{ posts: true }` instead, the plugin only loads each post's columns. Anything selected\nunder the field, such as a post's `author`, needs an extra batched query. Use `nestedSelection`\nwhenever the field's type has relations or selects of its own."},"2226":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","content":"`nestedSelection` takes an optional object with the same options as a\nrelation entry, plus `path`:"},"2227":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","content":"`where`, `orderBy`, `limit`, `offset`: the relation's\nquery options. A to-one relation takes none."},"2228":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","content":"`select`: anything else to load for each row, on top of what the query selects."},"2229":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","content":"`path`: the path to a nested field to read the selection from. A segment can be written as\n`{ name, type }` to only read the field inside a fragment on that type."},"2230":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","content":"`type`: the type to read the selection as, when the field returns an interface or union. You can\npass anything a field's `type` takes, or a model's name for its default type. The type's model\nmust be the relation's target, and the query and rows are typed for that model."},"2231":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","content":"The rows only include the primary key and the selected columns, not every column."},"2232":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations#selecting-fields-from-a-nested-graphql-field","content":"When the field returns a plain object type that wraps the rows, pass the `path` to the field\ninside it that returns the relation's type:"},"2233":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations#selecting-as-a-specific-type","content":"When the field returns an interface or union, use `type` to pick the object type to read the\nselection as. Fragments on other types are ignored:"},"2234":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations#join-models","content":"Relations defined with `rel.manyToMany` work with `t.relation` and `nestedSelection` like any\nother to-many relation."},"2235":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations#join-models","content":"When the join table is its own model, give the relation to the join model a `select`, and use\n`nestedSelection` inside it. Here comments join users to the posts they commented on:"},"2236":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/indirect-relations#join-models","content":"Everything still loads in the parent's query. To load only one variant of a relation's model, add\n`type` to the relation's entry, with the variant's ref or model name:\n`{ type: Bug, select: { reviewer: true } }`."},"2237":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","type":"page","content":"Model inheritance","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma ORM 8"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance"},"2238":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","content":"Exposing inherited Prisma models through a GraphQL interface"},"2239":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#define-the-interface-and-its-objects","content":"Define the interface and its objects"},"2240":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#query-a-variant","content":"Query a variant"},"2241":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#expose-related-variants","content":"Expose related variants"},"2242":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#mutations","content":"Mutations"},"2243":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#return-an-updated-record","content":"Return an updated record"},"2244":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#return-a-deleted-record","content":"Return a deleted record"},"2245":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#upsert-a-variant","content":"Upsert a variant"},"2246":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","content":"When your Prisma contract defines inherited models, such as a `Task` that can be a `Bug` or a\n`Feature`, expose their shared fields through a GraphQL interface. Each kind gets an object type,\nand the plugin chooses the right type for each row automatically."},"2247":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","content":"For multiple GraphQL views of a single Prisma model, use Type variants."},"2248":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#define-the-interface-and-its-objects","content":"This example assumes a contract with `Task`, its `Bug` and `Feature` variants, and a `User` model\nwith a `tasks` relation. `Bug` also has an optional `reviewer` relation to `User`. Use the builder\nand imported `db` from Setup, with `User` already defined as a GraphQL type."},"2249":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#define-the-interface-and-its-objects","content":"Define an object for each variant in the contract. You don't need a `resolveType`. Clients can\nquery shared fields and add fragments for variant-specific fields:"},"2250":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#define-the-interface-and-its-objects","content":"A variant's `select` works like it does on any other type. Here `Bug` loads its reviewer so\n`reviewerEmail` can read it. Anything in the interface's `select` is available to the types that\nimplement it too."},"2251":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#query-a-variant","content":"Use a variant's type on a field to return only that variant's rows. To filter on the variant's\nown fields, pass `where` as a function:"},"2252":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#query-a-variant","content":"If you pass your own `collection`, start from the base model narrowed to the variant, such as\n`db.orm.Task.variant('bug')`. This is what the plugin uses when you omit `collection`."},"2253":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#expose-related-variants","content":"For a relation to `Task`, return the interface to include every variant, or pass a variant's ref\nas `type` to narrow the field:"},"2254":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#expose-related-variants","content":"The plugin sometimes runs an extra batched query to load what each variant's type needs."},"2255":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#expose-related-variants","content":"When a variant is stored in its own table, a relation to it can filter and sort by the variant's\nown fields, but not by fields inherited from the base model. To use both, start from the base\nCollection narrowed with `variant()`, as in the previous section."},"2256":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#expose-related-variants","content":"Custom resolvers that load rows can use collectionFromInfo\nto load what the query selects. Variant rows loaded this way can also be returned through a\ncompatible `prismaInterface`. The plugin loads anything else it needs to resolve their type."},"2257":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#mutations","content":"When a `t.prismaField` returns `Bug`, its resolver receives a Collection for\n`orm.Task.variant('bug')`. `variant('bug')` only narrows to bugs, and `update()` and `delete()` act\non the first match, so add a filter such as `where({ id })` to change one task."},"2258":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#return-an-updated-record","content":"Some Prisma write methods return only the base model's columns when the variant has its own\ntable. For example, an updated `Feature` may be missing its `priority`. Use `updateAndCount()`\nto make the change, then `first()` on the same filtered Collection to read the response."},"2259":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#return-an-updated-record","content":"When the field returns the `Task` interface, the plugin may run another query after the update\nto load a relation such as `Bug.reviewer`."},"2260":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#return-a-deleted-record","content":"A deleted row cannot be read again. To return a deleted bug with its reviewer, return the `Bug`\nobject type, or prepare the query with `collectionFromInfo({ ..., typeName: 'Bug' })` before\ncalling `delete()`. Either way the reviewer is loaded as part of the delete.\n`collectionFromInfo` requires `typeName` for a Collection that uses `variant('bug')`."},"2261":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#return-a-deleted-record","content":"For a variant stored in its own table, Prisma's `delete()` result contains only the base model's\ncolumns too. Return an ID or a payload that does not need the missing fields. Avoid `@defer` on a\ndeleted record, because deferred fields may need to read the row after it is gone."},"2262":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/inheritance#upsert-a-variant","content":"For variants stored in the base model's table, `upsert()` can match a unique key belonging to a\ndifferent variant. For example, `Task.variant('bug').upsert(...)` can update a feature with that\nkey and return it as a bug. Check the existing task's kind before choosing whether to create or\nupdate when your mutation must preserve it."},"2263":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","type":"page","content":"Interfaces","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma ORM 8"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces"},"2264":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","content":"GraphQL interfaces backed by contract models"},"2265":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces#native-inheritance","content":"Native inheritance"},"2266":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","content":"`builder.prismaInterface` defines a GraphQL interface for a Prisma model. It takes the same\noptions as `builder.prismaObject`."},"2267":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","content":"Building on Type variants, update the `User` and `Viewer` definitions to share a\n`Person` interface. Here `resolveType` chooses `Viewer` for the signed-in user's own row, and `User`\nfor other authors:"},"2268":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","content":"Implementing types must use the interface's Prisma model or one of its inherited models."},"2269":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","content":"`t.prismaField`, `t.prismaConnection`, `t.relation` and `t.relatedConnection` all accept an\ninterface ref as `type`. Fields on an implementing type can read the interface's `select`.\nImplementing objects don't accept `isTypeOf`."},"2270":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","content":"`resolveType` always has the row's `id`. If it needs other columns or to-one relations, add them\nto the interface's `select`. To-many relations aren't available to it."},"2271":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","content":"To add fields to the interface from another file, use `prismaInterfaceField` or\n`prismaInterfaceFields`:"},"2272":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","content":"To return rows through `Node` or another interface from a plain field, see\nReturning rows from other fields."},"2273":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","content":"An object type from `builder.objectRef` can implement a `prismaInterface` if its shape is\n`PrismaPlanned` for the model, such as\n`builder.objectRef<{ id: string; email: string; firstName: string } & PrismaPlanned<'User'>>`. The\nshape must include the interface's `select` columns and the columns its fields expose."},"2274":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/interfaces#native-inheritance","content":"When Prisma itself defines inherited models, such as `Task`, `Bug` and `Feature`, the plugin can\nchoose the GraphQL type automatically. See Model inheritance."},"2275":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","type":"page","content":"Migrating from the Prisma plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma ORM 8"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma"},"2276":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","content":"Updating an existing Pothos schema for Prisma ORM 8"},"2277":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#connect-the-new-client","content":"Connect the new client"},"2278":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#what-changed","content":"What changed"},"2279":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#declare-data-for-computed-fields","content":"Declare data for computed fields"},"2280":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#update-queries-and-mutations","content":"Update queries and mutations"},"2281":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#keep-your-connection-queries","content":"Keep your connection queries"},"2282":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#adapt-custom-relation-fields","content":"Adapt custom relation fields"},"2283":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","content":"Start with your Prisma ORM 8 client and contract, then update Pothos to use\n`@pothos/plugin-prisma-next`. You can keep the structure of your GraphQL schema: `prismaObject`,\n`t.expose*`, `t.relation`, nodes and connections still define the fields your clients use."},"2284":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#connect-the-new-client","content":"Follow Setup to replace the plugin import, pass your contract as `PrismaContract`, and\nconfigure `prisma: { client: db }`. The new client replaces the Prisma ORM 7 client and the\nPothos generator output used by `@pothos/plugin-prisma`. Use one plugin per program."},"2285":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#what-changed","content":"`take` and `skip` are now `limit` and `offset`."},"2286":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#what-changed","content":"`where` and `orderBy` use Prisma ORM 8's shapes, not Prisma Client's."},"2287":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#what-changed","content":"The `dmmf`, `exposeDescriptions` and `onUnusedQuery` plugin options are gone."},"2288":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#what-changed","content":"A `client` function also needs the base client's `context`. See\nPlugin options."},"2289":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#what-changed","content":"`prismaObject` and `prismaNode` take no `include` or `findUnique`. Use `select`."},"2290":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#what-changed","content":"A connection helper's `getQuery` takes `(args, ctx)`, without `nestedSelection`."},"2291":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#what-changed","content":"`@pothos/plugin-prisma-utils` has no counterpart."},"2292":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#declare-data-for-computed-fields","content":"Exposed fields and relations load their own data. If a custom resolver reads additional columns,\ndeclare those with `select` so they are loaded whenever the field is requested:"},"2293":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#declare-data-for-computed-fields","content":"Use a type-level `select` for data shared by every field or authorization check on that type.\nSelections covers both forms."},"2294":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#update-queries-and-mutations","content":"For a read, `query` describes which rows to return. A field that previously spread the plugin's\nquery into `findUnique` can become:"},"2295":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#update-queries-and-mutations","content":"If you already compose a native ORM query, return it from `collection`, for example\n`collection: () => db.orm.Post.where({ published: 1 })` for published posts.\nFor writes, `resolve` receives the selected ORM query and lets you call `create`, `update`, or\n`delete` on it. See Mutations for complete examples and\nTransactions for multi-step operations."},"2296":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#update-queries-and-mutations","content":"Custom `t.field` resolvers that return database rows should use\n`collectionFromInfo` when loading those rows.\nThis loads the relations and other data the GraphQL query selects."},"2297":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#keep-your-connection-queries","content":"GraphQL clients can keep using `first`, `after`, `last` and `before`. In the schema, replace a\nconnection's `cursor` option with the order you want users to see:"},"2298":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#keep-your-connection-queries","content":"The plugin adds a unique key to break ties. See Ordering and cursors\nfor nullable columns and supported sort fields. Root connections no longer take a `resolve` callback.\nUse `collection` for checks before loading, and\nconnection helpers when a custom resolver needs the loaded rows."},"2299":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#adapt-custom-relation-fields","content":"A filtered count now takes `query: { where }`, for example\n`t.relationCount('posts', { query: { where: { published: 1 } } })`."},"2300":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/migrating-from-prisma#adapt-custom-relation-fields","content":"For a relation with custom resolver logic, use `t.field` with a `select`.\n`nestedSelection` loads the relations requested beneath that field, and\ntakes its options in one object, such as `nestedSelection({ path: ['post'] })`."},"2301":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","type":"page","content":"Objects","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma ORM 8"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects"},"2302":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","content":"Defining GraphQL types backed by contract models"},"2303":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#define-a-type","content":"Define a type"},"2304":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selections","content":"Selections"},"2305":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selecting-per-request","content":"Selecting per request"},"2306":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#resolver-data","content":"Resolver data"},"2307":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#auth-checks","content":"Auth checks"},"2308":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#mutations","content":"Mutations"},"2309":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#return-a-payload","content":"Return a payload"},"2310":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#prismafieldwithinput","content":"`prismaFieldWithInput`"},"2311":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#adding-fields","content":"Adding fields"},"2312":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","content":"`builder.prismaObject` defines a GraphQL object type for a model in your contract."},"2313":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#define-a-type","content":"Pass the model name and the usual object type options. Column types come from the contract."},"2314":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#define-a-type","content":"The GraphQL type is named after the model. Pass `name` to call it something else."},"2315":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#define-a-type","content":"To add fields to a type elsewhere, use `prismaObjectFields`. To expose the same\nmodel as another GraphQL type, define a `variant` or a\n`prismaInterface`.\nTo return these objects from a query field, use `t.prismaField`; see\nQueries and selections."},"2316":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selections","content":"Use `select` when a resolver needs columns or relations that the field doesn't expose. The plugin\nloads them with the rest of the query:"},"2317":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selections","content":"A `select` accepts:"},"2318":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selections","content":"Columns: `{ email: true }` or `['email']`."},"2319":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selections","content":"Relations: `{ posts: true }`, which loads the relation's scalar columns."},"2320":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selections","content":"Filtered or sorted relations: `{ posts: { where: { published: 1 }, orderBy: { createdAt: 'desc' }, limit: 5 } }`.\n`where` and `orderBy` take the same values as in `t.relation`'s `query`."},"2321":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selections","content":"A nested `select` for the relation's rows: `{ comments: { limit: 5, select: { post: true } } }`."},"2322":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selections","content":"`type`: a ref or model name for a variant of the related model. Only that\nvariant's rows are loaded: `{ tasks: { type: Bug } }`."},"2323":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selections","content":"Counts and aggregates of a to-many relation, in a field's `select` only:\n`{ posts: (posts) => ({ postCount: posts.count() }) }`. Each key (`postCount`) is added to the\nrow, so don't use the name of a column or relation."},"2324":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selections","content":"A to-one relation only takes `type` and `select`. Aggregates have to be read in a resolver, because\n`t.expose*` only works on columns."},"2325":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selections","content":"A model's types (the object, its variants and interfaces) can each\nhave a `select`. If two of them select the same relation, they have to use the same options,\nincluding in nested `select`s, or the schema build throws. `where` and `orderBy`\nfunctions are compared by reference, so share one function between the types. To load a relation\nwith different options, use `t.relation` or a field's `select`."},"2326":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selections","content":"To filter a to-one relation, select it and return `null` from the resolver. Here a comment shows its\npost's title only when the post is published:"},"2327":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selecting-per-request","content":"A field's `select` can also be a function of its arguments and context. Here the field selects\n`lastName` only when the client asks for the full name. A column that only some branches select is\noptional on the parent:"},"2328":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selecting-per-request","content":"With `AsyncSelections: true` in the builder's types, the function can be async."},"2329":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selecting-per-request","content":"The function's third argument, `nestedSelection`, is for custom fields that return objects. Use\nit for a relation to load what the query selects under the field. The rows only include the\nprimary key and the selected columns, so pass it a `select` for anything else the resolver reads.\nSee Indirect relations for examples."},"2330":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#selecting-per-request","content":"`select`, `query` and `where` functions are called while the plugin builds a query, not once per\nrow. They can be called more than once in a request, so they shouldn't have side effects."},"2331":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#resolver-data","content":"A resolver can read the model's primary or non-null unique key, the type's `select`, and its own\nfield's `select`. Other columns may not be loaded."},"2332":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#resolver-data","content":"The type of a row loaded by the plugin includes `PrismaPlanned<'User'>`. To return rows from custom\nresolvers, see\nReturning rows from other fields."},"2333":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#auth-checks","content":"Auth checks on the type, and field options from other plugins such as `authScopes` and\n`grantScopes`, can read:"},"2334":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#auth-checks","content":"the primary key"},"2335":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#auth-checks","content":"the columns and relations in the `select`s of the type and its interfaces"},"2336":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#auth-checks","content":"on a `t.expose*` field, the exposed column"},"2337":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#auth-checks","content":"A field's own `select` is only available in `resolve`. If a row is missing any of these, the\nplugin reloads it by its key before the check."},"2338":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#mutations","content":"For a field that writes data, use `resolve`. Its first argument is the model's Collection, set up\nto load what the GraphQL query selects. Filter it to the row you want to change, then call one of\nPrisma's write methods:"},"2339":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-37","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#mutations","content":"The mutation returns the updated row with the fields the client selected."},"2340":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-38","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#return-a-payload","content":"For a result shaped like `{ user: ... }`, use `collectionFromInfo` to load the fields requested on\n`user`. The `path: ['user']` option tells it where that object appears in the result:"},"2341":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-39","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#return-a-payload","content":"If the payload field is an interface or union, pass `typeName` to choose the GraphQL object type\nto load. For mutations on inherited models, see Model inheritance."},"2342":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-40","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#return-a-payload","content":"If you export a function that returns rows from `collectionFromInfo`, give it a return type such\nas `Promise<PrismaPlanned<'User'> | null>` so TypeScript can emit its declaration."},"2343":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-41","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#return-a-payload","content":"See Transactions to run the write and the reads in one transaction."},"2344":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-42","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#prismafieldwithinput","content":"With the with-input plugin, `t.prismaFieldWithInput` combines\n`t.prismaField` with `t.fieldWithInput`, and takes `collection`, `query` or `resolve` the same way.\nThe `input` fields become an input object argument:"},"2345":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-43","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#adding-fields","content":"To add fields to a type from another file, use `prismaObjectField` or `prismaObjectFields`.\n`builder.objectField` won't work here because it doesn't support `select`."},"2346":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects-44","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/objects#adding-fields","content":"If the resolver reads the type's `select`, pass the ref that `prismaObject` returns instead of the\nmodel name. Passing a variant's ref adds fields to that variant."},"2347":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","type":"page","content":"Queries and selections","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma ORM 8"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries"},"2348":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","content":"Combining an ORM collection with field queries and selected data"},"2349":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"Choose rows and declare field dependencies"},"2350":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#query-fields","content":"Query fields"},"2351":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#paginating-a-list","content":"Paginating a list"},"2352":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#using-a-custom-collection","content":"Using a custom Collection"},"2353":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#how-options-combine","content":"How options combine"},"2354":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#connections","content":"Connections"},"2355":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","content":"Most query fields need only a type and a `query`. Use `collection` when you want to start from a\nnative ORM query, and `select` on a custom field to declare the data its resolver reads."},"2356":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","content":"The examples use the builder and imported `db` from Setup, with `User` and `Post` models, including\n`content`, `published`, `createdAt` and `score` fields on `Post`."},"2357":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"For this query:"},"2358":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"The plugin filters published posts by author, orders them by creation date, and loads `content`\nfor `preview` and the author's `email`."},"2359":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"Piece"},"2360":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"What it is"},"2361":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"In this example"},"2362":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"`collection`"},"2363":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"An optional `(parent, args, ctx, info) => Collection` callback supplying the starting ORM query"},"2364":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"Omitted: the default Post Collection"},"2365":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"`query`"},"2366":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"Filter, ordering and pagination options, or `(args, ctx) => options`, applied to the starting Collection"},"2367":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"Published posts by the requested author, newest first"},"2368":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"Field `select`"},"2369":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"Column names, a dependency object, or `(args, ctx, nestedSelection) => dependencies`"},"2370":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"`content` when `preview` is requested"},"2371":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"GraphQL selection"},"2372":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"The fields requested by the client"},"2373":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"`preview` and the author's `email`"},"2374":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"On a connection, `args` also includes the pagination arguments. `nestedSelection` is covered in\nIndirect relations."},"2375":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#choose-rows-and-declare-field-dependencies","content":"`collection` may return a promise. On a nullable field it may return `null` to skip the query."},"2376":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#query-fields","content":"`t.prismaField` returns a model's objects and loads the data requested beneath the field. Use\n`type: ['Post']` for a list, or `type: 'Post'` for one object. `query` accepts `where`, `orderBy`,\n`limit` and `offset`, either as an object or a function of the field's arguments and context."},"2377":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#query-fields","content":"For a single post lookup:"},"2378":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#query-fields","content":"A single-row field returns the first row found and requires `query`, `collection` or `resolve`.\nA list field with no filters or pagination returns every row. Use `query.limit` or a connection\nto limit the result."},"2379":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#paginating-a-list","content":"For simple offset pagination, apply the field's arguments with `limit` and `offset`. Always include\nan `orderBy` so pages are stable:"},"2380":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#paginating-a-list","content":"For cursor-based pagination with page info and total counts, use `t.prismaConnection` instead. See\nConnections."},"2381":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#using-a-custom-collection","content":"By default rows are loaded from `orm.<Model>` on the plugin's `client`. To start from your own\nORM query, return a Collection from `collection`. Don't call `.all()`, `.first()` or `.include()`\non it. The plugin applies `query` and what the GraphQL query selects, then runs it."},"2382":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#using-a-custom-collection","content":"For writes, or anything else that runs the query itself, use `resolve` instead. It receives the\nselected Collection. See Mutations."},"2383":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#how-options-combine","content":"`query` is applied on top of the Collection returned by `collection`:"},"2384":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#how-options-combine","content":"A `where` in `query` is added to filters already on `collection`. Both must match."},"2385":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-37","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#how-options-combine","content":"An `orderBy` in `query` is appended to ordering already on `collection`. It does not override it."},"2386":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-38","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#how-options-combine","content":"Outside of connections, `query.limit` and `query.offset` set those values on the Collection. A\nsingle-row `prismaField` always limits the result to one row."},"2387":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-39","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#how-options-combine","content":"For example, if `collection` already orders posts by title and `query` orders by creation date,\ntitle stays the primary sort and creation date breaks ties. It's usually simplest to put ordering\nonly in `query.orderBy`."},"2388":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-40","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#how-options-combine","content":"A relation's `query` works the same way on the parent's related rows. A `where` on\n`t.relation('posts')` filters each user's posts, not the users."},"2389":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-41","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#connections","content":"`t.prismaConnection` handles ordering and pagination itself. Put ordering in `query.orderBy`, and\nlet `first` and `last` set the page size. A `collection` the connection can't use, such as one\nthat already has an `orderBy()` or `limit()`, is rejected by TypeScript or with an error before any\nSQL runs. See Connections for the options, checks and cursor rules."},"2390":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries-42","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/queries#connections","content":"For the full `select` syntax, shared type dependencies and resolver guarantees, see\nObject selections. Use Indirect relations when a\ncustom field returns related objects."},"2391":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","type":"page","content":"Relations","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma ORM 8"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations"},"2392":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","content":"Adding relations to Prisma-backed types"},"2393":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#nullable-relations-and-onnull","content":"Nullable relations and `onNull`"},"2394":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#filtering-and-sorting","content":"Filtering and sorting"},"2395":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#paginating-a-list","content":"Paginating a list"},"2396":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#aliases","content":"Aliases"},"2397":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#counts-and-other-aggregates","content":"Counts and other aggregates"},"2398":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#many-to-many","content":"Many-to-many"},"2399":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#returning-rows-from-other-fields","content":"Returning rows from other fields"},"2400":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","content":"Use `t.relation` to expose a relation from your contract as a field. The plugin loads it with the\nparent's query, so a user's posts come back with the user:"},"2401":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","content":"Relation fields use the builder's\ndefault field nullability, whether or not the relation\nis optional in the contract. Pass `nullable` to override."},"2402":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#nullable-relations-and-onnull","content":"A to-one relation can be `null` when it is optional in the contract, or when a variant `type`\nfilters the row out. To expose one as a non-null field, you have to set `onNull`. `'error'` makes\nGraphQL raise its usual non-null error for the field. A function can return an `Error` to throw\ninstead, or a replacement row loaded with `collectionFromInfo`. This example uses the optional\n`Bug.reviewer` relation from Model inheritance:"},"2403":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#filtering-and-sorting","content":"Pass `query` to filter, sort, or limit the related rows. It takes `{ where, orderBy, limit, offset }`,\nor a function of the field's arguments and context that returns one. `where` takes anything the\nCollection's `where()` does. `orderBy` takes columns, each with a direction, and sorts nulls last\nunless you set `nulls`, as in a connection. Here clients\nchoose the sort order:"},"2404":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#filtering-and-sorting","content":"With `AsyncSelections: true` in the builder's types, the function can be async."},"2405":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#filtering-and-sorting","content":"To sort by an expression, `orderBy` also takes the selector functions that the Collection's\n`orderBy()` takes, one or an array of them:"},"2406":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#filtering-and-sorting","content":"A to-one relation takes no `query`. To filter one, select it in a field's `select` and return `null`\nfrom the resolver."},"2407":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#paginating-a-list","content":"The offset pattern for root fields works for relations too, through\n`query`:"},"2408":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#paginating-a-list","content":"For cursor-based pagination, use `t.relatedConnection`."},"2409":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#aliases","content":"A query can select the same relation field more than once with different arguments:"},"2410":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#aliases","content":"Each alias loads its own rows in the parent's query."},"2411":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#counts-and-other-aggregates","content":"`t.relationCount` adds an `Int` field that counts a to-many relation. Pass a `query` with a `where`\nto count only some rows:"},"2412":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#counts-and-other-aggregates","content":"`query` works as it does on `t.relation`, but only takes `where`. `where` can also be a predicate:\n`query: { where: (post) => post.published.eq(1) }`."},"2413":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#counts-and-other-aggregates","content":"Count fields are always non-null. If an auth check on one fails, GraphQL sets the parent to `null`\ninstead. To hide a count from some users, use a nullable field with a `select`, as below."},"2414":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#counts-and-other-aggregates","content":"For sums and other aggregates, use a function for the relation in a field's `select`. It gets the\nrelation's Collection and returns an object of aggregates. Each key is added to the parent row:"},"2415":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#counts-and-other-aggregates","content":"Aggregate functions shouldn't have side effects."},"2416":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#many-to-many","content":"Relations defined with `rel.manyToMany({ through, from, to })` work with `t.relation`. To expose\ncolumns from the junction table, define the junction as its own model and use two relations."},"2417":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#returning-rows-from-other-fields","content":"A plain `t.field` that returns a model's type has to return rows loaded by the plugin: the\nparent, relation rows loaded by a `select`, or rows from a query built with `collectionFromInfo`.\nThese rows are typed as `PrismaPlanned<'User'>`. Returning a row you built by hand or loaded with\na plain ORM query is a type error."},"2418":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#returning-rows-from-other-fields","content":"To load a relation as one of its variants, set `type` to the variant's ref or\nmodel name. Only that variant's rows are loaded:"},"2419":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#returning-rows-from-other-fields","content":"`collectionFromInfo` loads everything the query selects under the field, including relations and\ncounts, in one query, like a `t.prismaField`. See Mutations for its `path`\nand `typeName` options:"},"2420":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#returning-rows-from-other-fields","content":"In a subscription, call it in `resolve`, which runs for each event. Rows returned from `subscribe`\nare loaded again."},"2421":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#returning-rows-from-other-fields","content":"To return a row from a field whose type is `Node` or an interface from `builder.interfaceRef()`:"},"2422":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#returning-rows-from-other-fields","content":"Brand it with the type's `addBrand`, such as `User.addBrand(row)`. Rows without a brand fail with\n`Abstract type ... must resolve`."},"2423":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relations#returning-rows-from-other-fields","content":"If you set `__typename` yourself, or use a `resolveType` that returns a type name, TypeScript\ndoesn't check that the row was loaded by the plugin."},"2424":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","type":"page","content":"Relay nodes","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma ORM 8"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay"},"2425":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","content":"Defining Relay nodes for contract-backed models"},"2426":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#define-a-node","content":"Define a node"},"2427":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#filter-node-lookups","content":"Filter node lookups"},"2428":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#several-node-types-for-one-model","content":"Several node types for one model"},"2429":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","content":"`builder.prismaNode` defines a model type that implements the Relay `Node` interface, so clients can\nfetch it by its global ID with the `node` and `nodes` query fields. You'll need the\nRelay plugin. For pagination, see Connections."},"2430":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#define-a-node","content":"`builder.prismaNode` takes the same options as `builder.prismaObject`, plus an `id`:"},"2431":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#define-a-node","content":"Any client can load a node directly by its ID through `node` and `nodes`, so add auth checks on\nthe type, such as Scope Auth's `authScopes`, for rows not everyone\nshould read. Filtering lookups with `collection` or a per-request `client`\nisn't access control."},"2432":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#define-a-node","content":"By default the ID is the column's value as a string, or a JSON array of the values when `id.field`\nlists several columns. This matches the Prisma plugin's IDs. To\nchange the format, pass `resolve` and `parse`:"},"2433":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#define-a-node","content":"`prismaNode` adds these options:"},"2434":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#define-a-node","content":"`id.field`: a column, or a list of columns, covering a non-null primary or unique key."},"2435":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#define-a-node","content":"`id.parse` / `id.resolve`: custom ID serialization. Pass both or neither."},"2436":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#define-a-node","content":"Other `id` options, such as `description`, are passed to the ID field."},"2437":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#define-a-node","content":"`collection`: the Collection nodes load from. See Filter node lookups."},"2438":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#define-a-node","content":"A timestamp in `id.field` has the same precision limit as a\nconnection's order."},"2439":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#filter-node-lookups","content":"To limit which rows `node` and `nodes` can return, pass `collection`. It receives the context, so it\ncan filter by the current user. Here `node` only finds the current user's posts:"},"2440":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#filter-node-lookups","content":"`node` and `nodes` return `null` for rows the filter excludes. Only add filters to the Collection.\nDon't call `limit()`, `offset()`, `cursor()` or `include()` on it."},"2441":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#filter-node-lookups","content":"`collection` only applies to `node` and `nodes`. Rows loaded through relations and other fields\naren't filtered by it."},"2442":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/relay#several-node-types-for-one-model","content":"If you define more than one node type for a model, such as a `DraftPost` variant of\n`Post`, each type has its own global IDs. When one field can return more than one of these types,\nreturn a `prismaInterface` with a `resolveType`, or brand each row with its type's\n`addBrand`:"},"2443":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","type":"page","content":"Setup","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma ORM 8"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup"},"2444":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","content":"Setting up the Prisma ORM 8 plugin"},"2445":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#install","content":"Install"},"2446":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#configure-the-builder","content":"Configure the builder"},"2447":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#models-in-several-namespaces","content":"Models in several namespaces"},"2448":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#plugin-options","content":"Plugin options"},"2449":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#transactions","content":"Transactions"},"2450":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#typescript-and-temporal","content":"TypeScript and Temporal"},"2451":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#limitations","content":"Limitations"},"2452":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#next-steps","content":"Next steps"},"2453":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#install","content":"Set up Prisma ORM 8 and your contract with the\nPrisma ORM documentation, then install the plugin with its Prisma\npeers:"},"2454":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#install","content":"The plugin targets Prisma ORM `8.0.0-rc.23` exactly. Install every `@prisma/orm-*` package,\nincluding the one for your database (such as `@prisma/orm-postgres`), at that version. Two copies\nof the same package won't work together. The database packages need `temporal-polyfill`. npm and\npnpm install it automatically, but with Yarn you have to add it yourself."},"2455":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#install","content":"Requires Node.js 22.20+ or 24.7+. This plugin and\n`@pothos/plugin-prisma` can't be used in the same program.\nIf you use `@pothos/plugin-scope-auth`, it needs to be 4.3.0 or newer."},"2456":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#configure-the-builder","content":"Pass your Prisma client to the plugin, and your contract's type as `PrismaContract`:"},"2457":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#configure-the-builder","content":"Later examples pass the `User` and `Post` refs as a field's `type`. A model name such as `'Post'`\nworks too. Later pages add more fields to these types, which you can also do with\n`prismaObjectFields`."},"2458":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#configure-the-builder","content":"For nodes and connections, also install and add `@pothos/plugin-relay`."},"2459":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#configure-the-builder","content":"This example uses PostgreSQL:"},"2460":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#configure-the-builder","content":"The client groups models by schema (`db.orm.public.Post`). The plugin chooses that Collection\nautomatically for the field above."},"2461":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#configure-the-builder","content":"SQLite and MySQL work the same way with their own Prisma packages. Their clients don't group\nmodels, and the rest of these docs use that shorter form, `db.orm.Post`."},"2462":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#configure-the-builder","content":"Pass a context object, such as `contextValue: {}`, when you execute an operation. The plugin keeps\nper-request state on it.\nExamples using authentication assume context fields `userId: string` and `isAdmin: boolean`. Add\n`Context: YourContext` alongside `PrismaContract` in the builder's type parameter, and supply those\nvalues for each request. See Context for configuring and typing it."},"2463":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#models-in-several-namespaces","content":"If your contract has more than one namespace, refer to models by their full name, such as\n`'auth.User'`. GraphQL type names leave the namespace out, so when two namespaces both have a\n`User`, give one of them a `name`:"},"2464":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#plugin-options","content":"These go under `prisma` in the builder options."},"2465":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#plugin-options","content":"`client`: your Prisma client. Required. For a request-scoped client,\npass `(ctx) => client` and set `context` to the base client's `context`."},"2466":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#plugin-options","content":"`skipDeferredFragments`: load `@defer` fragments when they execute instead of in the initial\nquery. Defaults to `true`."},"2467":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#plugin-options","content":"`defaultConnectionSize` / `maxConnectionSize`: default and maximum page sizes. Default to 20 and\n100\\."},"2468":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#plugin-options","content":"`filterConnectionTotalCount`: when `true` (the default), a `relatedConnection`'s `totalCount`\nonly counts rows matching its `query.where`."},"2469":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#transactions","content":"For multi-step mutations and request-scoped clients, see the Transactions guide."},"2470":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#typescript-and-temporal","content":"If you use Temporal columns and declaration emit fails with TS2742 or TS2883 mentioning\n`temporal-spec`, add it as a direct dependency:"},"2471":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#typescript-and-temporal","content":"If TypeScript reports missing `Temporal` globals, import their declarations once in your project\n(for example in a `globals.d.ts` file):"},"2472":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#typescript-and-temporal","content":"This only adds types. You still need Temporal at runtime, natively or from a polyfill."},"2473":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#limitations","content":"On MySQL, Prisma reads rows but can't write them, count them or run aggregates yet. MongoDB isn't\nsupported."},"2474":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#limitations","content":"Clients see `Invalid ID.` or `Invalid cursor.` for a malformed ID or cursor, and the Relay\nplugin's `Invalid global ID: …` for an ID that isn't a global ID. If the plugin finds a problem\nin your configuration while running a request, the field fails with `Unexpected error.`. The\noriginal error is on `error.cause`. Errors from the ORM and from your code are not changed and\ncan include model and column names, so mask them in your GraphQL server."},"2475":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#limitations","content":"Prisma's packages are ESM-only, so use `\"moduleResolution\": \"nodenext\"` or `\"bundler\"`. A\nCommonJS project on `node16` gets TS1541 errors unless `skipLibCheck` is on."},"2476":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#limitations","content":"graphql-jit isn't supported. Fields that load rows fail with `Unexpected error.` under it."},"2477":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/setup#next-steps","content":"Read Queries and selections for how `collection`, `query` and `select` work together,\nthen Objects and Relations for defining the rest of your schema."},"2478":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","type":"page","content":"Transactions","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma ORM 8"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions"},"2479":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","content":"Running mutations and GraphQL operations in a Prisma transaction"},"2480":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions#write-several-rows-atomically","content":"Write several rows atomically"},"2481":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions#share-a-transaction-across-an-operation","content":"Share a transaction across an operation"},"2482":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","content":"Use Prisma's `db.transaction` when several writes must succeed or fail together.\nThe examples use the imported `db` and builder from Setup, and the `User` ref from your\nschema. They use the shorter SQLite client paths. On PostgreSQL, use `db.orm.public.User` and\n`tx.orm.public.User` for models in the public schema."},"2483":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions#write-several-rows-atomically","content":"Pothos doesn't create transactions for you. To run a mutation's writes in one, call `transaction`\nin the mutation field, then load the result once it commits:"},"2484":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions#write-several-rows-atomically","content":"To read the result inside the transaction too, build the last write with\n`collectionFromInfo` and return the row it writes:"},"2485":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions#write-several-rows-atomically","content":"Don't return a `tx.orm` Collection from the callback. It runs after the transaction ends, and\nfails."},"2486":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions#share-a-transaction-across-an-operation","content":"To give each request its own client, such as a transaction, pass a function as `client` along with\nthe base client's `context`:"},"2487":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions#share-a-transaction-across-an-operation","content":"The plugin uses this client for the queries it starts itself. In your own `collection` and\n`resolve` callbacks, use `ctx.db` too. Calls to the imported `db` use the base client and run\noutside this transaction."},"2488":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions#share-a-transaction-across-an-operation","content":"Call `graphql()` inside the transaction and pass `tx` in the GraphQL context:"},"2489":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/transactions#share-a-transaction-across-an-operation","content":"With `@defer` or `@stream`, read every incremental result inside the callback. They load after\nthe initial result, and fail once the transaction has ended."},"2490":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants","type":"page","content":"Type variants","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma ORM 8"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants"},"2491":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants","content":"Multiple GraphQL types backed by the same contract model"},"2492":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants#return-a-variant","content":"Return a variant"},"2493":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants#switch-to-another-variant","content":"Switch to another variant"},"2494":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants#adding-fields","content":"Adding fields"},"2495":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants","content":"Use `variant` to expose the same Prisma model as another GraphQL type. For example, a `Viewer`\ntype can show signed-in users their own email and drafts, which the public `User` type leaves out:"},"2496":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants#return-a-variant","content":"Wherever you use the model name, such as `'User'`, you get the default type. To return the variant,\npass its ref as `type`, for example from a root field:"},"2497":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants#return-a-variant","content":"`t.relation` takes a variant's ref as `type` the same way."},"2498":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants#switch-to-another-variant","content":"`t.variant` adds a field that returns the same row as another variant, without another query. To\nreturn `null` for some rows, pass `isNull`, which needs a nullable field. `isNull` can read the\nprimary key, the type's `select`, and the columns in the field's `select`. Here `User.viewer`\nreturns the `Viewer` type only for the signed-in user:"},"2499":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants#adding-fields","content":"To add fields to a variant, pass its ref to `prismaObjectField` or `prismaObjectFields`:"},"2500":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma-next/variants#adding-fields","content":"To return either `User` or `Viewer` from one field, give them a shared\nGraphQL interface."},"2501":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","type":"page","content":"Connections","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections"},"2502":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","content":"Creating relay connections with the Prisma plugin"},"2503":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#prismaconnection","content":"`prismaConnection`"},"2504":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"options"},"2505":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#relatedconnection","content":"`relatedConnection`"},"2506":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options-1","content":"options"},"2507":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#indirect-relations-as-connections","content":"Indirect relations as connections"},"2508":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#sharing-connections-objects","content":"Sharing Connections objects"},"2509":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#extending-connection-edges","content":"Extending connection edges"},"2510":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#total-count-on-shared-connection-objects","content":"Total count on shared connection objects"},"2511":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#parseprismacursor-and-formatprismacursor","content":"`parsePrismaCursor` and `formatPrismaCursor`"},"2512":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#page-through-published-posts","content":"Page through published posts"},"2513":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","content":"These examples use the Prisma builder setup with the Relay plugin.\nRegister the Prisma object types used by each field. Later examples replace earlier definitions of\nthe same field or helper."},"2514":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#prismaconnection","content":"The `prismaConnection` method on a field builder can be used to create a relay `connection` field\nthat also pre-loads all the data nested inside that connection."},"2515":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"`type`: the name of the prisma model being connected to"},"2516":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"`cursor`: a `@unique` column of the model being connected to. This is used as the `cursor` option\npassed to prisma. If the column is nullable, paging from a row where it is null fails."},"2517":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"`defaultSize`: (default: 20) The default page size to use if `first` and `last` are not provided."},"2518":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"`maxSize`: (default: 100) The maximum number of nodes returned for a connection."},"2519":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"`resolve`: Like the resolver for `prismaField`, the first argument is a `query` object that should\nbe spread into your prisma query. The `resolve` function should return an array of nodes for the\nconnection. The `query` will contain the correct `take`, `skip`, and `cursor` options based on the\nconnection arguments (`before`, `after`, `first`, `last`), along with `include` options for nested\nselections. The `orderBy` you pass to prisma should end in a unique column, or pages read\nbackward with `last` may not match the pages read forward."},"2520":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"`totalCount`: A function for loading the total count for the connection. This will add a\n`totalCount` field to the connection object. The `totalCount` method will receive (`connection`,\n`args`, `context`, `info`) as arguments. Note that this will not work when using a shared\nconnection object (see details below)"},"2521":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"The created connection queries currently support the following combinations of connection arguments:"},"2522":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"`first`, `last`, `before`, or `after` on their own"},"2523":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"`first` and `after`"},"2524":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"`last` and `before`"},"2525":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"The following combinations are not supported:"},"2526":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"`before` and `after`"},"2527":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"`first` and `before`"},"2528":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"`last` and `after`"},"2529":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"Queries for these combinations are not as useful, and generally requiring loading all records\nbetween 2 cursors, or between a cursor and the end of the set. Generating query options for these\ncases is more complex and likely very inefficient, so they will currently throw an Error indicating\nthe argument combinations are not supported."},"2530":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"Paging from the cursor of a row that has since been deleted returns an empty page rather than an\nerror."},"2531":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options","content":"The `maxSize` and `defaultSize` can also be configured globally using `maxConnectionSize` and\n`defaultConnectionSize` options in the `prisma` plugin options."},"2532":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#relatedconnection","content":"The `relatedConnection` method can be used to create a relay `connection` field based on a relation\nof the current model."},"2533":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options-1","content":"`cursor`: a `@unique` column of the model being connected to. This is used as the `cursor` option\npassed to prisma."},"2534":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options-1","content":"`defaultSize`: (default: 20) The default page size to use if `first` and `last` are not provided."},"2535":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options-1","content":"`maxSize`: (default: 100) The maximum number of nodes returned for a connection."},"2536":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options-1","content":"`query`: A method that accepts the `args` and `context` for the connection field, and returns\nfiltering and sorting logic that will be merged into the query for the relation. If the\n`orderBy` is not unique, the plugin also sorts by the cursor's fields last, in the direction of\nthe last `orderBy` entry, so tied rows keep the same order on every page. End `orderBy` with a\nunique column to choose that order yourself."},"2537":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#options-1","content":"`totalCount`: when set to true, this will add a `totalCount` field to the connection object. see\n`relationCount` for more details. Note that this will not work when\nusing a shared connection object (see details below)"},"2538":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#indirect-relations-as-connections","content":"`prismaConnectionHelpers` connects join rows to a different GraphQL node type. The example uses\nPost.media → PostMedia.media → Media: pagination follows attachment IDs, while each node is an\nimage. A caption belongs to the attachment, so the same image can have different captions on two\nposts. Import the helper from `@pothos/plugin-prisma` and register Post and Media first."},"2539":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-37","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#indirect-relations-as-connections","content":"`select` adds the join data used by the edge and plans the Media fields requested beneath `node`.\n`resolveNode` maps each attachment to its selected image. The helper also accepts `defaultSize`\nand `maxSize` (defaults 20 and 100). Selecting the node ID also keeps the Prisma selection nonempty\nwhen the operation requests only an edge caption or connection count. Other default node fields\ncan be added through the same `nodeSelection` argument."},"2540":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-38","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#indirect-relations-as-connections","content":"Use `t.connection` to expose the result. It does not load the relation automatically: its field\nselection includes `getQuery`, and its resolver passes the loaded attachments to the helper:"},"2541":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-39","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#indirect-relations-as-connections","content":"The parent selection counts attachments separately from the page. The second configuration\nargument adds `totalCount` to the connection; the third adds fields to its edges. A page containing\none attachment can therefore report two total attachments."},"2542":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-40","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#indirect-relations-as-connections","content":"Both posts return the same first image, but with different captions. “Starting a seed library”\nhas two attachments and a next page; the cursor advances through PostMedia rows, not Media IDs.\nThe second page contains the seed-packets image. An attachment-free post has an empty edge list\nand a zero count. The local example includes this operation."},"2543":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-41","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#indirect-relations-as-connections","content":"`prismaConnectionHelpers` can also be used to manually create a connection where the edge and\nconnections share the same model, and pagination happens directly on a relation to nodes type (even\nif that relation is nested)."},"2544":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-42","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#indirect-relations-as-connections","content":"To add arguments for a connection defined with a helper, it is often easiest to define the arguments\non the connection field rather than the connection helper. This allows connection helpers to be\nshared between fields that may not share the same arguments:"},"2545":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-43","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#indirect-relations-as-connections","content":"Arguments, ordering and filtering can also be defined on the helpers themselves:"},"2546":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-44","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#indirect-relations-as-connections","content":"If the `orderBy` in the helper's `query` is not unique, the plugin also sorts by the cursor's\nfields last, like `relatedConnection`, and `getQuery` returns `orderBy` as an array. If you set\n`orderBy` after spreading `getQuery`, as in the earlier example, the plugin does not change it. It\nshould end in a unique column."},"2547":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-45","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#sharing-connections-objects","content":"You can create reusable connection objects by using `builder.connectionObject`."},"2548":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-46","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#sharing-connections-objects","content":"These connection objects can be used with `t.prismaConnection`, `t.relatedConnection`, or\n`t.connection`"},"2549":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-47","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#sharing-connections-objects","content":"Shared edges can also be created using `builder.edgeObject`"},"2550":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-48","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#extending-connection-edges","content":"Edge fields can read data from the row paginated by the helper. In the attachment connection,\n`select` loads `caption` from PostMedia and the third `t.connection` argument exposes it:"},"2551":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-49","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#extending-connection-edges","content":"The edge parent is inferred from the helper's resolved rows. Its caption describes the attachment;\n`node.url` describes the shared Media record."},"2552":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-50","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#extending-connection-edges","content":"For a timestamp on the join model instead, select `createdAt: true` in the helper and add this\nedge field. This alternative requires a `PostMedia.createdAt` column and a registered `DateTime`\nscalar whose output is `Date`:"},"2553":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-51","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#total-count-on-shared-connection-objects","content":"If you set the `totalCount: true` on a `prismaConnection` or `relatedConnection` field, and are\nusing a custom connection object, you will need to add the `totalCount` field to the\nconnection object manually. The parent object on the connection will have a `totalCount` property\nthat is either the totalCount, or a function that will return the totalCount."},"2554":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-52","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#total-count-on-shared-connection-objects","content":"If you want to add a global `totalCount` field, you can do something similar using\n`builder.globalConnectionField`:"},"2555":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-53","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#parseprismacursor-and-formatprismacursor","content":"These functions can be used to manually parse and format cursors that are compatible with prisma\nconnections."},"2556":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-54","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#parseprismacursor-and-formatprismacursor","content":"Parsing a cursor will return the value from the column used for the cursor (often the `id`), this\nvalue may be an array or object when a compound index is used as the cursor. Similarly, to format a\ncursor, you must provide the column(s) that make up the cursor."},"2557":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-55","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#page-through-published-posts","content":"These queries use `nodes` on connections. Enable `relay: { nodesOnConnection: true }` in the\nbuilder options, or select `edges { node { title } }` with the default Relay configuration."},"2558":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-56","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#page-through-published-posts","content":"A public post connection applies the same `published` filter as author pages. The publishing\nschema uses this root connection alongside its private viewer:"},"2559":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections-57","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/connections#page-through-published-posts","content":"There are three published posts and two drafts in the seed data. The first page contains two\npublished posts and has a next page. Passing its `endCursor` as `after` returns the remaining\npublished post. The related author connection uses the same filter for its nodes and `totalCount`,\nso Maya's count is two, including when the client requests only the count."},"2560":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma","type":"page","content":"Prisma plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma"},"2561":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma","content":"Prisma plugin docs for Pothos"},"2562":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma#example","content":"Example"},"2563":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma","content":"This plugin only supports Prisma ORM 7 (`prisma@^7`). For Prisma ORM 8, see the\nPrisma ORM 8 plugin."},"2564":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma","content":"The Prisma plugin defines GraphQL types from Prisma models and builds selections for the data\nrequested by a GraphQL query. It supports relations, counts, type variants, and Relay nodes and\nconnections. GraphQL fields can have different names and shapes from the underlying models."},"2565":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma","content":"You can also use Prisma with plain object refs."},"2566":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma","content":"The guides also use a publishing API with public author pages, private drafts, and shared media.\nIts local setup runs the real Prisma client against a temporary\nSQLite database. Prisma Utils filters and mutations reuse the same schema and data."},"2567":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma#example","content":"This example exposes users and their posts. See Objects, Relations, and Connections.\nUse the builder setup, including its `userId` context, and add the\nRelay plugin for nodes and connections. The Prisma schema has User, Post, and Profile\nmodels with the fields and relations used below."},"2568":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma#example","content":"Given this schema, you would be able to resolve a query like the following with a single prisma\nquery (which will still result in a few optimized SQL queries)."},"2569":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma#example","content":"A query like"},"2570":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma#example","content":"Will result in 2 calls to prisma, one to resolve everything except `oldPosts`, and a second to\nresolve everything inside `oldPosts`. Prisma can only resolve each relation once in a single query,\nso we need a separate query to handle the second `posts` relation."},"2571":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","type":"page","content":"Indirect relations","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations"},"2572":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","content":"Indirect relations and join tables"},"2573":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#selecting-fields-from-a-nested-graphql-field","content":"Selecting fields from a nested GraphQL field"},"2574":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#pinning-a-type-in-the-path","content":"Pinning a type in the path"},"2575":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#selecting-as-a-specific-type","content":"Selecting as a specific type"},"2576":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#indirect-relations-eg-join-tables","content":"Indirect relations (eg. Join tables)"},"2577":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#shared-media-on-published-posts","content":"Shared media on published posts"},"2578":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#selecting-fields-from-a-nested-graphql-field","content":"By default, the `nestedSelection` function will return selections based on the type of the current\nfield. `nestedSelection` can also be used to get a selection from a field nested deeper inside other\nfields. This is useful if the field returns a type that is not a `prismaObject`, but a field nested\ninside the returned type is."},"2579":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#selecting-fields-from-a-nested-graphql-field","content":"`nestedSelection` returns the relation query for the type it selected, which for a `Post` is\n`{ select?, include?, where?, orderBy?, take?, skip?, cursor? }`. Any keys you pass in are kept as\nthey were given, so a `select` passed to `nestedSelection` will still narrow the parent's shape.\nWith no argument, or with `true`, it returns the planned selection on its own."},"2580":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#pinning-a-type-in-the-path","content":"The `path` is followed through fragments, so a segment is found whether the field is selected\ndirectly, or under a fragment on an implementation of the field's type. When several\nimplementations share the same field name, a segment can be written as `{ name, type }` to name the\nimplementation the field must be found under. Only selections of that field under a fragment on\nthat type, or one of its subtypes, will be planned:"},"2581":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#pinning-a-type-in-the-path","content":"The same segments can be used in `queryFromInfo`'s `path` and `paths` options. The type is exported\nas `PathSegment`."},"2582":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#selecting-as-a-specific-type","content":"When the field returns an interface or union, the third argument names the object type the\nselection should be read as. Its type-level selection and the fields selected under a fragment on\nit are planned, and fragments on other types are left out. With an empty path, this applies to the\nfield's own return type:"},"2583":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#indirect-relations-eg-join-tables","content":"If you want to define a GraphQL field that directly exposes data from a nested relationship (many to\nmany relations using a custom join table is a common example of this) you can use the\n`nestedSelection` function passed to `select`."},"2584":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#indirect-relations-eg-join-tables","content":"Given a prisma schema like the following:"},"2585":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#indirect-relations-eg-join-tables","content":"You can define a media field that can pre-load the correct relations based on the graphql query:"},"2586":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#shared-media-on-published-posts","content":"The publishing schema stores attachment rows in PostMedia, but clients request Media objects.\n`nestedSelection` follows the media field's selection through that join, including the uploader\nwhen requested:"},"2587":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/indirect-relations#shared-media-on-published-posts","content":"Both “Starting a seed library” and “A guide to composting” attach the same image, uploaded by Leo.\nQuerying `posts { title media { url uploadedBy { name } } }` through Maya's author page returns\nthat uploader for both posts. The GraphQL shape need not expose the join table just because the\ndatabase uses one."},"2588":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","type":"page","content":"Interfaces","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces"},"2589":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","content":"Creating interfaces for prisma models that can be shared by variants"},"2590":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces#selecting-a-viewer-implementation","content":"Selecting a viewer implementation"},"2591":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","content":"`builder.prismaInterface` works just like builder.prismaObject and can be used to define either the\nprimary type or a variant for a model."},"2592":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","content":"The following example creates a `User` interface, and 2 variants Admin and Member. The `resolveType`\nmethod returns the typenames as strings to avoid issues with circular references."},"2593":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","content":"When using select mode, it's recommended to add selections to both the interface and the object\ntypes that implement them. Selections are not inherited and will fallback to the default selection\nwhich includes all scalar columns."},"2594":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","content":"You will not be able to extend an interface for a different prisma model, doing so will result in an\nerror at build time."},"2595":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces#selecting-a-viewer-implementation","content":"The writing desk uses a Viewer interface for the signed-in\nauthor and two object variants for account capabilities. Its type-level selection includes the\ndiscriminator (`isAdmin`) used by `resolveType`. Each implementation also selects the fields\nrequired by that interface; configuring a selection on the interface does not replace the\nimplementation's selection."},"2596":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/interfaces#selecting-a-viewer-implementation","content":"The editor result includes `canReviewSubmissions: true`. An author result has no field from that\nfragment, while retaining the interface's `drafts` field. This keeps the public User type separate\nfrom account-specific capabilities."},"2597":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","type":"page","content":"Prisma Objects","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects"},"2598":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","content":"Prisma plugin docs for Pothos"},"2599":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#creating-types-with-builderprismaobject","content":"Creating types with `builder.prismaObject`"},"2600":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#adding-prisma-fields-to-non-prisma-objects-including-query-and-mutation","content":"Adding prisma fields to non-prisma objects (including Query and Mutation)"},"2601":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#prismafieldwithinput","content":"`prismaFieldWithInput`"},"2602":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#extending-prisma-objects","content":"Extending prisma objects"},"2603":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#a-nullable-author-lookup","content":"A nullable author lookup"},"2604":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#creating-types-with-builderprismaobject","content":"`builder.prismaObject` takes 2 arguments:"},"2605":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#creating-types-with-builderprismaobject","content":"`name`: The name of the prisma model this new type represents"},"2606":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#creating-types-with-builderprismaobject","content":"`options`: options for the type being created, this is very similar to the options for any other\nobject type"},"2607":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#creating-types-with-builderprismaobject","content":"So far, this is just creating some simple object types. They work just like any other object type in\nPothos. The main advantage of this is that we get the type information without using object refs, or\nneeding imports from prisma client."},"2608":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#adding-prisma-fields-to-non-prisma-objects-including-query-and-mutation","content":"`t.prismaField` defines fields that return Prisma objects. This example uses the client and\n`userId` context from Setup:"},"2609":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#adding-prisma-fields-to-non-prisma-objects-including-query-and-mutation","content":"This method works just like the normal `t.field` method with a couple of differences:"},"2610":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#adding-prisma-fields-to-non-prisma-objects-including-query-and-mutation","content":"The `type` option must contain the name of the prisma model (eg. `User` or `[User]` for a list\nfield)."},"2611":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#adding-prisma-fields-to-non-prisma-objects-including-query-and-mutation","content":"The `resolve` function has a new first argument `query` which should be spread into your prisma\nquery. This will be used to load data for nested relationships."},"2612":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#adding-prisma-fields-to-non-prisma-objects-including-query-and-mutation","content":"You do not need to use this method, and the `builder.prismaObject` method returns an object ref that\ncan be used like any other object ref (with `t.field`), but using `t.prismaField` will allow you to\ntake advantage of more efficient queries."},"2613":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#adding-prisma-fields-to-non-prisma-objects-including-query-and-mutation","content":"The `query` object will contain an object with `include` or `select` options to pre-load data needed\nto resolve nested parts of the current query. The included/selected fields are based on which fields\nare being queried, and the options provided when defining those fields and types."},"2614":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#prismafieldwithinput","content":"With the with-input plugin,\n`t.prismaFieldWithInput` combines `t.prismaField` with `t.fieldWithInput`. The `input` fields become\nan input object argument, and the resolver still receives the `query` to spread as its first\nargument."},"2615":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#extending-prisma-objects","content":"The normal `builder.objectField(s)` methods can be used to extend prisma objects, but do not support\nusing selections, or exposing fields not in the default selection. To use these features, you can\nuse"},"2616":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#extending-prisma-objects","content":"`builder.prismaObjectField` or `builder.prismaObjectFields` instead."},"2617":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#a-nullable-author-lookup","content":"An author page should return null when the requested author does not exist. A `t.prismaField`\npasses the requested selections into `findUnique`; its GraphQL nullability matches that method's\nresult. This field uses the publishing schema described in Setup:"},"2618":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/objects#a-nullable-author-lookup","content":"`author(id: 1) { name }` returns Maya Chen. `author(id: 999) { name }` returns null.\nThe type's relations can be requested through the same field without changing the resolver."},"2619":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"page","content":"Prisma Utils","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils"},"2620":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","content":"Prisma utils for creating input types"},"2621":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#setup","content":"Setup"},"2622":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#what-can-you-do-with-this-plugin","content":"What can you do with this plugin"},"2623":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#what-is-supported-so-far","content":"What is supported so far"},"2624":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#creating-filter-types-for-scalars-and-enums","content":"Creating filter types for scalars and enums"},"2625":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#creating-filters-for-prisma-objects-compatible-with-a-where-clause","content":"Creating filters for Prisma objects (compatible with a \"where\" clause)"},"2626":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#creating-list-filters-for-scalars","content":"Creating list filters for scalars"},"2627":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#creating-list-filters-for-prisma-objects","content":"Creating list filters for Prisma objects"},"2628":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#creating-orderby-input-types","content":"Creating OrderBy input types"},"2629":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#inputs-for-create-mutations","content":"Inputs for create mutations"},"2630":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#inputs-for-update-mutations","content":"Inputs for update mutations"},"2631":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#atomic-int-update-operations","content":"Atomic Int Update operations"},"2632":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#generators","content":"Generators"},"2633":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#static-generator","content":"Static generator"},"2634":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#dynamic-generator","content":"Dynamic generator"},"2635":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#filters-and-draft-inputs-in-one-api","content":"Filters and draft inputs in one API"},"2636":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","content":"This package is highly experimental and not recommended for production use"},"2637":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","content":"The plugin adds new helpers for creating prisma compatible input types. Use it alongside the Prisma plugin when you want these input helpers."},"2638":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#setup","content":"To use this plugin, you will need to enable prismaUtils option in the generator in your\nschema.prisma:"},"2639":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#setup","content":"Once this is enabled, add the plugin alongside the Prisma plugin. This example also uses\n`graphql-scalars` to register the `DateTime` scalar used by the input helpers:"},"2640":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#what-can-you-do-with-this-plugin","content":"The helpers build filters, ordering, create inputs, and update inputs using the generated Prisma\ntypes. You choose which fields and operations your API exposes."},"2641":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#inputs-for-create-mutations","content":"You can use `builder.prismaCreate` to create input types for create mutations."},"2642":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#inputs-for-create-mutations","content":"To get these types to work correctly for circular references, it is recommended to add explicit type\nannotations, but for simple types that do not have circular references the explicit types can be\nomitted."},"2643":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#inputs-for-update-mutations","content":"You can use `builder.prismaUpdate` to create input types for update mutations."},"2644":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#inputs-for-update-mutations","content":"To get these types to work correctly for circular references, it is recommended to add explicit type\nannotations, but for simple types that do not have circular references the explicit types can be\nomitted."},"2645":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#generators","content":"Manually defining all the different input types shown above for a large number of tables can become\nvery repetitive. These utilities are designed to be building blocks for generators or utility\nfunctions, so that you don't need to hand write these types yourself."},"2646":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#generators","content":"Pothos does not currently ship an official generator for prisma types, but there are a couple of\nexample generators that can be copied and modified to suit your needs. These are intentionally\nsomewhat limited in functionality and not written to be easily exported because they will be updated\nwith breaking changes as these utilities are developed further. They are only intended as building\nblocks for you to build your own generators."},"2647":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#generators","content":"There are 2 main approaches:"},"2648":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#generators","content":"Static Generation: Types are generated and written as a typescript file which can be imported\nfrom as part of your schema"},"2649":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#generators","content":"Dynamic Generation: Types are generated dynamically at runtime through helpers imported from your\nApp"},"2650":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#static-generator","content":"You can find an\nexample static generator here"},"2651":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#static-generator","content":"This generator will generate a file with input types for every table in your schema as shown\nhere"},"2652":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#static-generator","content":"These generated types can be used in your schema as shown\nhere"},"2653":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#dynamic-generator","content":"You can find an example\ndynamic generator here"},"2654":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#dynamic-generator","content":"This generator exports a class that can be used to dynamically create input types for your builder\nas shown\nhere"},"2655":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#filters-and-draft-inputs-in-one-api","content":"The publishing schema uses these helpers to expose a small, intentional input surface. A title\nfilter and ordering input support public post search:"},"2656":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#filters-and-draft-inputs-in-one-api","content":"The `searchPosts` resolver combines the caller's filter with `published: true` using `AND`.\nA caller can narrow the public result set, but cannot expand it to drafts. The selected ordering\nalso gets an ID tie breaker for deterministic results."},"2657":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-37","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#filters-and-draft-inputs-in-one-api","content":"Draft creation and editing expose only title and content. The resolver supplies ownership from\nthe authenticated context and keeps publication state outside the input:"},"2658":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-38","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#filters-and-draft-inputs-in-one-api","content":"Creating “Mulching paths” returns an unpublished post owned by the current author, including its\nrequested author relation. Updating another author's draft fails and leaves its title unchanged.\n`createDraftWithPayload` returns a post inside a result object, so `queryFromInfo` uses `path: ['post']` to plan the nested selection. These are application decisions; input generation does not\nprovide authorization by itself."},"2659":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils-39","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/prisma-utils#filters-and-draft-inputs-in-one-api","content":"The local setup generates Prisma Utils types alongside the client\nand runs these operations against SQLite. Scalar-list filters remain an alternative for databases\nthat support Prisma scalar lists."},"2660":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","type":"page","content":"Query planning","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning"},"2661":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","content":"How the plugin turns a GraphQL query into prisma queries"},"2662":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#how-fields-get-their-data","content":"How fields get their data"},"2663":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#async-selections","content":"Async selections"},"2664":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#optimized-queries-without-tprismafield","content":"Optimized queries without `t.prismaField`"},"2665":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#conflicting-selections-between-variants","content":"Conflicting selections between variants"},"2666":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#compare-two-orderings-of-one-relation","content":"Compare two orderings of one relation"},"2667":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","content":"This page describes how the plugin turns a GraphQL query into prisma queries, which is worth\nknowing when a schema issues more queries than you expect."},"2668":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#how-fields-get-their-data","content":"A field either reads its data from a row that has already been loaded, or runs a query of its own."},"2669":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#how-fields-get-their-data","content":"A field's `select` is planned into the query of the nearest ancestor that runs one: a\n`t.prismaField`, a `t.relation`, a connection, or a\nfallback query. The field then reads what it needs off the loaded\nrow, without a query of its own. A field runs its own query when its `resolve` queries prisma\ndirectly, and when the plugin issues a fallback query for a `t.relation` that is missing from the\nrow."},"2670":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#how-fields-get-their-data","content":"A field can do both. A `t.prismaField`, or any other field with a `select`, nested under one of\nthose ancestors has its `select` planned into the parent's row, and still runs its own query when\nit resolves."},"2671":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#how-fields-get-their-data","content":"A field-level `select` is merged into the same query as its siblings and the type-level selection,\nrather than getting a copy of the row for that field alone. Two selections of the same relation\nshare a place in that query only when their arguments (`where`, `orderBy`, `take`, ...) match. When\nthey differ, the first one planned wins, and the other is loaded with a query of its own. A\ntype-level `select` or `include` is planned before any field's selection, no matter where they\nappear in the document, and fields are planned in the order they are selected."},"2672":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#async-selections","content":"Selections are synchronous unless the schema opts in with `AsyncSelections: true`. This example\nuses request context methods that asynchronously return a user ID and a preview limit:"},"2673":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#async-selections","content":"With the opt-in, `select` functions, relation `query` callbacks, `relationCount` `where` callbacks,\nand the `select` and `query` callbacks of `prismaConnectionHelpers` may be async. Without it they\nare typed as synchronous, and an async callback is a type error."},"2674":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#async-selections","content":"The plugin still builds a single query. It waits for the callbacks, and merges what they return\nafter every synchronous selection, in document order. `t.relation`, `t.relationCount`,\n`t.prismaField`, `t.prismaConnection` and `t.relatedConnection` settle their plan before the\nresolver runs, and need no changes. The following example uses a registered `Comment` Prisma\nobject ref and its Post.comments relation:"},"2675":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#async-selections","content":"`await` what `nestedSelection` returns before putting it in the selection. A selection that\ncontains the promise itself will throw, and so will a `select` that returns while a nested\nselection it started is still pending. Calling `nestedSelection` and discarding a synchronous\nresult is not detected, and the nested selection will not be loaded with the parent, so the field\nfalls back to its own query."},"2676":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#async-selections","content":"Pass `awaitSelections: true` to `queryFromInfo` and `prismaConnectionHelpers(...).getQuery`, and\n`await` the query they return. Without it, an async selection beneath the field throws, and\nwhether there is one depends on the incoming document rather than on the callback you wrote. A\nconnection helper also throws when its own `select` or `query` is async, whatever the document\nasked for:"},"2677":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#async-selections","content":"`awaitSelections` is a per-call option, and is available whether or not the schema sets\n`AsyncSelections`."},"2678":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#optimized-queries-without-tprismafield","content":"In some cases, it may be useful to get an optimized query for fields where you can't use\n`t.prismaField`."},"2679":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#optimized-queries-without-tprismafield","content":"This may be required for combining with other plugins, or because your query does not directly\nreturn a `PrismaObject`. In these cases, you can use the `queryFromInfo` helper. An example of this\nmight be a mutation that wraps the prisma object in a result type."},"2680":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#optimized-queries-without-tprismafield","content":"The example assumes a Post model with `id`, `title`, and `authorId` columns, an existing User\nrecord for `context.userId`, and the builder setup with that context type."},"2681":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#optimized-queries-without-tprismafield","content":"The columns and relations the query selected come back on the rows, along with anything you passed\nin as `select`, and the rows are typed to match."},"2682":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#optimized-queries-without-tprismafield","content":"To require data even when the client does not request it, pass an initial `select` (or `include`)\nto `queryFromInfo`. For example, add `select: { id: true }` beside `path` in the call above to\nensure the returned post includes its ID. When nothing is selected at `path`, the helper returns\nthat initial selection unchanged, or an empty query object if no initial selection was supplied."},"2683":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#optimized-queries-without-tprismafield","content":"The `path` is followed through fragments in the query, including inline fragments and fragment\nspreads that narrow an interface or union to one of its implementations. Every selection of the\nfield that is found is merged into the query. If several implementations share a field name,\nmatches whose field returns a different Prisma model are ignored. When you need to target a\nspecific implementation, a segment can be written as `{ name, type }`. The field then only matches\nwhen it is selected directly, or under a fragment on that type or one of its subtypes:"},"2684":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#conflicting-selections-between-variants","content":"When a query selects two variants of one model for the same row, either with a fragment on each\nunder one field, or through a `t.variant` field, the request fails if their type-level\n`select`/`include` ask for the same relation with different arguments. The client sees\n`Unexpected error.`. The original error is on `error.cause`:"},"2685":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#conflicting-selections-between-variants","content":"Both variants describe one row, so their type-level selections are merged into a single query."},"2686":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#conflicting-selections-between-variants","content":"To fix this, keep the relation with its arguments in the `select` of the field that needs it, on\none of the variants. A field-level selection that conflicts with what the row already holds falls\nback to a query of its own, rather than failing the request."},"2687":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#compare-two-orderings-of-one-relation","content":"A client may need both the newest and oldest published posts on one author page:"},"2688":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#compare-two-orderings-of-one-relation","content":"The published-posts field translates those arguments\ninto different database orderings. Both results must retain their own order: “A guide to\ncomposting” comes first in `newest`, and “Starting a seed library” comes first in `oldest`.\nThey cannot reuse the same loaded relation. The additional ordering is loaded through a fallback\nquery. The publishing example checks both results and the emitted SQL; a response alone would not\nestablish how the relation was loaded."},"2689":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/query-planning#compare-two-orderings-of-one-relation","content":"Prisma client calls and SQL statements are different measures: a single Prisma query can issue\nseveral SQL statements to load related tables. Compare the emitted SQL without assuming one\nstatement per Prisma call."},"2690":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","type":"page","content":"Relations","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations"},"2691":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","content":"Adding relations to prisma objects"},"2692":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#fallback-queries","content":"Fallback queries"},"2693":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#filters-sorting-and-arguments","content":"Filters, Sorting, and arguments"},"2694":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#nullable-relations-and-onnull","content":"Nullable relations and `onNull`"},"2695":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#relationcount","content":"relationCount"},"2696":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#published-posts-and-profiles","content":"Published posts and profiles"},"2697":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","content":"Use `t.relation` to expose relations between models. This example uses the client and authenticated\n`userId` context from Setup:"},"2698":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","content":"`t.relation` defines a field that can be pre-loaded by a parent resolver. This will create something\nlike `{ include: { author: true }}` that will be passed as part of the `query` argument of a\n`prismaField` resolver. If the parent is another `relation` field, the includes will become nested,\nand the full relation chain will be passed to the `prismaField` that started the chain."},"2699":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","content":"For example the query:"},"2700":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","content":"the `me` `prismaField` would receive something like the following as its query parameter:"},"2701":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","content":"When selections cannot share a Prisma query, Pothos loads the missing data with fallback queries."},"2702":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#fallback-queries","content":"There are some cases where data can not be pre-loaded by a prisma field. In these cases, pothos will\nissue a `findUnique` query for the parent of any fields that were not pre-loaded, and select the\nmissing relations so those fields can be resolved with the correct data. These queries should be\nvery efficient, are batched by pothos to combine requirements for multiple fields into one query,\nand batched by Prisma to combine multiple queries (in an n+1 situation) to a single sql query."},"2703":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#fallback-queries","content":"The following are some edge cases that could cause an additional query to be necessary:"},"2704":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#fallback-queries","content":"The parent object was not loaded through a field defined with `t.prismaField`, or `t.relation`"},"2705":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#fallback-queries","content":"The root `prismaField` did not correctly spread the `query` arguments in its prisma call."},"2706":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#fallback-queries","content":"The query selects multiple fields that use the same relation with different filters, sorting, or\nlimits"},"2707":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#fallback-queries","content":"The query contains multiple aliases for the same relation field with different arguments in a way\nthat results in different query options for the relation."},"2708":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#fallback-queries","content":"A relation field has a query that is incompatible with the default includes of the parent object"},"2709":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#fallback-queries","content":"A fallback query loads the parent row again by its primary key, or by the first required unique\nfield or index when the model has no primary key, selecting what the missing fields need. To load\nit some other way, add a `findUnique` option to the type that returns the `where` for\n`prisma.<model>.findUnique`:"},"2710":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#fallback-queries","content":"A type in include mode can also opt out of fallback queries with `findUnique: null`. A field that\nwould need one fails with `Unexpected error.` instead of querying. The original error\n(`Missing findUnique for User`) is on `error.cause`."},"2711":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#fallback-queries","content":"With `ScopeAuthPlugin`, scope checks that read the parent see what its type loads (its `select`, or\nevery scalar and its `include`), even for a row returned from a plain `t.field`, unless the type\nsets `findUnique: null`. A row missing that data is reloaded, so a row that no longer exists fails\nthe field with `Unexpected error.`. To return a deleted row, use `t.prismaField` and pass its\n`query` to `delete`. Only top-level keys are checked, so a row with a partly loaded relation isn't\nreloaded. When `ScopeAuthPlugin` comes before `PrismaPlugin`, a field's own `select` is loaded after\nits `authScopes` run."},"2712":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#filters-sorting-and-arguments","content":"So far we have been describing very simple queries without any arguments, filtering, or sorting. For\n`t.prismaField` definitions, you can add arguments to your field like normal, and pass them into\nyour prisma query as needed. For `t.relation` the flow is slightly different because we are not\nmaking a prisma query directly. We do this by adding a `query` option to our field options. Query\ncan either be a query object, or a method that returns a query object based on the field arguments."},"2713":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#filters-sorting-and-arguments","content":"The returned query object will be added to the include section of the `query` argument that gets\npassed into the first argument of the parent `t.prismaField`, and can include things like `where`,\n`skip`, `take`, and `orderBy`. The `query` function will be passed the arguments for the field, and\nthe context for the current request. Because it is used for pre-loading data, and solving n+1\nissues, it can not be passed the `parent` object because it may not be loaded yet."},"2714":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#nullable-relations-and-onnull","content":"A relation that is optional in the prisma schema (`profile Profile?`) can be exposed as a nullable\nfield with `nullable: true`. To expose it as non-nullable, `t.relation` requires an `onNull` option\ndescribing what should happen when the related row is missing. Setting it to `'error'` lets GraphQL\nraise the non-null error for the field. A function can return a replacement value instead, or an\n`Error` to raise:"},"2715":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#relationcount","content":"`t.relationCount` adds a field that counts related rows without loading them. Its `where` option\nfilters the rows included in the count:"},"2716":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#published-posts-and-profiles","content":"In a publishing API, a public author page exposes published posts and an optional profile.\nDrafts belong on the private viewer. Filtering the list and\nits count consistently prevents the count from revealing unpublished posts. Sorting by both the\ntimestamp and ID makes the order deterministic when two posts share a timestamp."},"2717":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#published-posts-and-profiles","content":"The following type is used by the nullable `author` lookup in Objects:"},"2718":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relations#published-posts-and-profiles","content":"`author(id: 1) { name bio postCount posts { title } }` returns Maya's profile and two\npublished posts. Nora (`id: 3`) has no profile or posts, so `bio` is null, `posts` is empty, and\n`postCount` is zero. A missing relation is represented as missing data, without manufacturing a\nprofile record."},"2719":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","type":"page","content":"Relay nodes","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay"},"2720":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","content":"Using the Prisma and Relay plugins together"},"2721":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay#prismanode","content":"`prismaNode`"},"2722":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay#refetch-a-public-author","content":"Refetch a public author"},"2723":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","content":"`prismaNode` adds Relay IDs and root node lookups to Prisma objects. Register the\nRelay plugin; see Connections for pagination."},"2724":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","content":"Defining a node creates a direct lookup through `node` and `nodes`. These lookups bypass custom\nroot resolvers, so permission checks or visibility filters on a list or parent field do not protect\nnode refetches. An encoded global ID is an identifier, not proof of permission."},"2725":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","content":"With Scope Auth, require scopes on the `node` and `nodes` fields, put scopes on the `Node`\ninterface or each node type, or disable the lookup fields. You can also enforce visibility where the\nnode loads, so inaccessible records look missing. See\nRelay node authorization for each option and its\nlimits."},"2726":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay#prismanode","content":"The `prismaNode` method works just like the `prismaObject` method with a couple of small\ndifferences:"},"2727":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay#prismanode","content":"there is a new `id` option that mirrors the `id` option from `node` method of the relay plugin,\nand must contain a resolve function that returns the id from an instance of the node. Rather than\ndefining a resolver for the id field, you can set the `field` option to the name of a unique\ncolumn or index."},"2728":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay#prismanode","content":"If you need to customize how ids are formatted, you can add a resolver for the `id`, and provide a\n`findUnique` option that can be used to load the node by its id. This is generally not necessary."},"2729":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay#prismanode","content":"When executing the `node(id: ID!)` query with a global ID for which prisma cannot find a record in\nthe database, the default behavior is to throw an error. There are some scenarios where it is\npreferable to return `null` instead of throwing an error. For this you can add the `nullable: true`\noption:"},"2730":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay#refetch-a-public-author","content":"The publishing schema defines User as a Relay node. Its returned ID can be passed back to\n`node` to fetch the author with a new selection:"},"2731":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay#refetch-a-public-author","content":"The ID identifies User 1. The node returns the same public fields as the author lookup, including\nonly published posts. A node lookup does not route through a custom root resolver, so any\naccess restrictions on an entity must also hold when it is loaded as a node."},"2732":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/relay#refetch-a-public-author","content":"Post node lookups additionally restrict rows to published posts or the requesting author's own\ndrafts. With `nullable: true`, a missing or inaccessible Post returns null. Merely hiding a draft\nfrom the public connection would not prevent a client from refetching it by a known node ID."},"2733":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","type":"page","content":"Selections","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections"},"2734":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","content":"how to use custom includes and selections to optimize your prisma queries"},"2735":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections#includes-on-types","content":"Includes on types"},"2736":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections#select-mode-for-types","content":"Select mode for types"},"2737":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections#using-arguments-or-context-in-your-selections","content":"Using arguments or context in your selections"},"2738":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections#only-load-a-profile-when-requested","content":"Only load a profile when requested"},"2739":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections#includes-on-types","content":"In some cases, you may want to always pre-load certain relations. This can be helpful for defining\nfields directly on type where the underlying data may come from a related table."},"2740":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections#select-mode-for-types","content":"By default, the prisma plugin will use `include` when including relations, or generating fallback\nqueries. This means we are always loading all columns of a table when loading it in a\n`t.prismaField` or a `t.relation`. This is usually what we want, but in some cases, you may want to\nselect specific columns instead. This can be useful if you have tables with either a very large\nnumber of columns, or specific columns with large payloads you want to avoid loading."},"2741":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections#select-mode-for-types","content":"To do this, you can add a `select` instead of an include to your `prismaObject`:"},"2742":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections#select-mode-for-types","content":"The `t.expose*` and `t.relation` methods will all automatically add selections for the exposed\nfields when those fields are queried, ensuring that only the requested columns will be loaded from the\ndatabase."},"2743":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections#select-mode-for-types","content":"In addition to the `t.expose` and `t.relation`, you can also add custom selections to other fields:"},"2744":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections#select-mode-for-types","content":"A field-level `select` always adds to the row of the model the field is defined on, whatever type\nthe field returns. A `select` on a `t.prismaField` defined on `User` adds columns to the user its\nresolver receives as the parent, not to the model the field returns."},"2745":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections#using-arguments-or-context-in-your-selections","content":"This field selects the first comment created after the supplied date, or returns null when none\nexists. It assumes a registered `Date` scalar accepting JavaScript dates:"},"2746":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections#only-load-a-profile-when-requested","content":"The public author type keeps its default selection\nsmall. Its `bio` field declares the profile relation it needs, so a query for the author's name\nalone does not load a profile. Adding `bio` adds that relation to the database query; the resolver\nthen reads the selected row. A nullable profile still produces a nullable biography."},"2747":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/selections#only-load-a-profile-when-requested","content":"This distinction matters for computed fields: a resolver accessing related data must declare that\ndata in its selection, even when the GraphQL field itself is just a string."},"2748":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","type":"page","content":"Setup","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup"},"2749":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","content":"Setting up the Prisma plugin"},"2750":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#setup","content":"Setup"},"2751":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#add-the-pothos-generator-to-your-prisma-schema","content":"Add the `pothos` generator to your prisma schema"},"2752":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#set-up-the-builder","content":"Set up the builder"},"2753":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#detecting-unused-query-arguments","content":"Detecting unused query arguments"},"2754":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#deferred-fragments","content":"Deferred fragments"},"2755":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#run-the-publishing-api","content":"Run the publishing API"},"2756":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","content":"This plugin only supports Prisma ORM 7 (`prisma@^7`). For Prisma ORM 8, see the\nPrisma ORM 8 plugin."},"2757":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#setup","content":"The Prisma plugin uses generated types to describe your models and relations. Add its generator\nalongside your Prisma client generator, then pass the generated types and datamodel to the builder."},"2758":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#add-the-pothos-generator-to-your-prisma-schema","content":"Now the types Pothos uses will be generated whenever you re-generate your prisma client. Run the\nfollowing command to re-generate the client and create the new types:"},"2759":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#add-the-pothos-generator-to-your-prisma-schema","content":"Generator options:"},"2760":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#add-the-pothos-generator-to-your-prisma-schema","content":"`clientOutput`: Where the generated code will import the PrismaClient from. The default is the\nfull path of wherever the client is generated. If you are checking in the generated file,\nyou should specify a relative path for this import"},"2761":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#add-the-pothos-generator-to-your-prisma-schema","content":"`output`: Where to write the generated types"},"2762":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#add-the-pothos-generator-to-your-prisma-schema","content":"Example with more options:"},"2763":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#add-the-pothos-generator-to-your-prisma-schema","content":"If model or relation completions are missing, check the client import in the generated file."},"2764":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#set-up-the-builder","content":"This example uses the generated client above and a SQLite database. Install\n`@prisma/adapter-better-sqlite3` for this adapter; use the adapter for your database if it differs.\n`exposeDescriptions` also accepts `{ models: true, fields: true }` to configure descriptions separately."},"2765":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#set-up-the-builder","content":"The examples use an authenticated request context with `userId: number`. Supply it through your\nGraphQL server; see Context."},"2766":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#set-up-the-builder","content":"Pass the Prisma client through the plugin options. Including its full type in `Context` can slow\nTypeScript checking; see this TypeScript issue."},"2767":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#set-up-the-builder","content":"You can also load or create the prisma client dynamically for each request. This can be used to\nperiodically re-create clients or create read-only clients for certain types of users."},"2768":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#set-up-the-builder","content":"Replace the builder above with the following to select between `prisma` and a second client.\nThis SQLite example uses `READ_ONLY_REPLICA_URL` for a replica database maintained by your application;\nthe adapter does not configure replication or enforce read-only access. Configure database access\npermissions separately. Both clients must use the same generated Prisma client\nand schema. For another database, use its driver adapter and replica connection options."},"2769":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#detecting-unused-query-arguments","content":"Forgetting to spread the `query` argument from `t.prismaField` or `t.prismaConnection` into your\nprisma query can result in inefficient queries, or even missing data. To help catch these issues,\nthe plugin can warn you when you are not using the query argument correctly."},"2770":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#detecting-unused-query-arguments","content":"The `onUnusedQuery` option can be set to `warn` or `error` to enable this feature. When set to\n`warn` it will log a warning to the console if Pothos detects that you have not properly used the\nquery in your resolver. Similarly if you set the option to `error` it will throw an error instead.\nYou can also pass a function which will receive the `info` object which can be used to log or throw\nyour own error."},"2771":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#detecting-unused-query-arguments","content":"The check tracks access to properties on the query object. If no properties are accessed on the query object before the\nresolver returns, it will trigger the `onUnusedQuery` condition."},"2772":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#detecting-unused-query-arguments","content":"It's recommended to enable this check in development to more quickly find potential issues."},"2773":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#deferred-fragments","content":"`skipDeferredFragments` controls query planning; it does not enable incremental execution. The application\nmust register the defer directive and use an executor, server transport, and client that support\nthe same incremental delivery protocol. With GraphQL.js 17, ordinary `execute` rejects schemas\ncontaining `@defer` or `@stream`; incremental execution uses `experimentalExecuteIncrementally`.\nFor a server integration, see GraphQL Yoga's defer and stream setup,\nwhich uses `@graphql-yoga/plugin-defer-stream`. Check the integration's supported versions when\nchoosing an executor and client; adding directives or changing `skipDeferredFragments` alone is\nnot sufficient."},"2774":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#deferred-fragments","content":"Selections inside a `@defer` fragment are left out of the planned query by default, so the initial\npayload is not delayed by data the client has agreed to wait for. When the deferred fragment\nresolves, its fields are loaded through fallback queries, batched as\nusual."},"2775":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#deferred-fragments","content":"Set `skipDeferredFragments: false` in the plugin options to plan deferred selections with the rest\nof the query. `queryFromInfo` accepts the same option per call."},"2776":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#run-the-publishing-api","content":"The publishing example\nuses this SQLite adapter and generated client with one schema for author pages, private drafts,\nmedia attachments, and pagination. Prisma Utils adds filters and draft inputs to that same schema.\nThe example's `schema.prisma` defines User, Profile, Post, Comment, Media, and PostMedia, including\nthe foreign keys and unique constraints needed by their relations."},"2777":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#run-the-publishing-api","content":"From a checkout of the Pothos repository, with Node.js 22 or newer:"},"2778":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#run-the-publishing-api","content":"The setup generates both clients and SQLite DDL from the Prisma schema. Each run creates a fresh\ntemporary database and removes it afterward. The output contains the GraphQL response and emitted\nSQL. The example guide\nlists the operations and suggested edits. Applications should use Prisma migrations for their\npersistent databases."},"2779":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/setup#run-the-publishing-api","content":"The request's `userId` comes from authentication in an application. The local runner supplies it as\na fixture. Public author fields return published posts; private viewer fields return only the\ncurrent author's drafts. Ownership and publication checks also apply to draft updates and node\nrefetches. Selecting a GraphQL type does not by itself enforce those restrictions."},"2780":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","type":"page","content":"Type variants","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants"},"2781":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","content":"How to define multiple GraphQL types based on the same prisma model"},"2782":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants#the-authors-writing-desk","content":"The author’s writing desk"},"2783":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","content":"The prisma plugin supports defining multiple GraphQL types based on the same prisma model.\nAdditional types are called `variants`. Define a primary type as shown in Objects.\nThe examples below are alternatives using the User and Post models, with an integer User id,\na User.posts relation, and a Boolean Post.published column. Node examples require the\nRelay plugin. Use the authenticated `userId` context from Setup."},"2784":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","content":"Define an additional variant by providing `variant` instead of `name`:"},"2785":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","content":"You can define variant fields that reference one variant from another:"},"2786":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","content":"You can also use variants when defining relations by providing a `type` option:"},"2787":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","content":"You may run into circular reference issues if you use 2 prisma object refs to reference each other.\nTo avoid this, you can split out the field definition for one of the relationships using\n`builder.prismaObjectField`"},"2788":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","content":"This same workaround applies when defining relations using variants."},"2789":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","content":"Two variants of one model selected for the same row have their type-level selections merged into a\nsingle query, which can fail if they disagree. See\nConflicting selections between variants."},"2790":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants#the-authors-writing-desk","content":"A public User and a private Viewer can represent the same row. The Viewer exposes the current\nauthor's email and drafts, and its `user` field returns the public representation. The root `me`\nresolver uses the authenticated context ID; it does not accept an arbitrary author's ID."},"2791":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants#the-authors-writing-desk","content":"This version makes Viewer an interface so editor and author accounts can expose different fields:"},"2792":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/variants#the-authors-writing-desk","content":"With Maya's context (`userId: 1`), `me` is an EditorViewer with the draft “Planning the spring\nexchange.” With Leo's context (`userId: 2`), it is an AuthorViewer with “Saving rainwater.”\nThe interface describes the result shape; the root lookup supplies the ownership restriction.\nNeither public author fields nor public post connections return those drafts."},"2793":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","type":"page","content":"Prisma without a plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Prisma"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin"},"2794":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","content":"Using Prisma without a plugin"},"2795":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin#compare-the-same-author-page","content":"Compare the same author page"},"2796":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","content":"Use `builder.objectRef` with the generated Prisma model types and write resolvers that query your client."},"2797":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","content":"This example uses a generated Prisma client with User and Post models, backed by SQLite.\nUse the driver adapter for your database, and adjust the client import to your generated output.\nCreate a builder with an authenticated `userId` context:"},"2798":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","content":"This sets up User, and Post objects with a few fields, and a `me` query that returns the current\nuser. There are a few things to note in this setup:"},"2799":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","content":"We split up the `builder.objectRef` and the `implement` calls, rather than calling\n`builder.objectRef(...).implement(...)`. This prevents typescript from getting tripped up by the\ncircular references between posts and users."},"2800":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","content":"We use `findUniqueOrThrow` because those fields are not nullable. Using `findUnique`, prisma will\nreturn a null if the object is not found. An alternative is to mark these fields as nullable."},"2801":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","content":"The refs to our object types are called `UserObject` and `PostObject`, this is because `User` and\n`Post` are the names of the types imported from prisma. We could instead alias the types when we\nimport them so we can name the refs to our GraphQL types after the models."},"2802":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","content":"This setup is fairly simple, but it is easy to see the n+1 issues we might run into. Prisma helps\nwith this by batching queries together, but there are also things we can do in our implementation to\nimprove things."},"2803":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","content":"One thing we could do if we know we will usually be loading the author any time we load a post is to\ninclude the author in the backing type for a post. Replace the refs and implementations above with:"},"2804":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","content":"We may not always want to query for the author though, so we could make the author optional and fall\nback to a query if the parent resolver did not include it. Replace `PostObject` and its implementation with:"},"2805":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","content":"With this setup, a parent resolver has the option to include the author, but we have a fallback\nin case it does not."},"2806":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","content":"The Dataloader plugin provides another way to batch loads across resolvers."},"2807":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin#compare-the-same-author-page","content":"The publishing schema also defines the author lookup with ordinary object refs, using the same\nPrisma models and seeded database. The refs describe the backing rows; each relation resolver\nqueries Prisma explicitly:"},"2808":{"id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/prisma/without-a-plugin#compare-the-same-author-page","content":"`author(id: 1) { name posts { title author { name } } }` returns the same published posts as the\nplugin-backed lookup. The plugin version plans relation selections into the root query; this\nversion makes those calls in its field resolvers. The selection, eager-loading, and fallback\nalternatives above remain useful when choosing how to manage those calls yourself."},"2809":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"page","content":"Scope auth plugin","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Scope auth"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth"},"2810":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","content":"Authorize fields and types with request scopes, permission loaders, and ownership checks."},"2811":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#usage","content":"Usage"},"2812":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#install","content":"Install"},"2813":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#plugin-order","content":"Plugin order"},"2814":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setup","content":"Setup"},"2815":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#loading-permissions-asynchronously","content":"Loading permissions asynchronously"},"2816":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#using-a-scope-on-a-field","content":"Using a scope on a field"},"2817":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#terminology","content":"Terminology"},"2818":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#use-cases","content":"Use cases"},"2819":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#top-level-auth-on-queries-and-mutations","content":"Top level auth on queries and mutations"},"2820":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#auth-on-nested-fields","content":"Auth on nested fields"},"2821":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#default-auth-for-all-fields-on-types","content":"Default auth for all fields on types"},"2822":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#overwriting-default-auth-on-field","content":"Overwriting default auth on field"},"2823":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#running-scopes-on-types-rather-than-fields","content":"Running scopes on types rather than fields"},"2824":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#generalized-auth-functions-with-field-specific-arguments","content":"Generalized auth functions with field specific arguments"},"2825":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#customizing-error-messages","content":"Customizing error messages"},"2826":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#globally","content":"Globally"},"2827":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#surfacing-errors-thrown-in-authorization-checks","content":"Surfacing errors thrown in authorization checks"},"2828":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#on-individual-fields","content":"On individual fields"},"2829":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#returning-a-custom-value-when-unauthorized","content":"Returning a custom value when unauthorized"},"2830":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setting-scopes-that-apply-for-a-full-request","content":"Setting scopes that apply for a full request"},"2831":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#change-context-types-based-on-scopes","content":"Change context types based on scopes"},"2832":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#using-withauth-with-prisma-fields","content":"Using `withAuth` with Prisma fields"},"2833":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#logical-operations-on-auth-scopes-anyall","content":"Logical operations on auth scopes (any/all)"},"2834":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#auth-that-depends-on-parent-value","content":"Auth that depends on parent value"},"2835":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setting-type-level-scopes-based-on-the-parent-value","content":"Setting type level scopes based on the parent value"},"2836":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setting-scopes-based-on-the-return-value-of-a-field","content":"Setting scopes based on the return value of a field"},"2837":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#granting-access-to-a-resource-based-on-how-it-is-accessed","content":"Granting access to a resource based on how it is accessed"},"2838":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#reusing-checks-for-multiple-but-not-all-fields","content":"Reusing checks for multiple, but not all fields"},"2839":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#interfaces","content":"Interfaces"},"2840":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#cache-keys","content":"Cache keys"},"2841":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#when-checks-are-run-and-how-things-are-cached","content":"When checks are run, and how things are cached"},"2842":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#scope-initializer","content":"Scope Initializer"},"2843":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#authscopes-functions-on-fields","content":"authScopes functions on fields"},"2844":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#authscopes-functions-on-types","content":"authScopes functions on types"},"2845":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#scope-loaders","content":"scope loaders"},"2846":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#grantscope-on-field","content":"grantScope on field"},"2847":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-37","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#grantscope-on-type","content":"grantScope on type"},"2848":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-38","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#api","content":"API"},"2849":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-39","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#types","content":"Types"},"2850":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-40","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#builder","content":"Builder"},"2851":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-41","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#object-and-interface-options","content":"Object and Interface options"},"2852":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-42","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#field-options","content":"Field Options"},"2853":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-43","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#toschema-options","content":"toSchema options"},"2854":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-44","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","content":"The scope auth plugin checks authorization before a field resolver runs. Define named scopes for\nroles, permissions, or ownership checks, then require those scopes on fields and types. Checks and\nscope loaders are cached for the current request."},"2855":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-45","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","content":"Relay's `node` and `nodes` fields can load any node by ID. Require scopes on those fields, put\nscopes on the `Node` interface or each node type, disable the lookup fields, or hide inaccessible\nrecords in the node loader. See\nRelay node authorization for each option and its limits."},"2856":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-46","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#plugin-order","content":"When using `scope-auth` with other plugins, the `scope-auth` plugin should generally be listed first\nto ensure that other plugins that wrap resolvers do not execute before the `scope-auth` logic.\nHowever, exceptions do exist where it is desirable for a plugin to run before `scope-auth`. For\ninstance, putting the relay plugin before the\n`scope-auth` plugin results in the `authScopes` function correctly receiving parsed `globalID`s."},"2857":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-47","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setup","content":"The setup defines two parts of the authorization model:"},"2858":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-48","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setup","content":"The `AuthScopes` type in the builder `SchemaTypes`. This is a map of types that defines the types\nused by each of your scopes. We'll see how this is used in more detail below."},"2859":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-49","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setup","content":"The `scope initializer` function, which is the implementation of each of the scopes defined in\nthe type above. This function returns a map of either booleans (indicating if the request has\nthe scope) or functions that load the scope (with an optional parameter)."},"2860":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-50","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setup","content":"The names of the scopes (`loggedIn`, `employee`, `canReadArticles`, and `customPerm`) are all\narbitrary, and are not part of the plugin. You can use whatever scope names you prefer, and can add\nas many you need."},"2861":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-51","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#loading-permissions-asynchronously","content":"Scope initializers and loaders can return promises. This alternative builder uses request-scoped\n`isEmployee` and `hasPermission` methods supplied by your application:"},"2862":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-52","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#loading-permissions-asynchronously","content":"The initializer waits for `isEmployee` before returning its scope map. The permission loaders run\nonly when a field requires their scopes; their results are cached for the request and parameter."},"2863":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-53","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#using-a-scope-on-a-field","content":"For this nullable field, an unauthenticated request receives `message: null` and a GraphQL error;\nan authenticated request receives `\"hi\"`. Other fields that pass their own checks can still return\ndata. The application must authenticate the user before constructing the context: checking a\nscope authorizes access using that context, but does not establish the user's identity."},"2864":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-54","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#using-a-scope-on-a-field","content":"Set `authorizeOnSubscribe: true` to check authorization when a subscription is created. Without\nthis option, checks run when subscription events resolve."},"2865":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-55","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#terminology","content":"A lot of terms around authorization are overloaded, and can mean different things to different\npeople. Here is a short list of a few terms used in this document, and how they should be\ninterpreted:"},"2866":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-56","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#terminology","content":"`scope`: A scope is the unit of authorization that can be used to authorize a request to resolve a\nfield."},"2867":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-57","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#terminology","content":"`scope map`: A map of scope names and scope parameters. This defines the set of scopes that will\nbe checked for a field or type to authorize the request the resolve a resource."},"2868":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-58","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#terminology","content":"`scope loader`: A function for dynamically loading scope given a scope parameter. Scope loaders\nare ideal for integrating with a permission service, or creating scopes that can be customized\nbased on the field or values that they are authorizing."},"2869":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-59","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#terminology","content":"`scope parameter`: A parameter that will be passed to a scope loader. These are the values in the\nauthScopes objects."},"2870":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-60","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#terminology","content":"`scope initializer`: The function that creates the scopes or scope loaders for the current\nrequest."},"2871":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-61","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#terminology","content":"While this plugin uses `scopes` as the term for its authorization mechanism, this plugin can easily\nbe used for role or permission based schemes, and is not intended to dictate a specific philosophy\naround how to authorize requests/access to resources."},"2872":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-62","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#use-cases","content":"The examples below use the scopes from the setup above. Examples that introduce a different\nbuilder are alternatives. Each `Article` definition below is also an alternative, using this\nbacking data and ref:"},"2873":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-63","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#use-cases","content":"If your application already uses an `Article` class, you can pass it to `builder.objectType`\ninstead, with `name: 'Article'`, and return class instances from resolvers."},"2874":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-64","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#top-level-auth-on-queries-and-mutations","content":"Authorization runs before the field resolver. A denied mutation therefore cannot perform the\nwrites inside that resolver; an authorized mutation can. Put writes in the protected resolver,\nnot in the scope initializer or permission loader."},"2875":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-65","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#top-level-auth-on-queries-and-mutations","content":"To add an auth check to root level queries or mutations, add authScopes to the field options:"},"2876":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-66","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#top-level-auth-on-queries-and-mutations","content":"This will require the requests to have the `employee` scope. Adding multiple scopes to the\n`authScopes` object will check all the scopes, and if the user has any of the scopes, the request\nwill be considered authorized for the current field. Subscription and Mutation root fields work the\nsame way."},"2877":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-67","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#auth-on-nested-fields","content":"Fields on nested objects can be authorized the same way scopes are authorized on the root types."},"2878":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-68","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#default-auth-for-all-fields-on-types","content":"To apply the same scope requirements to all fields on a type, you can define an `authScopes` map in\nthe type options rather than on the individual fields."},"2879":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-69","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#overwriting-default-auth-on-field","content":"In some cases you may want to use default auth scopes for a type, but need to change the behavior\nfor one specific field."},"2880":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-70","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#overwriting-default-auth-on-field","content":"To add additional requirements for a specific field you can simply add additional scopes on the\nfield itself."},"2881":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-71","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#overwriting-default-auth-on-field","content":"To remove the type level scopes for a field, you can use the `skipTypeScopes` option:"},"2882":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-72","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#overwriting-default-auth-on-field","content":"This will allow non-logged in users to resolve the title, but not the content of an Article.\n`skipTypeScopes` can be used in conjunction with `authScopes` on a field to completely overwrite the\ndefault scopes."},"2883":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-73","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#running-scopes-on-types-rather-than-fields","content":"By default, all auth scopes are tested before a field resolves. This includes both scopes defined on\na type and scopes defined on a field. When scopes for a `type` fail, you will end up with an error\nfor each field of that type. Type level scopes are only executed once, but the errors are emitted\nfor each affected field."},"2884":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-74","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#running-scopes-on-types-rather-than-fields","content":"The behavior may not be desirable for all users. You can set `runScopesOnType` to true, either on\nobject types, or in the `scopeAuth` options of the builder"},"2885":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-75","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#running-scopes-on-types-rather-than-fields","content":"Enabling this has a couple of limitations:"},"2886":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-76","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#running-scopes-on-types-rather-than-fields","content":"This does not work with `graphql-jit`. This option uses the `isTypeOf` function, but\n`graphql-jit` does not support async `isTypeOf`, and also does not correctly pass the context\nobject to the isTypeOf checks. Until this is resolved, this option will not work with\n`graphql-jit`."},"2887":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-77","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#running-scopes-on-types-rather-than-fields","content":"Fields of types that set `runScopesOnType` to true will not be able to use `skipTypeScopes` or\n`skipInterfaceScopes`."},"2888":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-78","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#generalized-auth-functions-with-field-specific-arguments","content":"The scopes we have covered so far have all been related to information that applies to a full\nrequest. In more complex applications it may not make sense to enumerate all the scopes a request\nis authorized for ahead of time. To handle these cases you can define a scope loader which takes a\nparameter and dynamically determines if a request is authorized for a scope using that parameter."},"2889":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-79","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#generalized-auth-functions-with-field-specific-arguments","content":"One common example of this would be a permission service that can check if a user or request has a\ncertain permission, and you want to specify the specific permission each field requires."},"2890":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-80","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#generalized-auth-functions-with-field-specific-arguments","content":"In the example above, the authScopes map uses the customPerm scope loader with a parameter of\n`readArticle`. The first time a field requests this scope, the customPerm loader will be called with\n`readArticle` as its argument. This scope will be cached, so that if multiple fields request the\nsame scope, the scope loader will still only be called once."},"2891":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-81","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#generalized-auth-functions-with-field-specific-arguments","content":"The types for the parameters you provide for each scope are based on the types provided to the\nbuilder in the `AuthScopes` type."},"2892":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-82","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#customizing-error-messages","content":"Error messages (and error instances) can be customized either globally or on specific fields."},"2893":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-83","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#globally","content":"The `unauthorizedError` callback will be called with the parent, context, and info object of the\nunauthorized field. It will also include a 4th argument `result` that has the default message for\nthis type of failure, and a `failure` property with some details about what caused the field to be\nunauthorized. This callback can either return an `Error` instance (or an instance of a class that\nextends `Error`), or a `string`. If a string is returned, it will be converted to a\n`ForbiddenError`."},"2894":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-84","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#globally","content":"The `treatErrorsAsUnauthorized` option changes how errors in authorization functions are handled. By\ndefault errors are not caught by the plugin, and will act as if thrown directly from the resolver.\nThis means that thrown errors bypass the `unauthorizedError` callback, and will cause requests to\nfail even when another scope in an `$any` passes."},"2895":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-85","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#globally","content":"Setting `treatErrorsAsUnauthorized` will cause errors to be caught and treated as if the scope was\nnot authorized."},"2896":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-86","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#surfacing-errors-thrown-in-authorization-checks","content":"When `treatErrorsAsUnauthorized` is set to true, errors are caught and attached to the `result`\nobject in the `unauthorizedError` callback. This allows you to surface the error to the client."},"2897":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-87","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#surfacing-errors-thrown-in-authorization-checks","content":"For example, if you want to re-throw errors thrown by authorization functions you could do this by\nwriting a custom `unauthorizedError` callback like this:"},"2898":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-88","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#returning-a-custom-value-when-unauthorized","content":"In some cases you may want to return null, and empty array, throw a custom error, or return a custom\nresult when a user is not authorized. To do this you can add a `unauthorizedResolver` option to your\nfield."},"2899":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-89","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#returning-a-custom-value-when-unauthorized","content":"In the example above, if a user is not authorized they will simply receive an empty array in the\nresponse. The `unauthorizedResolver` option takes the same arguments as a resolver, but also\nreceives a 5th argument that is an instance of `ForbiddenError`."},"2900":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-90","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setting-scopes-that-apply-for-a-full-request","content":"We have already seen several examples of this. For scopes that apply to a full request like `loggedIn`\nor `employee`, rather than using a scope loader, the scope initializer can simply use a boolean to\nindicate if the request has the given scope. If you know ahead of time that a scope loader will\nalways return false for a specific request, you can do something like the following to avoid the\nadditional overhead of running the loader:"},"2901":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-91","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setting-scopes-that-apply-for-a-full-request","content":"This will ensure that if a request accesses a field that requests a `humanPermission` scope, and the\nrequest has no signed-in user, we don't have to run the permission check at all for\nthose requests, since we know it would return false anyways."},"2902":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-92","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#change-context-types-based-on-scopes","content":"Sometimes you need to change your context typings depending on the applied scopes. You can provide\ncustom context for your defined scopes and use the `authField` method to access the custom context:"},"2903":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-93","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#change-context-types-based-on-scopes","content":"`t.withAuth` returns a field builder with scopes already applied. It also works with methods\nadded by other plugins, and if the field also sets `authScopes`, both checks must pass. With the\nsame builder as above, an equivalent field is:"},"2904":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-94","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#using-withauth-with-prisma-fields","content":"`withAuth` preserves plugin-specific field methods while refining their context. This alternative\nbuilder uses the client `prisma` and generated types from Prisma setup, with a\nUser model containing an integer `id`. The request context contains either the\nsigned-in user's ID or `null`:"},"2905":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-95","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#using-withauth-with-prisma-fields","content":"The resolver receives a non-null `context.user` and Prisma's selection `query`. A denied request\ndoes not call the resolver or issue its database query."},"2906":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-96","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#logical-operations-on-auth-scopes-anyall","content":"By default the scopes in a scope map are evaluated in parallel, and if the request has any of\nthe requested scopes, the field will be resolved. In some cases, you may want to require multiple\nscopes:"},"2907":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-97","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#logical-operations-on-auth-scopes-anyall","content":"You can use the built in `$any` and `$all` scope loaders to combine requirements for scopes. The\nabove example requires a request to have either the `employee` or `canReadArticles` scopes, and the\n`loggedIn` scope. `$any` and `$all` each take a scope map as their parameters, and can be nested\ninside each other."},"2908":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-98","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#logical-operations-on-auth-scopes-anyall","content":"The same pattern can require both login and a permission:\n`{ $all: { loggedIn: true, customPerm: 'editArticle' } }`. A user with only `readArticle` access\ncan read a field requiring that permission but cannot pass the edit check. A user with\n`editArticle` access passes it only when `loggedIn` also succeeds."},"2909":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-99","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#logical-operations-on-auth-scopes-anyall","content":"You can change the default strategy used for top level auth scopes by setting the `defaultStrategy`\noption in the builder (defaults to `any`):"},"2910":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-100","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#auth-that-depends-on-parent-value","content":"For cases where the required scopes depend on the value of the requested resource you can use a\nfunction in the `authScopes` option that returns the scope map for the field."},"2911":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-101","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#auth-that-depends-on-parent-value","content":"authScopes functions on fields will receive the same arguments as the field resolver, and will be\ncalled each time the resolve for the field would be called. This means the same authScopes function\ncould be called multiple time for the same resource if the field is requested multiple times using\nan alias."},"2912":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-102","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#auth-that-depends-on-parent-value","content":"Returning a boolean from an auth scope function is an easy way to allow or disallow a request from\nresolving a field without needing to evaluate additional scopes."},"2913":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-103","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setting-type-level-scopes-based-on-the-parent-value","content":"You can also use a function in the authScopes option for types. This function receives the parent,\nthe context, and an `info` object described below, and should return a scope map."},"2914":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-104","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setting-type-level-scopes-based-on-the-parent-value","content":"This requires an employee for unpublished articles and a signed-in user for published articles."},"2915":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-105","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setting-type-level-scopes-based-on-the-parent-value","content":"`info` has `parentType`, the object type being checked, and `schema`, for both `authScopes` and\n`grantScopes` functions. On an interface, `parentType` is the implementing type, so\none interface can apply different rules to each of its types:"},"2916":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-106","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setting-type-level-scopes-based-on-the-parent-value","content":"`info` is not the field's `GraphQLResolveInfo`: the result is cached for each object and type, so it\ndoes not depend on which field or path triggered the check. For the Relay `Node` interface, and a\nhelper that narrows the parent by ref, see Vary the policy by node type."},"2917":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-107","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#setting-scopes-based-on-the-return-value-of-a-field","content":"This is a use that is not currently supported. The current work around is to move those checks down\nto the returned type. Combining this with `runScopesOnType` should work for most cases."},"2918":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-108","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#granting-access-to-a-resource-based-on-how-it-is-accessed","content":"In some cases, you may want to grant a request scopes to access certain fields on a child type. To\ndo this you can use `$granted` scopes."},"2919":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-109","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#granting-access-to-a-resource-based-on-how-it-is-accessed","content":"In the above example, the fields of the `Article` type normally require the `loggedIn` scope granted\nto logged in users, but can also be accessed with the `$granted` scope `readArticle`. This means\nthat if the field that returned the Article \"granted\" the scope, the article can be read. The\n`freeArticle` field on the `Query` type grants this scope, allowing anyone querying that field to\naccess fields of the free article. `$granted` scopes are separate from other scopes, and do not give\na request access to normal scopes of the same name. `$granted` scopes are also not inherited by\nnested children, and would need to be explicitly passed down for each field if you wanted to grant\naccess to nested children."},"2920":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-110","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#reusing-checks-for-multiple-but-not-all-fields","content":"You may have cases where groups of fields on a type are accessible using some shared condition. This\nis another case where `$granted` scopes can be helpful."},"2921":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-111","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#reusing-checks-for-multiple-but-not-all-fields","content":"In the above example, `title`, `content`, and `viewCount` each use `$granted` scopes. In this case,\nrather than scopes being granted by the parent field, they are granted by the Article type\nitself. This allows the access to each field to change based on some dynamic conditions (if the\nrequest is from the author, and if the article is a draft) without having to duplicate that logic\nin each individual field."},"2922":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-112","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#interfaces","content":"Interfaces can define auth scopes on their fields the same way objects do. Fields for a type will\nrun checks for each interface it implements separately, meaning that a request would need to satisfy\nthe scope requirements for each interface separately before the field is resolved."},"2923":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-113","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#interfaces","content":"Object types can set `skipInterfaceScopes` to `true` to skip interface checks when resolving fields\nfor that Object type."},"2924":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-114","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#interfaces","content":"Inherited fields use the object's `authScopes` and `grantScopes`, along with the policies of the\ninterface that declared the field and the other interfaces the object implements. All required\nchecks must pass, including when querying through an interface."},"2925":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-115","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#interfaces","content":"`skipInterfaceScopes` on the field or object skips all interface checks, including the declaring\ninterface's check. The object's own `authScopes` still runs. `skipTypeScopes` on the field skips the\nobject's and declaring interface's own checks. Neither option skips grants."},"2926":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-116","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#interfaces","content":"An interface's `runScopesOnType` does not bypass its policy: implementing objects enforce it on\nboth inherited fields and fields they declare themselves."},"2927":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-117","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#cache-keys","content":"Auth scopes by default are cached based on the identity of the scope parameter. This works great for\nstatically defined scopes, and scopes that take primitive values as their parameters. If you define\nauth scopes that take complex objects, and create those objects in a scope function (based on\narguments, or parent values) You won't get cache hits on those checks."},"2928":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-118","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#cache-keys","content":"To work around this, you can provide a `cacheKey` option to the builder for generating a cache key\nfrom your scope checks."},"2929":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-119","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#cache-keys","content":"Use a key that includes every value affecting the permission check. `JSON.stringify` works for\nJSON-compatible parameters with consistent property ordering; it does not handle circular values.\nFor resource checks, a stable resource ID is often sufficient."},"2930":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-120","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#when-checks-are-run-and-how-things-are-cached","content":"Create a new context object for every request. Reusing a context also reuses cached authorization\nresults, even if you change the user on it. When a server copies the context, initialize its cache\nwith `initContextCache()` from `@pothos/core` in the request context factory."},"2931":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-121","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#scope-initializer","content":"The scope initializer would be run once the first time a field protected by auth scopes is resolved,\nits result will be cached for the current request."},"2932":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-122","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#authscopes-functions-on-fields","content":"When using a function for `authScopes` on a field, the function will be run each time the field is\nresolved, since it has access to all the arguments passed to the resolver"},"2933":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-123","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#authscopes-functions-on-types","content":"When using a function for `authScopes` on a type, the function will be run once for each\ninstance of that type in the response. It will be run lazily when the first field for that object is\nresolved, and its result will be cached and reused by all fields for that instance of the type."},"2934":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-124","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#scope-loaders","content":"Scope loaders will be run whenever a field requires the corresponding scope with a unique\nparameter. The scope loader results are cached per request based on a combination of the name of the\nscope, and its parameter."},"2935":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-125","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#grantscope-on-field","content":"`grantScopes` on a field will run after the field is resolved, and is not cached"},"2936":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-126","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#grantscope-on-type","content":"`grantScopes` on a type (object or interface) will run when the first field on the type is\nresolved. It's result will be cached and reused for each field of the same instance of the type."},"2937":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-127","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#types","content":"`AuthScopes`: `extends {}`. Each property is the name of its scope, each value is the type for the\nscopes parameter."},"2938":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-128","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#types","content":"`ScopeLoaderMap`: Object whose keys are scope names (from `AuthScopes`) and whose values are\neither booleans (indicating whether or not the request has the scope) or function that take a\nparameter (type from `AuthScope`) and return `MaybePromise<boolean>`"},"2939":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-129","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#types","content":"`ScopeMap`: A map of scope names to parameters. Based on `AuthScopes`, may also contain `$all`,\n`$any` or `$granted`."},"2940":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-130","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#builder","content":"Set the initializer in `scopeAuth.authScopes`, alongside options such as `defaultStrategy`,\n`authorizeOnSubscribe`, `unauthorizedError`, and `cacheKey`."},"2941":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-131","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#builder","content":"`authScopes`: (context: Types\\['Context']) => `MaybePromise<ScopeLoaderMap<Types>>`"},"2942":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-132","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#object-and-interface-options","content":"`authScopes`: `ScopeMap` or `function`, accepts `parent`, `context` and `info`\n(`{ parentType, schema }`), returns `MaybePromise<ScopeMap>`"},"2943":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-133","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#object-and-interface-options","content":"`grantScopes`: `function`, accepts `parent`, `context` and `info`, returns\n`MaybePromise<string[]>`"},"2944":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-134","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#field-options","content":"`authScopes`: `ScopeMap` or `function`, accepts same arguments as resolver, returns\n`MaybePromise<ScopeMap>`"},"2945":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-135","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#field-options","content":"`grantScopes`: `string[]` or `function`, accepts same arguments as resolver, returns\n`MaybePromise<string[]>`"},"2946":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-136","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#field-options","content":"`skipTypeScopes`: `boolean`"},"2947":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-137","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#field-options","content":"`skipInterfaceScopes`: `boolean`"},"2948":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth-138","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth#toschema-options","content":"`disableScopeAuth`: disable the scope auth plugin. Useful for testing."},"2949":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","type":"page","content":"Relay node authorization","breadcrumbs":["Pothos Docs","Plugins","Scope auth"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes"},"2950":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-0","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","content":"Protect direct node lookups and use scopes to distinguish refetchable access from access granted through a parent field."},"2951":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-1","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#choose-a-default-policy","content":"Choose a default policy"},"2952":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-2","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#gate-the-lookup-fields","content":"Gate the lookup fields"},"2953":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-3","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#put-a-policy-on-the-node-interface","content":"Put a policy on the Node interface"},"2954":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-4","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#vary-the-policy-by-node-type","content":"Vary the policy by node type"},"2955":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-5","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#make-query-fields-private-by-default","content":"Make Query fields private by default"},"2956":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-6","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#disable-the-lookup-fields","content":"Disable the lookup fields"},"2957":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-7","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#apply-a-policy-to-the-returned-object","content":"Apply a policy to the returned object"},"2958":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-8","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#grant-access-through-a-particular-path","content":"Grant access through a particular path"},"2959":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-9","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#decide-what-node-loading-may-reveal","content":"Decide what node loading may reveal"},"2960":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-10","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#keep-authorization-tied-to-the-request","content":"Keep authorization tied to the request"},"2961":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-11","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","content":"Registering a Relay node creates another way to reach an object: `Query.node` and `Query.nodes`\ncan load it from its global ID. A filter or authorization check on `Query.articles`, a connection,\nor a parent relationship does not run when a client uses that lookup. Global IDs identify objects;\nthey are not proof that a caller may read them."},"2962":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-12","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","content":"This applies to `builder.node`, `builder.prismaNode`, and `builder.drizzleNode`. Decide which\nobjects should be refetchable and what access a direct lookup should allow. A type can participate\nin a connection without implementing `Node`."},"2963":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-13","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","content":"The options, most of which can be combined:"},"2964":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-14","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","content":"Gate the `node` and `nodes` fields so lookups require a scope before\nanything loads."},"2965":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-15","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","content":"Put scopes on the `Node` interface, either one baseline\nfor every type or a policy for each node type."},"2966":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-16","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","content":"Make Query private by default, which also covers `node`\nand `nodes`."},"2967":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-17","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","content":"Disable `node` and `nodes` if clients don't need direct\nrefetching."},"2968":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-18","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","content":"Put scopes on each node type, which also protects it\nwhen reached through other fields."},"2969":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-19","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","content":"Hide entities in the node loader, so unauthorized ones\nlook missing."},"2970":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-20","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#gate-the-lookup-fields","content":"For an API where direct refetches require a signed-in user, put that requirement on **both**\ngenerated lookup fields. These options belong in the builder's `relay` configuration, alongside\nthe Scope Auth setup shown below. The private-policy example combines these gates with the\nNode interface and Query defaults discussed next; the original example keeps its public\nfeatured-article path:"},"2971":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-21","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#gate-the-lookup-fields","content":"These field checks run before Relay's node resolver, so an anonymous request cannot trigger its\nnode loaders, even with a `__typename`-only selection. A successful login check is only a baseline:\nkeep resource-specific permissions on the returned types or in their loaders. These options do not\nprotect custom fields that return the same objects or use node-loading helpers themselves."},"2972":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-22","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#put-a-policy-on-the-node-interface","content":"A shared policy on the `Node` interface applies to every node type:"},"2973":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-23","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#put-a-policy-on-the-node-interface","content":"This applies the interface requirement to fields of its implementing objects, including objects\nreturned through ordinary root fields, relationships, and connections. It adds to each object's\nown scopes; satisfying one does not bypass the other. For example, a signed-in reader can still\nfail an article's separate `readArticle` permission check."},"2974":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-24","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#put-a-policy-on-the-node-interface","content":"Interface scopes are useful for a shared baseline, but their default execution still happens at\nthe object's fields, after loading. They do not hide `__typename`. Use the root-field gates when\nyou also want to reject anonymous node lookups before loading. The two configurations can be used\ntogether. An object or field can opt out of interface checks with `skipInterfaceScopes: true`, so\naudit these exceptions as part of the policy."},"2975":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-25","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#put-a-policy-on-the-node-interface","content":"An interface login requirement would also block the anonymous featured-article path later in this\nguide. Its parent grant satisfies the article's own policy; it does not override a separate\ninterface requirement. Choose a baseline that matches which parts of your API are public."},"2976":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-26","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#vary-the-policy-by-node-type","content":"An `authScopes` function on the interface receives `info.parentType`, the node type being checked.\nA small helper can compare it with a ref, using the builder's config for the ref's type name, and\nnarrow the node to that type's shape:"},"2977":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-27","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#vary-the-policy-by-node-type","content":"Here `User` and `Post` are node refs, and `author` is a scope that takes a user ID:"},"2978":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-28","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#vary-the-policy-by-node-type","content":"Options passed to `builder.nodeInterfaceRef` are set after the builder is created, so they can\nreference node types defined elsewhere. Call it before building the schema. Each option can be set\nonce, in either `relay.nodeTypeOptions` or one `nodeInterfaceRef` call; `extensions` are merged."},"2979":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-29","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#vary-the-policy-by-node-type","content":"Returning `false` by default means a new node type is denied until it is added to the policy. A\nfailed `isType` check narrows away that type's shape, so check types whose shape matches another's\nlast."},"2980":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-30","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#make-query-fields-private-by-default","content":"If most of the API requires login, `authScopes` on `builder.queryType` also applies to the generated\n`node` and `nodes` fields. Public fields can explicitly skip the Query type requirement:"},"2981":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-31","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#make-query-fields-private-by-default","content":"This is a Query-root policy, not a schema-wide authorization default. Configure Mutation and\nSubscription separately, and keep entity-level checks for access through other paths. Field scopes\nstill apply when `skipTypeScopes` is used; it only removes the parent type's requirement.\n`scopeAuth.defaultStrategy` controls whether scopes in a map use AND or OR, not whether unannotated\nfields are private."},"2982":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-32","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#disable-the-lookup-fields","content":"If direct refetching is not part of the API, set both `relay.nodeQueryOptions` and\n`relay.nodesQueryOptions` to `false`. Node types and their IDs can remain in the schema without\nthose generated root lookups; custom lookup fields still need their own policy."},"2983":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-33","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#apply-a-policy-to-the-returned-object","content":"Type `authScopes` let the same policy protect fields whether the object came from a root field,\na relationship, a connection, or a node lookup. Parameterized scopes are useful for checks such as\nownership, project membership, or permission to read a particular article. Their results are cached\nby scope name and parameter for the request."},"2984":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-34","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#apply-a-policy-to-the-returned-object","content":"In this example, readers with the `readArticle` permission may read articles through any path.\nOther readers can read the featured article through a field that explicitly grants access.\nThe builder defines a `permission` scope using the current user:"},"2985":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-35","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#apply-a-policy-to-the-returned-object","content":"The node accepts either the read permission or a grant from its parent field:"},"2986":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-36","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#apply-a-policy-to-the-returned-object","content":"`$any` makes this an explicit choice between two authorization paths. For policies requiring both\nmembership and a resource permission, use `$all` instead. A scope parameter should identify the\nresource or permission being checked; include every value that changes the decision in its cache\nkey when using object parameters."},"2987":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-37","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#apply-a-policy-to-the-returned-object","content":"The loader retrieves the article before scopes run. By default, type scopes run before the\nobject's individual field resolvers. Those fields are protected, but `__typename` does not use a\nPothos field resolver. The existence-check operation\ntherefore returns `Article` even for an anonymous reader. Field-level checks do not hide existence."},"2988":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-38","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#apply-a-policy-to-the-returned-object","content":"For an ordinary, path-independent policy, `runScopesOnType: true` moves type checks to GraphQL\nobject completion, including selections containing only `__typename`. Denial then produces an error\nat the field returning the object. It still does not prevent the loader from running. Review its\nexecution compatibility and override limitations.\nKeep the parent-grant pattern below on the default field-level execution: parent grants are not\nresolved at the same response path when scopes run through `isTypeOf`."},"2989":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-39","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#grant-access-through-a-particular-path","content":"A featured-article field can deliberately allow a reader to view one selected article without\nmaking every article available through node lookups:"},"2990":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-40","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#grant-access-through-a-particular-path","content":"`grantScopes` applies to the returned object's response path. It does not add an ordinary scope to\nthe request, and it does not turn the returned ID into a transferable permission. An anonymous\nreader can receive the featured article and still be denied when refetching the same ID:"},"2991":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-41","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#grant-access-through-a-particular-path","content":"Run both access paths. The featured article resolves,\nwhile `node` is `null` with a `Not authorized` error at `node.id`. The Relay ID is non-null, so\nits denied field check nulls the containing node. A reader with the `readArticle` permission can\nrefetch it directly. This distinction can be useful for previews or access granted by a parent\nresource, but clients must not assume every ID they receive can be refetched successfully."},"2992":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-42","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#grant-access-through-a-particular-path","content":"Grants are not inherited by nested children. A grant on a connection field does not automatically\nreach objects beneath `edges.node`; put the policy on the returned entity, or deliberately propagate\nthe grant through the intervening fields. Likewise, a grant on one alias does not authorize another\nalias or a separate `node` / `nodes` selection. The underlying object or permission result may be\ncached while its response paths still have different granted scopes."},"2993":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-43","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#grant-access-through-a-particular-path","content":"When access should follow the entity across all paths, use an ordinary scope such as `canReadArticle`\nwith the article ID, and use that same permission policy on the object regardless of its parent. Reserve parent-field\ngrants for access that is intentionally tied to that path. Field scopes can then add stricter permissions for\nsensitive fields, such as an article's editorial notes, without restricting its public title."},"2994":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-44","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#grant-access-through-a-particular-path","content":"A type `authScopes` function runs once for each object, so it cannot see which path returned it.\nThe scope map it returns is still checked against each path, so `$granted` works there too. Field\n`authScopes` functions are another option when a check needs the current field's arguments."},"2995":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-45","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#decide-what-node-loading-may-reveal","content":"If an unauthorized entity should behave like a missing one, enforce visibility in the node loader\nor the data-access layer it uses. Return `null` for both cases if that is the API's policy. Do not\nrely on a type scope to prevent a database read, an eager relationship load, or side effects in a\nloader. With `loadMany`, check every requested ID and preserve the input order, including missing\nor inaccessible entries."},"2996":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-46","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#decide-what-node-loading-may-reveal","content":"The database integrations supply their own node loaders. A `where` clause in an unrelated list\nresolver does not configure them. See Prisma Relay nodes and\nDrizzle Relay nodes for integration-specific guidance.\nKeep an entity as an\nordinary object if its direct lookup policy is not implemented."},"2997":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-47","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#decide-what-node-loading-may-reveal","content":"A failed scope normally produces a GraphQL error; a loader returning `null` can represent absence\nwithout an authorization error. Null propagation still follows the configured field and list-item\nnullability, so a non-null node field or item can affect a larger part of the response. Returning\n`null` alone is not a guarantee against every form of information disclosure, such as differences\nin errors or timing."},"2998":{"id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes-48","page_id":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/plugins/scope-auth/relay-nodes#keep-authorization-tied-to-the-request","content":"Relay's `loadOne` and `loadMany` cache node loads on the context, and Scope Auth caches scope\ninitialization and permission decisions for that context. Use a fresh context for each request and\nkeep