e primary key to the end, in the same\ndirection as the last column. If the primary key can't be ordered, it uses the first non-null unique\nkey that can."],[2341,"If the payload field is an interface or union, pass `typeName` to choose the GraphQL object type\nto load. For mutations on inherited models, see Model inheritance."],[2346,"If the resolver reads the type's `select`, pass the ref that `prismaObject` returns instead of the\nmodel name. Passing a variant's ref adds fields to that variant."],[1942,"If the rows and nodes are the same type, omit `resolveNode` and the node-mapping `select` when\ncreating the helper. Its `ref` provides the node type for `t.connection`. Prefer\n`t.relatedConnection` when you can expose that relation directly without custom loading."],[421,"If two modules import each other, reading an export before its initialization can produce an\nundefined value or a `ReferenceError`, depending on the module system. Pothos defers field callbacks\nuntil `builder.toSchema()`, so references inside those callbacks can work even when the modules\nimport each other."],[2471,"If TypeScript reports missing `Temporal` globals, import their declarations once in your project\n(for example in a `globals.d.ts` file):"],[2295,"If you already compose a native ORM query, return it from `collection`, for example\n`collection: () => db.orm.Post.where({ published: 1 })` for published posts.\nFor writes, `resolve` receives the selected ORM query and lets you call `create`, `update`, or\n`delete` on it. See Mutations for complete examples and\nTransactions for multi-step operations."],[1423,"If you are printing the schema as a string for any reason, and then using the printed schema for\nApollo Federation(submitting if using Managed Federation, or composing manually with `rover`), you\nmust use `printSubgraphSchema`(from `@apollo/subgraph`) or another compatible way of printing the\nschema(that includes directives) in order for it to work."],[1968,"If you are upgra