ide,\nand enums accept `values`. Object and interface descriptions and type-resolution functions are\ncopied from the source, so configure those on the source type. The `name` option renames the import,\nand `extensions` are merged with the imported extensions."],[608,"Objects"],[2301,"Objects"],[646,"Objects and Interfaces"],[32,"Objects shows how to expose your application's data. Fields covers\nresolvers, lists, and nullability, and Arguments explains how clients pass values to them.\nWhen your schema needs request data, see Context. App layout shows how\nto organize a larger schema across files."],[453,"Objects with `as const`"],[31,"Omit the `name` argument to get `\"hello, World\"`. Stop the server with Ctrl+C when you're done."],[2364,"Omitted: the default Post Collection"],[2336,"on a `t.expose*` field, the exposed column"],[2374,"On a connection, `args` also includes the pagination arguments. `nestedSelection` is covered in\nIndirect relations."],[2199,"On a root field, use `load` to page a Collection of the helper's model. It loads the page, along\nwith whatever the query selects on the nodes, in one statement, and the count when `totalCount` is\nselected. The Collection has the same restrictions as a `t.prismaConnection`'s `collection`. Pass\nthe rows to `resolve`. With `resolveNode`, each edge also has its row, so edge fields can read it:"],[737,"On GraphQL 17, you can provide `coerceOutputValue` instead of `serialize`. If both are present,\nGraphQL uses `serialize`. A `coerceInputLiteral` implementation must be paired with\n`coerceInputValue`; otherwise schema construction throws. `addScalarType` preserves these hooks\nwhen registering an existing scalar that provides them."],[2828,"On individual fields"],[2473,"On MySQL, Prisma reads rows but can't write them, count them or run aggregates yet. MongoDB isn't\nsupported."],[2639,"Once this is enabled, add the plugin alongside the Prisma plugin. This example also uses\n`graphql-scalars` to register the `D