lver"],[2933,"When using a function for `authScopes` on a type, the function will be run once for each\ninstance of that type in the response. It will be run lazily when the first field for that object is\nresolved, and its result will be cached and reused by all fields for that instance of the type."],[2593,"When using select mode, it's recommended to add selections to both the interface and the object\ntypes that implement them. Selections are not inherited and will fallback to the default selection\nwhich includes all scalar columns."],[1625,"When using simpleObjects in combination with other plugins like authorization, those plugins may use\n`unknown` as the parent type in some custom fields (eg. `parent` of a permission check function on\na field)."],[1803,"When using the chaining API, you can transform data as part of the validation process:"],[1655,"When using this schema in a server, pass a new listener map for each operation and arrange for the\ntransport to close its iterator on disconnect. The local check makes that lifecycle explicit:"],[2155,"When viewer fields differ by role, Viewer can instead be an interface with object variants\nfor each role. The following definition uses this structure; Interfaces explains\nits implementations:"],[1772,"When your HTTP middleware already owns a request segment, use that segment as `parent` and leave\nclosing it to the middleware. Do not create a second root segment or close a segment owned by another\nintegration. Configure the X-Ray daemon or collector using the SDK's application setup so closed\nsegments can be exported."],[2246,"When your Prisma contract defines inherited models, such as a `Task` that can be a `Bug` or a\n`Feature`, expose their shared fields through a GraphQL interface. Each kind gets an object type,\nand the plugin chooses the right type for each row automatically."],[1278,"Whenever a resolver returns a User or list of Users, those objects will automatically be added to the\ndataloaders cache, so they can be re-used in other parts of the query."],[2496,"Wherever you use the model name, such as `'User'`, you get the default type. To return the variant,\npass its ref as `type`, for example from a root field:"],[377,"Which plugins you use is completely up to you. For my own projects, I will use the `simple-objects`,\n`scope-auth`, and `mocks` plugins in every project, and some of the other plugins as needed."],[1584,"Which produces the following graphql types:"],[1051,"While the internals of Pothos have almost entirely been re-written, the public API surface should\nhave minimal changes for most users. The first 2 sets of changes will cover the majority of\nchanges relevant to the majority of applications. To make the upgrade as simple as\npossible, some options were added to maintain the defaults and option names from `3.x` which are\ndescribed in the simple upgrade section below."],[2871,"While this plugin uses `scopes` as the term for its authorization mechanism, this plugin can easily\nbe used for role or permission based schemes, and is not intended to dictate a specific philosophy\naround how to authorize requests/access to resources."],[2570,"Will result in 2 calls to prisma, one to resolve everything except `oldPosts`, and a second to\nresolve everything inside `oldPosts`. Prisma can only resolve each relation once in a single query,\nso we need a separate query to handle the second `posts` relation."],[2225,"With `{ posts: true }` instead, the plugin only loads each post's columns. Anything selected\nunder the field, such as a post's `author`, needs an extra batched query. Use `nestedSelection`\nwhenever the field's type has relations or selects of its own."],[2489,"With `@defer` or `@stream`, read every incremental result inside the callback. They load after\nthe initial result, and fail once the transaction has ended."],[2328,"With `AsyncSelections: true` in the builder's types, the function can be async."],[2404,"With `AsyncSelections: true` in the builder's types, the function can be async."],[2008,"With `AsyncSelections: true`, await the result before passing it to Drizzle:"],[2036,"With `drizzleConnectionHelpers`, declare the extra in `query` rather than in `select`. Only `query`\nis read when the ordering is resolved."],[2122,"With `ScopeAuthPlugin`, scope checks that read the parent see what its type loads (its `select`, or\nevery column), even for a row returned from a plain `t.field`. A row missing that data is reloaded,\nso a row that no longer exists fails the field with `Unexpected error.`. To return a deleted row,\nload it with a `t.drizzleField` query before deleting it. Only top-level keys are checked, so a row\nwith a partly loaded relation isn't reloaded."],[2711,"With `ScopeAuthPlugin`, scope checks that read the parent see what its type loads (its `select`, or\nevery scalar and its `include`), even for a row returned from a plain `t.field`, unless the type\nsets `findUnique: null`. A row missing that data is reloaded, so a row that no longer exists fails\nthe field with `Unexpected error.`. To return a deleted row, use `t.prismaField` and pass its\n`query` to `delete`. Only top-level keys are checked, so a row with a partly loaded relation isn't\nreloaded. When `ScopeAuthPlugin` comes before `PrismaPlugin`, a field's own `select` is loaded after\nits `authScopes` run."],[330,"With `StrictResolveType: true` in `SchemaTypes`, use the record form, or an array of type names;\n`resolveType` may only return members. See\nStrict resolveType."],[2210,"With a configured builder, define the posts your API exposes\nand their author relation. These examples use the playground's `Post` and `User` models:"],[703,"With an initial count of `0`, the mutation returns `1` and the subscription emits\n`{ \"data\": { \"incrementedCount\": 1 } }`. Configure a subscription transport in your GraphQL server\nto deliver these results to clients."],[2792,"With Maya's context (`userId: 1`), `me` is an EditorViewer with the draft “Planning the spring\nexchange.” With Leo's context (`userId: 2`), it is an AuthorViewer with “Saving rainwater.”\nThe interface describes the result shape; the root lookup supplies the ownership restriction.\nNeither public author fields nor public post connections return those drafts."],[1458,"With Node.js 22 or newer, run from a checkout of the Pothos repository:"],[1652,"With Node.js 22 or newer, run from a checkout of the Pothos repository:"],[1556,"With Scope Auth, require scopes on the `node` and `nodes` fields, put scopes on the `Node`\ninterface or each node type, or disable the lookup fields. You can also enforce visibility where the\nnode loads, so inaccessible records look missing. See\nRelay node authorization for each option and its\nlimits."],[2102,"With Scope Auth, require scopes on the `node` and `nodes` fields, put scopes on the `Node`\ninterface or each node type, or disable the lookup fields. You can also enforce visibility where the\nnode loads, so inaccessible records look missing. See\nRelay node authorization for each option and its\nlimits."],[2725,"With Scope Auth, require scopes on the `node` and `nodes` fields, put scopes on the `Node`\ninterface or each node type, or disable the lookup fields. You can also enforce visibility where the\nnode loads, so inaccessible records look missing. See\nRelay node authorization for each option and its\nlimits."],[1724,"With static options, `wrap` runs once per field when the schema is built. A function-valued\n`tracing` option is evaluated per resolver invocation, and `wrap` runs for that invocation unless\nthe result is `false` or `null`. The builder's `default` callback can return a function in the same way."],[1324,"With the dataloader plugin"],[1559,"With the default encoding, Ada's local ID `\"1\"` becomes the global ID `\"VXNlcjox\"`.\nA `node(id: \"VXNlcjox\")` query returns Ada with that same global ID, wherever she appears in the\nschema. Global IDs identify records; they do not grant access. The loader must still enforce the\napplication's access rules when needed."],[1920,"With the opt-in, `select` functions, relation `query` callbacks, `relatedCount` `where` callbacks,\nand the `select` and `query` callbacks of `drizzleConnectionHelpers` may be async. Without it they\nare typed as synchronous, and an async callback is a type error."],[2673,"With the opt-in, `select` functions, relation `query` callbacks, `relationCount` `where` callbacks,\nand the `select` and `query` callbacks of `prismaConnectionHelpers` may be async. Without it they\nare typed as synchronous, and an async callback is a type error."],[1325,"With the prisma plugin"],[2214,"With the Relay plugin, `t.relatedConnection` adds a paginated list of\npublished posts to an author profile:"],[2004,"With the with-input plugin,\n`t.drizzleFieldWithInput` combines `t.drizzleField` with `t.fieldWithInput`. The `input` fields\nbecome an input object argument, and the resolver still receives the `query` function as its first\nargument. This alternative adds an `authorWithInput` lookup to the same public User type.\nInstall and register WithInputPlugin as described in Setup:"],[2614,"With the with-input plugin,\n`t.prismaFieldWithInput` combines `t.prismaField` with `t.fieldWithInput`. The `input` fields become\nan input object argument, and the resolver still receives the `query` to spread as its first\nargument."],[2344,"With the with-input plugin, `t.prismaFieldWithInput` combines\n`t.prismaField` with `t.fieldWithInput`, and takes `collection`, `query` or `resolve` the same way.\nThe `input` fields become an input object argument:"],[2805,"With this setup, a parent resolver has the option to include the author, but we have a fallback\nin case it does not."],[1783,"With transforms"],[1323,"With validation plugin"],[1824,"With-Input plugin"],[1825,"With-Input plugin docs for Pothos"],[1247,"Within one request, repeated keys share the same loader entry. For example, requesting\n`[\"3\", \"1\", \"3\"]` can load keys `\"3\"` and `\"1\"` once while returning three results in the\nrequested order. Later loads of the same key reuse the cached result. Pass a fresh context object\nfor each request to keep those cached records separate."],[2009,"Without `AsyncSelections`, the helper is typed as synchronous, consistent with the schema's\nselection callbacks. There is no per-call async option."],[804,"Without a `resolveType` function, GraphQL can use a `__typename` property on the returned data or\nan `isTypeOf` check on the member object types, just as it does for\ninterfaces. If your application uses classes,\nset `isTypeOf: (value) => value instanceof YourClass` on each member object type and return class\ninstances. Registering a class does not add this check automatically. If an object defines\n`isTypeOf`, that check must pass even when its type was selected by `resolveType` or `__typename`."],[399,"Wrap a type in an array to create a list argument, or use a list helper:"],[839,"Wrapping arguments and inputs"],[834,"Wrapping resolvers to add runtime functionality"],[680,"Write its SDL from a separate Node.js script:"],[2480,"Write several rows atomically"],[818,"Writing plugins"],[843,"Writing plugins for Pothos may seem a little intimidating at first, because the types used by Pothos\nare fairly complex. Fortunately, for many types of plugins, the process is actually pretty easy,\nonce you understand the core concepts of how Pothos's type system works. Don't worry if the\ndescriptions don't make complete sense at first. Going through the examples in this guide will\nhopefully make things seem a lot easier. This guide aims to cover a lot of the most common use cases\nfor creating plugins, but does not contain full API documentation. Exploring the types or source\ncode to see what all is available is highly encouraged, but should not be required for most use\ncases."],[686,"You can add the `schema-ast` plugin to have graphql-code-generator generate your schema file for\nyou."],[1594,"You can also access refs for the created input and payload objects so you can re-use them in other\nfields:"],[462,"You can also derive enum values from an existing object:"],[2767,"You can also load or create the prisma client dynamically for each request. This can be used to\nperiodically re-create clients or create read-only clients for certain types of users."],[636,"You can also pass a ref to `builder.objectType(GiraffeRef, { ... })` with the same options."],[428,"You can also pass the declared ref to `builder.objectType`. As an alternative to the `Post.implement`\ncall above, define its fields in separate calls:"],[1737,"You can also pass these options as the third argument to `createSpan` for an individual field.\nBoolean options there override the defaults; both `onSpan` callbacks run when both are supplied."],[2913,"You can also use a function in the authScopes option for types. This function receives the parent,\nthe context, and an `info` object described below, and should return a scope map."],[2565,"You can also use Prisma with plain object refs."],[797,"You can also use registered classes or object type names declared in `SchemaTypes` in the `types`\narray. See the Objects guide for these alternatives."],[2786,"You can also use variants when defining relations by providing a `type` option:"],[1430,"You can apply the `composeDirective` directive when building the subgraph schema:"],[2909,"You can change the default strategy used for top level auth scopes by setting the `defaultStrategy`\noption in the builder (defaults to `any`):"],[409,"You can change these defaults for a builder. Set each default in both the `SchemaTypes` generic\nfor TypeScript checking and the constructor options for runtime schema generation:"],[1872,"You can combine `schema` with the constraint keys. The plugin pipes your schema into the generated validator, so both run. Validation uses `parseAsync` before the resolver, so refinements may return a `Promise<boolean>`. The parsed value is passed to the resolver. This plugin does not infer transformed argument types; use the validation plugin when transforms change the resolver argument shape."],[1839,"You can configure the global default for input args when creating the builder by providing\n`WithInputArgRequired` in the builder's `SchemaTypes`, and setting `withInput.argOptions.required`."],[634,"You can create and implement a ref in one expression:"],[2547,"You can create reusable connection objects by using `builder.connectionObject`."],[527,"You can customize this config as needed, but the relevant parts are:"],[640,"You can declare backing models in `SchemaTypes` and pass it to `SchemaBuilder`. This lets you\nreference object types by their GraphQL names instead of importing refs. Using the `Giraffe`\ninterface from above:"],[2585,"You can define a media field that can pre-load the correct relations based on the graphql query:"],[2785,"You can define variant fields that reference one variant from another:"],[2650,"You can find an\nexample static generator here"],[2653,"You can find an example\ndynamic generator here"],[532,"You can generate a schema.graphql file with graphql-code-generator by adding the `schema-ast`\nplugin:"],[1493,"You can mock any field by adding a mock in the options passed to `builder.toSchema` under\n`mocks.{typeName}.{fieldName}`."],[1451,"You can now define a query to resolve this interface:"],[723,"You can override the TypeScript types of built-in scalars through `Scalars` too. This changes\nresolver checking; it does not replace GraphQL's parsing or serialization behavior. For example,\nto restrict your application to string IDs:"],[1256,"You can provide additional options for your dataloaders using `loaderOptions`."],[2078,"You can read more about the relation query builder api\nhere"],[1055,"You can restore the 3.x defaults by adding the Defaults versions to both the SchemaTypes and the\nbuilder options:"],[629,"You can run this schema immediately with a Playground action above. To run it in your own\napplication, add the server below."],[909,"You can then access the options through `this.builder.options` in your plugin, with everything\ncorrectly typed. The example reflects `nestedOptionsObject.exampleOption` in the object description:"],[540,"You can then configure graphql-code-generator to use introspection by passing the URL to your\ngraphql endpoint:"],[17,"You can try the schema immediately with its Playground action below. Follow the setup steps to run\nthe same schema in your own server. Using the playground explains the editor controls."],[1378,"You can use `builder.errorUnion` to manually construct an error union type that can be used with any field.  Fields returning an error union will automatically handle returned or thrown errors."],[2641,"You can use `builder.prismaCreate` to create input types for create mutations."],[2643,"You can use `builder.prismaUpdate` to create input types for update mutations."],[507,"You can use `t.listRef` to create a list of lists"],[536,"You can use the `printSchema` function from `graphql` to print your schema to a file, see\nPrinting Schemas for more details:"],[1582,"You can use the `relayMutationField` method to define relay compliant mutation fields. This method\ngenerates a mutation field, input object, and payload object. By default it omits\n`clientMutationId`; set `relay.clientMutationId` to `required` or `optional` to include matching\ninput and payload fields."],[2907,"You can use the built in `$any` and `$all` scope loaders to combine requirements for scopes. The\nabove example requires a request to have either the `employee` or `canReadArticles` scopes, and the\n`loggedIn` scope. `$any` and `$all` each take a scope map as their parameters, and can be nested\ninside each other."],[379,"You can use your application's TypeScript types as backing models with `objectRef`. For example,\nkeep the user data type in `src/types.ts` and define its GraphQL fields in `src/schema/user.ts`:"],[2612,"You do not need to use this method, and the `builder.prismaObject` method returns an object ref that\ncan be used like any other object ref (with `t.field`), but using `t.prismaField` will allow you to\ntake advantage of more efficient queries."],[2920,"You may have cases where groups of fields on a type are accessible using some shared condition. This\nis another case where `$granted` scopes can be helpful."],[925,"You may have noticed that almost every interface and type in `@pothos/core` take a generic that\nlooks like: `Types extends SchemaTypes`. This type is what allows Pothos and its plugins to share\ntype information across the entire schema, and to incorporate user defined types into that system.\nThese SchemaTypes are a combination of default types merged with the Types provided in the Generic\nparameter of the SchemaBuilder constructor, and includes a wide variety of useful types:"],[907,"You may have noticed that plugins are not instantiated by the user, and therefore users can't pass\noptions directly into your plugin when creating it. Instead, the recommended way to configure your\nplugin is by contributing new properties to the options object passed to the SchemaBuilder\nconstructor. This can be done by extending the `SchemaBuilderOptions` interface."],[2787,"You may run into circular reference issues if you use 2 prisma object refs to reference each other.\nTo avoid this, you can split out the field definition for one of the relationships using\n`builder.prismaObjectField`"],[1271,"You may run into type errors if you define 2 loadable objects that circularly reference each other\nin their definitions."],[1996,"You will be able to \"expose\" any column in the table, and GraphQL fields do not need to match the\nnames of the columns in your database. The returned `User` can be used like any other `ObjectRef`\nin Pothos."],[1403,"You will likely want to install @apollo/server as well, but it is not re